/* Definitions for the bulb under the Illumination Translation reader.
   Two sources, both keyed chapter ref -> Illumination verse-block label
   -> definition text: most from Chris's own book, "The Illumination
   Translation" (C.S. Knight), via tools/illum_defs_build.py; the rest
   authored directly for blocks that book doesn't cover, via
   tools/illum_defs_author.py -- plain, elementary retellings of what the
   verse says, not interpretation. Never hand-edit this file itself. */
const ILLUM_DEFS = {"Matthew 1": {"1:17": "The genealogy of Jesus traces His lineage through Abraham and David, fulfilling the Messianic prophecies about His royal descent.", "1:20–21": "Joseph, a just man, contemplates ending his engagement quietly when he learns of Mary's pregnancy. However, an angel appears to reassure him that Mary’s child is from the Holy Spirit, and that the child will save people from their sins.", "1:22–23": "The birth of Jesus fulfills the prophecy from Isaiah 7:14, that a virgin would give birth to a child called Emmanuel, meaning  God with us.", "1:24–25": "Joseph obeys the angel’s command and takes Mary as his wife. Jesus is born, and His name, meaning the Lord saves, signifies His mission.", "1:1–3": "This lists Jesus' family line starting with Abraham, going through Isaac, Jacob, and Judah, down to Perez and Ram.", "1:4–6": "The family line continues from Ram to Boaz, whose mother was Rahab, and Obed, whose mother was Ruth, down to King David.", "1:7–11": "The list continues through the kings of Judah, from Solomon to Josiah, up to the time the people were taken to Babylon.", "1:12–16": "After Babylon, the family line continues through Zerubbabel down to Joseph, the husband of Mary, whose son was Jesus.", "1:18–19": "Mary was engaged to Joseph, but before they were married she was found to be pregnant by the Holy Spirit. Joseph, not wanting to embarrass her, planned to quietly end the engagement."}, "Matthew 2": {"2:3–6": "The wise men from the east come to Jerusalem seeking the newborn King of the Jews, guided by a star. King Herod, troubled by this, consults the religious leaders, who confirm that the Messiah is to be born in Bethlehem, fulfilling the prophecy from Micah 5:2.", "2:12": "The wise men find Jesus, guided by the star, and worship Him, offering gifts of gold, frankincense, and myrrh. After being warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they leave by another route.", "2:13–15": "An angel warns Joseph in a dream to flee to Egypt with Mary and Jesus to escape Herod’s wrath. This flight to Egypt fulfills the prophecy from Hosea 11:1:  Out of Egypt have I called my son.", "2:16–18": "Herod, furious at being outwitted by the wise men, orders the massacre of all boys in Bethlehem aged two and under, fulfilling the prophecy from Jeremiah 31:15, which speaks of Rachel weeping for her children.", "2:22–23": "After Herod’s death, an angel instructs Joseph to return to Israel with Jesus and Mary. However, because Herod’s son Archelaus is ruling in Judaea, Joseph takes the family to Galilee, where they settle in Nazareth, fulfilling the prophecy that Jesus would be called a Nazarene.", "2:1–2": "Wise men came to Jerusalem from the east, asking where they could find the newborn King of the Jews — they had seen His star and came to worship Him.", "2:7–8": "Herod secretly asked the wise men exactly when the star had appeared, then sent them to Bethlehem, telling them to report back so he could go worship the child too.", "2:9–11": "The star led the wise men to the house where Jesus was. They worshiped Him and gave Him gifts of gold, frankincense, and myrrh.", "2:19–21": "After Herod died, an angel told Joseph in a dream that it was safe to take Mary and Jesus back to Israel."}, "Matthew 3": {"3:1–4": "John the Baptist begins his ministry in the wilderness of Judea, calling people to change their minds because the kingdom of heaven is near. His ministry fulfills the prophecy from Isaiah 40:3.", "3:5–6": "John lived a simple and humble life in the wilderness, wearing clothes made of camel’s hair and eating locusts and wild honey. Large crowds from Jerusalem and the surrounding region came to him, confessing their sins and being baptized in the Jordan River.", "3:7–10": "John confronts the Pharisees and Sadducees, calling them vipers and warning them that claiming descent from Abraham is not enough to escape God’s judgment. He emphasizes the need for true repentance and warns that judgment is near.", "3:11–12": "John humbly acknowledges that he is not worthy to carry the shoes of the one who is coming after him—the Messiah, Jesus—who will baptize with the Holy Spirit and fire. John describes the Messiah’s role in separating the righteous from the wicked.", "3:16–17": "Jesus comes to John to be baptized, and though John hesitates, Jesus insists that it is necessary to fulfill all righteousness. After Jesus is baptized, the heavens open, the Holy Spirit descends on Him like a dove, and God’s voice declares that Jesus is His beloved Son in whom He is well pleased.", "3:13–15": "Jesus came to John to be baptized. John didn't think he should baptize Jesus, but Jesus told him to go ahead, so John did."}, "Matthew 4": {"4:1–4": "Jesus, after fasting for forty days, is tempted by the devil in the wilderness. The first temptation challenges Him to turn stones into bread, but Jesus responds by quoting Scripture, affirming that life depends on God’s word, not physical sustenance alone.", "4:5–7": "The devil takes Jesus to the pinnacle of the temple and tempts Him to throw Himself down, quoting scripture to support his challenge. Jesus counters by quoting Deuteronomy 6:16, reminding the devil that it is wrong to test God.", "4:8–11": "The devil offers Jesus all the kingdoms of the world in exchange for worship. Jesus rebukes him, quoting Deuteronomy 6:13, declaring that only God is worthy of worship. After this, the devil leaves, and angels come to minister to Jesus.", "4:12–17": "Jesus begins His ministry in Galilee after John the Baptist is imprisoned. His move to Capernaum fulfills the prophecy from Isaiah 9:1-2, which foretold that those dwelling in darkness would see a great light.", "4:18–22": "Jesus calls His first disciples, Simon Peter, Andrew, James, and John, inviting them to follow Him and become  fishers of men.  They immediately leave their work and families to follow Jesus.", "4:23–25": "Jesus travels throughout Galilee, preaching the good news and healing all kinds of diseases and afflictions. His fame spreads, and great crowds follow Him from all over the region."}, "Matthew 5": {"5:3–12": "Jesus begins the Sermon on the Mount with the Beatitudes, describing the blessedness and happiness of those who are humble, merciful, and righteous, and encouraging those who suffer for His sake.", "5:13–16": "Jesus calls His disciples the salt of the earth and the light of the world,  encouraging them to live in a way that reflects God’s goodness and brings glory to Him.", "5:17–20": "Jesus clarifies that He has not come to abolish the Law but to fulfill it, and He warns that true righteousness must go beyond mere outward obedience.", "5:21–26": "Jesus  expands the commandment against murder to include anger and insults.", "5:27–30": "In this teaching, Jesus brings the law to perfection by revealing the heart of God’s standard. He shows that God’s requirement goes beyond outward actions to the inner motives of the heart. While the law forbids adultery, Jesus demonstrates that even lustful thoughts violate God’s perfect holiness. By addressing the root of sin, Jesus reveals the impossibility of achieving righteousness through human effort alone, pointing to the need for a Savior.", "5:31–32": "Jesus emphasizes the sanctity of marriage, allowing divorce only in cases of marital unfaithfulness.", "5:33–37": "Jesus teaches that oaths are unnecessary; a simple yes or no is sufficient, and anything beyond that is from evil.", "5:38–42": "Jesus instructs His followers to respond to evil with kindness, generosity, and love, rather than seeking revenge.", "5:43–48": "Jesus calls His disciples to love their enemies and to act with the same kindness and mercy that God extends to all people. He brings them to the true perfection of the law.", "5:1–2": "Jesus saw the crowds, went up a mountain, sat down, and began teaching His disciples."}, "Matthew 6": {"6:2–4": "Jesus teaches that acts of charity should be done in secret, not for public recognition. God, who sees in secret, will reward those who give with pure motives.", "6:5–8": "Jesus emphasizes that prayer should be sincere and private, not a public display for attention. He reminds His followers that God knows their needs before they ask.", "6:9–13": "Jesus provides a kingdom prayer, often called The Lord’s Prayer,  which emphasizes reverence for God, a desire for His will to be on earth as it is in heaven, daily provision, forgiveness, and deliverance from testing and evil.", "6:14–15": "Jesus teaches that receiving forgiveness from God is closely tied to our willingness to forgive others.", "6:16–18": "When fasting, Jesus instructs that it should be done discreetly, not for public recognition, but as a private act of devotion to God.", "6:19–21": "Jesus teaches that eternal treasures, not worldly riches, should be the focus of a believer’s life. What we value most reveals where our heart truly lies.", "6:22–23": "Jesus compares the eye to a lamp, teaching that what we focus on determines whether we are filled with spiritual light or darkness.", "6:24": "Jesus warns that it is impossible to serve both God and money. A person’s allegiance must be to one or the other.", "6:25–34": "Jesus encourages His followers not to worry about everyday needs like food or clothing. God, who provides for the birds and the flowers, will surely care for them. Instead, believers should prioritize seeking God’s kingdom and his righteousness, trusting that as you seek this first all else will be abundantly provided.", "6:1": "Jesus said not to give to the poor just to be seen and praised by others — if that is why you do it, you will not be rewarded by God."}, "Matthew 7": {"7:1–5": "Jesus warns against hypocrisy in judgment, teaching that we should first examine our own faults before criticizing others.", "7:6": "Jesus advises discernment in sharing spiritual truths, warning that sacred things should not be carelessly given to those who will not appreciate or respect them.", "7:7–11": "Jesus encourages persistence in prayer, assuring His followers that God, their loving Father, will give them good things when they ask.", "7:12": "Jesus summarizes the essence of the Law and the Prophets with the Golden Rule, encouraging people to treat others as they would like to be treated.", "7:13–14": "Jesus contrasts the narrow gate that leads to life with the wide gate that leads to destruction, urging His followers to choose the narrow way. Jesus was talking to the Jewish people here specifically. There were few Jews that believed he was God’s son.", "7:15–20": "Jesus warns against false prophets, teaching that their true character will be revealed by the  fruit  of their actions. Good trees produce good fruit, while bad trees produce bad fruit.", "7:21–23": "Jesus emphasizes that true discipleship is not just about calling Him  Lord  but about doing the will of God. Which is believing in the son John 6:40.", "7:24–27": "Jesus compares those who hear His teachings and obey them to a wise builder who constructs his house on a solid foundation. In contrast, those who hear but do not obey are like a foolish builder whose house collapses when storms come.", "7:28–29": "The crowd is amazed at Jesus’ teaching because He speaks with authority, unlike the scribes."}, "Matthew 8": {"8:1–4": "A leper comes to Jesus, seeking healing. Jesus, moved with compassion, touches and heals him. He instructs the man to follow Mosaic law by presenting himself to the priest.", "8:10–13": "A Roman centurion approaches Jesus to heal his servant. He shows remarkable faith, believing that Jesus' word alone is sufficient for healing. Jesus marvels at the centurion's faith and heals the servant instantly.", "8:14–17": "Jesus heals Peter’s mother-in-law by touching her hand, and she immediately recovers. That evening, He heals many others, fulfilling the prophecy from Isaiah that the Messiah would bear our sicknesses.", "8:18–22": "Two individuals express a desire to follow Jesus, but He explains the cost of discipleship. True disciples must prioritize following Him over their excuses.", "8:23–27": "In the middle of a violent storm, Jesus is asleep while the disciples are fearful for their lives. When they wake Him, Jesus rebukes the winds and the sea, demonstrating His authority over nature.", "8:28–34": "Jesus encounters two demon-possessed men who recognize Him as the Son of God. The demons plead to be sent into a herd of swine, and when Jesus grants their request, the swine run into the sea and drown. The locals, witnessing the event, ask Jesus to leave their region.", "8:5–9": "A Roman officer asked Jesus to heal his sick servant. When Jesus offered to come to his house, the officer said that wasn't necessary — Jesus could just say the word and the servant would be healed."}, "Matthew 9": {"9:1–8": "Jesus forgives the sins of a paralyzed man, causing controversy among the religious leaders. To demonstrate His authority, He heals the man, and the crowd glorifies God.", "9:9–13": "Jesus calls Matthew, a tax collector, to follow Him, leading to criticism from the Pharisees for associating with sinners. Jesus explains that His mission is to save sinners.", "9:14–17": "Jesus explains that His presence brings joy, but there will be a time for fasting after He departs. He uses the metaphor of new cloth and new wine to describe the new way He is bringing.", "9:23–26": "On His way to raise Jairus’ daughter, Jesus heals a woman who had been suffering from a bleeding condition for twelve years. When He arrives at Jairus’ house, He raises the young girl from the dead.", "9:27–31": "Jesus heals two blind men who follow Him, asking for mercy. He responds to their faith, restoring their sight.", "9:32–34": "Jesus casts out a demon from a man who was mute, enabling him to speak. The crowd is amazed, but the Pharisees accuse Jesus of working by the power of the devil.", "9:35–38": "Jesus is moved with compassion for the crowds, comparing them to sheep without a shepherd. He tells His disciples to pray for more laborers to work in the plentiful harvest.", "9:18–22": "A synagogue leader asked Jesus to come heal his daughter, who had just died. On the way, a woman who had been sick for twelve years touched Jesus' cloak and was healed."}, "Matthew 10": {"10:1–4": "Jesus calls His twelve apostles, giving them authority over unclean spirits and the power to heal every sickness and disease. The twelve include Simon Peter, James, John, and Judas Iscariot, who would later betray Jesus.", "10:11–15": "Jesus instructs His apostles to focus their ministry on the lost sheep of Israel, preaching that the kingdom of heaven is near. They are to heal the sick, raise the dead, and cast out demons without expecting payment. Jesus emphasizes the urgency and gravity of their mission, warning that those who reject their message will face severe judgment.", "10:21–23": "Jesus warns His disciples of the persecution they will face. They will be brought before rulers and falsely accused, but the Holy Spirit will guide their words. Jesus encourages them to endure the trials and promises salvation to those who persevere.", "10:32–39": "Jesus assures His disciples that they are valuable to God, even more than the sparrows. He calls them to boldness, urging them to proclaim the truth without fear of persecution. Jesus promises that those who confess Him before men will be acknowledged before His Father in heaven. Jesus warns that His message will bring division, even among families. Those who choose Him must be willing to forsake all else, taking up their cross and following Him. In losing their lives for Christ’s sake, they will find true life.", "10:40–42": "Jesus concludes by promising that those who receive His disciples are receiving Him, and they will be rewarded for their kindness and hospitality, even for the smallest acts of service.", "10:5–10": "Jesus sent His twelve disciples out to Israel, telling them to heal the sick and cast out demons, and not to bring extra money or supplies with them.", "10:16–20": "Jesus warned the disciples they would be sent out like sheep among wolves, and told them not to worry about what to say if they were arrested — God would give them the words.", "10:24–27": "Jesus told the disciples they shouldn't expect to be treated better than He was, and that whatever is hidden now will eventually be made known.", "10:28–31": "Jesus told them not to be afraid of people who can only harm the body — God cares for them more than sparrows and knows every hair on their heads."}, "Matthew 11": {"11:1–6": "John the Baptist, in prison, sends his disciples to ask if Jesus is the Messiah. Jesus affirms His identity by pointing to His works: healing the sick, raising the dead, and preaching to the poor.", "11:7–11": "Jesus affirms that John the Baptist is the greatest among those born of women but emphasizes that even the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than John.", "11:12–15": "Jesus acknowledges John the Baptist as the pivotal figure in God’s redemptive plan, marking the transition from the Old Testament prophets to the proclamation of the kingdom of heaven. He describes the kingdom as experiencing a kind of “violence,” meaning it faces opposition. He further identifies John as the prophesied Elijah who was to prepare the way for the Messiah.", "11:16–19": "Jesus compares His generation to children who are impossible to please. They criticized John for his ascetic lifestyle, claiming he had a demon, and they dismissed Jesus for eating and drinking with sinners. Their rejection of both John and Jesus reveals their resistance to God’s wisdom, but true wisdom is vindicated by its results—by the transformed lives of those who embrace it.", "11:25–27": "Jesus rebukes the cities of Chorazin, Bethsaida, and Capernaum for their lack of repentance, warning them of severe judgment. Jesus shifts from rebuke to praise, thanking the Father for revealing divine truths not to the wise and self-righteous, but to the humble and childlike.", "11:28–30": "Jesus invites the weary and burdened to come to Him for rest, offering a gentle and light burden in contrast to the legalistic demands of the Pharisees.", "11:20–24": "Jesus criticized the towns of Chorazin, Bethsaida, and Capernaum for not changing after seeing His miracles, saying other cities would have repented if they had seen the same things."}, "Matthew 12": {"12:1–8": "When the Pharisees accuse Jesus’ disciples of breaking the Sabbath by plucking and eating grain, Jesus responds by pointing out examples in Scripture where human need took precedence over ritual law. He declares that He is Lord of the Sabbath, emphasizing the importance of mercy over sacrifice.", "12:9–14": "Jesus heals a man with a withered hand on the Sabbath, challenging the Pharisees' legalistic understanding of the law. He highlights that doing good and showing mercy is lawful on the Sabbath, further angering the Pharisees, who begin plotting to destroy Him.", "12:15–21": "Jesus continues healing and ministering, fulfilling the prophecy of Isaiah, which foretold the coming of the Messiah as a gentle and compassionate servant who would bring justice and hope, not only to the Jews but also to the Gentiles.", "12:22–30": "The Pharisees accuse Jesus of casting out demons by the power of Beelzebub, the prince of demons. Jesus refutes their claim, explaining that a kingdom divided against itself cannot stand. He points out that His power to cast out demons is evidence that the kingdom of God has come upon them.", "12:31–32": "Jesus warns the Pharisees that all sins and blasphemies can be forgiven except blasphemy against the Holy Spirit, which is an unforgivable sin. This severe warning underscores the gravity of attributing the work of the Holy Spirit to evil.", "12:33–37": "Jesus teaches that a tree is known by its fruit, and likewise, people are known by their words and actions. He emphasizes the importance of our words, warning that everyone will give an account for every idle word they speak.", "12:38–42": "The Pharisees ask for a sign from Jesus, but He rebukes them, saying the only sign they will receive is the sign of Jonah. Just as Jonah was in the belly of the whale for three days, so the Son of Man will be in the earth for three days. Jesus references the repentance of Nineveh and the wisdom of Solomon, declaring that someone greater than both is standing before them.", "12:43–45": "Jesus warns that when an evil spirit leaves a person, it may return with even more wicked spirits if the person’s life remains empty and untransformed. He likens this to the state of the current generation, warning of the dangers of spiritual complacency.", "12:46–50": "When Jesus’ family comes to speak with Him, He redefines what it means to be part of His family. He declares that those who do the will of His Father in heaven are His true family."}, "Matthew 13": {"13:1–9": "Jesus tells the parable of the sower, describing how the seed (representing the Word of God) falls on different types of ground (representing people's hearts) with varying results. Some seeds are eaten by birds, some fall on rocky ground and wither, some are choked by thorns, but others fall on good soil and bear fruit.", "13:10–17": "The disciples ask Jesus why He speaks in parables. He explains that the parables reveal the mysteries of the kingdom to those who are open to hearing the truth, but they also hide it from those whose hearts are hardened.", "13:18–23": "Jesus explains the parable of the sower, revealing that the different types of soil represent the different responses people have to hearing God’s Word. The fruitful soil represents those who hear, understand, and bear fruit.", "13:24–30": "In this parable, Jesus compares the kingdom of heaven to a farmer who sowed good seed, but an enemy sowed weeds among the wheat. The farmer allows both to grow together until the harvest when the weeds will be separated and burned, and the wheat gathered into his barn.", "13:33": "The parables of the mustard seed and the leaven illustrate how the kingdom of heaven starts small but grows into something great. The mustard seed grows into a large tree, and the leaven transforms the entire batch of dough.", "13:34–35": "Jesus spoke to the crowds in parables to fulfill prophecy and reveal the hidden mysteries of the kingdom of God.", "13:36–43": "Jesus explains that the good seed in the parable of the weeds represents the children of the kingdom, while the weeds represent the children of the evil one. At the end of the age, the wicked will be separated and judged, and the righteous will shine in God's kingdom.", "13:47–50": "Jesus tells parables about the kingdom of heaven being like hidden treasure, a pearl of great value, and a fishing net. Each parable emphasizes the worth of the kingdom and the final separation of the righteous and the wicked at the end of the age.", "13:51–52": "Jesus concludes by saying that those instructed in the kingdom of heaven bring out both new and old treasures—representing the wisdom of the kingdom in both the new revelation of Jesus and the ancient truths of Scripture.", "13:53–58": "When Jesus returns to His hometown, the people are amazed at His wisdom and power but refuse to believe in Him because they know His family. As a result, Jesus does not perform many miracles there due to their lack of faith.", "13:31–32": "Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to a mustard seed — the smallest seed, which grows into a large tree.", "13:44": "Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and then sold everything he had to buy that field.", "13:45–46": "Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to a merchant who found one very valuable pearl and sold everything he had to buy it."}, "Matthew 14": {"14:6–12": "John the Baptist is imprisoned by Herod for condemning Herod’s unlawful marriage to Herodias. At Herod’s birthday celebration, Herodias’ daughter requests John’s head, and Herod reluctantly orders John’s execution. John’s disciples take his body and inform Jesus.", "14:15–21": "After hearing of John’s death, Jesus withdraws to a remote place, but the crowds follow Him. Jesus, moved with compassion, heals the sick and performs a miracle by feeding over five thousand people with just five loaves and two fish, leaving twelve baskets of leftovers.", "14:28–33": "Jesus sends the disciples ahead by boat while He prays alone. During the night, a storm arises, and Jesus walks on the water to them. Peter, at Jesus’ invitation, also walks on water but begins to sink when he doubts. Jesus rescues Peter, and when they return to the boat, the storm ceases.", "14:34–36": "Arriving in Gennesaret, the people recognize Jesus and bring the sick to Him. Those who touch even the hem of His garment are completely healed.", "14:1–5": "Herod heard about Jesus and worried it was John the Baptist raised from the dead. Herod had arrested John and wanted him dead because of Herodias.", "14:13–14": "Jesus tried to get away alone by boat, but the crowds followed Him on foot. When He saw them, He felt compassion and healed the sick among them.", "14:22–27": "Jesus sent His disciples ahead by boat, then came to them later, walking on the water. They were scared, thinking He was a ghost, but He told them not to be afraid."}, "Matthew 15": {"15:1–9": "Jesus rebukes the Pharisees for valuing human traditions over God’s commandments, exposing their hypocrisy.", "15:10–20": "Jesus teaches that it is not external things like food that defile a person, but what comes from within—their thoughts and actions.", "15:21–28": "A Canaanite woman persistently asked for her daughter’s healing. Jesus praises her great faith and grants her request.", "15:32–39": "Jesus miraculously feeds four thousand men, besides women and children, with seven loaves and a few small fish.", "15:29–31": "By the Sea of Galilee, crowds brought the lame, blind, and mute to Jesus, and He healed them all, which amazed the people."}, "Matthew 16": {"16:1–4": "The Pharisees and Sadducees demand a sign, but Jesus rebukes them for their spiritual blindness and says they will only receive the sign of Jonah (a reference to His death and resurrection).", "16:5–12": "Jesus warns the disciples against the corrupt teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees, using leaven as a metaphor for their influence.", "16:13–20": "Peter declares Jesus to be the Christ, the Son of the living God. Jesus blesses Peter and speaks of building His church upon the  rock  of this confession.", "16:24–28": "Jesus foretells His suffering, death, and resurrection. Peter tries to prevent this, but Jesus rebukes him for opposing God’s plan.", "16:21–23": "Jesus told His disciples He would suffer, die, and rise again. Peter argued with Him about it, and Jesus told Peter he was thinking the way people think, not the way God thinks."}, "Matthew 17": {"17:1–8": "Jesus takes Peter, James, and John up a high mountain, where He is transfigured before them. His face shines like the sun, and His clothes become dazzling white. Moses and Elijah appear and speak with Jesus. A voice from the cloud affirms that Jesus is God's beloved Son, and the disciples are instructed to listen to Him.", "17:9–13": "As they descend from the mountain, Jesus tells the disciples not to speak of the vision until after His resurrection. When they ask about Elijah, Jesus explains that Elijah has already come in the form of John the Baptist, who was not recognized and was mistreated—just as the Son of Man will suffer.", "17:14–21": "A man approaches Jesus, asking for mercy on his son, who is possessed by a demon. The disciples had tried but were unable to cast out the demon. Jesus rebukes the demon, and the boy is healed. Later, Jesus tells His disciples that their inability to heal was due to their lack of faith, and He emphasizes the power of faith, even as small as a mustard seed.", "17:22–23": "Jesus predicts His death and resurrection, telling His disciples that He will be betrayed, killed, and raised again on the third day. The disciples are filled with sorrow upon hearing this.", "17:24–27": "When asked about paying the temple tax, Jesus teaches Peter that as children of the King (God), they are exempt. However, to avoid causing offense, Jesus instructs Peter to catch a fish, in whose mouth he will find the money needed to pay the tax for both of them."}, "Matthew 18": {"18:1–5": "Jesus teaches that humility, like that of a child, is the mark of greatness in the kingdom of heaven.", "18:10–14": "Jesus highlights the seriousness of sin and the need to protect those who are humble in faith. He warns against causing others to stumble, declaring that it is better to lose something valuable than to allow sin to separate us from God. Jesus also emphasizes the worth of every individual, reminding us that even the \"little ones\" are deeply valued by God, whose angels watch over them. His mission is to save the lost, demonstrating God’s love and pursuit of sinners. Jesus uses the parable of the lost sheep to illustrate God's desire to save every person who goes astray.", "18:15–20": "Jesus provides a step-by-step process for resolving conflict among believers. He emphasizes addressing the issue privately first, then escalating it to involve witnesses, and finally bringing it before the church if necessary. If reconciliation fails, the offender is to be treated as an outsider, reflecting the seriousness of unrepentance. Jesus also highlights the authority given to the church in binding and loosing matters, which reflects heavenly approval of righteous decisions. Furthermore, He reassures believers that when they gather in His name and agree in prayer, He is present with them, affirming His role in guiding unity and decision-making.", "18:23–35": "Jesus teaches the importance of forgiveness, using the parable of the unmerciful servant to illustrate the consequences of failing to forgive others as God has forgiven us.", "18:6–9": "Jesus warned strongly against causing others to sin, saying it would be better to lose a hand, foot, or eye than to be led into sin by it.", "18:21–22": "Peter asked how many times he should forgive someone — seven times? Jesus said seventy times seven, meaning there shouldn't be a limit."}, "Matthew 19": {"19:7–9": "The Pharisees challenge Jesus with questions about divorce. Jesus responds by pointing to God's original design for marriage, stating that a man and woman become one flesh and should not be separated. He also explains that Moses allowed divorce due to the hardness of people’s hearts, but it was not God’s original intention.", "19:10–12": "The disciples suggest that if marriage is so binding, it might be better not to marry. Jesus explains that celibacy is a calling for some, but not for all, and it is for those who can accept it for the sake of the kingdom of heaven.", "19:13–15": "When people bring children to Jesus for a blessing, the disciples try to send them away, but Jesus welcomes the children, teaching that the kingdom of heaven belongs to those with childlike faith.", "19:16–22": "A rich young man asks Jesus what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus tells him to keep the commandments, and when the young man claims to have done so, Jesus instructs him to sell his possessions and follow Him. The man, unwilling to part with his wealth, goes away sorrowful.", "19:23–26": "Jesus warns that it is difficult for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven, using the metaphor of a camel passing through the eye of a needle. The disciples, astonished, ask who can be saved, and Jesus replies that with God, all things are possible.", "19:27–30": "Peter asks what the disciples will receive for leaving everything to follow Jesus. Jesus promises that in the new world, they will sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel. He also assures that everyone who sacrifices for His sake will be rewarded many times over, and will inherit eternal life, but warns that the first will be last, and the last will be first.", "19:1–2": "Jesus left Galilee and went to Judea, and large crowds followed Him there, and He healed them.", "19:3–6": "The Pharisees asked Jesus if a man could divorce his wife for any reason. He answered that God made male and female to become one, and what God has joined together should not be separated."}, "Matthew 20": {"20:13–16": "In this parable, Jesus illustrates the kingdom of heaven as a vineyard owner who hires workers throughout the day. At the end of the day, all workers, regardless of when they started, receive the same wage. The first workers grumble, but the owner reminds them that they agreed to the wage. The parable teaches that God's grace and generosity are not based on human standards of fairness. The last will be first, and the first will be last.", "20:17–19": "Jesus, for the third time, predicts His suffering, death, and resurrection. He foretells the betrayal, condemnation by the religious leaders, and His delivery to the Gentiles, who will mock, scourge, and crucify Him, but assures His disciples that He will rise on the third day.", "20:24–28": "The mother of James and John asks for her sons to have positions of honor in Jesus’ kingdom, but Jesus explains that seat is not his to give but his fathers. He emphasizes that greatness in the kingdom of God is defined by service, not power. Jesus models this by stating that He came to serve and give His life as a ransom for many.", "20:29–34": "As Jesus leaves Jericho, two blind men call out to Him, recognizing Him as the Son of David. Despite the crowd's attempt to silence them, they persist. Jesus, moved with compassion, restores their sight, and they immediately begin to follow Him. This story highlights both the persistence of faith and Jesus' mercy.", "20:1–7": "Jesus told a story about a landowner who hired workers at different times of day — morning, midday, and late afternoon — promising each of them fair pay.", "20:8–12": "The landowner paid every worker the same amount, even the ones hired last. The workers hired first complained that this wasn't fair.", "20:20–23": "The mother of James and John asked Jesus to give her sons special seats next to Him in His kingdom. Jesus said that wasn't His decision to make — it belongs to the Father."}, "Matthew 21": {"21:6–11": "Jesus enters Jerusalem on a donkey, fulfilling the prophecy from Zechariah that the King would come in humility. The crowds celebrate His arrival by shouting,  Hosanna to the Son of David,  and spreading their garments and palm branches on the road, welcoming Him as the Messiah. The entire city is stirred, asking who this man is.", "21:12–17": "Upon entering the temple, Jesus drives out the merchants and moneychangers, declaring that the temple is meant to be a house of prayer, not a place for commerce. He heals the blind and the lame, and even the children in the temple praise Him as the Son of David. The religious leaders are displeased, but Jesus affirms the praise of the children, quoting from the Psalms.", "21:18–22": "Jesus curses a fig tree that bears no fruit, and it immediately withers, symbolizing judgment on fruitlessness. When the disciples express amazement, Jesus teaches them about the power of faith and prayer, explaining that with faith, nothing is impossible, and that whatever they ask in prayer, believing, will be granted.", "21:23–27": "The religious leaders question Jesus’ authority, but Jesus responds by asking them about the origin of John the Baptist’s authority. When they refuse to answer, fearing the reaction of the people, Jesus also refuses to explain His authority, revealing their lack of integrity.", "21:28–32": "Jesus tells the parable of two sons, one who initially refuses to obey his father but later repents, and another who agrees to obey but does not. Jesus uses this parable to expose the hypocrisy of the religious leaders, explaining that sinners who repent, like tax collectors and prostitutes, will enter the kingdom of God before those who claim righteousness but do not obey.", "21:42–46": "Jesus tells the parable of the wicked tenants, in which a landowner leases a vineyard to tenants who refuse to give him the fruit of the harvest and even kill his son. The parable symbolizes Israel's rejection of God's prophets and ultimately His Son, Jesus. Jesus warns that the kingdom of God will be taken away from those who reject Him and given to those who bear fruit. The religious leaders realize that Jesus is speaking about them and plot to arrest Him but fear the crowd.", "21:1–5": "Jesus sent two disciples to get a donkey and her colt, so He could ride into Jerusalem the way a prophecy said a king would.", "21:33–41": "Jesus told a story about tenant farmers who beat and killed the servants a landowner sent to collect his share of the harvest, and then killed his son too."}, "Matthew 22": {"22:8–14": "Jesus tells the parable of a king who invites guests to his son’s wedding feast, but the invited guests refuse to come. The king then invites anyone from the streets, both good and bad, but one man attends without the proper garment and is cast out. This parable illustrates God's invitation to His kingdom and the importance of being prepared.", "22:15–22": "The Pharisees and Herodians attempt to trap Jesus with a question about paying taxes to Caesar. Jesus, knowing their intentions, replies that they should give to Caesar what belongs to him and to God what belongs to God, leaving His challengers amazed.", "22:23–33": "The Sadducees, who deny the resurrection, present Jesus with a hypothetical situation to challenge Him. Jesus corrects them by explaining that in the resurrection, people will not marry, but will be like angels. He also affirms the resurrection by pointing out that God is the God of the living, not the dead.", "22:34–40": "When asked which commandment is the greatest, Jesus responds by summarizing the entire law with two commandments: to love God with all your heart, soul, and mind, and to love your neighbor as yourself.", "22:41–46": "Jesus turns the tables on the Pharisees by asking them a question about the Messiah. He quotes David, who called the Messiah  Lord,  to show that the Christ is more than just the son of David. The Pharisees are unable to answer, and they stop questioning Him.", "22:1–7": "Jesus told a story about a king who invited people to his son's wedding feast, but they refused to come and even killed his messengers, so the king destroyed them and burned their city."}, "Matthew 23": {"23:1–7": "Jesus criticizes the scribes and Pharisees for their hypocrisy. While they teach the law, they do not practice it. They place heavy burdens on others but are unwilling to help. Their actions are done for public praise, seeking honor and recognition.", "23:8–12": "Jesus instructs His disciples not to seek titles of honor, reminding them that there is only one true Master and Father, who is God. Greatness in His kingdom comes through humility and service to others.", "23:29–33": "Jesus delivers seven woes to the scribes and Pharisees, exposing their hypocrisy. They focus on outward appearances, legal technicalities, and rituals, but neglect the more important matters of justice, mercy, and faith. Jesus condemns their spiritual blindness and calls out their self-righteousness.", "23:37–39": "Jesus mourns over Jerusalem, lamenting their rejection of the prophets and His own ministry. He longs to gather them under His protection like a hen gathers her chicks, but they were unwilling. He warns of the desolation that is coming upon the city until they recognize Him as the Messiah.", "23:13–15": "Jesus criticized the religious leaders for keeping people out of the kingdom, for taking advantage of widows while pretending to be pious, and for making new converts even worse than themselves.", "23:16–22": "Jesus criticized the leaders for their confusing rules about oaths — treating the gold as more important than the temple it belongs to — and said swearing by anything really means swearing by God.", "23:23–24": "Jesus criticized the leaders for carefully tithing small things like spices while ignoring bigger things like justice, mercy, and faithfulness.", "23:25–28": "Jesus said the leaders were like a cup that is clean outside but dirty inside, or like tombs that look nice outside but are full of dead bones inside.", "23:34–36": "Jesus said He would send prophets and teachers, and some would be killed — and He held that generation responsible for all the innocent blood shed since Abel."}, "Matthew 24": {"24:1–3": "Jesus predicts the destruction of the temple, saying that not one stone will be left upon another. This prophecy speaks of the future fall of Jerusalem and the temple’s destruction, which occurred in AD 70.", "24:9–14": "Jesus describes the condition of Israel at end of the age: false prophets, wars, famines, pestilences, and earthquakes. He warns his disciples to be on guard against deception and encourages them to endure to the end. The gospel of the kingdom will be preached to all nations before the end comes.", "24:23–28": "Jesus warns of a time of great tribulation, unlike anything the world has seen before. He cautions against false messiahs and prophets who will perform deceptive signs and wonders. The return of Christ will be unmistakable, as clear as lightning flashing across the sky.", "24:29–31": "After the tribulation, cosmic disturbances will signal the return of Christ. He will come in glory on the clouds of heaven, and His angels will gather the elect (The Jews) from every corner of the earth.", "24:32–35": "Jesus uses the parable of the fig tree to illustrate that when the signs of the end begin to appear, the return of Christ is near. His words are eternal, even though heaven and earth will pass away.", "24:42–44": "Jesus warns that no one knows the day or hour of His return. It will be as sudden and unexpected as in the days of Noah, when people were unprepared for the flood. He calls His followers to remain watchful and ready at all times.", "24:45–51": "Jesus contrasts the faithful servant, who is found diligently working when his master returns, with the wicked servant, who lives carelessly because he thinks his master’s return is delayed. The faithful servant will be rewarded, while the wicked servant will face severe judgment.", "24:4–8": "Jesus warned of false messiahs, wars, famines, and earthquakes — but said these would only be the beginning, not the end.", "24:15–22": "Jesus warned that when a certain sign appears, people in Judea should flee at once, because a time of great suffering was coming, though it would be cut short for the sake of God's people.", "24:36–41": "No one knows the exact day Jesus will return, not even the angels. It will happen suddenly, like the flood in Noah's time, while people are going about their normal lives."}, "Matthew 25": {"25:1–13": "Jesus tells the parable of the ten virgins to emphasize the importance of being prepared for His return.", "25:24–30": "The parable of the talents teaches that we must faithfully use the resources God has entrusted to us, for we will be held accountable for how we have used them.", "25:41–46": "Jesus speaks of the final judgment, where the righteous will inherit eternal life, and the unrighteous will face eternal punishment based on how they treated  the least of these.", "25:14–18": "Jesus told a story about a master who gave his servants money to manage while he was away — five, two, and one — and two of them put the money to work while the third buried his.", "25:19–23": "When the master returned, he praised the two servants who had doubled his money, telling them they had done well and could now be trusted with more.", "25:31–33": "Jesus said that when He returns in glory, He will separate people the way a shepherd separates sheep from goats.", "25:34–40": "Jesus said that whatever people did for someone hungry, thirsty, a stranger, or in prison, they did for Him."}, "Matthew 26": {"26:1–5": "Jesus tells His disciples that His crucifixion is imminent, coinciding with the Passover. Meanwhile, the religious leaders plot to arrest and kill Him but decide not to do so during the feast to avoid causing a riot.", "26:6–13": "While Jesus is in Bethany, a woman anoints Him with expensive ointment, preparing Him for burial. Some of the disciples object to what they see as waste, but Jesus honors the woman’s act of devotion, stating that wherever the gospel is preached, her act will be remembered.", "26:14–16": "Judas Iscariot, one of Jesus' twelve disciples, goes to the chief priests and agrees to betray Jesus for thirty pieces of silver. From that point on, he looks for an opportunity to hand Jesus over.", "26:26–30": "During the Last Supper, Jesus reveals that one of His disciples will betray Him. Each disciple asks if it is them, and Jesus identifies Judas as the betrayer. Jesus then institutes the Lord’s Supper, sharing bread and wine as symbols of His body and blood, signifying the new covenant that will be established through His sacrifice.", "26:31–35": "Jesus predicts that all His disciples will fall away that night, quoting a prophecy about the shepherd being struck and the sheep scattered. Peter insists that he will never deny Jesus, but Jesus foretells that Peter will deny Him three times before the rooster crows. Peter and the other disciples affirm their loyalty, but they will soon face the reality of their weakness.", "26:40–46": "Jesus prays in great distress in the Garden of Gethsemane, asking if there is any way to avoid the suffering ahead but ultimately submitting to the Father’s will. He finds His disciples asleep, despite His call for them to watch and pray, recognizing that their spirit is willing, but their flesh is weak. After praying three times, Jesus prepares to face His betrayal and arrest.", "26:51–56": "Judas leads a crowd to arrest Jesus, identifying Him with a kiss. Peter tries to defend Jesus with a sword, cutting off the ear of the high priest’s servant, but Jesus stops him, reminding him that He could call on divine help if He wished. Jesus submits to arrest, fulfilling the Scriptures, and His disciples abandon Him.", "26:64–68": "Jesus is brought before the Sanhedrin, where false witnesses testify against Him. When asked directly if He is the Christ, the Son of God, Jesus affirms it, and the high priest declares Him guilty of blasphemy, a crime punishable by death. The religious leaders mock and abuse Jesus.", "26:69–75": "As Jesus faces trial, Peter, who had promised loyalty, denies knowing Him three times, just as Jesus had foretold. When the rooster crows, Peter realizes what he has done and is filled with sorrow, weeping bitterly over his betrayal.", "26:17–19": "Jesus told His disciples where to go to prepare the Passover meal, and they did exactly as He said.", "26:20–25": "At the meal, Jesus told the disciples one of them would betray Him. Each one asked if it was him, and when Judas asked, Jesus told him it was.", "26:36–39": "In the garden of Gethsemane, Jesus was deeply sad and prayed that He wouldn't have to go through what was coming — but said He wanted God's will, not His own.", "26:47–50": "Judas arrived with an armed crowd and pointed out Jesus by kissing Him. Jesus didn't resist, and they arrested Him.", "26:57–63": "Jesus was brought before the high priest and the council. False witnesses couldn't agree on their stories, and Jesus stayed quiet until the high priest asked directly if He was the Son of God."}, "Matthew 27": {"27:1–2": "The chief priests and elders conspire to put Jesus to death and deliver Him to the Roman governor, Pontius Pilate.", "27:3–10": "Judas, filled with remorse, returns the thirty pieces of silver and confesses his sin. After throwing the money into the temple, he goes and hangs himself. The chief priests use the silver to buy a potter’s field, fulfilling prophecy.", "27:11–14": "Jesus is brought before Pilate, who questions Him about the accusations of the Jews. Despite the false charges, Jesus remains silent, causing Pilate to marvel at His composure.", "27:15–23": "Pilate offers to release one prisoner, either Jesus or Barabbas. Influenced by the chief priests, the crowd chooses Barabbas and demands Jesus’ crucifixion, despite Pilate’s recognition of Jesus’ innocence.", "27:24–26": "Pilate, seeing he could not change the crowd’s decision, symbolically washes his hands, declaring himself innocent of Jesus' blood. The people accept responsibility for His death. Pilate then releases Barabbas and has Jesus scourged and handed over to be crucified.", "27:27–31": "The soldiers mock Jesus, dressing Him in a scarlet robe and placing a crown of thorns on His head. They bow in false homage and beat Him before leading Him away to be crucified.", "27:39–44": "Jesus is crucified at Golgotha, and a sign is placed above Him declaring,  This is Jesus, the King of the Jews.  The crowds, the religious leaders, and even the criminals crucified with Him mock and insult Him.", "27:54–56": "At the moment of Jesus' death, darkness covers the land, and the veil of the temple is torn in two, symbolizing the new access to God. The earth quakes, and even the Roman centurion recognizes Jesus as the Son of God.", "27:57–61": "Joseph of Arimathaea, a wealthy disciple of Jesus, takes His body and buries it in his own tomb. Mary Magdalene and the other women observe where He is laid.", "27:62–66": "The religious leaders, remembering Jesus' prophecy about His resurrection, request that Pilate secure the tomb to prevent His disciples from stealing the body. Pilate grants their request, and a guard is placed at the tomb, sealing it with a stone.", "27:32–34": "A man named Simon was made to carry Jesus' cross. At Golgotha, Jesus was offered wine mixed with something bitter, but He wouldn't drink it.", "27:35–38": "The soldiers crucified Jesus and divided His clothes among themselves, then put up a sign above Him that read 'King of the Jews.' Two criminals were crucified next to Him.", "27:45–47": "Darkness covered the land for three hours, and Jesus cried out asking why God had forsaken Him. Some people thought He was calling for Elijah.", "27:48–50": "Someone gave Jesus sour wine on a sponge to drink, and then Jesus cried out again and died.", "27:51–53": "When Jesus died, the temple curtain tore in two, the ground shook, and tombs broke open — some who had died were raised to life."}, "Matthew 28": {"28:8–10": "Mary Magdalene and the other Mary go to the tomb at dawn, only to find the stone rolled away by an angel. The angel tells them not to be afraid because Jesus has risen. They are instructed to go and tell the disciples that He will meet them in Galilee. On their way to inform the disciples, the women encounter the risen Jesus, who greets them. They fall at His feet and worship Him. Jesus reassures them and repeats the message that He will meet His disciples in Galilee.", "28:11–15": "The soldiers who were guarding the tomb report what happened to the chief priests. The religious leaders bribe the soldiers to spread a false story, claiming that Jesus' disciples stole His body while they slept.", "28:16–20": "Jesus meets His disciples on a mountain in Galilee. He commissions them to go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. He promises to be with them always, even to the end of the world.", "28:1–4": "When the women came to the tomb, there was an earthquake — an angel rolled the stone away and sat on it, and the guards fainted from fear.", "28:5–7": "The angel told the women not to be afraid — Jesus wasn't there because He had risen, just as He said He would — and told them to tell the disciples He would meet them in Galilee."}, "Mark 1": {"1:1–3": "Mark opens with a reference to prophecy, introducing the gospel by pointing to John the Baptist, the forerunner who prepares the way for Jesus.", "1:7–8": "John the Baptist baptizes people in the Jordan River, preaching repentance and announcing the coming of Jesus, who will baptize with the Holy Spirit.", "1:9–11": "Jesus is baptized by John, and as He comes out of the water, the heavens open, and the Holy Spirit descends upon Him like a dove. The voice of God the Father declares Jesus to be His beloved Son.", "1:12–13": "After His baptism, Jesus is led by the Spirit into the wilderness, where He fasts for forty days and is tempted by Satan. Despite the trials, He remains steadfast, and angels minister to Him.", "1:14–15": "Following John’s imprisonment, Jesus begins His public ministry, proclaiming that the kingdom of God has arrived and calling people to repentance and faith.", "1:16–20": "Jesus calls Simon (Peter) and Andrew, as well as James and John, to follow Him and become fishers of men. They leave their work as fishermen and immediately follow Him.", "1:23–28": "Jesus teaches with authority in the synagogue, and the people are astonished. He casts out an unclean spirit from a man, and news of His power spreads rapidly throughout the region.", "1:29–31": "Jesus heals Peter’s mother-in-law, who was bedridden with a fever. She is immediately restored to health and begins to serve them.", "1:32–34": "At sunset, many people come to Jesus, bringing their sick and those possessed by demons. Jesus heals them and casts out the demons, commanding them not to speak because they knew who He was.", "1:35–39": "Despite His growing popularity, Jesus makes time to pray in a solitary place. When the disciples find Him, He expresses His mission to preach in other towns and continues His ministry throughout Galilee.", "1:43–45": "A leper approaches Jesus with faith, asking to be made clean. Jesus, moved by compassion, heals him and instructs him to tell no one, but the man spreads the news, causing large crowds to seek out Jesus.", "1:4–6": "John was in the wilderness, baptizing people and telling them to turn back to God so their sins would be forgiven. People from all over Judea and Jerusalem came out to be baptized by him. He wore clothes made of camel hair and ate locusts and wild honey.", "1:21–22": "Jesus went into the synagogue in Capernaum on the Sabbath and taught there. The people were amazed, because He taught with authority, unlike the teachers of the law.", "1:40–42": "A man with leprosy knelt before Jesus and asked to be made clean. Jesus touched him and said, 'I am willing, be clean,' and the leprosy left him right away."}, "Mark 2": {"2:6–12": "A paralytic man is brought to Jesus by his friends, and Jesus forgives his sins, seeing their faith. When the scribes question this in their hearts, Jesus proves His authority by also healing the man, who then walks away, leaving the crowd amazed.", "2:15–17": "Jesus calls Levi (Matthew), a tax collector, to follow Him. When Jesus eats with tax collectors and sinners, the Pharisees question His actions, but Jesus responds by stating that His mission is to call sinners to repentance, not the righteous.", "2:21–22": "When questioned about why His disciples do not fast like the disciples of John or the Pharisees, Jesus explains that fasting is inappropriate while He, the bridegroom, is with them. He also uses metaphors of new cloth and new wine to show that His coming represents something new and cannot be confined to old practices.", "2:23–28": "Jesus and His disciples are criticized by the Pharisees for plucking grain on the Sabbath. Jesus reminds them of how David ate the consecrated bread when in need and teaches that the Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath, asserting His authority as Lord of the Sabbath.", "2:1–5": "So many people crowded into the house to hear Jesus that four men lowered their paralyzed friend down through a hole in the roof to reach Him. Seeing their faith, Jesus told the man his sins were forgiven.", "2:13–14": "Jesus saw a man named Levi sitting at the tax booth and told him, 'Follow Me.' Levi got up and followed Him.", "2:18–20": "People asked Jesus why His disciples didn't fast like John's disciples and the Pharisees did. Jesus said His followers wouldn't fast while He, the bridegroom, was still with them — but they would fast after He was taken away."}, "Mark 3": {"3:1–6": "Jesus heals a man with a withered hand in the synagogue on the Sabbath, despite being closely watched by the Pharisees. He questions them about whether it is lawful to do good on the Sabbath, and when they remain silent, He heals the man. The Pharisees, angered by this, begin to plot with the Herodians to kill Jesus.", "3:7–12": "Jesus continues His healing ministry, drawing large crowds from various regions. Many come to Him for healing, and even demons recognize Him as the Son of God. Jesus commands the unclean spirits not to reveal His identity.", "3:13–19": "Jesus appoints twelve apostles to be with Him, to preach, and to have authority to heal and cast out demons. These twelve include Simon Peter, James, John, and Judas Iscariot, who would eventually betray Him.", "3:28–30": "The scribes reject Jesus' deity and accuse Him of casting out demons by the power of Beelzebub (Satan). In response, Jesus explains that a kingdom divided against itself cannot stand. He then warns the unbelievers that blasphemy against the Holy Spirit is the one sin that will not be forgiven.", "3:31–35": "When Jesus is told that His mother and brothers are seeking Him, He uses the moment to teach that His true family consists of those who do the will of God. Which is to believe on the son. (John 6:40)", "3:20–22": "Jesus was so busy with the crowds that He and His disciples couldn't even eat. His family set out to take charge of Him because people were saying He had lost His mind, and religious leaders from Jerusalem said He cast out demons by the power of the ruler of demons.", "3:23–27": "Jesus answered that Satan can't cast out Satan — a kingdom or household divided against itself can't stand. He said no one can rob a strong man's house without first tying him up."}, "Mark 4": {"4:1–9": "Jesus teaches the Parable of the Sower, describing how the Word of God is received differently by people, depending on the condition of their hearts. The seed that falls on good soil represents those who hear the Word, accept it, and bear fruit.", "4:10–12": "Jesus explains to His disciples that the parables are meant to reveal the mysteries of the kingdom of God to those who are open to hearing, while others may hear but not understand because their hearts are closed.", "4:13–20": "Jesus explains the parable, identifying the different types of soil as different responses to the Word of God. Only those who receive the Word with an open heart, represented by the good soil, will produce fruit in their lives.", "4:24–25": "Jesus compares the truth of the kingdom to a lamp, meant to shine and be seen. He warns His listeners to be careful how they hear and respond, as those who seek to understand will be given more, while those who ignore the truth will lose even what they have.", "4:26–29": "Jesus tells the parable of the growing seed, illustrating the mysterious way the kingdom of God grows in the hearts of people. Though we may not fully understand the process, God is at work, and the harvest will come in due time.", "4:30–32": "Jesus compares the kingdom of God to a mustard seed, the smallest of all seeds, which grows into the largest of plants. This parable illustrates how the kingdom starts small but grows into something great and expansive.", "4:33–34": "Jesus consistently uses parables to teach the crowds, but He takes time to explain the meaning of the parables to His disciples in private.", "4:35–41": "While crossing the sea, a violent storm arises, threatening to sink the boat. Jesus, asleep during the storm, is awakened by His fearful disciples. He rebukes the wind and waves, bringing a great calm, and questions their lack of faith, leaving the disciples in awe of His authority over nature.", "4:21–23": "Jesus said no one lights a lamp and then hides it — it's meant to be put where it can be seen. Whatever is hidden will eventually be brought out into the open."}, "Mark 5": {"5:18–20": "Jesus encounters a man possessed by a legion of demons. After casting the demons into a herd of pigs, which rush into the sea and drown, the man is restored to his right mind. Jesus tells him to share what God has done for him, and the man spreads the news throughout Decapolis.", "5:38–43": "While on His way to heal Jairus’ dying daughter, a woman with a bleeding condition for twelve years touches Jesus' garment in faith and is healed. Jesus acknowledges her faith before continuing to Jairus' home, where He raises Jairus' daughter from the dead, astonishing all who witness the miracle.", "5:1–5": "Jesus and His disciples arrived in the country of the Gadarenes, where a man with an unclean spirit lived among the tombs. No chains could hold him, and he spent his time crying out and cutting himself with stones.", "5:6–10": "The man ran to Jesus and begged Him not to torment him. When Jesus asked his name, he answered 'Legion,' because many spirits were in him, and they begged Jesus not to send them out of the region.", "5:11–13": "The evil spirits begged to be sent into a nearby herd of pigs. Jesus allowed it, and the pigs rushed down the hillside into the lake and drowned.", "5:14–17": "The men caring for the pigs ran and told everyone what had happened. When the people came and saw the man sitting there calm and in his right mind, they were afraid and asked Jesus to leave their region.", "5:21–24": "A synagogue leader named Jairus came and fell at Jesus' feet, begging Him to come heal his dying daughter. Jesus went with him, and a large crowd followed, pressing in from every side.", "5:25–29": "A woman who had been bleeding for twelve years, and who had spent all her money on doctors with no help, came up behind Jesus in the crowd and touched His clothes. The moment she touched Him, the bleeding stopped.", "5:30–34": "Jesus felt power go out from Him and asked who had touched Him. The frightened woman came forward and told Him the truth, and He told her that her faith had healed her.", "5:35–37": "Messengers arrived to say Jairus's daughter had died and there was no need to bother Jesus anymore. But Jesus told Jairus not to be afraid, just to keep trusting, and He took only Peter, James, and John with Him."}, "Mark 6": {"6:1–6": "Jesus returns to His hometown of Nazareth, where the people are astonished by His wisdom and miracles but are offended because they knew Him as a carpenter. Because of their unbelief, Jesus is unable to perform many miracles there, except for healing a few sick people.", "6:7–13": "Jesus sends the twelve disciples out in pairs, giving them authority over unclean spirits and instructing them to travel lightly, depending on the hospitality of others. They go out, preaching repentance, casting out demons, and healing the sick.", "6:25–29": "King Herod, after hearing about Jesus, fears that He might be John the Baptist risen from the dead. Mark recounts how Herod imprisoned John for condemning his marriage to Herodias, and how, due to a rash promise, Herod was manipulated into beheading John.", "6:42–44": "Jesus performs the miracle of feeding five thousand men (not including women and children) with just five loaves and two fish. After blessing and breaking the bread, everyone eats, and twelve baskets of leftovers are collected.", "6:45–52": "Jesus sees His disciples struggling to row against the wind in the middle of the night. He walks on water to reach them, calming their fears and stilling the wind when He enters the boat. The disciples are astonished, still not fully understanding the miracles He performed.", "6:53–56": "After arriving in Gennesaret, Jesus is recognized by the people, who bring their sick to Him, believing that even touching the hem of His garment will heal them. Everyone who touches Him is healed.", "6:14–16": "King Herod heard about Jesus and worried that John the Baptist, whom he had beheaded, had come back to life. Other people thought Jesus was Elijah or one of the prophets.", "6:17–20": "Herod had arrested John and put him in prison because John had told him it was wrong to marry his brother's wife, Herodias. Herodias wanted John dead, but Herod protected him because he knew John was a righteous man.", "6:21–24": "At Herod's birthday banquet, Herodias's daughter danced and pleased him so much that he promised to give her anything she asked, even half his kingdom. She asked her mother what to request, and her mother told her to ask for John the Baptist's head.", "6:30–34": "The apostles returned and told Jesus everything they had done. He invited them to come rest in a quiet place, but the crowds followed anyway, and Jesus felt compassion for them and taught them.", "6:35–41": "It was getting late, and the disciples wanted to send the crowd away to buy food. Jesus had them bring five loaves and two fish, gave thanks, and had the disciples hand out the food to everyone."}, "Mark 7": {"7:9–13": "Jesus rebukes the Pharisees and scribes for prioritizing man-made traditions over the commandments of God. He points out how they twist the law to avoid responsibility, such as neglecting the care of their parents under the guise of Corban (a gift to God).", "7:17–23": "Jesus teaches that true defilement comes from within a person’s heart, not from external things like food. Sinful actions and attitudes—such as evil thoughts, greed, and pride—are what make a person unclean.", "7:24–30": "A Gentile woman approaches Jesus, pleading for her daughter to be healed. Despite an initial challenge from Jesus about His mission to Israel, her persistent faith moves Him to heal her daughter, showing that God’s mercy extends beyond Israel.", "7:31–37": "Jesus heals a deaf and mute man by touching his ears and tongue and commanding them to be opened. Despite His instructions to keep it quiet, the news spreads that everything he does is good, and the people are amazed by His power.", "7:1–8": "Pharisees and teachers of the law criticized Jesus' disciples for eating without washing their hands the ritual way. Jesus told them they were following human traditions while ignoring the command of God.", "7:14–16": "Jesus told the crowd that nothing that goes into a person from outside can make them unclean — it's what comes out of a person that makes them unclean."}, "Mark 8": {"8:1–10": "Jesus has compassion on the crowd, which has been with Him for three days without food. He miraculously feeds four thousand people with seven loaves of bread and a few small fish, and seven baskets of leftovers are collected.", "8:11–13": "The Pharisees demand a sign from Jesus to test Him. He refuses, expressing frustration at their unbelief, and departs without granting their request.", "8:18–21": "Jesus warns His disciples to beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Herod, which refers to their corrupt influence. The disciples misunderstand, thinking Jesus is concerned about bread, but He reminds them of His miracles of provision and rebukes their lack of understanding.", "8:22–26": "Jesus heals a blind man in Bethsaida, but the healing occurs in stages. At first, the man sees people as blurry shapes, like trees walking, but after Jesus touches him again, his sight is fully restored. Jesus instructs him to keep the miracle quiet.", "8:27–30": "Jesus asks His disciples who people say He is, and they respond with various answers. Peter boldly declares that Jesus is the Christ, the Messiah. Jesus instructs them to keep this revelation private for the time being.", "8:31–33": "Jesus begins to teach His disciples that He must suffer, be rejected, killed, and rise again after three days. Peter, not understanding, tries to rebuke Him, but Jesus strongly rebukes Peter, identifying the rebuke as coming from a misunderstanding of God's plan.", "8:34–38": "Jesus teaches that true discipleship requires looking away from self and a willingness to take up one's cross and follow Him. He emphasizes that gaining the world means nothing if one loses their soul, and warns that those ashamed of Him will face His judgment.", "8:14–17": "The disciples had forgotten to bring bread and had only one loaf. Jesus warned them to watch out for the 'yeast' of the Pharisees and of Herod, but they thought He was talking about their lack of bread."}, "Mark 9": {"9:1–8": "Jesus is transfigured before Peter, James, and John on a high mountain, His appearance becoming dazzlingly bright. Moses and Elijah appear, symbolizing the Law and the Prophets, and they speak with Jesus. A voice from a cloud, representing God the Father, affirms Jesus as His beloved Son and commands the disciples to listen to Him. The disciples are filled with awe and fear during this divine revelation.", "9:9–13": "As they descend from the mountain, Jesus instructs the disciples not to speak of the transfiguration until after His resurrection. The disciples are puzzled about the meaning of the resurrection and ask about the prophecy that Elijah must come first. Jesus explains that Elijah (symbolized by John the Baptist) has already come, but was rejected, just as the Son of Man will suffer and be rejected.", "9:25–29": "A desperate father brings his son, who is possessed by an unclean spirit, to Jesus after the disciples are unable to heal him. Jesus heals the boy, and later explains to the disciples that this kind of demon can only come out through prayer and fasting.", "9:30–32": "Jesus again predicts His death and resurrection, but the disciples do not fully understand and are afraid to ask Him about it.", "9:33–37": "The disciples argue about who is the greatest, but Jesus teaches them that true greatness comes from being a servant. He uses a child as an example of humility, explaining that whoever receives a child in His name receives Him.", "9:38–41": "Jesus teaches His disciples not to stop someone who is casting out demons in His name, even if that person is not part of their group, explaining that anyone who is not against them is for them.", "9:49–50": "Jesus warns His followers about the seriousness of sin and temptation. He uses vivid imagery to emphasize that it is better to remove the source of sin, even if it means losing a part of oneself, than to face eternal judgment in hell.", "9:14–24": "A man brought his son, who had a spirit that took away his speech and caused convulsions, to Jesus after the disciples couldn't heal him. The boy's father said, 'I believe — help my unbelief,' and Jesus healed the boy.", "9:42–48": "Jesus warned that it would be better to lose a hand, foot, or eye than to let it cause you to sin and be thrown into hell, where the fire never goes out."}, "Mark 10": {"10:10–12": "Jesus teaches on the sacredness of marriage, emphasizing that divorce was permitted by Moses because of the hardness of people's hearts, but God's original design for marriage is that the two become one and should not be separated.", "10:13–16": "Jesus blesses the little children brought to Him, despite the disciples’ objections. He teaches that the kingdom of God belongs to those who receive it with childlike faith and humility.", "10:23–27": "A rich young ruler comes to Jesus, seeking eternal life. Jesus tells him to sell his possessions and give to the poor, but the young man is grieved because of his wealth. Jesus uses this moment to teach about the difficulty for those who trust in wealth to enter the kingdom of God, but He also emphasizes that what is impossible with man is possible with God.", "10:28–31": "Peter reminds Jesus that the disciples have left everything to follow Him. Jesus assures them that those who have given up earthly relationships or possessions for His sake will receive many times more in this life—along with persecutions—and eternal life in the world to come.", "10:32–34": "Jesus again predicts His coming suffering, death, and resurrection as they approach Jerusalem. He tells His disciples that He will be condemned, mocked, scourged, and killed, but that He will rise on the third day.", "10:41–45": "James and John ask Jesus for positions of honor in His kingdom, but Jesus explains that true greatness comes from being a servant. He emphasizes that even He, the Son of Man, came not to be served but to serve and to give His life as a ransom for many.", "10:46–52": "Blind Bartimaeus calls out to Jesus as He passes by, addressing Him as the Son of David. Despite being told to be quiet, Bartimaeus persists, and Jesus heals him, telling him that his faith has made him whole. Bartimaeus immediately receives his sight and follows Jesus.", "10:1–9": "Pharisees asked Jesus if divorce was allowed. He told them Moses permitted it because of hard hearts, but that God's original plan was for a man and woman to become one and not be separated.", "10:17–22": "A man asked Jesus what he must do to have eternal life. He said he had kept all the commandments since he was young, but when Jesus told him to sell everything and give to the poor, he walked away sad, because he was very wealthy.", "10:35–40": "James and John asked Jesus to let them sit at His right and left in His glory. Jesus told them that wasn't His to give — it belongs to those for whom it has been prepared."}, "Mark 11": {"11:7–11": "Jesus enters Jerusalem riding on a colt, fulfilling prophecy. The crowd welcomes Him by spreading their garments and palm branches on the road, shouting Hosanna and declaring blessings on Him as the one who comes in the name of the Lord.", "11:12–14": "On His way back to Jerusalem, Jesus curses a fig tree for not bearing fruit, even though it had leaves. This serves as a symbolic act representing judgment on fruitlessness.", "11:15–19": "Jesus enters the temple and drives out those who are buying and selling, overturning the tables of the moneychangers. He declares that the temple should be a house of prayer for all nations, but they have made it a den of thieves. The religious leaders begin plotting to kill Him.", "11:20–26": "Jesus uses the withered fig tree as a lesson on faith. He teaches His disciples to have faith in God and believe that their prayers can move mountains. He also emphasizes the importance of forgiving others as he has forgiven them.", "11:27–33": "The religious leaders question Jesus' authority, but Jesus counters by asking them about John the Baptist. When they refuse to answer, He declines to reveal the source of His authority.", "11:1–6": "Jesus sent two disciples ahead to get a colt that had never been ridden, telling them what to say if anyone asked what they were doing. It happened just as He said."}, "Mark 12": {"12:9–12": "Jesus tells a parable about a vineyard owner whose servants and son are rejected and killed by the tenants. The religious leaders realize that the parable is directed at them, foretelling their rejection of Jesus, and they seek to arrest Him but fear the crowds.", "12:13–17": "The Pharisees and Herodians try to trap Jesus by asking about paying taxes to Caesar. Jesus responds wisely, telling them to render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's, leaving them amazed.", "12:24–27": "The Sadducees, who do not believe in the resurrection, challenge Jesus with a hypothetical situation about marriage after the resurrection. Jesus corrects their misunderstanding, teaching that there is no marriage in the resurrection and that God is the God of the living.", "12:28–34": "A scribe asks Jesus which commandment is the greatest, and Jesus responds with the command to love God with all one’s heart, soul, mind, and strength, and to love one’s neighbor as oneself. The scribe agrees, and Jesus commends him for his understanding.", "12:35–37": "Here, Jesus is asking a question to the religious leaders. The scribes taught that the Messiah (Christ) would be a descendant of King David, which was correct. But Jesus points out something they missed: in Psalm 110, David himself calls the Messiah \"Lord.\"", "12:38–40": "Jesus warns the people to beware of the scribes, who seek honor and status but oppress the vulnerable and make a show of their piety. He declares that they will receive greater condemnation for their hypocrisy.", "12:41–44": "Jesus observes the people giving offerings in the temple, and He highlights the offering of a poor widow, who gives two small coins. Though others give more in terms of money, the widow’s gift is greater in God's eyes because she gave all she had.", "12:1–8": "Jesus told a story about a man who rented out his vineyard to tenants. When he sent servants to collect his share, the tenants beat and killed them, and finally killed his son too.", "12:18–23": "The Sadducees, who didn't believe in resurrection, asked Jesus a trick question about a woman who had married seven brothers, one after another, asking whose wife she would be after the resurrection."}, "Mark 13": {"13:1–2": "As Jesus leaves the temple, His disciples remark on the grandeur of the buildings, but Jesus prophesies that the temple will be completely destroyed, with no stone left standing on another.", "13:9–13": "Jesus warns His disciples that many will come in His name claiming to be the Messiah, and they should not be deceived. He describes wars, earthquakes, famines, and persecution as signs of the end times, but emphasizes that the gospel must be preached to all nations before the end comes.", "13:21–23": "Jesus speaks of the abomination of desolation, a prophecy from Daniel, as a sign of great tribulation. He urges those in Judea to flee when they see this event, warning that it will be a time of unprecedented distress. False messiahs and prophets will arise, but the elect should not be deceived.", "13:24–27": "After the tribulation, cosmic signs will appear—the sun and moon will be darkened, and the stars will fall. Then, Jesus (the Son of Man) will come in great power and glory, and His angels will gather the elect from all over the world.", "13:28–31": "Jesus compares the signs of the end times to a fig tree sprouting leaves, indicating that summer is near. When these events occur, the disciples will know that the end is near, though Jesus’ words will endure even when heaven and earth pass away.", "13:32–37": "Jesus warns that no one, not even the angels or the Son, knows the exact day or hour of His return—only the Father knows. Therefore, His followers must always be watchful, ready for His return at any moment.", "13:3–8": "Peter, James, John, and Andrew privately asked Jesus when the temple would be destroyed. He warned them about false messiahs, wars, earthquakes, and famines, saying these were only the beginning.", "13:14–20": "Jesus warned that when a certain sign appears, people in Judea must flee at once, because a time of suffering worse than any before would come — though it would be shortened for the sake of God's chosen people."}, "Mark 14": {"14:1–2": "The chief priests and scribes plot to arrest and kill Jesus but decide to wait until after the Passover feast to avoid causing a public uproar.", "14:3–9": "A woman anoints Jesus' head with expensive perfume, an act of devotion that some criticize as wasteful. Jesus defends her, saying that she has done a beautiful thing in preparing His body for burial and that her act will be remembered wherever the gospel is preached.", "14:10–11": "Judas Iscariot, one of Jesus’ twelve disciples, goes to the chief priests and agrees to betray Jesus for money.", "14:17–21": "Jesus and His disciples prepare for the Passover meal, known as the Last Supper. During the meal, Jesus reveals that one of His disciples will betray Him, and the disciples react with sorrow and concern.", "14:22–26": "Jesus institutes the Lord’s Supper by breaking bread and sharing wine with His disciples, symbolizing His body and blood that will be given for many. He speaks of the new covenant and promises to drink it again in the kingdom of God.", "14:27–31": "Jesus tells His disciples that they will all fall away that night, quoting a prophecy. Peter insists he will not deny Jesus, even if it means death, but Jesus predicts that Peter will deny Him three times before the rooster crows twice.", "14:37–42": "In the Garden of Gethsemane, Jesus prays in deep distress, asking the Father if it is possible to remove the cup of suffering but submitting to God's will. His disciples, despite being asked to keep watch, fall asleep.", "14:47–52": "Judas betrays Jesus with a kiss, and a crowd armed with swords and clubs arrests Him. Though one of His followers strikes a servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear, Jesus does not resist, fulfilling Scripture. The disciples all flee in fear.", "14:60–65": "Jesus is brought before the high priest and the Sanhedrin, where false witnesses testify against Him. When asked directly if He is the Messiah, Jesus affirms it, declaring that He will be seen at the right hand of power. This leads to a condemnation of death and mockery by the council.", "14:66–72": "Peter, who had vowed never to deny Jesus, does so three times, as Jesus had predicted. After the rooster crows a second time, Peter remembers Jesus' words and weeps bitterly.", "14:12–16": "Jesus sent two disciples into the city to find a man carrying a water jar and prepare the Passover meal in the room he would show them. They found everything just as Jesus said.", "14:32–36": "In the garden of Gethsemane, Jesus was overwhelmed with grief and prayed for the suffering ahead to be taken from Him, but said He wanted the Father's will, not His own.", "14:43–46": "Judas arrived with an armed crowd and identified Jesus with a kiss, just as he had arranged. Jesus was seized and arrested.", "14:53–59": "Jesus was brought before the high priest and the council, where many people gave false testimony against Him, but their stories didn't match up."}, "Mark 15": {"15:1–5": "The chief priests and elders deliver Jesus to Pilate, the Roman governor, who questions Him about being the King of the Jews. Though accused of many things, Jesus remains silent, which amazes Pilate.", "15:12–15": "At the feast, Pilate customarily released a prisoner chosen by the people. Though Pilate tries to release Jesus, the crowd, influenced by the chief priests, demands that Barabbas, a murderer, be freed instead. Pilate, wanting to satisfy the crowd, releases Barabbas and condemns Jesus to crucifixion.", "15:16–20": "The soldiers mock Jesus by dressing Him in a purple robe and placing a crown of thorns on His head. They sarcastically hail Him as King of the Jews while beating and spitting on Him before leading Him out to be crucified.", "15:29–32": "Jesus is led to Golgotha (The Place of a Skull) and crucified between two thieves. The soldiers cast lots for His clothing, and a sign is placed above Him reading, The King of the Jews. Passersby and religious leaders mock Him, challenging Him to save Himself and come down from the cross.", "15:37–39": "Darkness covers the land for three hours, and Jesus cries out, My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me? before dying. The temple veil is torn in two, symbolizing the end of the separation between God and humanity. A Roman centurion witnessing Jesus’ death declares, Truly this man was the Son of God.", "15:42–47": "Joseph of Arimathea, a respected member of the council, boldly asks Pilate for Jesus' body. After confirming Jesus’ death with the centurion, Pilate releases the body to Joseph, who wraps it in linen and places it in a tomb. Mary Magdalene and Mary, the mother of Joses, witness where Jesus is laid.", "15:6–11": "At the festival, Pilate offered to release a prisoner the crowd chose. The chief priests stirred up the crowd to ask for Barabbas, a murderer, instead of Jesus.", "15:21–24": "Simon of Cyrene was forced to carry Jesus' cross. They brought Him to Golgotha, offered Him wine mixed with myrrh, which He refused, then crucified Him and divided His clothes by casting lots.", "15:25–28": "Jesus was crucified at nine in the morning, with a sign above Him reading 'The King of the Jews.' Two criminals were crucified on either side of Him.", "15:33–36": "Darkness covered the land from noon until three, and Jesus cried out asking why God had forsaken Him. Someone offered Him sour wine on a sponge while others waited to see if Elijah would come.", "15:40–41": "Several women, including Mary Magdalene, watched the crucifixion from a distance. They had followed Jesus and cared for Him back in Galilee."}, "Mark 16": {"16:5–8": "On the first day of the week, after the Sabbath, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, and Salome come to Jesus' tomb to anoint His body. They find the stone rolled away and are greeted by an angel who tells them that Jesus has risen. The women are told to inform the disciples that Jesus will meet them in Galilee.", "16:9–11": "Jesus first appears to Mary Magdalene after His resurrection. She goes to tell the disciples, but they do not believe her report that He is alive.", "16:12–14": "Jesus appears to two other disciples as they are walking in the countryside. When they report this to the rest of the disciples, they are not believed.", "16:15–18": "Jesus appears to the eleven disciples and rebukes them for their unbelief. He then commissions them to go into all the world and preach the gospel to every creature. Jesus promises that those who believe will be saved and that miraculous signs will accompany believers, such as casting out demons, speaking in new tongues, and healing the sick.", "16:19–20": "After giving His commission, Jesus ascends into heaven and sits at the right hand of God. The disciples go forth, preaching everywhere, with the Lord confirming their message through miraculous signs.", "16:1–4": "After the Sabbath, the women bought spices to anoint Jesus' body and went to the tomb early Sunday morning, wondering who would roll the stone away — but when they arrived, it had already been moved."}, "Luke 1": {"1:1–4": "Luke begins by explaining his purpose for writing: to provide an orderly account of the events concerning Jesus Christ so that Theophilus may have certainty in the teachings he has received.", "1:21–25": "Zacharias and Elisabeth, a righteous and elderly couple, are promised a son by the angel Gabriel. This child, John, will be filled with the Holy Spirit and will prepare the way for the Lord. Because of Zacharias’ doubt, he is struck mute until the prophecy is fulfilled.", "1:34–38": "The angel Gabriel announces to Mary that she will conceive by the Holy Spirit and give birth to Jesus, the Son of God, who will rule over an eternal kingdom. Despite her initial confusion, Mary humbly accepts God's plan, displaying faith in God's power to accomplish the impossible.", "1:39–45": "When Mary visits her cousin Elizabeth, the unborn John the Baptist leaps for joy in Elizabeth's womb, and Elizabeth is filled with the Holy Spirit. She proclaims Mary blessed for her faith in God’s promises.", "1:46–55": "Mary praises God for His mighty deeds, His mercy, and His faithfulness to His promises. Her song celebrates God's care for the humble and His power to bring down the proud, all while keeping His covenant with Israel.", "1:59–67": "John the Baptist is born, and though the people expect him to be named after his father, both Elizabeth and Zacharias insist that his name is John. Immediately, Zacharias regains his speech, and the people marvel at the miraculous events surrounding John's birth, wondering what this child will become.", "1:80": "Filled with the Holy Spirit, Zacharias prophesies about the coming salvation through Jesus, the fulfillment of God's promises to Israel, and the special role of his son John, who will prepare the way for the Lord by preaching salvation and forgiveness of sins.", "1:5–7": "Zechariah was a priest and his wife Elizabeth came from a priestly family. Both of them tried to obey God faithfully, but they had no children because Elizabeth couldn't have any, and they were both very old.", "1:8–12": "While Zechariah was serving as priest and burning incense in the temple, an angel of the Lord suddenly appeared next to the incense altar, and Zechariah was terrified.", "1:13–17": "The angel told Zechariah not to be afraid — his wife Elizabeth would have a son named John, who would be filled with the Holy Spirit and would prepare people for the Lord, like Elijah did.", "1:18–20": "Zechariah doubted the angel because he and his wife were old. The angel told him he would be unable to speak until the promise came true, because he hadn't believed.", "1:26–29": "God sent the angel Gabriel to Mary in Nazareth. He told her she was greatly favored by God, and Mary was confused, wondering what his greeting meant.", "1:30–33": "The angel told Mary not to be afraid — she would have a son named Jesus, who would be great and would reign on David's throne forever.", "1:56–58": "Mary stayed with Elizabeth about three months before going home. When Elizabeth's baby was born, her neighbors and relatives rejoiced with her.", "1:68–79": "This is Zechariah's song after his son John was born. He praised God for keeping His promise to rescue His people, and said his son would go ahead of the Lord to prepare the way and tell people their sins would be forgiven."}, "Luke 2": {"2:6–7": "Luke tells of the humble circumstances surrounding the birth of Jesus. Joseph and Mary travel to Bethlehem due to a decree from Caesar Augustus. While there, Mary gives birth to Jesus and places Him in a manger, as there was no room for them in the inn.", "2:15–20": "The first announcement of Jesus’ birth is made to shepherds, considered lowly by society, yet chosen by God to receive the good news. The angels proclaim peace on earth and God's goodwill toward humanity. The shepherds visit the newborn Jesus and spread the word of what they have seen, while Mary treasures these moments in her heart.", "2:22–24": "In obedience to the Law, Mary and Joseph present Jesus at the temple, offering the required sacrifice for their purification and dedication of the firstborn male.", "2:33–35": "Simeon, a devout man who had been promised by the Holy Spirit that he would see the Messiah before his death, takes the infant Jesus in his arms and prophesies. He declares that Jesus will be a light for the Gentiles and the glory of Israel, though He will also be a source of conflict and division, revealing the thoughts of many hearts.", "2:36–38": "Anna, a prophetess, who spent her life worshiping at the temple, recognizes Jesus as the promised Redeemer. She thanks God and shares the news with all those awaiting the redemption of Israel.", "2:39–40": "After fulfilling all the requirements of the Law, Mary and Joseph return to Nazareth, where Jesus grows in strength, wisdom, and the favor of God.", "2:51–52": "When Jesus is twelve years old, He stays behind in the temple after the Passover feast, engaging with the teachers and astonishing them with His wisdom. When His parents find Him, Jesus expresses that He is about His Father's business, yet He returns to Nazareth and continues to grow in wisdom and favor with God and people.", "2:1–5": "Caesar Augustus ordered everyone to be counted in a census, so Joseph traveled from Nazareth to Bethlehem, his family's hometown, taking Mary with him since she was expecting a baby.", "2:8–12": "An angel appeared to shepherds watching their flocks at night and told them not to be afraid — a Savior, the Messiah, had been born in Bethlehem, and they would find Him wrapped in cloth and lying in a feeding trough.", "2:13–14": "A large group of angels appeared with the first one, praising God and saying, 'Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, goodwill toward people.'", "2:21": "On the eighth day, the baby was circumcised and given the name Jesus, just as the angel had said before He was conceived.", "2:25–28": "Simeon was a devout man in Jerusalem who had been told by the Holy Spirit that he wouldn't die before seeing the Messiah. When Mary and Joseph brought the baby Jesus to the temple, Simeon took Him in his arms and praised God.", "2:29–32": "This is what Simeon said as he held baby Jesus: he could now die in peace, because he had seen with his own eyes the salvation God had prepared for all people, a light for the nations and the glory of Israel.", "2:41–45": "Every year Jesus' family went to Jerusalem for Passover. When He was twelve, He stayed behind in Jerusalem after the feast without His parents realizing it, and they had to go back to search for Him.", "2:46–50": "After three days, they found Jesus in the temple, sitting with the teachers, listening and asking questions, and everyone was amazed at His understanding. When His parents asked why He'd stayed, He said He had to be in His Father's house — but they didn't understand what He meant."}, "Luke 3": {"3:3–6": "John the Baptist begins his ministry in the wilderness, preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins. His ministry fulfills the prophecy of Isaiah, who spoke of a voice in the wilderness preparing the way for the Lord.", "3:10–14": "John’s message is a call to repentance that bears fruit in the way people live. He tells the crowds to share with those in need, instructs tax collectors to collect no more than required, and urges soldiers to avoid violence and false accusations, urging all to live justly.", "3:18–20": "John clarifies that he is not the Christ but is preparing the way for one who is far greater—Jesus—who will baptize with the Holy Spirit and fire. Jesus will bring both salvation and judgment, gathering the wheat (the righteous) and burning the chaff (the wicked). Herod imprisons John the Baptist because of John's condemnation of his immoral relationship with Herodias, his brother’s wife, along with other evil deeds Herod had committed.", "3:21–22": "Jesus is baptized by John, and as He prays, the heavens open, and the Holy Spirit descends on Him like a dove. God the Father speaks from heaven, affirming Jesus as His beloved Son, in whom He is well pleased.", "3:35–38": "The genealogy of Jesus traces His lineage through Joseph, back to King David, and all the way to Adam, emphasizing that Jesus is the fulfillment of God’s promises to Israel and to humanity, as He is both the Son of David and the Son of God.", "3:1–2": "Luke lists the rulers of the time — Tiberius Caesar, Pilate, Herod, and others — and says this was when God's message came to John in the wilderness.", "3:7–9": "John warned the crowds coming to be baptized not to rely on being descended from Abraham, and told them to show real change in their lives — warning that judgment, like an axe at the root of a tree, was already close.", "3:15–17": "People wondered if John himself was the Messiah, but John told them someone greater was coming who would baptize with the Holy Spirit and fire, while he only baptized with water.", "3:23–27": "Luke lists Jesus' family line starting with Joseph, going back through Heli and many others to Neri.", "3:28–31": "The family line continues further back through Melchi, Addi, and others, down to Nathan, a son of David.", "3:32–34": "The family line keeps going back through Jesse, Boaz, Judah, and Abraham, to Terah and Nahor."}, "Luke 4": {"4:9–13": "Jesus is led by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil. He resists every temptation by quoting Scripture, showing His reliance on God’s Word.", "4:14–15": "After overcoming the temptations, Jesus begins His public ministry, teaching in synagogues with power and authority.", "4:28–30": "Jesus is rejected in His hometown of Nazareth after proclaiming favor and that He is the fulfillment of Isaiah’s prophecy. The people are enraged, but He miraculously escapes from their attempt to kill Him.", "4:33–37": "Jesus demonstrates His authority over demons by casting out an unclean spirit from a man in the synagogue, astonishing the people with His power.", "4:40–41": "Jesus heals Simon Peter's mother-in-law and many others with various diseases. Demons also acknowledge Him as the Son of God, but He silences them.", "4:42–44": "Jesus continues to preach the good news of the kingdom of God, traveling to other cities to fulfill His mission.", "4:1–4": "The Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness, where He fasted forty days and was tempted by the devil. When the devil told Him to turn a stone into bread, Jesus answered that people don't live on bread alone.", "4:5–8": "The devil showed Jesus all the kingdoms of the world and offered them to Him if He would worship him. Jesus refused, saying only God should be worshiped.", "4:16–21": "In the synagogue at Nazareth, Jesus read from Isaiah about bringing good news to the poor and freedom to captives, then told the people this Scripture had come true that day.", "4:22–24": "The people were amazed at His words but also questioned Him, since they knew Him as Joseph's son. Jesus said a prophet is never welcomed in his own hometown.", "4:25–27": "Jesus reminded them that Elijah was sent to help a widow outside Israel, and Elisha healed a leper from Syria, not any of the many Israelites with those same needs.", "4:31–32": "Jesus went to Capernaum and taught on the Sabbath, and the people were amazed because He spoke with real authority.", "4:38–39": "Jesus went to Simon's house, where Simon's mother-in-law had a high fever. He spoke to the fever, it left her, and she got up and began serving them."}, "Luke 5": {"5:8–11": "Jesus calls Simon Peter, James, and John to follow Him, after a miraculous catch of fish, signifying their new calling to become fishers of men.", "5:12–16": "Jesus heals a man with leprosy and instructs him to follow the law of Moses by presenting himself to the priests, demonstrating that Jesus respects the Law even as He performs miraculous healings.", "5:21–26": "Jesus forgives a paralytic’s sins and heals him, demonstrating His authority both to forgive sins and to heal, which amazes the people and causes conflict with the Pharisees.", "5:30–32": "Jesus calls Levi (Matthew), a tax collector, to follow Him. He faces criticism from the Pharisees for dining with tax collectors and sinners, but Jesus explains that He came to call sinners to repentance.", "5:33–39": "Jesus teaches that His presence is like a wedding feast—while He is with His disciples, they do not fast, but there will be a time for fasting. He explains through a parable that His message brings new life and cannot be contained by old traditions.", "5:1–3": "As crowds pressed in to hear Jesus by the lake, He got into Simon's boat and asked him to push out a little, then taught the people from there.", "5:4–7": "Jesus told Simon to let his nets down in deep water. Even though they had caught nothing all night, Simon obeyed, and they caught so many fish that the nets began to tear.", "5:17–20": "Some men carrying a paralyzed man couldn't get through the crowd to Jesus, so they lowered him through the roof. Seeing their faith, Jesus told the man his sins were forgiven.", "5:27–29": "Jesus saw a tax collector named Levi and told him, 'Follow Me.' Levi left everything and followed Him, then held a large banquet for Jesus at his house."}, "Luke 6": {"6:1–5": "Jesus teaches that He is Lord of the Sabbath, challenging the Pharisees' strict interpretation of the Sabbath law by pointing to David's example.", "6:6–11": "Jesus heals a man with a withered hand on the Sabbath, teaching that it is lawful to do good on the Sabbath, which angers the Pharisees.", "6:12–16": "Jesus selects twelve of His disciples and appoints them as apostles, who would become His closest followers and witnesses to His ministry.", "6:17–19": "A great crowd gathers to hear Jesus teach and to be healed by Him. His power is so great that people seek just to touch Him for healing.", "6:20–23": "Jesus pronounces blessings on those who suffer for righteousness' sake, promising them comfort, fulfillment, and reward in heaven.", "6:24–26": "Jesus contrasts the blessings with warnings to those who find their satisfaction in worldly riches and approval, foretelling that their present comfort will turn to future loss.", "6:32–36": "Jesus teaches radical love and generosity, instructing His followers to love their enemies and treat others as they would want to be treated, regardless of how they are treated in return.", "6:37–38": "Jesus teaches that judgment, condemnation, and forgiveness are reciprocal—what you give to others will be given back to you, whether it is mercy or judgment.", "6:39–42": "Jesus warns against hypocrisy, instructing His followers to address their own faults before attempting to correct others.", "6:43–45": "Jesus teaches that people's words and actions reflect the true condition of their hearts—good people produce good fruit, and evil people produce evil fruit.", "6:46–49": "Jesus concludes His teaching by comparing those who obey His words to a wise builder who builds on a strong foundation, while those who ignore His words are like a foolish builder whose house is easily destroyed.", "6:27–31": "Jesus told His listeners to love their enemies, do good to those who hate them, and treat others the way they want to be treated — even offering the other cheek if struck."}, "Luke 7": {"7:1–10": "Jesus marvels at the centurion’s great faith, which is based on his understanding of authority. Jesus heals the centurion’s servant without even needing to go to the man’s house.", "7:11–17": "Jesus demonstrates His compassion and power over death by raising the widow’s son, which leads the people to glorify God and recognize Jesus as a great prophet.", "7:18–23": "John the Baptist sends messengers to confirm if Jesus is the Messiah. Jesus points to His miraculous works as evidence that He is indeed the One who was to come.", "7:31–35": "Jesus compares the people of His generation to children who complain no matter what is offered to them. They rejected John the Baptist for his austerity and criticized Jesus for His association with sinners. Yet, true wisdom is proven right by her actions and followers, showing that those who follow God will ultimately be vindicated.", "7:44–50": "A sinful woman anoints Jesus’ feet, and He forgives her sins. This act of great love shows her deep gratitude for the forgiveness she receives. Jesus contrasts her actions with the Pharisee’s lack of hospitality.", "7:24–30": "After John's messengers left, Jesus told the crowd that John was more than a prophet — the greatest person ever born — though even the least in God's kingdom is greater than him.", "7:36–39": "A sinful woman came into a Pharisee's house where Jesus was eating, and she washed His feet with her tears, wiped them with her hair, and poured perfume on them. The Pharisee doubted Jesus was really a prophet for letting her touch Him.", "7:40–43": "Jesus told Simon the Pharisee about two men who owed money — one much more than the other — and both debts were canceled. He asked which one would love the lender more, and Simon answered correctly: the one who owed more."}, "Luke 8": {"8:9–10": "Jesus teaches the parable of the sower to describe how different hearts respond to God's Word. Some reject it, some are distracted, but others, like good soil, produce abundant fruit.", "8:11–15": "Jesus explains the meaning of the parable of the sower. The seed represents the word of God, and the different types of soil represent the conditions of people’s hearts. Some hear the word but let the devil steal it away; others receive it joyfully but fall away during trials. Some are distracted by life’s cares and pleasures, while others hear the word, keep it, and produce lasting fruit through patience.", "8:16–18": "Jesus teaches that those who receive the light of truth should not hide it but should share it for others to see. He warns that everything hidden will one day be revealed, and He calls for careful attention to how we hear and respond to the word. Those who embrace it will receive more, while those who neglect it will lose even what they have.", "8:19–21": "When told that His mother and brothers are waiting to see Him, Jesus redefines family as those who hear and obey the word of God. His response emphasizes that spiritual relationships are as significant as biological ones in the kingdom of God.", "8:22–25": "Jesus demonstrates His authority over nature by calming a violent storm, revealing His divine power and calling His disciples to have faith in Him.", "8:34–39": "Jesus encounters a man possessed by many demons who lived in the tombs and could not be controlled by any human means. The demons, identifying themselves as Legion, beg Jesus not to send them into the abyss, and He allows them to enter a herd of swine, which then rush into the lake and drown. The people of the region, witnessing the dramatic healing and fearing the power of Jesus, ask Him to leave.", "8:40–42": "The man who was freed from the demons desires to follow Jesus, but Jesus sends him home to testify of God’s work in his life. The man faithfully shares his testimony throughout the region, and upon Jesus' return, the people welcome Him eagerly, having heard of the miraculous healing", "8:49–56": "Jesus heals a woman with a long-term illness and raises Jairus' daughter from the dead, demonstrating His power over both disease and death.", "8:1–3": "Jesus traveled from town to town preaching, along with the twelve disciples and some women, including Mary Magdalene, who had been healed and were now helping support His ministry.", "8:4–8": "Jesus told a story about a farmer scattering seed — some fell on the path, some on rocky ground, some among thorns, and some on good soil, which produced a large crop.", "8:26–29": "Jesus arrived in the region of the Gadarenes, where a man possessed by demons lived among the tombs, wore no clothes, and couldn't be controlled even with chains.", "8:30–33": "Jesus asked the man's name, and he said 'Legion' because many demons were in him. The demons begged to enter a herd of pigs instead of being sent to the abyss, and when Jesus allowed it, the pigs rushed into the lake and drowned.", "8:43–48": "A woman who had been bleeding for twelve years touched Jesus' clothes in the crowd and was instantly healed. When Jesus asked who touched Him, she came forward trembling and told Him the truth."}, "Luke 9": {"9:1–6": "Jesus empowers the twelve apostles with authority to preach the kingdom of God, heal the sick, and cast out demons, instructing them to rely on God’s provision as they minister.", "9:7–9": "Herod, having heard of Jesus' growing influence, is confused and curious about His identity, especially with rumors circulating that Jesus could be John the Baptist raised from the dead or a prophet like Elijah.", "9:10–17": "Jesus performs the miracle of feeding five thousand people with only five loaves and two fish. The abundance of leftovers reveals His power to provide beyond expectation.", "9:18–20": "Peter confesses that Jesus is the Christ, the Anointed One sent by God, signifying a moment of revelation about Jesus’ divine mission.", "9:21–22": "Jesus begins to reveal the necessity of His suffering, death, and resurrection, preparing His disciples for the path ahead.", "9:23–27": "Jesus outlines the cost of discipleship, calling His followers to self-denial, a willingness to bear suffering, and faithfulness in following Him. The reward for losing one's life for Christ's sake is true salvation.", "9:28–36": "Jesus is transfigured before Peter, James, and John, revealing His divine glory. Moses and Elijah appear, symbolizing the Law and the Prophets. God's voice affirms Jesus as His beloved Son, commanding the disciples to listen to Him.", "9:37–43": "Jesus heals a boy possessed by a demon after His disciples were unable to. This miracle highlights both the power of Jesus and the need for faith in His followers.", "9:44–45": "Jesus once again predicts His betrayal and death, but the disciples do not understand the significance of His words and are afraid to ask Him for clarification.", "9:46–48": "The disciples argue about who is the greatest among them, and Jesus uses a child as an example of humility, teaching that true greatness comes from being the least and serving others.", "9:49–50": "John expresses concern about someone casting out demons in Jesus' name who is not part of their group. Jesus teaches tolerance and unity, saying that anyone working for the kingdom of God is not against them.", "9:51–56": "When a Samaritan village rejects Jesus, James and John suggest calling down fire from heaven in judgment. Jesus rebukes them, reminding them that His mission is to save lives, not destroy them.", "9:57–62": "Jesus emphasizes the cost of discipleship, explaining that following Him requires total commitment and a willingness to leave behind earthly concerns. Those who hesitate or look back are not fit for the kingdom of God."}, "Luke 10": {"10:5–12": "Jesus sends out seventy disciples in pairs, instructing them to preach the kingdom of God and to heal the sick. He tells them to rely on the hospitality of those who welcome them and warns that those who reject them are rejecting the kingdom of God.", "10:13–16": "Jesus pronounces woes on cities like Chorazin and Bethsaida for their lack of repentance, despite the miracles performed there. He emphasizes that those who reject His messengers are rejecting Him and the Father who sent Him.", "10:17–20": "The seventy disciples return with joy, reporting their authority over demons. Jesus tells them to rejoice not only in their power but more importantly in the fact that their names are written in heaven.", "10:21–24": "Jesus praises the Father for revealing the truths of the kingdom to the humble and childlike, not to the wise and learned. He highlights the special privilege the disciples have in witnessing what prophets and kings longed to see.", "10:30–37": "In response to the question, Who is my neighbor? Jesus tells the parable of the Good Samaritan, showing that true love for one’s neighbor transcends societal and religious boundaries, emphasizing compassion and mercy.", "10:38–42": "Martha is busy serving, while Mary chooses to sit at Jesus’ feet and listen to His teaching. Jesus affirms Mary’s choice as the better part, highlighting the importance of prioritizing time with Him over worldly concerns.", "10:1–4": "Jesus sent seventy more followers out ahead of Him in pairs, telling them the harvest was plentiful but the workers were few, and to travel light.", "10:25–29": "An expert in the law asked Jesus what he must do to inherit eternal life. He answered his own question correctly — love God and love your neighbor — but then asked who his neighbor really was."}, "Luke 11": {"11:1–4": "Jesus teaches His disciples to pray by providing the Lord’s Prayer, focusing on God’s holiness, provision, forgiveness, and protection from evil.", "11:9–13": "Jesus uses the parable of the persistent friend to encourage persistence in prayer, teaching that those who continually ask, seek, and knock will receive, find, and have doors opened to them. Jesus reassures His disciples that God, as the perfect Father, will always give good gifts to His children, particularly the gift of the Holy Spirit to those who ask.", "11:21–26": "Jesus responds to those accusing Him of casting out demons by the power of Beelzebub, explaining that a kingdom divided against itself cannot stand. He asserts that His works are done by the power of God, and the arrival of the kingdom of God is at hand. Jesus warns that when an unclean spirit leaves a person but returns to find the person empty and unguarded, it can come back with more spirits, leaving the person in a worse condition than before.", "11:27–28": "Jesus responds to a woman who praises His mother by emphasizing that true blessedness comes from hearing and obeying the word of God.", "11:29–32": "Jesus condemns the generation seeking a sign, telling them that the only sign they will receive is the sign of Jonah, who was a sign to the Ninevites of impending judgment.", "11:33–36": "Jesus uses the metaphor of a lamp to teach that spiritual health depends on how we perceive truth. If our eyes are focused on light (God's truth), our entire being will be filled with light. But if we focus on darkness, we will be filled with spiritual darkness.", "11:45–54": "Jesus rebukes the Pharisees and lawyers for their hypocrisy, legalism, and oppression. He warns them that their outward displays of righteousness mean nothing when their hearts are full of wickedness.", "11:5–8": "Jesus told a story about a man who wakes up his neighbor at midnight asking for bread. Even if the neighbor won't get up out of friendship, he will get up because of the man's persistence.", "11:14–20": "After Jesus cast out a demon that had made a man unable to speak, some people accused Him of doing it by the power of Beelzebul. Jesus answered that a divided kingdom can't stand, and that He was casting out demons by the power of God.", "11:37–44": "A Pharisee was surprised Jesus didn't wash His hands before the meal. Jesus criticized the Pharisees for being clean on the outside but full of greed inside, and for caring more about small religious rules than justice and the love of God."}, "Luke 12": {"12:4–7": "Jesus warns His disciples to beware of the hypocrisy of the Pharisees and to fear God, not men. He encourages them with the truth that God values them greatly and knows even the smallest details of their lives.", "12:8–12": "Jesus stresses the importance of acknowledging Him publicly, warning that those who deny Him will face eternal consequences. He also teaches about the unforgivable sin: blasphemy against the Holy Spirit. Jesus assures His disciples that when they are brought before authorities for their faith, the Holy Spirit will give them the words they need in that moment.", "12:13–21": "Jesus tells the parable of the rich fool, who focuses on storing up wealth for himself without considering his relationship with God. The parable warns against covetousness and the futility of earthly riches when faced with eternal realities.", "12:32–34": "Jesus teaches His disciples not to worry about their physical needs, encouraging them to trust in God's provision and to keep his righteousness as their primary focus. He promises that God will meet all their needs if they prioritize His kingdom.", "12:35–40": "Jesus urges His followers to always be prepared for His return, comparing His coming to a master returning unexpectedly or a thief coming in the night. Those who are watchful and ready will be blessed.", "12:41–48": "Jesus tells the parable of the faithful and wise servant, emphasizing that those entrusted with responsibility must remain faithful, as they will be held accountable when the Lord returns.", "12:49–53": "Jesus explains that His coming will cause division, even among families, as people will be divided over their response to Him.", "12:54–59": "Jesus rebukes the people for their ability to interpret weather signs but their failure to recognize the spiritual significance of His ministry and the times they are living in. Jesus encourages settling disputes quickly to avoid severe judgment, using the metaphor of being taken to court to highlight the urgency of making things right.", "12:1–3": "Jesus warned His disciples about the hypocrisy of the Pharisees, saying that whatever is hidden will eventually be brought into the open.", "12:22–31": "Jesus told His disciples not to worry about food or clothing, pointing to how God feeds the ravens and clothes the wildflowers, and saying God would take care of them too."}, "Luke 13": {"13:1–5": "Jesus responds to news about the tragic deaths of Galileans and others in Jerusalem by warning that unless people repent, they will also perish. He emphasizes that these events should serve as a reminder of the need for all to repent.", "13:6–9": "Jesus tells the parable of the barren fig tree to illustrate God's patience and mercy in giving people time to repent and bear fruit. However, if they continue to be unfruitful, judgment will eventually come.", "13:10–17": "Jesus heals a woman crippled for eighteen years on the Sabbath, causing controversy with the religious leaders who are focused on the law and work. He rebukes their hypocrisy, pointing out that they would care for their animals on the Sabbath, so how much more should they care for this woman who is a daughter of Abraham.", "13:18–21": "Jesus compares the kingdom of God to a mustard seed, the smallest of seeds that grows into a large tree. This illustrates how the kingdom of God starts small but grows into something great and expansive. The parable of the yeast illustrates how the kingdom of God works inwardly and spreads quietly but powerfully, affecting everything it touches.", "13:22–30": "Jesus teaches about the narrow door, warning that many will seek to enter the kingdom of God but will not be able to. He emphasizes the urgency of entering through the narrow door (Jesus), as time will run out, and those who thought they were secure will find themselves shut out.", "13:31–35": "Jesus laments over Jerusalem, expressing His sorrow that they have rejected Him and the prophets sent to them. He wishes he could protect them from the desolation that was coming to the city and hints at His future return."}, "Luke 14": {"14:1–6": "Jesus heals a man suffering from dropsy on the Sabbath, challenging the Pharisees' rigid interpretation of the law by reminding them of their own practice of rescuing animals in need on the Sabbath.", "14:7–11": "Jesus advises humility by teaching the parable of the wedding feast, illustrating that those who exalt themselves will be humbled, while those who humble themselves will be exalted.", "14:21–24": "Through the parable of the great banquet, Jesus highlights the importance of humility and willingness to accept God's invitation. Those who make excuses will miss the blessings of the kingdom, while the humble and outcast are welcomed.", "14:28–33": "Jesus teaches the cost of discipleship, warning that following Him requires complete dedication, even to the point of forsaking family and personal desires. Discipleship involves self-denial and bearing one's cross.", "14:34–35": "Jesus compares discipleship to salt, emphasizing that if salt loses its flavor, it is useless. In the same way, a disciple who loses their commitment to Christ is no longer effective.", "14:12–14": "Jesus told the man who invited Him to dinner that instead of inviting friends and relatives who could repay him, he should invite the poor and disabled, who couldn't repay him — and he would be rewarded at the resurrection.", "14:15–20": "Jesus told a story about a man who invited many guests to a banquet, but one after another they made excuses not to come — one had bought a field, another oxen, another had just married.", "14:25–27": "Jesus told the crowds following Him that anyone who wanted to be His disciple had to love Him more than family or even their own life, and be willing to carry their own cross."}, "Luke 15": {"15:3–7": "Jesus uses the parable of the lost sheep to show that God rejoices over one sinner who repents, just as a shepherd celebrates finding a lost sheep. The parable emphasizes God's love for the lost and His desire for their return.", "15:8–10": "The parable of the lost coin illustrates the same principle: God actively seeks those who are lost, and there is great rejoicing in heaven when a sinner repents.", "15:29–32": "The parable of the prodigal son is one of the most powerful illustrations of God’s unearned favor and mercy. The younger son represents those who rebel and stray from God, while the father’s loving reception demonstrates God’s readiness to forgive and restore. The elder son symbolizes those who struggle with resentment when others receive mercy.", "15:1–2": "Tax collectors and sinners were gathering to hear Jesus, and the Pharisees complained that He welcomed sinners and ate with them.", "15:11–16": "Jesus told a story about a younger son who asked for his inheritance early, left home, and wasted it all on reckless living. When a famine came, he ended up feeding pigs and was so hungry he wanted to eat their food.", "15:17–20": "The son decided to go back home and admit he had sinned, planning to ask to be treated like a hired servant. But while he was still far off, his father saw him, ran to him, and embraced him.", "15:21–24": "The son confessed he had sinned, but his father called for the best robe, a ring, and sandals for him, and ordered a celebration because his son who was lost had been found.", "15:25–28": "The older son heard the celebration and learned his brother had come home. He became angry and refused to go inside, so his father came out to plead with him."}, "Luke 16": {"16:9–13": "The parable of the unjust steward teaches the importance of using worldly resources wisely for the sake of eternal rewards. Jesus highlights how even a dishonest manager uses shrewdness to secure his future and calls His followers to wisely use their resources for the kingdom of God. Jesus teaches that faithfulness in small things leads to greater responsibility, and warns that one cannot serve both God and money. True loyalty must be given to God alone.", "16:14–18": "Jesus rebukes the Pharisees for justifying themselves before others while ignoring the condition of their hearts. He emphasizes that God's kingdom is now being preached and that His moral law remains intact.", "16:27–31": "In the parable of the rich man and Lazarus, Jesus teaches the reality of eternal destinies. The rich man enjoyed worldly comforts but neglected righteousness, while Lazarus suffered but was carried to paradise. The parable highlights that earthly wealth is no guarantee of eternal security, and even a resurrection will not convince those who refuse to believe God's word.", "16:1–8": "Jesus told a story about a dishonest manager who, about to lose his job, reduced what people owed his master so they would help him later. His master admitted the man had acted shrewdly.", "16:19–21": "Jesus told a story about a rich man who lived in luxury and a poor man named Lazarus who lay at his gate covered in sores, longing for scraps from his table.", "16:22–26": "Both men died. Lazarus was comforted at Abraham's side, while the rich man suffered in torment and begged for relief — but Abraham told him a great chasm separated them and no one could cross it."}, "Luke 17": {"17:3–4": "Jesus warns His disciples about the seriousness of causing others to stumble in their faith. He also emphasizes the importance of forgiveness, even if a person sins repeatedly but repents.", "17:5–6": "In response to the disciples' request to increase their faith, Jesus teaches that even a small amount of faith, like a mustard seed, has great power and can accomplish the impossible.", "17:7–10": "Jesus reminds His disciples that their service to God should be done with humility, not expecting praise or reward, but simply fulfilling their duty as servants of God.", "17:15–19": "Jesus heals ten lepers, but only one, a Samaritan, returns to thank Him. Jesus highlights the importance of gratitude, noting that only one out of the ten returned to give glory to God.", "17:20–21": "Jesus teaches that the kingdom of God is not something that can be observed externally, but rather it is present within the hearts of those who believe and follow Him.", "17:34–37": "Jesus teaches His disciples about the sudden and unexpected nature of His second coming, comparing it to the days of Noah and Lot when judgment came without warning. He stresses the need to be prepared and not look back, as Lot’s wife did.", "17:1–2": "Jesus warned that causing someone to sin brings serious consequences — it would be better to be thrown into the sea with a millstone around your neck than to cause one of His followers to fall.", "17:11–14": "Ten men with leprosy called out to Jesus for mercy. He told them to go show themselves to the priests, and as they went, they were healed.", "17:22–25": "Jesus told His disciples that His return would be sudden and visible to everyone, like lightning across the sky — but first He had to suffer and be rejected.", "17:26–30": "Jesus compared His return to the days of Noah and Lot — people were going about their normal lives right up until sudden destruction came.", "17:31–33": "Jesus warned that when that day comes, people shouldn't turn back for their belongings, and reminded them of Lot's wife, who looked back and was lost."}, "Luke 18": {"18:6–8": "Jesus uses the parable of the persistent widow to teach the importance of perseverance in prayer. He encourages believers to continue praying and not lose heart, knowing that God will bring justice to His people.", "18:13–14": "Through the parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector, Jesus teaches that humility is essential in prayer and before God. Those who humble themselves will be exalted, while those who exalt themselves will be humbled.", "18:15–17": "Jesus welcomes little children, using them as an example of the type of humility and openness required to enter the kingdom of God.", "18:28–30": "The rich young ruler struggles to follow Jesus because of his attachment to wealth. Jesus teaches that it is difficult for those who trust in riches to enter the kingdom of God but reminds His disciples that with God, all things are possible.", "18:31–34": "Jesus again foretells His suffering, death, and resurrection, but His disciples do not fully understand the significance of His words at this time.", "18:40–43": "Jesus heals a blind beggar who calls out to Him in faith, addressing Him as the Son of David. The man's persistence and faith lead to his healing, and the miracle causes many to praise God.", "18:1–5": "Jesus told a story about a widow who kept asking an uncaring judge for justice until he finally gave in just to be rid of her — to show His disciples they should keep praying and not give up.", "18:9–12": "Jesus told a story about a proud Pharisee who thanked God he wasn't like other sinful people, listing his own good deeds.", "18:18–23": "A ruler asked Jesus what he needed to do to inherit eternal life. He said he had kept the commandments since childhood, but when Jesus told him to sell everything and give to the poor, he went away sad because he was very wealthy.", "18:24–27": "Jesus said it's very hard for wealthy people to enter God's kingdom — as hard as a camel fitting through the eye of a needle — but said what's impossible for people is possible for God.", "18:35–39": "A blind man begging near Jericho heard the crowd and asked what was happening. When he learned Jesus was passing by, he shouted for mercy, even though people told him to be quiet."}, "Luke 19": {"19:1–10": "Zacchaeus, a tax collector, seeks out Jesus, and upon meeting Him, experiences a radical change of heart. Jesus declares that salvation has come to Zacchaeus’ house, as He came to seek and save the lost.", "19:24–27": "In the parable of the ten minas, Jesus teaches about the importance of faithfulness in service to God. The parable illustrates that those who wisely use what God has entrusted to them will be rewarded, while those who neglect their responsibilities will face consequences.", "19:36–40": "The triumphal entry of Jesus into Jerusalem fulfills prophecy, as the people welcome Him as King. However, the Pharisees object to the celebration, but Jesus declares that even the stones would cry out if the people were silent.", "19:41–44": "As Jesus approaches Jerusalem, He weeps over the city, knowing that its people will face destruction because they did not recognize the time of God’s visitation.", "19:45–48": "Jesus cleanses the temple, driving out those who were buying and selling, declaring that the temple should be a house of prayer. The religious leaders plot to kill Him, but they are hindered by the crowds who eagerly listen to His teaching.", "19:11–15": "Jesus told a story about a nobleman who gave ten of his servants money to invest while he went away to be made king, and later returned to see what each had done with it.", "19:16–19": "The first servant reported his money had earned ten times more, and was put in charge of ten towns. The second servant's money earned five times more, and he was put in charge of five towns.", "19:20–23": "Another servant said he had kept his master's money safely wrapped up because he was afraid of him. His master called him worthless for not even putting the money in the bank to earn interest.", "19:28–35": "Jesus sent two disciples ahead to get a colt that had never been ridden. Everything happened just as He said, and they brought the colt to Him and helped Him onto it."}, "Luke 20": {"20:1–8": "When the religious leaders question Jesus’ authority, He turns the question back to them, asking about John the Baptist's authority. Their inability to answer reveals their fear of the people and unwillingness to acknowledge the truth.", "20:17–19": "Jesus tells the parable of the wicked tenants to illustrate how the religious leaders have rejected God's messengers and even His Son. He warns that their rejection will result in judgment, and others will receive the blessings they forfeited.", "20:23–26": "When asked whether it is lawful to pay taxes to Caesar, Jesus responds by affirming that what belongs to Caesar should be given to Caesar, but what belongs to God must be given to God. His wise answer leaves His critics unable to trap Him.", "20:34–40": "Jesus answers the Sadducees' question about the resurrection by teaching that in the age to come, there will be no marriage, and those who are resurrected will be like angels. He also affirms the reality of the resurrection by pointing to the fact that God is the God of the living, not the dead.", "20:41–44": "Jesus challenges the common understanding of the Messiah being David’s son by quoting David, who calls the Messiah Lord, pointing to the divine nature of the Christ.", "20:45–47": "Jesus warns the people to beware of the scribes, who seek honor and power but are corrupt in their hearts, exploiting the vulnerable. He declares that they will face greater condemnation for their hypocrisy.", "20:9–16": "Jesus told a story about tenant farmers who beat the servants a landowner sent to collect his share of the crop, and finally killed his son too — so the owner destroyed them and gave the vineyard to others.", "20:20–22": "Spies pretending to be sincere asked Jesus if it was right to pay taxes to Caesar, hoping to trap Him in His words.", "20:27–33": "Sadducees, who didn't believe in resurrection, asked Jesus about a woman who had married seven brothers one after another, asking whose wife she would be after the resurrection."}, "Luke 21": {"21:1–4": "Jesus highlights the offering of a poor widow, who gave all she had, contrasting her small gift with the large gifts of the rich. He teaches that true giving is not measured by the amount, but by the heart and sacrifice behind it.", "21:5–6": "Jesus foretells the destruction of the temple in Jerusalem, indicating that even the most magnificent structures on earth will not last forever.", "21:10–19": "Jesus warns His disciples about false prophets, wars, natural disasters, and other signs that will precede the end times. He instructs them not to be afraid, as these things must happen before the end comes. Jesus warns His disciples that they will face persecution for His name's sake but encourages them not to fear. He promises that He will give them the words they need and that despite the trials, they will ultimately be preserved.", "21:20–24": "Jesus prophesies the destruction of Jerusalem and urges His followers to flee when they see the city surrounded by armies. This prophecy was fulfilled in AD 70 when the Roman army destroyed Jerusalem.", "21:25–28": "Jesus speaks of cosmic signs that will precede His return. He encourages His followers to take heart and look up, for their redemption is near when these things begin to happen.", "21:29–33": "Jesus uses the fig tree as a metaphor, telling His disciples that just as they know summer is near when the tree's leaves appear, they should recognize the signs of His coming and know that the kingdom of God is near.", "21:34–36": "Jesus warns His disciples to be vigilant and avoid being weighed down by the cares of this world. He encourages them to stay alert and prayerful so they will be ready for His return.", "21:37–38": "Jesus spends His days teaching in the temple, and crowds gather early each morning to listen to Him. At night, He retreats to the Mount of Olives.", "21:7–9": "The disciples asked when the temple would be destroyed. Jesus warned them not to be deceived by people claiming to be Him, and not to be afraid of wars and uprisings, since the end wouldn't come right away."}, "Luke 22": {"22:1–6": "As the Passover approaches, the religious leaders plot to kill Jesus, but they fear the reaction of the people. Judas Iscariot, one of the twelve disciples, agrees to betray Jesus to the chief priests, accepting money in exchange for leading them to Jesus when He is away from the crowds.", "22:7–13": "Jesus sends Peter and John to prepare for the Passover meal, providing them with specific instructions on how to find the location. They follow His directions and make the preparations.", "22:19–20": "During the Last Supper, Jesus shares bread and wine with His disciples, symbolizing His body and blood, which will be given for them. He establishes this as a new covenant and instructs them to continue this practice in remembrance of Him.", "22:21–23": "Jesus reveals to His disciples that one of them will betray Him. This causes them to question among themselves who it might be.", "22:24–30": "Jesus addresses the disciples’ dispute over who is the greatest by teaching that true greatness comes through humility and service. He reminds them that He has come to serve, and He promises them a place in His kingdom.", "22:31–34": "Jesus warns Peter that Satan desires to test him, but He has prayed for Peter’s faith. Though Peter declares his loyalty, Jesus predicts that Peter will deny Him three times before the rooster crows.", "22:35–38": "Jesus prepares His disciples for the difficult times ahead, telling them that they will now need to be self-sufficient. He refers to the prophecy that He will be counted among the transgressors.", "22:39–46": "In the Garden of Gethsemane, Jesus prays in agony, asking God to remove the cup of suffering from Him, yet submitting to the Father’s will. An angel strengthens Him, and He returns to find the disciples sleeping instead of praying.", "22:47–53": "Judas betrays Jesus with a kiss, and Jesus is arrested. One of the disciples cuts off the ear of the high priest’s servant, but Jesus heals him. Jesus then confronts the mob, questioning why they come to arrest Him as if He were a criminal.", "22:54–62": "Just as Jesus predicted, Peter denies knowing Him three times before the rooster crows. When Peter realizes what he has done, he is filled with sorrow and weeps bitterly.", "22:63–65": "Jesus is mocked, beaten, and taunted by the guards who blindfold Him and challenge Him to prophesy who struck Him.", "22:66–71": "Jesus is brought before the Sanhedrin, and they question Him about being the Messiah. Jesus affirms that He is the Son of God, which leads them to conclude that they need no further testimony to condemn Him.", "22:14–18": "Jesus sat down with His twelve apostles for the Passover meal and told them He had longed to eat it with them before His suffering, then shared a cup with them."}, "Luke 23": {"23:1–5": "The religious leaders bring Jesus before Pilate, accusing Him of claiming to be a king and opposing Caesar. Pilate questions Jesus and declares that he finds no fault in Him, but the leaders persist with their accusations.", "23:6–12": "Pilate sends Jesus to Herod, hoping to pass off the responsibility. Herod, curious to see Jesus perform a miracle, questions Him but receives no answer. Herod mocks Jesus and sends Him back to Pilate.", "23:18–25": "Pilate tries multiple times to release Jesus, finding no fault in Him, but the crowd demands His crucifixion and calls for the release of Barabbas, a murderer. Eventually, Pilate gives in to the crowd's demands.", "23:39–43": "As Jesus is led to the cross, He speaks to the women of Jerusalem, warning of difficult days ahead. On the cross, He prays for forgiveness for those crucifying Him and promises one of the criminals next to Him that he will be with Him in paradise.", "23:47–49": "At Jesus' death, darkness covers the land, and the temple veil is torn in two. Jesus commends His spirit to the Father and dies. A centurion recognizes Jesus’ righteousness, and the crowd leaves in sorrow.", "23:50–56": "Joseph of Arimathea, a good and just man, requests Jesus’ body from Pilate. He buries Jesus in a tomb that had never been used. The women who followed Jesus prepare spices to anoint His body but rest on the Sabbath.", "23:13–17": "Pilate told the chief priests and the people that he had found no reason to sentence Jesus to death, and neither had Herod, so he planned to punish Him lightly and release Him.", "23:26–31": "As Jesus was led away, Simon of Cyrene was made to carry His cross. Jesus told the women weeping for Him to weep for themselves and their children instead, because hard times were coming.", "23:32–38": "Jesus was crucified between two criminals. He asked God to forgive those responsible, and the soldiers and rulers mocked Him while a sign above Him read 'This is the King of the Jews.'", "23:44–46": "Darkness covered the land for three hours, the temple curtain tore in two, and Jesus cried out, 'Father, into Your hands I commit My spirit,' and died."}, "Luke 24": {"24:9–12": "The women discover the empty tomb on the first day of the week and are told by angels that Jesus has risen. They report this to the apostles, but their words are initially met with disbelief. Peter runs to the tomb and finds it empty, marveling at what has happened.", "24:33–35": "Two disciples on the road to Emmaus encounter the risen Jesus, but they do not recognize Him until He breaks bread with them. They reflect on how their hearts burned while He explained the Scriptures to them and rush back to Jerusalem to share the news.", "24:36–43": "Jesus appears to His disciples and shows them His hands and feet, reassuring them that He is not a spirit but has risen bodily. He even eats in their presence to prove His physical resurrection.", "24:44–49": "Jesus explains to His disciples how the Scriptures foretold His suffering, death, and resurrection. He commissions them to preach repentance and forgiveness of sins to all nations, beginning in Jerusalem, and promises the coming of the Holy Spirit.", "24:50–53": "Jesus ascends into heaven after blessing His disciples. They return to Jerusalem with great joy and continue praising God in the temple, awaiting the promised Holy Spirit.", "24:1–8": "Women came to the tomb early in the morning with spices and found the stone rolled away and the body gone. Two angels told them Jesus had risen, just as He said He would.", "24:13–16": "Two disciples were walking to Emmaus, talking about everything that had happened, when Jesus joined them — but they didn't recognize Him.", "24:17–24": "Jesus asked what they were discussing, and they told Him about Jesus' death and how some women had found the tomb empty and reported seeing angels who said He was alive.", "24:25–27": "Jesus told them they were slow to believe what the prophets had said, and He explained from the Scriptures, starting with Moses, everything written about Himself.", "24:28–32": "At the table, Jesus took bread, gave thanks, and broke it — and suddenly they recognized Him, right before He disappeared. They realized their hearts had been burning as He talked with them on the road."}, "John 1": {"1:1–5": "John introduces Jesus as the Word, who existed with God from the beginning, and through whom all things were made. Jesus is the light that shines in the darkness, but the darkness did not understand it.", "1:6–13": "John the Baptist was sent by God to testify about Jesus, the true Light. Though the world was made through Him, the world did not recognize Him.", "1:14–18": "Jesus, the Word, became flesh and lived among us, revealing the glory of God. John the Baptist testified that Jesus was greater than him because He existed before him. Through Jesus, we receive grace and truth. He reveals the Father, whom no one has ever seen.", "1:19–23": "John the Baptist denies being the Christ or Elijah but declares that he is the voice crying in the wilderness, preparing the way for the Lord.", "1:24–28": "John humbly acknowledges his role, pointing to Jesus as the One far greater than himself.", "1:29–34": "John the Baptist identifies Jesus as the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world and testifies that Jesus is the Son of God.", "1:35–39": "John’s disciples follow Jesus after hearing John’s testimony, and Jesus invites them to come and see where He is staying. They stay with Him, marking the beginning of their discipleship.", "1:47–51": "Jesus calls Philip, and Philip brings Nathanael to meet Jesus. Despite Nathanael's initial skepticism, Jesus reveals His supernatural knowledge of Nathanael, leading him to declare that Jesus is the Son of God and King of Israel. Jesus promises Nathanael and the other disciples that they will see even greater things, including heavenly revelations involving the Son of Man.", "1:40–42": "Andrew found his brother Simon and told him they had found the Messiah, then brought him to Jesus. Jesus told Simon he would be called Cephas, meaning 'a stone.'", "1:43–46": "Jesus found Philip and told him to follow Him. Philip then found Nathanael and told him they'd found the one Moses and the prophets wrote about — Jesus of Nazareth. Nathanael doubted anything good could come from Nazareth, so Philip told him to come and see for himself."}, "John 2": {"2:9–11": "At a wedding in Cana, Jesus performs His first miracle by turning water into wine, revealing His glory and leading His disciples to believe in Him.", "2:13–17": "Jesus clears the temple of merchants and money changers, declaring that His Father’s house is not a place of business but a house of worship.", "2:18–22": "Jesus predicts His death and resurrection by referring to His body as the temple that would be destroyed and raised up in three days.", "2:23–25": "Many people believe in Jesus because of His miracles, but Jesus knows the hearts of all people and does not entrust Himself to them.", "2:1–5": "At a wedding in Cana, the wine ran out. Jesus' mother told Him, and though He said His time hadn't come yet, she told the servants to do whatever He said.", "2:6–8": "There were six large stone jars nearby. Jesus told the servants to fill them with water, then to take some to the master of the feast.", "2:12": "After this, Jesus went to Capernaum with His mother, brothers, and disciples, and they stayed there a few days."}, "John 3": {"3:4–8": "Nicodemus, a Pharisee, visits Jesus at night, seeking to understand His teachings. Jesus tells him that no one can enter the kingdom of God unless they are born again of water and the Spirit.", "3:14–18": "Jesus explains that God loved the world so much that He sent His only Son to save it, not to condemn it. Belief in Jesus brings eternal life, but rejecting Him leads to condemnation.", "3:19–21": "Jesus speaks of the judgment that comes when people reject the light in favor of darkness because they do not want their evil deeds exposed.", "3:25–30": "John the Baptist humbly exalts Jesus, declaring that Jesus must increase while he, John, must decrease. He rejoices in being the friend of the bridegroom who announces the arrival of the Messiah.", "3:31–36": "John the Baptist declares the supremacy of Christ, emphasizing that eternal life is found in believing in the Son of God, while those who reject Him remain under God’s wrath.", "3:1–3": "Nicodemus, a Pharisee, came to Jesus at night and said he knew Jesus was from God because of the signs He did. Jesus told him that no one can see the kingdom of God unless they are born again.", "3:9–13": "Nicodemus asked how this could happen. Jesus told him that He speaks about what He knows and has seen, and that no one has gone up to heaven except the Son of Man, who came down from heaven.", "3:22–24": "Jesus and His disciples went into the Judean countryside, where He baptized people. John was also baptizing nearby, before he was put in prison."}, "John 4": {"4:10–15": "Jesus engages a Samaritan woman at a well, offering her living water that leads to eternal life. This conversation breaks cultural barriers and reveals His identity as the Messiah.", "4:25–26": "Jesus teaches the Samaritan woman about true worship, which is not bound to a location but is in spirit and in truth. He reveals Himself as the Messiah she has been waiting for.", "4:39–42": "Many Samaritans come to believe in Jesus after hearing the woman’s testimony and meeting Jesus themselves. They recognize Him as the Savior of the world.", "4:51–54": "Jesus heals an official's son, demonstrating His power and leading the man and his entire household to believe in Him.", "4:1–6": "Jesus left Judea for Galilee, traveling through Samaria. Tired from the journey, He sat down by Jacob's well around midday.", "4:7–9": "A Samaritan woman came to draw water, and Jesus asked her for a drink. She was surprised, since Jews and Samaritans normally had nothing to do with each other.", "4:16–19": "Jesus told her to go get her husband. She said she had none, and Jesus told her she was right — she had actually had five husbands, and the man she was with now wasn't her husband. She realized He must be a prophet.", "4:20–24": "The woman asked about where people should worship — this mountain or Jerusalem. Jesus told her that true worshipers would soon worship the Father in spirit and truth, wherever they were.", "4:27–30": "The disciples were surprised to find Jesus talking with the woman. She left her water jar and ran to tell the town about a man who told her everything she'd ever done, wondering if He could be the Messiah.", "4:31–34": "The disciples urged Jesus to eat something, but He told them His true food was doing the will of the One who sent Him and finishing His work.", "4:35–38": "Jesus told the disciples to look at the fields — they were already ripe for harvest — and said He was sending them to reap a harvest that others had worked hard to prepare.", "4:43–45": "Jesus went on to Galilee, and even though He'd said a prophet isn't honored in his own country, the Galileans welcomed Him because of what they'd seen Him do in Jerusalem.", "4:46–50": "A royal official begged Jesus to come heal his dying son in Capernaum. Jesus simply told him, 'Go, your son lives,' and the man believed Jesus' word and went home."}, "John 5": {"5:6–9": "At the pool of Bethesda, Jesus encounters a man who had been suffering for 38 years. After asking if the man wanted to be healed, Jesus commands him to rise, take up his bed, and walk. The man is healed instantly, even though it was the Sabbath.", "5:14–18": "After the man is healed, the Jewish leaders criticize him for carrying his bed on the Sabbath. When they discover that Jesus had healed him, they begin to persecute Jesus, accusing Him of violating the Sabbath and claiming equality with God.", "5:24–27": "Jesus explains that His authority comes directly from the Father. He only does what He sees the Father doing, and the Father has entrusted Him with the power to give life and execute judgment. To honor the Son is to honor the Father, and those who believe in Him receive eternal life.", "5:28–30": "Jesus declares that a time is coming when the dead will hear His voice and be raised to life—some to eternal life and others to condemnation. He emphasizes that His judgment is just, as He seeks only the will of the Father.", "5:36–40": "Jesus explains that He does not bear witness to Himself; instead, His works and the testimony of John the Baptist, the Father, and the Scriptures testify to His divine mission. Despite this, many refuse to believe in Him, even though the Scriptures point directly to Him.", "5:41–47": "Jesus concludes by rebuking the religious leaders for seeking human honor rather than the honor that comes from God. He points out that if they truly believed Moses, they would believe in Him, as Moses wrote about Him. Their rejection of Jesus shows their lack of faith in the Scriptures.", "5:1–5": "At a pool in Jerusalem called Bethesda, many sick people waited for the water to stir, believing the first one in would be healed. One man had been sick there for thirty-eight years.", "5:10–13": "The Jewish leaders told the healed man he wasn't allowed to carry his mat on the Sabbath. He said the man who healed him had told him to, but he didn't know who it was, since Jesus had slipped away.", "5:19–23": "Jesus explained that He only does what He sees the Father doing, and that the Father has given Him the authority to give life and to judge, so that everyone would honor the Son just as they honor the Father.", "5:31–35": "Jesus said His own testimony about Himself wasn't the only proof — John the Baptist had also told the truth about Him, and people had been glad to listen to John for a while."}, "John 6": {"6:14–15": "Jesus performs the miraculous feeding of the five thousand with five loaves and two fish, demonstrating His divine power to provide abundantly. The people, recognizing the miracle, proclaim Jesus as the promised Prophet.", "6:16–21": "After retreating to the mountains, Jesus walks on water to reach His disciples, who are struggling against the wind. His presence calms their fears, and He safely guides them to shore, revealing His mastery over nature.", "6:35–40": "Jesus identifies Himself as the bread of life, offering eternal satisfaction and life to all who come to Him. He challenges the people to seek not earthly bread but the spiritual nourishment that only He can provide—eternal life through belief in Him.", "6:47–51": "Jesus faces opposition as He claims to be the bread of life who came down from heaven. He reveals that His flesh is the bread He will give for the life of the world, symbolizing His sacrificial death. He emphasizes that only those drawn by the Father can come to Him and receive eternal life.", "6:52–59": "Jesus speaks symbolically about eating His flesh and drinking His blood, pointing to His future sacrifice on the cross. This teaching emphasizes the necessity of fully partaking in His life, death, and resurrection in order to have eternal life.", "6:66–71": "Many of Jesus' disciples struggle to accept His teaching, finding it too difficult to understand or believe. As a result, many turn away from following Him, revealing the challenge of true discipleship and belief in His words. In response to the departure of many disciples, Peter boldly affirms the faith of the Twelve, declaring that Jesus alone has the words of eternal life. Despite this, Jesus foreshadows the betrayal by Judas, one of His chosen twelve disciples.", "6:1–4": "A large crowd followed Jesus across the Sea of Galilee because they had seen Him healing the sick. Jesus went up a mountain and sat down with His disciples as Passover approached.", "6:5–9": "Jesus asked Philip where they could buy bread for the crowd, testing him, since He already knew what He'd do. Andrew mentioned a boy with five loaves and two small fish, though it seemed like far too little.", "6:10–13": "Jesus had the crowd sit down, then gave thanks and distributed the bread and fish until everyone had eaten enough. Afterward the leftover pieces filled twelve baskets.", "6:22–27": "The crowd went looking for Jesus and found Him on the other side of the lake. He told them they were following Him because they'd eaten bread and were full, not because they understood the signs He'd done.", "6:28–31": "The people asked what they should do to do God's work. Jesus told them to believe in the One God sent. They asked for a sign, mentioning how their ancestors ate manna in the wilderness.", "6:32–34": "Jesus told them it wasn't Moses who gave the bread from heaven — His Father gives the true bread from heaven, which gives life to the world. They asked Him to always give them this bread.", "6:41–46": "The people grumbled because Jesus said He had come down from heaven, since they knew Him as Joseph's son. Jesus told them no one can come to Him unless the Father draws them.", "6:60–65": "Many of Jesus' followers said this teaching was hard to accept. Jesus told them it is the Spirit who gives life, and said He knew from the start which of them didn't believe and who would betray Him."}, "John 7": {"7:10–13": "Jesus' brothers, who did not yet believe in Him, urge Him to go to the Feast of Tabernacles to perform miracles and gain public recognition. However, Jesus explains that His time has not yet come. He later goes to the feast secretly, where opinions about Him are divided, but no one speaks openly for fear of the religious authorities.", "7:19–24": "In the middle of the feast, Jesus teaches in the temple. The Jews marvel at His wisdom, questioning how He could know the Scriptures without formal education. Jesus explains that His teachings come from God, and He challenges their hypocrisy in accusing Him of breaking the Sabbath while they themselves perform circumcisions on the Sabbath.", "7:25–31": "Jesus’ bold teaching causes many to question whether He is the Messiah. While some doubt because they know where Jesus is from, others believe in Him because of the miracles He has performed. Despite attempts to seize Him, no one can lay hands on Him, as His time had not yet come.", "7:32–36": "The Pharisees send officers to arrest Jesus, but He tells them that He will soon return to the Father, where they cannot follow. The Jews are confused, wondering if He plans to leave and teach among the Gentiles.", "7:37–39": "On the final day of the feast, Jesus offers the promise of living water to those who believe in Him, referring to the Holy Spirit, which would be given after His glorification.", "7:40–44": "Jesus’ words cause division among the people. Some believe He is the Prophet or the Messiah, while others doubt because He comes from Galilee, not recognizing that He was born in Bethlehem, fulfilling the Scriptures.", "7:45–53": "The officers sent to arrest Jesus are amazed by His teaching and return without arresting Him. The Pharisees express their disbelief and disdain for the common people, while Nicodemus, who had previously met with Jesus, defends the idea of giving Him a fair hearing.", "7:1–5": "Jesus stayed in Galilee because the Jewish leaders in Judea wanted to kill Him. His own brothers, who didn't believe in Him, told Him to go to Judea and show Himself publicly at the festival.", "7:6–9": "Jesus told His brothers His time hadn't come yet, and that the world hated Him because He exposed its evil. He stayed behind in Galilee while they went up to the festival.", "7:14–18": "Jesus began teaching in the temple partway through the festival, and the people were amazed since He hadn't been formally trained. He told them His teaching came from God, not from Himself."}, "John 8": {"8:7–11": "The scribes and Pharisees attempt to trap Jesus by bringing a woman caught in adultery, seeking to test His response to the law of Moses. Jesus, however, challenges them, saying that only the one without sin should cast the first stone. One by one, they leave, and Jesus forgives the woman, telling her to go and sin no more.", "8:15–20": "Jesus declares Himself as the light of the world, offering life and guidance to all who follow Him. Despite the Pharisees’ attempts to discredit Him, Jesus explains that His testimony is valid because both He and the Father bear witness to the truth.", "8:25–30": "Jesus predicts His departure, warning the Pharisees that unless they believe in Him, they will die in their sins. He points to His coming crucifixion, saying that once the Son of Man is lifted up, they will realize He was sent by the Father. Many who heard Him speak believed in Him.", "8:37–41": "Jesus tells the believers that true discipleship involves continuing in His word. By doing so, they will know the truth, and the truth will set them free from the bondage of sin. He explains that those who sin are slaves to sin but that true freedom comes through the Son.", "8:42–47": "Jesus rebukes the Jews for claiming to be children of Abraham while seeking to kill Him. He declares that if they truly were Abraham's children, they would believe as Abraham did. Instead, they are acting like their true father, the devil, who is the father of lies.", "8:57–59": "Jesus responds to the Jews' accusation by declaring His eternal existence. He reveals that before Abraham existed, He is the eternal I AM, a direct claim of divinity. This statement causes the Jews to attempt to stone Him, but Jesus escapes from their midst.", "8:1–6": "Teachers of the law brought a woman caught in adultery to Jesus, asking if she should be stoned as the law required, trying to trap Him. Jesus bent down and wrote on the ground instead of answering right away.", "8:12–14": "Jesus said He was the light of the world, and that whoever followed Him would have the light of life. The Pharisees said He couldn't testify about Himself, but Jesus said His testimony was still true because He knew where He came from.", "8:21–24": "Jesus told the Jewish leaders He was going away, and they would not be able to follow. He said they would die in their sins if they didn't believe He is who He said He was.", "8:31–36": "Jesus told those who believed in Him that if they held to His teaching, they would know the truth, and the truth would set them free. When they objected that they'd never been slaves, He explained that everyone who sins is a slave to sin.", "8:48–51": "The Jewish leaders accused Jesus of being a Samaritan with a demon. He denied it, saying He honors His Father, and that whoever keeps His word will never see death.", "8:52–56": "They pointed out that Abraham and the prophets had died, and asked if Jesus thought He was greater than Abraham. Jesus said His Father glorifies Him, and that Abraham had rejoiced to see His day."}, "John 9": {"9:8–12": "Jesus heals a man who was blind from birth, demonstrating that the man’s blindness was not due to sin but so that the works of God might be revealed. The man obeys Jesus' command to wash in the pool of Siloam, and his sight is miraculously restored, leading to wonder and questioning among his neighbors.", "9:18–23": "The Pharisees begin investigating the healing, focusing on the fact that it occurred on the Sabbath. They question the man and his parents, but despite the miraculous nature of the healing, the religious leaders are divided in their judgments about Jesus. Out of fear, the man's parents direct the Pharisees back to their son for further questioning.", "9:28–34": "The healed man stands firm in his testimony despite pressure from the Pharisees to denounce Jesus. His simple yet powerful statement—I was blind, now I see—profoundly impacts the discussion. However, the Pharisees, refusing to accept the man’s testimony, expel him from the synagogue.", "9:39–41": "Jesus finds the man and reveals Himself as the Son of God, and the man responds in faith and worship. Jesus contrasts physical blindness with spiritual blindness, explaining that those who admit their need for sight (spiritual understanding) can be healed, while those who claim to see (but are spiritually blind) remain in their sin.", "9:1–5": "Jesus' disciples asked whose sin caused a man to be born blind. Jesus said it wasn't anyone's sin — it happened so God's work could be shown in him.", "9:6–7": "Jesus made mud with spit and put it on the blind man's eyes, then told him to go wash in the Pool of Siloam. The man went, washed, and came back able to see.", "9:13–17": "The healed man was brought to the Pharisees, who were divided about Jesus because the healing happened on the Sabbath. When they asked the man what he thought, he said Jesus was a prophet.", "9:24–27": "The Pharisees questioned the man again, insisting Jesus was a sinner. He simply said he didn't know about that, but he knew he had been blind and now could see.", "9:35–38": "Jesus found the man after he'd been thrown out and asked if he believed in the Son of God. When Jesus told him it was Him speaking, the man said, 'Lord, I believe,' and worshiped Him."}, "John 10": {"10:16–18": "Jesus teaches that He is the good shepherd, who knows His sheep, and they know Him. Unlike hired hands who flee when danger comes, Jesus lays down His life for His sheep. He contrasts Himself with thieves and robbers who seek to harm and steal from the sheep. Through Jesus, the door, people find salvation and abundant life.", "10:19–21": "Jesus’ claims about being the Good Shepherd cause division among the Jews. Some accuse Him of being demon-possessed, while others defend Him, pointing to the miracle of opening the eyes of the blind as evidence that He is not possessed.", "10:31–33": "During the Feast of Dedication, the Jews confront Jesus and demand a clear answer about whether He is the Messiah. Jesus affirms that His works testify to His identity, and His sheep hear His voice and follow Him. He declares that He and the Father are one, which leads the Jews to attempt to stone Him for blasphemy.", "10:34–39": "Jesus defends His claim of divinity by pointing to Scripture, referencing how it refers to certain people as gods in the sense that they received God’s word. He emphasizes that His works bear witness to His divine nature. Despite His explanation, the Jews again seek to arrest Him, but He escapes.", "10:40–42": "Jesus withdraws beyond the Jordan River, where many people come to Him and believe in Him, acknowledging that everything John the Baptist said about Him was true.", "10:1–6": "Jesus described a shepherd who enters through the gate and whose sheep recognize his voice and follow him, while they run from a stranger's voice. The people didn't understand what He meant.", "10:7–10": "Jesus explained further, saying He is the gate for the sheep, and that whoever comes in through Him will be saved, while a thief only comes to steal and destroy.", "10:11–15": "Jesus said He is the good shepherd who lays down His life for the sheep, unlike a hired hand who runs away when danger comes because the sheep aren't really his.", "10:22–26": "During the Festival of Dedication, the Jewish leaders asked Jesus to tell them plainly if He was the Messiah. He said He already had, through the works He did, but they didn't believe because they weren't His sheep.", "10:27–30": "Jesus said His sheep listen to His voice and follow Him, and that no one can snatch them out of His hand or His Father's hand. He said, 'I and the Father are one.'"}, "John 11": {"11:11–16": "When Lazarus, the brother of Mary and Martha, becomes seriously ill, Jesus delays His visit for two days, explaining that the sickness is for the glory of God. He refers to Lazarus' death as sleep, indicating that He will raise him, but the disciples misunderstand. Despite the danger of returning to Judaea, Jesus resolves to go, and Thomas expresses a willingness to go with Him, even if it means death.", "11:33–37": "Martha expresses her faith in Jesus and her belief in the resurrection, but she doesn’t fully understand that Jesus is about to raise Lazarus immediately. Jesus proclaims, I am the resurrection and the life, revealing that faith in Him brings eternal life. When He sees Mary and the others weeping, Jesus is deeply moved, and He weeps with them.", "11:41–44": "In a powerful display of divine authority, Jesus raises Lazarus from the dead. This miracle, performed in the presence of many witnesses, demonstrates the glory of God and confirms Jesus’ claim to be the resurrection and the life.", "11:49–53": "Many Jews believe in Jesus after witnessing the miracle of Lazarus, but others report it to the Pharisees, prompting the religious leaders to intensify their plot to kill Jesus. Caiaphas, the high priest, unknowingly prophesies that Jesus’ death will be for the salvation of the nation and the world.", "11:55–57": "As the Passover approaches, the tension around Jesus’ ministry intensifies. The religious authorities issue orders for Jesus' arrest, but Jesus withdraws to Ephraim with His disciples to avoid public attention.", "11:1–6": "Lazarus of Bethany, the brother of Mary and Martha, became sick. His sisters sent word to Jesus, but when He heard about it, He stayed where He was two more days.", "11:7–10": "Jesus told His disciples they were going back to Judea. They reminded Him people there had just tried to stone Him, but Jesus answered by talking about walking in daylight so as not to stumble.", "11:17–22": "When Jesus arrived, Lazarus had already been dead four days. Martha went out to meet Him and told Him that if He had been there, her brother wouldn't have died, but she still believed God would give Him whatever He asked.", "11:23–27": "Jesus told Martha her brother would rise again. When she said she knew he'd rise on the last day, Jesus told her He Himself is the resurrection and the life, and asked if she believed this. She said yes, and that He was the Messiah.", "11:28–32": "Martha quietly told Mary that Jesus was asking for her. Mary got up quickly and went to Him, and when she reached Him she fell at His feet and said the same thing Martha had said — that if He'd been there, her brother wouldn't have died.", "11:38–40": "Jesus, deeply moved, came to the tomb and asked for the stone to be taken away. Martha warned there would be a bad smell after four days, but Jesus reminded her she would see God's glory if she believed.", "11:45–48": "Many people who saw what Jesus did believed in Him, but others reported it to the Pharisees. The chief priests and Pharisees worried that if they let Jesus continue, everyone would believe in Him and the Romans would come and destroy their nation.", "11:54": "Because of this, Jesus stopped moving about publicly and went instead to a town called Ephraim near the wilderness, staying there with His disciples."}, "John 12": {"12:1–8": "Six days before Passover, Mary anoints Jesus with costly perfume, foreshadowing His burial. Judas criticizes the act, under the guise of concern for the poor, but Jesus defends Mary, recognizing the significance of her act of worship.", "12:9–11": "The resurrection of Lazarus draws a crowd, and many believe in Jesus because of this miracle. However, the chief priests plot to kill Lazarus as well, fearing the impact of his testimony on the people.", "12:17–19": "Jesus enters Jerusalem in what is known as the Triumphal Entry, fulfilling prophecy as He rides a donkey. The crowd greets Him as their King, shouting Hosanna, and many are there because of the miracle of raising Lazarus. The Pharisees, seeing His growing influence, feel powerless to stop Him.", "12:34–36": "Jesus speaks of His approaching death, using the metaphor of a grain of wheat that must die to produce fruit. He calls His followers to be willing to give up their lives for the sake of eternal life. A voice from heaven confirms God’s glory in Jesus’ mission. Jesus hints at His crucifixion, saying that when He is lifted up, He will draw all people to Himself.", "12:42–43": "Despite the many miracles Jesus performed, many people still did not believe in Him, fulfilling the prophecy of Isaiah. Even some of the rulers believed but were afraid to openly confess their faith because they feared the Pharisees and loved human approval more than God’s.", "12:44–50": "In His final public address, Jesus makes a powerful appeal for faith, declaring that believing in Him is believing in the One who sent Him. He came as a light to save the world, not to judge it, but rejection of His words will lead to judgment in the last day.", "12:12–16": "The crowd that had come for the festival heard Jesus was coming and went out with palm branches to meet Him, shouting 'Hosanna.' Jesus rode in on a young donkey, just as the Scriptures had said He would.", "12:20–23": "Some Greeks at the festival asked Philip if they could see Jesus. When Philip and Andrew told Jesus, He said the time had come for Him to be glorified.", "12:24–26": "Jesus said a grain of wheat has to fall into the ground and die before it can produce a harvest, and that whoever loses their life for His sake will keep it forever.", "12:27–30": "Jesus said His soul was troubled, but that He wouldn't ask to be saved from this moment, since it was the reason He'd come. A voice from heaven spoke, and Jesus said it was for the crowd's sake, not His.", "12:31–33": "Jesus said the ruler of this world was about to be driven out, and that when He was lifted up, He would draw everyone to Himself — pointing to the kind of death He would die.", "12:37–41": "Even after seeing so many signs, most people still didn't believe in Jesus, which Isaiah the prophet had said would happen."}, "John 13": {"13:6–11": "During the Last Supper, Jesus humbly washes His disciples' feet, symbolizing servanthood and purification. Peter resists at first but ultimately submits when Jesus explains that being washed is necessary for belonging to Him. Jesus also hints that not all the disciples are spiritually clean, alluding to Judas' impending betrayal.", "13:27–30": "Jesus explains the significance of His act of washing the disciples' feet, calling them to serve one another as He has served them. He then predicts His betrayal and identifies Judas as the betrayer by giving him a piece of bread, at which point Satan enters Judas. The other disciples, however, do not fully grasp what is happening.", "13:31–35": "After Judas departs, Jesus declares that His glorification is at hand. He gives His disciples a new commandment: to love one another as He has loved them. This love will be the defining mark of true discipleship and how others will recognize them as followers of Christ.", "13:36–38": "Peter, eager to follow Jesus wherever He goes, boldly declares his willingness to die for Him. However, Jesus predicts that before the rooster crows, Peter will deny Him three times.", "13:1–3": "Just before Passover, Jesus knew His time had come to leave this world and go to the Father. He loved His disciples to the very end, even though the devil had already put it in Judas's heart to betray Him.", "13:4–5": "Jesus got up from supper, wrapped a towel around His waist, and began washing His disciples' feet, drying them with the towel.", "13:12–17": "After washing their feet, Jesus asked if they understood what He'd done, and told them to follow His example by serving one another the same way.", "13:18–20": "Jesus said He wasn't speaking of all of them — one would betray Him, fulfilling Scripture — and He told them this ahead of time so they would believe when it happened.", "13:21–26": "Jesus said plainly that one of them would betray Him, and the disciples wondered who He meant. When Peter had John ask, Jesus said it would be the one He gave a piece of bread to, and then gave it to Judas."}, "John 14": {"14:5–7": "Jesus comforts His disciples by telling them not to be troubled. He reassures them that He is going to prepare a place for them in His Father’s house and that He will return for them. When Thomas expresses confusion, Jesus declares that He is the only way to the Father, emphasizing that to know Him is to know God.", "14:12–14": "When Philip asks to see the Father, Jesus responds that to see Him is to see the Father. He explains that He and the Father are one, and His words and works come directly from the Father. Jesus also promises that those who believe in Him will do greater works and that whatever they ask in His name will be done, bringing glory to the Father.", "14:25–27": "Jesus promises to send the Holy Spirit, the Comforter, who will be with His disciples forever, teaching them and reminding them of His words. He assures them that He will not leave them alone and that, through the Holy Spirit, they will know that He is in the Father and they are in Him. Those who love Him will obey His commandments, and both Jesus and the Father will dwell with them.", "14:28–31": "Jesus leaves His disciples with peace, a peace that is different from what the world offers. He tells them not to be troubled or afraid and reminds them that His return to the Father is a reason to rejoice. He also speaks of the coming of the enemy, but assures them that Satan has no hold over Him. Jesus’ obedience to the Father’s will is His demonstration of love for the Father.", "14:1–4": "Jesus told His disciples not to be troubled, and said He was going to prepare a place for them in His Father's house, and would come back to take them there.", "14:8–11": "Philip asked Jesus to show them the Father. Jesus told him that anyone who had seen Him had seen the Father, because He and the Father are one.", "14:15–17": "Jesus told His disciples that if they loved Him, they would obey His commands, and that He would ask the Father to send them another Helper — the Spirit of truth.", "14:18–21": "Jesus promised not to leave them like orphans — He would come to them. He said that because He lives, they would live too, and that whoever keeps His commands loves Him.", "14:22–24": "The other Judas asked why Jesus would show Himself to them and not to the world. Jesus answered that whoever loves Him keeps His word, and He and the Father would come and make their home with that person."}, "John 15": {"15:9–11": "Jesus uses the metaphor of a vine and branches to describe the relationship between Himself and His disciples. He is the true vine, and His followers are the branches. Those who remain connected to Him will bear fruit, while those who do not will wither. Jesus calls His disciples to abide in Him, keep His commandments, and live in His love, promising them fullness of joy.", "15:16–17": "Jesus gives a clear command to His disciples: love one another as He has loved them. He describes the greatest form of love as laying down one's life for friends, and He calls His disciples not just servants, but friends, because He has shared with them the Father’s will. They are chosen to bear lasting fruit, and their love for one another is the hallmark of true discipleship.", "15:22–25": "Jesus warns His disciples that the world will hate them just as it hated Him. He explains that this hatred stems from the world’s rejection of God. The world would love them if they belonged to it, but because they are chosen out of the world, they will face persecution. Jesus fulfills the prophecy that He would be hated without cause.", "15:26–27": "Jesus promises the coming of the Holy Spirit, the Comforter or Spirit of truth, who will testify about Him. The disciples, having been with Jesus from the beginning of His ministry, will also testify about Him and continue His work through the Spirit’s power.", "15:1–4": "Jesus described Himself as the true vine and His Father as the gardener, who prunes the branches so they bear more fruit. He told His disciples to remain in Him the way a branch stays connected to the vine.", "15:5–8": "Jesus said He is the vine and they are the branches — apart from Him they can do nothing, but if they remain in Him, they will bear much fruit and their prayers will be answered.", "15:12–15": "Jesus commanded His disciples to love one another as He loved them, saying the greatest love is to lay down your life for your friends, and that He now called them friends instead of servants.", "15:18–21": "Jesus warned His disciples that the world would hate them just as it hated Him, because He had chosen them out of the world, and a servant isn't greater than his master."}, "John 16": {"16:12–15": "Jesus prepares His disciples for the coming persecution, warning them that some will believe they are serving God by persecuting them. He explains that it is better for Him to go away because only then will the Holy Spirit—the Comforter—come. The Spirit will convict the world of sin because they don’t believe, righteousness, and judgment and will guide the disciples into all truth, continuing to glorify Jesus.", "16:23–24": "Jesus tells His disciples that their sorrow at His departure will be temporary and will turn to joy when they see Him again. He compares their sorrow to that of a woman in labor, which turns to joy after the birth of a child. He assures them that whatever they ask in His name, the Father will give them, and their joy will be complete.", "16:29–33": "Jesus speaks plainly to His disciples, telling them that the Father Himself loves them and that they will have access to Him in Jesus’ name. He predicts that the disciples will soon be scattered, leaving Him alone, but He reassures them that the Father will be with Him. Jesus leaves them with a powerful message of peace: though they will face tribulation in the world, they can have courage because He has overcome the world.", "16:1–4": "Jesus warned them that they would be put out of synagogues, and that a time was coming when people who killed them would think they were serving God — because those people didn't know the Father or Him.", "16:5–7": "Jesus told His disciples He was going to the Father, and though they were sad, He said it was actually better for them, because if He didn't go away, the Helper wouldn't come.", "16:8–11": "Jesus said the Helper would show the world it was wrong about sin, righteousness, and judgment — because people didn't believe in Him, because He was going to the Father, and because the ruler of this world was already judged.", "16:16–19": "Jesus said a little while and they wouldn't see Him, then a little while more and they would, because He was going to the Father. The disciples were confused about what He meant.", "16:20–22": "Jesus told them they would weep while the world rejoiced, but their grief would turn to joy, like a woman forgetting her labor pain once her baby is born — and no one would be able to take that joy away.", "16:25–28": "Jesus said He had been speaking in figures of speech, but a time was coming when He would speak to them plainly about the Father, since He had come from the Father and was returning to Him."}, "John 17": {"17:1–5": "In this prayer, Jesus asks the Father to glorify Him as He prepares to complete His mission. Jesus reflects on the authority given to Him to grant eternal life, defining eternal life as knowing the true God and Jesus Christ. He also prays to be restored to the glory He had with the Father before the world existed.", "17:17–19": "Jesus prays for His disciples, asking the Father to protect them and keep them united in His name. He acknowledges that the world hates them because they do not belong to the world, just as He does not. Jesus prays for their sanctification through the truth of God’s word and for their protection from evil as He sends them out into the world.", "17:24–26": "Jesus extends His prayer to include all future believers, asking that they may be one, just as He and the Father are one. He prays for their unity, which will serve as a testimony to the world of God’s love and Jesus’ divine mission. Jesus expresses His desire for all believers to be with Him and to see His glory, emphasizing the eternal love of the Father.", "17:6–8": "Jesus prayed to the Father, saying He had made God's name known to His disciples, who had received His words and believed that God sent Him.", "17:9–12": "Jesus prayed specifically for His disciples, not for the world, asking the Father to keep them safe and united, the way He had kept them safe while He was with them.", "17:13–16": "Jesus prayed that His disciples would have His joy in them, and asked the Father not to take them out of the world but to protect them from the evil one.", "17:20–23": "Jesus prayed not only for His disciples but for everyone who would later believe in Him, asking that they would all be united, just as He and the Father are united, so the world would believe."}, "John 18": {"18:10–11": "Jesus, fully aware of His coming betrayal and arrest, steps forward willingly when the soldiers arrive to take Him. Despite Peter's attempt to defend Him by cutting off Malchus’ ear, Jesus commands Peter to put away his sword, declaring that He must drink the cup the Father has given Him.", "18:12–14": "Jesus is taken to Annas, the father-in-law of Caiaphas, the high priest, who had previously advised that it would be better for one man (Jesus) to die for the people.", "18:15–18": "Peter, following Jesus after His arrest, denies being one of Jesus’ disciples when questioned by a servant girl at the door. This is the first of his three denials, as Jesus had foretold.", "18:19–24": "Jesus is questioned by the high priest about His teachings. He responds that He has taught openly and has nothing to hide. When struck by an officer for His response, Jesus calmly challenges the unjust act. After this, He is sent to Caiaphas, the high priest.", "18:25–27": "Peter denies knowing Jesus two more times, including to a relative of Malchus, the man whose ear Peter had cut off. After his third denial, the rooster crows, fulfilling Jesus' prophecy that Peter would deny Him three times before the rooster crowed.", "18:33–38": "Jesus is taken to Pontius Pilate, who questions Him about the accusations brought against Him. Jesus speaks of His kingdom being not of this world, indicating that His mission is spiritual rather than political. Pilate, unsure of what to make of Jesus, finds no fault in Him but faces pressure from the Jewish leaders.", "18:39–40": "Pilate, seeking a way to release Jesus, offers the crowd the option to free Him as part of their Passover tradition. However, the crowd demands the release of Barabbas, a known criminal, instead of Jesus.", "18:1–3": "After praying, Jesus went with His disciples to a garden across the Kidron Valley. Judas, who knew the place well, arrived there with soldiers and officers carrying lanterns, torches, and weapons.", "18:4–9": "Jesus stepped forward and asked who they were looking for. When He said, 'I am He,' the soldiers fell back to the ground. He asked again and let them arrest Him, asking that His disciples be let go.", "18:28–32": "Jesus was taken to Pilate's headquarters. The Jewish leaders wouldn't go inside so they could stay ceremonially clean for Passover, so Pilate came out to them and asked what charge they were bringing."}, "John 19": {"19:4–7": "Jesus is scourged by Pilate’s soldiers, mocked with a crown of thorns, and dressed in a purple robe. Though Pilate continues to declare Jesus' innocence, the Jewish leaders insist on His crucifixion, citing their law that demands death because Jesus claimed to be the Son of God.", "19:12–16": "Pilate becomes even more fearful after hearing that Jesus claimed to be the Son of God. Despite his attempts to release Jesus, the Jewish leaders pressure him by appealing to his loyalty to Caesar, forcing Pilate to make the final decision to hand Jesus over to be crucified.", "19:23–24": "Jesus is crucified at Golgotha, alongside two others. Pilate has a sign placed on the cross, declaring Jesus as The King of the Jews. Despite objections from the chief priests, Pilate refuses to change the inscription. As the soldiers divide His garments and cast lots for His seamless robe, they unknowingly fulfill prophecy.", "19:25–27": "As Jesus hangs on the cross, He ensures the care of His mother by entrusting her to John, the beloved disciple. In this act of compassion, Jesus, even in His suffering, shows concern for the well-being of His loved ones.", "19:28–30": "In His final moments, Jesus declares that His work is complete, fulfilling the Scriptures. After receiving a drink of vinegar, He utters, It is finished, and then surrenders His spirit, marking the completion of His sacrificial mission.", "19:35–37": "The soldiers break the legs of the two criminals crucified with Jesus to hasten their death, but when they find Jesus already dead, they do not break His legs, fulfilling prophecy. Instead, a soldier pierces His side, and blood and water flow out, further fulfilling Scripture.", "19:38–42": "Joseph of Arimathea, a secret disciple, requests Jesus’ body for burial, and with Nicodemus, they prepare His body with spices and wrap it in linen. Jesus is placed in a new tomb in a nearby garden, fulfilling Jewish burial customs in preparation for the Sabbath.", "19:1–3": "Pilate had Jesus flogged, and the soldiers put a crown of thorns on His head and a purple robe on Him, mocking Him as they struck Him.", "19:8–11": "Pilate, growing more afraid, asked Jesus where He was from, but Jesus stayed silent. When Pilate reminded Him he had power over His life, Jesus said Pilate would have no authority at all if it hadn't been given to him from above.", "19:17–18": "Jesus carried His own cross to Golgotha, where He was crucified between two other men, with Jesus in the middle.", "19:19–22": "Pilate had a sign placed on the cross reading 'Jesus of Nazareth, King of the Jews.' The chief priests objected and wanted it changed, but Pilate refused to alter what he had written.", "19:31–34": "Because it was the day before the Sabbath, the soldiers broke the legs of the two men crucified with Jesus so they would die quickly. But finding Jesus already dead, they didn't break His legs — instead, a soldier pierced His side, and blood and water came out."}, "John 20": {"20:3–10": "On the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene discovers the empty tomb and runs to tell Peter and John. Both disciples race to the tomb, where they find the linen cloths lying there. Though they don’t fully understand the resurrection yet, John sees and believes.", "20:14–18": "Mary Magdalene, weeping outside the tomb, encounters two angels and then Jesus Himself, though she doesn’t recognize Him at first. When Jesus calls her by name, she realizes who He is. Jesus instructs her to tell the disciples that He is ascending to His Father, and she shares this incredible news with them.", "20:21–23": "On the evening of the resurrection, Jesus appears to His disciples, even though the doors are locked. He shows them His wounds and gives them peace. Jesus commissions them to continue His mission, breathing on them and imparting the Holy Spirit.", "20:26–29": "Thomas, absent during Jesus’ first appearance, refuses to believe without physical proof. Eight days later, Jesus appears again and offers Thomas the evidence he demanded. Thomas responds with faith, declaring Jesus as My Lord and my God. Jesus blesses those who believe without seeing.", "20:30–31": "John explains that his Gospel was written so that readers may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that through believing in Him, they may have eternal life.", "20:1–2": "Early on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene came to the tomb and found the stone rolled away. She ran and told Peter and John that Jesus' body was gone.", "20:11–13": "Mary stood outside the tomb crying, then looked in and saw two angels where Jesus' body had been. They asked why she was crying, and she said someone had taken her Lord away.", "20:19–20": "That evening, with the doors locked, Jesus appeared among the disciples and said, 'Peace be with you,' showing them His hands and side. They were overjoyed to see Him.", "20:24–25": "Thomas wasn't there when Jesus appeared, and when the others told him they had seen the Lord, he said he wouldn't believe it unless he could see and touch the wounds himself."}, "John 21": {"21:12–14": "Jesus appears to His disciples by the Sea of Tiberias while they are fishing. After a night of catching nothing, Jesus instructs them to cast their net on the right side of the boat, resulting in a miraculous catch. Recognizing that it is the Lord, Peter jumps into the sea to swim to Him. Jesus invites them to share a meal with Him, marking His third appearance to them after His resurrection.", "21:18–19": "Jesus restores Peter by asking him three times if he loves Him, paralleling Peter’s three denials. Each time Peter affirms his love, Jesus instructs him to feed My sheep, entrusting Peter with the responsibility to care for His followers. Jesus also foretells the manner of Peter’s death, indicating that Peter will glorify God through his martyrdom.", "21:24–25": "Peter inquires about the future of the disciple whom Jesus loved (John), but Jesus reminds Peter to focus on following Him and not to concern himself with others’ futures. John, the author of this Gospel, testifies to the truth of these events, concluding by acknowledging that Jesus did many other remarkable things that are beyond recording.", "21:1–3": "Jesus appeared again to the disciples by the Sea of Tiberias. Peter decided to go fishing, and the others joined him, but they caught nothing all night.", "21:4–8": "In the morning, Jesus stood on the shore, though the disciples didn't recognize Him. He told them to cast their net on the other side, and it filled with so many fish they couldn't haul it in. When John realized it was the Lord, Peter jumped into the water to get to Him.", "21:9–11": "When they came ashore, Jesus already had a fire going with fish and bread. Peter hauled in the net, full of a hundred fifty-three large fish, and it didn't tear.", "21:15–17": "After breakfast, Jesus asked Peter three times if he loved Him, and each time told him to take care of His sheep — restoring Peter after he had denied Jesus three times.", "21:20–23": "Peter asked Jesus about the disciple John, and Jesus told him that wasn't Peter's concern — he should just follow Him. This led to a rumor that John wouldn't die, though Jesus had never actually said that."}, "Acts 1": {"1:4–5": "Luke addresses Theophilus, recounting the events after Jesus' resurrection. Jesus, after giving His final instructions to the apostles, tells them to remain in Jerusalem and wait for the promised Holy Spirit.", "1:9–11": "The disciples ask if Jesus will restore the kingdom to Israel, but Jesus replies that it is not for them to know the times. He promises them the power of the Holy Spirit and commissions them as witnesses to the ends of the earth. Afterward, Jesus ascends into heaven, and two angels promise His return in the same manner.", "1:23–26": "After returning to Jerusalem, the apostles gather in prayer along with others, including women and Mary, the mother of Jesus. Peter leads the group in selecting a replacement for Judas Iscariot, who had betrayed Jesus. The lot falls on Matthias, and he is added to the eleven apostles.", "1:1–3": "Luke reminds Theophilus that his first book told what Jesus did and taught, up until He was taken up to heaven. After His resurrection, Jesus appeared to the apostles over forty days, proving He was alive and teaching them about God's kingdom.", "1:6–8": "The apostles asked Jesus if He was about to restore Israel's kingdom. He told them it wasn't for them to know the timing, but that they would receive power when the Holy Spirit came, and would be His witnesses everywhere, starting in Jerusalem.", "1:12–14": "The apostles went back to Jerusalem and gathered in an upper room, praying together along with the women, Mary the mother of Jesus, and His brothers.", "1:15–17": "Peter stood up among about 120 believers and explained that the Scriptures had to be fulfilled concerning Judas, who had guided those who arrested Jesus even though he had been one of the apostles.", "1:18–20": "Judas bought a field with the money from his betrayal and died there. Peter quoted from the Psalms saying someone else should take his place.", "1:21–22": "Peter said the replacement had to be someone who had been with them the whole time Jesus was among them, so he could be a witness of the resurrection along with the others."}, "Acts 2": {"2:12–13": "On the day of Pentecost, the Holy Spirit comes upon the apostles with a rushing wind and tongues of fire. They begin to speak in various languages, and Jews from every nation hear them declaring the works of God in their native tongues, causing amazement and confusion. Some mockingly accuse the apostles of being drunk.", "2:34–36": "Peter stands and addresses the crowd, explaining that the events they are witnessing are the fulfillment of the prophecy from Joel. He proclaims that Jesus, whom they crucified, has been raised from the dead by God and is now exalted at the right hand of the Father. Peter declares that Jesus is both Lord and Christ.", "2:40–41": "Peter’s sermon deeply moves the people, and they ask what they should do. Peter tells them to repent and be baptized in the name of Jesus for the forgiveness of sins and to receive the Holy Spirit. About three thousand people are baptized and added to the church that day.", "2:46–47": "The early believers devote themselves to the apostles' teaching, fellowship, the breaking of bread, and prayer. They share their possessions and care for one another, and many signs and wonders are performed by the apostles. The Lord continues to add new believers to the church every day.", "2:1–4": "On the Day of Pentecost, the believers were together when a sound like a rushing wind filled the house, and what looked like flames rested on each of them. They were filled with the Holy Spirit and began speaking in other languages.", "2:5–8": "Jews from many nations were staying in Jerusalem, and when they heard the sound, they were amazed to hear these Galileans speaking in each person's own native language.", "2:9–11": "People from many different regions and nations were listed as hearing God's wonders described in their own languages.", "2:14–16": "Peter stood up with the eleven apostles and told the crowd these men weren't drunk — this was what the prophet Joel had spoken about.", "2:17–18": "Peter quoted Joel's prophecy that God would pour out His Spirit on all people, and that sons, daughters, young and old would prophesy and have visions.", "2:19–21": "The prophecy continued with signs in the sky and on earth, ending with the promise that everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved.", "2:22–24": "Peter told the crowd that Jesus, whom God confirmed through miracles, was crucified by their hands according to God's plan, but God raised Him from the dead.", "2:25–28": "Peter quoted David, who spoke of the Lord always being with him and of not being abandoned to the grave.", "2:29–31": "Peter explained that David, who died and was buried, had actually been prophesying about the Messiah's resurrection, not about himself, since David's body did see decay.", "2:32–33": "Peter said God raised this Jesus, and they were all witnesses of it — and now, exalted to God's right hand, Jesus had poured out the Holy Spirit they were seeing and hearing.", "2:37–39": "The crowd was deeply moved and asked what they should do. Peter told them to change their hearts and be baptized in Jesus' name for forgiveness, and they would receive the Holy Spirit — a promise for them and their children.", "2:42–45": "The believers devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching, fellowship, breaking bread, and prayer. They shared everything they had, selling property to help anyone in need."}, "Acts 3": {"3:9–11": "Peter and John encounter a man who had been lame from birth, begging at the temple gate. Peter, instead of giving him money, commands him to walk in the name of Jesus Christ. The man is healed instantly, and he enters the temple with Peter and John, leaping and praising God, astonishing everyone who recognized him as the beggar.", "3:24–26": "After the miraculous healing, Peter addresses the astonished crowd, explaining that the man was healed by faith in the name of Jesus, whom they had rejected and crucified. He calls the people to repent and turn to God so that their sins may be forgiven and they may experience the times of refreshing from the Lord. Peter connects Jesus' role as the fulfillment of prophecy and encourages the people to embrace Him as the promised Messiah.", "3:1–3": "Peter and John were going to the temple to pray when a man who had been lame from birth, who begged there daily, asked them for money.", "3:4–6": "Peter told the man he had no money to give, but in the name of Jesus Christ, told him to get up and walk.", "3:7–8": "Peter took his hand and helped him up, and immediately his feet grew strong. He walked into the temple with them, leaping and praising God.", "3:12–13": "Peter told the amazed crowd this healing wasn't done by his own power, but by the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, who had glorified His servant Jesus.", "3:14–16": "Peter told them they had rejected the Holy and Righteous One and killed the Author of life, but it was faith in Jesus' name that had healed this man.", "3:17–18": "Peter said he knew they had acted in ignorance, but that this was how God fulfilled what He had said through the prophets — that the Messiah would suffer.", "3:19–21": "Peter urged them to repent so their sins could be wiped away, and said Jesus would remain in heaven until the time when God restores all things.", "3:22–23": "Peter reminded them that Moses had said God would raise up a prophet like himself, and that everyone who wouldn't listen to Him would be cut off from the people."}, "Acts 4": {"4:1–4": "Peter and John are arrested by the temple authorities for preaching about the resurrection of Jesus. Despite this, many people who heard the message believed, and the number of believers grew to about five thousand.", "4:11–12": "Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, boldly declares before the Jewish leaders that the man was healed by the power of Jesus Christ, whom they had crucified but whom God had raised from the dead. Peter affirms that salvation is found in no one else but Jesus.", "4:21–22": "The Jewish leaders, seeing the boldness of Peter and John and the undeniable evidence of the healed man, are at a loss for what to do. They threaten Peter and John not to speak or teach in the name of Jesus, but the apostles declare that they must obey God rather than men. After further threats, they are released.", "4:29–31": "After being released, Peter and John return to the believers and report what had happened. The believers pray together for boldness to continue speaking God’s word despite the threats. As they pray, the place is shaken, and they are filled with the Holy Spirit, continuing to speak the word of God boldly.", "4:36–37": "The early believers were united in heart and mind, sharing everything they had. No one claimed personal ownership of their possessions, and those who owned property sold it, giving the proceeds to the apostles to distribute to anyone in need. One example is Barnabas, who sold a field and brought the money to the apostles.", "4:5–7": "The next day the Jewish leaders gathered and questioned Peter and John, asking by what power or in whose name they had healed the man.", "4:8–10": "Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, told them plainly that the man had been healed by the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom they had crucified and God had raised.", "4:13–14": "The council was amazed at Peter and John's boldness, since they were ordinary, untrained men, and recognized they had been with Jesus. They couldn't argue since the healed man stood right there.", "4:15–17": "The council discussed among themselves what to do, since everyone knew a miracle had happened, and decided to just warn Peter and John not to speak about Jesus' name anymore.", "4:18–20": "They called Peter and John back in and ordered them not to speak about Jesus at all. But Peter and John answered that they couldn't stop speaking about what they had seen and heard.", "4:23–24": "After being released, Peter and John reported everything to the other believers, and together they lifted their voices to God in prayer.", "4:25–26": "They prayed, quoting David's psalm about the nations raging and the rulers gathering together against the Lord and His Messiah.", "4:27–28": "They noted that Herod and Pilate, along with the Gentiles and the people of Israel, had gathered against Jesus, doing exactly what God's plan had already determined would happen.", "4:32–35": "The whole community of believers shared everything they had — no one claimed private ownership of their possessions, and those with land or houses sold them to help anyone in need."}, "Acts 5": {"5:7–11": "Ananias and Sapphira, a couple in the early church, sold some property but secretly kept part of the money while pretending to give the full amount. Peter, through the Holy Spirit, rebukes Ananias for lying to God, and Ananias dies on the spot. Later, his wife, Sapphira, also lies about the sale and suffers the same fate. Great fear falls upon the church as a result.", "5:12–16": "The apostles performed many miraculous signs and wonders among the people, and the believers continued to gather together in unity. The apostles' reputation grew so much that people brought the sick into the streets, hoping even Peter's shadow might heal them. As a result, many were healed, and more people were added to the church.", "5:22–26": "The high priest and the Sadducees, filled with jealousy, arrest the apostles and put them in prison. However, an angel of the Lord frees them during the night and commands them to continue preaching. The next day, when the authorities find the apostles preaching in the temple, they are perplexed but decide to bring them back for questioning without using force, fearing the reaction of the people.", "5:33–39": "The apostles are brought before the council, and the high priest accuses them of disobeying the command to stop teaching in Jesus' name. Peter boldly responds that they must obey God rather than men, affirming that Jesus, whom they crucified, was raised by God and exalted as the Savior. This enrages the council, and they consider killing the apostles.", "5:40–42": "Gamaliel, a respected Pharisee, advises the council to leave the apostles alone. He reminds them of past movements that failed and suggests that if the apostles' work is of human origin, it will fail on its own. However, if it is from God, they will be unable to stop it and may find themselves fighting against God. The council agrees, but they still beat the apostles and order them again not to speak in Jesus' name. Despite the beating and warnings, the apostles leave rejoicing that they were considered worthy to suffer for the name of Jesus. They continue to teach and preach Jesus Christ every day, both in the temple and from house to house.", "5:1–6": "Ananias sold property but secretly kept back part of the money while claiming to give it all. Peter told him he had lied to the Holy Spirit, and Ananias fell down dead.", "5:17–21": "The high priest and the Sadducees, filled with jealousy, arrested the apostles and put them in jail. But an angel opened the jail doors during the night and told them to go teach in the temple.", "5:27–32": "The apostles were brought before the council and questioned for continuing to teach about Jesus. Peter answered that they had to obey God rather than men, since God had raised up Jesus, whom the council had killed."}, "Acts 6": {"6:5–7": "As the number of disciples grows, there is a complaint that the Greek-speaking widows are being neglected in the daily distribution of food. The apostles decide to appoint seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom, to oversee this task, allowing the apostles to focus on prayer and the ministry of the Word. Among those chosen is Stephen, who is described as full of faith and the Holy Spirit. As a result of the apostles’ focus on the Word of God many people, including priests, come to faith in Jesus.", "6:12–15": "Stephen, full of God's faith and power, performs great signs and wonders. However, members of the synagogue rise up and argue with him, but they are unable to withstand his wisdom. They falsely accuse him of blasphemy, claiming he speaks against the temple and the law of Moses, and they bring him before the council. Despite these accusations, Stephen's face shines like that of an angel, reflecting the presence of God.", "6:1–4": "As the number of believers grew, the Greek-speaking widows were being overlooked in the daily food distribution. So the apostles told the community to choose seven trustworthy men to handle that work, so the apostles could focus on prayer and preaching.", "6:8–11": "Stephen, full of faith and power, performed great wonders among the people, but some men who couldn't out-argue him secretly arranged for false witnesses to accuse him of blasphemy."}, "Acts 7": {"7:1–8": "Stephen begins his defense before the Sanhedrin by recounting the history of Israel, starting with God's call to Abraham. He describes how Abraham was called out of Mesopotamia and given the promise of the land, even though he had no inheritance or children at the time. Stephen also recalls the prophecy of Israel's future enslavement and God's promise to deliver them.", "7:9–16": "Stephen continues the story with Joseph, who was sold into slavery by his jealous brothers but was delivered by God and elevated to a position of power in Egypt. During a time of famine, Jacob's family came to Egypt, and Joseph revealed himself to his brothers. Stephen recounts how the family of Jacob moved to Egypt, where their ancestors died and were later buried.", "7:30–34": "Stephen recounts the birth of Moses during the time of Israel's oppression in Egypt. Moses is raised in Pharaoh's household but later flees after killing an Egyptian in defense of an Israelite. After forty years in Midian, God appears to Moses in the burning bush, calling him to deliver His people from Egypt.", "7:35–43": "Stephen emphasizes how Israel rejected Moses as their leader, despite God's clear calling. He also reminds them that their ancestors turned to idolatry, even as God was delivering them from Egypt and giving them His commandments. Their rejection of Moses foreshadowed their rejection of Jesus.", "7:51–53": "Stephen concludes his speech by accusing the Sanhedrin of resisting the Holy Spirit, just as their ancestors did. He calls them stiffnecked and condemns their rejection of the prophets and their betrayal and murder of Jesus, the Righteous One.", "7:54–60": "Stephen’s bold message enrages the council, and they rush at him to stone him. As they do, Stephen, full of the Holy Spirit, sees a vision of Jesus standing at the right hand of God. As he is being stoned, Stephen prays for Jesus to receive his spirit and forgives his attackers, echoing Jesus’ words on the cross. Saul (later known as Paul) witnesses the stoning.", "7:17–29": "Stephen continued his speech, recounting how Moses was born and raised in Pharaoh's household, later defended a fellow Israelite by killing an Egyptian, and then fled to Midian after his own people rejected him as a leader.", "7:44–50": "Stephen described how Israel carried the tabernacle in the wilderness and how Solomon eventually built a temple, but pointed out that God, who made everything, doesn't actually live in houses built by human hands."}, "Acts 8": {"8:1–3": "After Stephen’s martyrdom, Saul (later known as Paul) leads a fierce persecution against the church in Jerusalem. The believers are scattered throughout Judea and Samaria, though the apostles remain in Jerusalem. Despite the persecution, the church continues to grow.", "8:4–8": "Philip, one of the seven deacons, preaches the gospel in Samaria, where he performs miracles, heals the sick, and casts out demons. The people eagerly listen to his message, and there is great joy in the city as many come to faith in Christ.", "8:9–13": "Simon, a sorcerer who had previously astonished the people of Samaria with his magic, is amazed by the power of God displayed through Philip. He believes in Jesus and is baptized, following Philip closely and marveling at the miracles he witnesses.", "8:14–17": "When the apostles in Jerusalem hear that the Samaritans have accepted the gospel, they send Peter and John to them. Peter and John pray for the new believers to receive the Holy Spirit, laying their hands on them, and they are filled with the Spirit.", "8:18–24": "Simon, fascinated by the power of the Holy Spirit, offers Peter and John money to buy the ability to impart the Spirit to others. Peter rebukes him sharply, warning that such a request reveals his heart is not right with God. Simon, in response, asks Peter to pray that he may be spared from judgment.", "8:39–40": "The Holy Spirit leads Philip to a desert road where he encounters an Ethiopian eunuch, an official in charge of the treasury of the queen of Ethiopia. The eunuch is reading from the prophet Isaiah but does not understand the passage. Philip explains that it refers to Jesus, and the eunuch believes. Upon finding water, the eunuch asks to be baptized, and Philip baptizes him. After the baptism, Philip is miraculously transported to another location, while the eunuch continues on his way rejoicing.", "8:25": "After Peter and John finished testifying, they headed back to Jerusalem, preaching the good news in many Samaritan villages along the way.", "8:26–29": "An angel told Philip to go toward the desert road to Gaza, where he met an Ethiopian official reading from Isaiah in his chariot. The Spirit told Philip to go near the chariot.", "8:30–35": "Philip heard the man reading about a suffering servant from Isaiah, and when the official asked who the prophet meant, Philip explained the good news about Jesus starting from that passage.", "8:36–38": "The official saw water along the road and asked what would stop him from being baptized. When he declared his belief that Jesus is the Son of God, Philip baptized him."}, "Acts 9": {"9:7–9": "Saul, who is persecuting Christians, receives permission from the high priest to arrest any followers of Jesus in Damascus. On his way there, a blinding light from heaven surrounds him, and he hears the voice of Jesus asking why Saul is persecuting Him. Struck with blindness, Saul is led into Damascus, where he fasts for three days.", "9:15–19": "God sends a disciple named Ananias to heal Saul, who has been blinded by the vision of Jesus. Although Ananias is initially hesitant because of Saul’s reputation as a persecutor of Christians, the Lord assures him that Saul is His chosen instrument to spread the gospel to the Gentiles. Ananias obeys, and Saul regains his sight, is baptized, and receives the Holy Spirit.", "9:20–22": "Immediately after his conversion, Saul begins preaching in the synagogues that Jesus is the Son of God. The people are astonished, knowing Saul’s former zeal in persecuting Christians, but he grows stronger in his faith and proves that Jesus is the Messiah.", "9:23–25": "The Jews in Damascus, unable to refute Saul’s teachings, plot to kill him. The disciples help Saul escape by lowering him in a basket through an opening in the city wall.", "9:26–31": "When Saul arrives in Jerusalem, the disciples are initially afraid of him because of his past. However, Barnabas vouches for him, explaining Saul’s encounter with the Lord and his bold preaching in Damascus. Saul preaches in Jerusalem but faces another plot to kill him, so the disciples send him to Tarsus for his safety. During this time, the church experiences peace and growth throughout Judea, Galilee, and Samaria. The believers walk in the fear of the Lord and the comfort of the Holy Spirit, and their numbers increase.", "9:32–35": "Peter heals a paralyzed man named Aeneas in the town of Lydda, proclaiming that Jesus Christ makes him whole. The man is healed instantly, and as a result, many people in the region turn to the Lord.", "9:36–43": "In Joppa, a beloved disciple named Dorcas (Tabitha), known for her good works, becomes ill and dies. Peter is called, and after praying, he raises her from the dead. This miracle leads many people in Joppa to believe in the Lord.", "9:1–2": "Saul, still determined to harm the believers, asked for letters authorizing him to arrest any followers of Jesus he found in Damascus and bring them back to Jerusalem.", "9:3–6": "As Saul neared Damascus, a bright light from heaven surrounded him, and he fell to the ground, hearing a voice ask why he was persecuting Him. The voice identified itself as Jesus and told Saul to go into the city for instructions.", "9:10–14": "The Lord appeared to a disciple named Ananias in a vision, telling him to go find Saul, who was praying and expecting Ananias to come. Ananias was hesitant, since he'd heard about the harm Saul had done to believers."}, "Acts 10": {"10:1–8": "Cornelius, a Roman centurion who is devout and God-fearing, has a vision in which an angel tells him that his prayers and alms have been noticed by God. The angel instructs Cornelius to send men to Joppa to bring Peter, who will tell him what to do.", "10:17–20": "Peter falls into a trance while praying and has a vision of a sheet filled with animals that were considered unclean by Jewish law. A voice tells Peter to kill and eat, but Peter refuses, saying he has never eaten anything unclean. The voice responds, What God hath cleansed, that call not thou common. This happens three times. As Peter is contemplating the vision, the men sent by Cornelius arrive.", "10:21–23": "The Holy Spirit instructs Peter to go with the men sent by Cornelius, without hesitation. Peter meets the men, who explain why Cornelius sent them, and Peter invites them to stay the night. The next day, Peter sets out with them to go to Cornelius' house, taking some fellow believers with him.", "10:24–29": "When Peter arrives at Cornelius’ house, Cornelius falls at his feet in reverence, but Peter insists that he is only a man. Peter then explains that although it is against Jewish law for him to associate with a Gentile, God has shown him that he should not call anyone unclean or common. He asks Cornelius why he sent for him.", "10:30–33": "Cornelius recounts the vision he received, in which an angel told him that his prayers and alms had been noticed by God. He was instructed to send for Peter, and now he and his household are eager to hear what Peter has to say from God.", "10:34–43": "Peter begins preaching to Cornelius and his household, emphasizing that God shows no favoritism and accepts people from every nation who fear Him. He proclaims the life, death, and resurrection of Jesus, explaining that everyone who believes in Jesus receives forgiveness of sins.", "10:44–48": "While Peter is still speaking, the Holy Spirit falls on all who are listening, and they begin speaking in tongues and praising God. The Jewish believers with Peter are astonished that the Holy Spirit is given to the Gentiles. Peter then orders that they be baptized in the name of the Lord, and they ask him to stay with them for a few days.", "10:9–16": "Peter fell into a trance while praying and saw a large sheet lowered from heaven full of animals, with a voice telling him to kill and eat. When Peter objected that he'd never eaten anything unclean, the voice told him not to call unclean what God had made clean."}, "Acts 11": {"11:15–18": "Peter explains his actions to the Jewish believers in Jerusalem, recounting his vision and how the Holy Spirit had directed him to go to Cornelius’ house. He emphasizes that the Holy Spirit was given to the Gentiles just as it had been given to the Jews, and that he could not oppose God’s will. The Jewish believers then praise God for extending repentance and salvation to the Gentiles.", "11:25–26": "As the believers scattered by persecution spread the gospel, many Gentiles in Antioch turn to the Lord. When the church in Jerusalem hears of this, they send Barnabas to encourage the new believers. Barnabas later seeks out Saul (Paul) in Tarsus, and they spend a year teaching and strengthening the church in Antioch, where the disciples are first called Christians.", "11:27–30": "A prophet named Agabus predicts a severe famine, which prompts the believers in Antioch to send financial aid to the church in Judea. Barnabas and Saul are entrusted with delivering the aid.", "11:1–3": "When Peter went up to Jerusalem, some believers criticized him for going into the homes of uncircumcised Gentiles and eating with them.", "11:4–14": "Peter explained the whole story in order — his vision of the sheet, the men who arrived from Caesarea right afterward, and how an angel had told Cornelius to send for Peter so he could hear a message that would save his household.", "11:19–24": "Believers scattered by persecution began preaching to Greeks as well as Jews in Antioch, and many believed. When the Jerusalem church heard, they sent Barnabas, who was glad to see God's grace and encouraged everyone to remain faithful."}, "Acts 12": {"12:1–5": "King Herod begins to persecute the church, killing James, the brother of John, and arresting Peter. Seeing that it pleases the Jewish leaders, Herod plans to bring Peter before the people after Passover, keeping him in prison under heavy guard.", "12:6–10": "While Peter is in prison, the church earnestly prays for him. On the night before his trial, an angel of the Lord miraculously frees Peter from his chains and leads him past the guards and out of the prison. At first, Peter thinks it is a vision, but he soon realizes that he has been truly set free.", "12:13–17": "Peter goes to the house where the church is gathered in prayer. A servant named Rhoda recognizes his voice but, in her excitement, forgets to open the door. When she tells the others, they don’t believe her until Peter keeps knocking. Finally, they open the door and are amazed to see him. Peter tells them how the Lord delivered him and instructs them to share the news with others.", "12:20–23": "The next morning, when Peter’s absence is discovered, Herod orders the execution of the guards responsible for watching him. Later, Herod gives a speech, and the people, seeking favor, flatter him by saying he speaks like a god. Because Herod does not give glory to God, he is struck down by an angel and dies a humiliating death.", "12:24–25": "Despite persecution and opposition, the Word of God continues to spread and flourish. Barnabas and Saul complete their mission in Jerusalem and return, bringing John Mark with them.", "12:11–12": "Peter realized the Lord had truly sent an angel to rescue him from Herod, and he went to the house of Mary, John Mark's mother, where many were gathered praying.", "12:18–19": "In the morning there was great confusion over Peter's disappearance. Herod searched for him without success and had the guards executed, then went to stay in Caesarea."}, "Acts 13": {"13:1–3": "While the leaders of the church in Antioch were worshiping and fasting, the Holy Spirit instructed them to set apart Barnabas and Saul (Paul) for the work to which God had called them. After fasting, praying, and laying hands on them, the church sent them on their mission.", "13:9–12": "In Cyprus, Paul and Barnabas encounter a sorcerer named Elymas, who tries to prevent the Roman proconsul Sergius Paulus from accepting the faith. Paul, filled with the Holy Spirit, rebukes Elymas and declares that he will be struck blind, and it happens immediately. Witnessing this miracle, Sergius Paulus believes in the gospel.", "13:23–25": "Paul and Barnabas travel to Antioch in Pisidia, where Paul is invited to speak in the synagogue. Paul recounts the history of Israel, from the exodus to the reign of King David, explaining how God's promise to raise up a Savior for Israel has been fulfilled in Jesus.", "13:40–41": "Paul declares the good news of salvation through Jesus, explaining that His death and resurrection fulfill the prophecies of Scripture. He emphasizes that forgiveness of sins and justification are available to all who believe in Jesus, something the law of Moses could not offer.", "13:48–52": "After Paul’s sermon, many Gentiles express a desire to hear more, and nearly the whole city gathers the next Sabbath to listen. However, the Jewish leaders, filled with jealousy, oppose Paul and stir up persecution against him and Barnabas, leading to their expulsion. Despite this, the gospel spreads, and the disciples are filled with joy and the Holy Spirit.", "13:4–8": "Sent out by the Holy Spirit, Barnabas and Saul traveled to Cyprus preaching, where they encountered a sorcerer named Elymas who tried to turn the governor away from the faith.", "13:13–15": "Paul's group traveled on to Antioch in Pisidia, where on the Sabbath the synagogue leaders invited them to speak a word of encouragement to the people.", "13:16–19": "Paul stood up and reviewed Israel's history, from being freed from slavery in Egypt to receiving the promised land after forty years in the wilderness.", "13:20–22": "Paul continued, describing how God gave Israel judges, then King Saul, and eventually raised up David as a king after God's own heart.", "13:26–29": "Paul told them this message of salvation had come to them, but the rulers in Jerusalem hadn't recognized Jesus and had Him executed, even though no real charge against Him could be found.", "13:30–33": "Paul declared that God raised Jesus from the dead, and many witnesses had seen Him — fulfilling the promise God had made long ago, as written in the Psalms.", "13:34–37": "Paul pointed out that while David died and his body decayed, Jesus, whom God raised, did not experience decay at all.", "13:38–39": "Paul told them that through Jesus, forgiveness of sins is offered, and everyone who believes is made right in a way the law of Moses never could accomplish.", "13:42–43": "After the synagogue meeting, the Gentiles asked to hear the same message again the next Sabbath, and many Jews and converts followed Paul and Barnabas, who urged them to continue in God's grace.", "13:44–47": "Nearly the whole city came to hear the word the next Sabbath, but the jealous Jewish leaders opposed Paul. So Paul and Barnabas boldly declared they would now turn to preach to the Gentiles instead."}, "Acts 14": {"14:5–7": "Paul and Barnabas preach in Iconium, where many Jews and Gentiles believe, but others stir up opposition. As the city becomes divided, an attempt is made to stone Paul and Barnabas. They escape to Lystra and Derbe, continuing to preach the gospel.", "14:11–13": "Paul heals a man who has been crippled from birth. Seeing this miracle, the people of Lystra believe Paul and Barnabas to be gods, calling Barnabas Jupiter and Paul Mercury. They prepare to offer sacrifices to them.", "14:14–18": "Paul and Barnabas tear their clothes in distress and run into the crowd, insisting that they are mere humans. They preach that the people should turn from their idolatry and worship the living God, the Creator of all things. Even with this plea, it is difficult to prevent the people from sacrificing to them.", "14:19–20": "Some Jews from Antioch and Iconium arrive in Lystra and turn the crowd against Paul. They stone him and drag him out of the city, thinking he is dead. However, Paul miraculously recovers, gets up, and returns to the city before leaving for Derbe the next day with Barnabas.", "14:21–23": "After preaching the gospel in Derbe and making many disciples, Paul and Barnabas return to Lystra, Iconium, and Antioch, encouraging the believers to persevere in their faith despite hardships. They appoint elders in each church, committing them to the Lord with prayer and fasting.", "14:24–28": "Paul and Barnabas complete their missionary journey, passing through several cities and finally returning to Antioch in Syria, where their journey had begun. They share with the church how God had opened the door of faith to the Gentiles, and they remain in Antioch for a significant period, encouraging the disciples.", "14:1–4": "At Iconium, Paul and Barnabas spoke so effectively that many Jews and Greeks believed, though some Jews stirred up opposition, and the city became divided over them.", "14:8–10": "At Lystra, Paul noticed a man who had never been able to walk. Seeing that he had faith to be healed, Paul told him to stand up, and the man jumped up and began walking."}, "Acts 15": {"15:1–5": "Some men from Judea teach that Gentile converts must be circumcised to be saved. This leads to a major disagreement with Paul and Barnabas, and it is decided that they should go to Jerusalem to discuss this matter with the apostles and elders.", "15:10–12": "At the council, there is much debate. Peter reminds them that God gave the Holy Spirit to the Gentiles without requiring them to follow the law of Moses. He emphasizes that salvation is through the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of the Lord Jesus Christ, not through works of the law. Paul and Barnabas then validate their ministry by sharing the signs and wonders that God had done among the Gentiles.", "15:19–21": "James, the leader of the Jerusalem church, speaks next. He affirms Peter’s statement and supports it with Scripture, concluding that the Gentile believers should not be burdened with keeping the law of Moses. However, he recommends they abstain from practices associated with idolatry and immorality to promote harmony between Jewish and Gentile believers.", "15:25–29": "The apostles and elders decide to send a letter to the Gentile believers in Antioch, Syria, and Cilicia, affirming that they are not required to be circumcised or keep the law of Moses. The letter encourages them to avoid practices associated with idolatry and immorality and reassures them of the council’s unity on this matter.", "15:35": "Paul, Barnabas, Judas, and Silas deliver the letter to Antioch, and the believers rejoice at its message. Judas and Silas, being prophets, also encourage the church. After some time, Judas returns to Jerusalem, but Silas remains in Antioch, and Paul and Barnabas continue teaching and preaching.", "15:36–41": "Paul suggests revisiting the churches they had established, but he and Barnabas disagree over whether to take John Mark with them, as he had previously left them during a mission. The disagreement becomes so sharp that they part ways. Barnabas takes John Mark and goes to Cyprus, while Paul takes Silas and goes through Syria and Cilicia, strengthening the churches.", "15:6–9": "The apostles and elders met to discuss whether Gentile believers needed to follow Jewish law. Peter reminded them that God had already given the Gentiles the Holy Spirit, making no distinction between them and Jewish believers.", "15:13–18": "James spoke up, agreeing that God's plan all along, according to the prophets, was to include people from every nation, not just Israel.", "15:22–24": "The apostles and elders decided to send Judas and Silas with Paul and Barnabas to deliver a letter to the Gentile believers, clarifying that no one had authorized requiring circumcision and the law.", "15:30–33": "The letter was delivered in Antioch, and the believers were glad for its encouraging message. Judas and Silas, who were prophets, encouraged the church there before being sent back.", "15:34": "Silas chose to stay behind in Antioch rather than return right away."}, "Acts 16": {"16:1–5": "Paul and Silas meet a young disciple named Timothy, whose mother is Jewish and father is Greek. Because of the Jewish communities they will encounter, Paul has Timothy circumcised, and together they deliver the decrees from the apostles and elders in Jerusalem, strengthening the churches in faith and numbers.", "16:9–10": "The Holy Spirit prevents Paul and his companions from preaching in certain regions. One night, Paul has a vision of a man from Macedonia pleading for help. Understanding this as God’s call, they set out for Macedonia to preach the gospel.", "16:14–15": "Paul and his companions arrive in Philippi and meet a group of women praying by the river. Among them is Lydia, a merchant who sells purple cloth. The Lord opens her heart to receive Paul's message, and she and her household are baptized. She invites Paul and his companions to stay at her home.", "16:22–24": "A slave girl possessed with a spirit of divination follows Paul and Silas, proclaiming that they are servants of God. Paul commands the spirit to leave her, and it does, but her masters, angry over losing their income from her fortune-telling, drag Paul and Silas before the magistrates. They are beaten and imprisoned.", "16:32–34": "At midnight, Paul and Silas pray and sing hymns to God, and an earthquake opens the prison doors and loosens their chains. The jailer, thinking the prisoners have escaped, prepares to kill himself, but Paul stops him. Trembling, the jailer asks how he can be saved. Paul tells him to believe in the Lord Jesus Christ. The jailer and his household believe, and they are baptized that very night.", "16:38–40": "The next morning, the magistrates send orders for Paul and Silas to be released. However, Paul demands that the magistrates come personally, as he and Silas, being Roman citizens, had been beaten and imprisoned without a trial. The magistrates, fearing repercussions, come and escort them out of the prison, asking them to leave the city. Before departing, Paul and Silas visit Lydia and encourage the believers.", "16:6–8": "Paul's group was kept by the Holy Spirit from preaching in the province of Asia and from entering Bithynia, so they traveled instead to Troas.", "16:11–13": "They sailed to Philippi and, on the Sabbath, went to a riverside place of prayer, where they spoke to the women gathered there.", "16:16–18": "A slave girl who made money for her owners through fortune-telling kept following Paul, until he cast the spirit out of her in Jesus' name.", "16:19–21": "When her owners realized their profit was gone, they had Paul and Silas seized and dragged before the authorities, accusing them of disturbing the city.", "16:25–28": "At midnight, Paul and Silas were praying and singing when an earthquake shook the prison open and loosened everyone's chains. The jailer, thinking the prisoners had escaped, was about to kill himself, but Paul stopped him, saying they were all still there.", "16:29–31": "The jailer fell trembling before Paul and Silas and asked what he must do to be saved. They told him to believe in the Lord Jesus, and he and his household would be saved.", "16:35–37": "The next morning the magistrates ordered Paul and Silas released, but Paul insisted they come and release them publicly, since they had been beaten and jailed without a trial despite being Roman citizens."}, "Acts 17": {"17:5–9": "Paul and Silas preach in the synagogue of Thessalonica for three Sabbaths, explaining that Jesus is the Christ who had to suffer and rise from the dead. Some Jews, many Greeks, and prominent women believe. However, some Jews, out of envy, stir up a mob and accuse Paul, Silas, and Jason of opposing Caesar by declaring Jesus as king. Jason is brought before the city officials, but after posting bail, they are released.", "17:13–15": "Paul and Silas travel to Berea, where the Jews receive the gospel with open hearts and diligently search the Scriptures to verify Paul's teaching. Many believe, but the Jews from Thessalonica follow them and stir up opposition. Paul is sent away to Athens, while Silas and Timothy remain in Berea.", "17:16–21": "While in Athens, Paul is deeply troubled by the idolatry he sees everywhere. He engages with Jews in the synagogue and debates daily in the marketplace. Some Epicurean and Stoic philosophers invite him to speak at the Areopagus, curious about his teachings on Jesus and the resurrection.", "17:29–31": "Paul addresses the Athenians at Mars' Hill, pointing out their altar to an unknown god. He explains that the true God, the Creator of all things, is not confined to temples or idols. Paul declares that God calls all people to change their minds (repent) because He has appointed a day of judgment through Jesus, whom He raised from the dead.", "17:32–34": "When Paul speaks of the resurrection, some mock him, but others express interest in hearing more. A few, including Dionysius the Areopagite and a woman named Damaris, believe and follow Paul.", "17:1–4": "In Thessalonica, Paul went to the synagogue and reasoned from the Scriptures that the Messiah had to suffer and rise again, and that Jesus was that Messiah. Some believed and joined him.", "17:10–12": "The believers sent Paul and Silas to Berea, where the Jews were eager to hear the message and searched the Scriptures daily to check if it was true, and many believed.", "17:22–25": "Paul stood before the Areopagus in Athens and pointed to an altar inscribed 'To an Unknown God,' telling them he was there to explain who that God actually is — the Creator who doesn't need anything from people.", "17:26–28": "Paul explained that God made all nations from one origin and set their times and places, so that people would seek Him, since He isn't far from any of us — in Him we live and move and exist."}, "Acts 18": {"18:9–11": "Paul arrives in Corinth and meets Aquila and Priscilla, Jewish tentmakers who had been expelled from Rome. He stays with them and works alongside them while continuing to preach in the synagogue every Sabbath. Despite opposition from some Jews, many Corinthians believe and are baptized. The Lord encourages Paul in a vision to continue preaching without fear, promising His protection. Paul stays in Corinth for a year and a half.", "18:12–17": "The Jews in Corinth bring Paul before Gallio, the Roman proconsul, accusing him of persuading people to worship God in ways that violate Jewish law. However, Gallio dismisses the case, stating that it is a matter of Jewish law and not a criminal offense. The crowd then beats Sosthenes, the ruler of the synagogue, but Gallio ignores the incident.", "18:22–23": "After staying in Corinth for a considerable time, Paul sets sail for Syria with Priscilla and Aquila. He stops briefly in Ephesus, where he reasons with the Jews in the synagogue. Although they ask him to stay longer, Paul declines, stating that he must go to Jerusalem for a feast but promises to return if it is God's will. He then travels to Caesarea, visits the church, and returns to Antioch.", "18:24–28": "While Paul is traveling through Galatia and Phrygia, a Jewish man named Apollos arrives in Ephesus. Apollos is eloquent and well-versed in the Scriptures, teaching accurately about Jesus, but only knows the baptism of John. When Priscilla and Aquila hear him, they take him aside and explain the way of God more fully. Apollos later travels to Achaia, where he powerfully refutes the Jews in public, proving from the Scriptures that Jesus is the Messiah.", "18:1–4": "Paul went to Corinth and stayed with Aquila and Priscilla, fellow tentmakers, reasoning in the synagogue every Sabbath to persuade both Jews and Greeks.", "18:5–8": "When Silas and Timothy arrived, Paul devoted himself to testifying that Jesus is the Messiah. When the Jews opposed him, he turned to preach to the Gentiles, and Crispus, the synagogue leader, believed along with his household.", "18:18–21": "Paul stayed in Corinth a while longer, then sailed for Syria, stopping in Ephesus to reason with the Jews before continuing on, promising to return if God willed it."}, "Acts 19": {"19:1–7": "When Paul arrives in Ephesus, he meets some disciples who had only received John’s baptism. Paul explains that John’s baptism was a preparation for faith in Jesus. After being baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus, Paul lays hands on them, and they receive the Holy Spirit, speaking in tongues and prophesying.", "19:8–10": "Paul spends three months preaching in the synagogue in Ephesus, but when some become hardened and speak against the faith, he moves his teaching to the school of Tyrannus. He continues teaching there for two years, and the gospel spreads throughout the entire region of Asia to both Jews and Greeks.", "19:11–12": "God performs extraordinary miracles through Paul, to the extent that even handkerchiefs and aprons that had touched Paul are brought to the sick, and they are healed, and evil spirits are cast out.", "19:17–20": "The seven sons of Sceva, Jewish exorcists, try to use the name of Jesus to cast out evil spirits, though they do not truly believe. When they attempt this, an evil spirit attacks and overpowers them, leaving them wounded and naked. This event causes great fear throughout Ephesus, and the name of Jesus is greatly honored. Many who had practiced sorcery come to faith, confess their deeds, and publicly burn their expensive magic books. The word of God grows mightily and prevails throughout the city.", "19:35–41": "Demetrius, a silversmith who makes idols of the goddess Diana, stirs up a riot against Paul because his preaching threatens their business. The people chant, Great is Diana of the Ephesians, and the city is thrown into confusion. Gaius and Aristarchus, Paul's companions, are seized and dragged into the theater. The town clerk eventually calms the crowd, urging them to resolve their grievances legally and warning them of the consequences of an unlawful assembly.", "19:13–16": "Some Jewish exorcists tried using Jesus' name to cast out spirits without believing in Him. An evil spirit overpowered them, saying it knew Jesus and Paul but not them.", "19:21–22": "Paul decided to travel through Macedonia and Achaia to Jerusalem, saying he must also see Rome, and sent Timothy and Erastus ahead to Macedonia.", "19:23–27": "A silversmith named Demetrius, worried that Paul's preaching against idols was hurting his business making shrines of Artemis, stirred up the craftsmen against Paul.", "19:28–31": "The crowd, enraged, began shouting in support of Artemis and rushed to the theater, dragging Paul's companions with them, though Paul's friends kept him from going in.", "19:32–34": "The assembly was in confusion, with most people not even knowing why they'd gathered. When they realized a spokesman was Jewish, the crowd shouted for two hours in support of Artemis."}, "Acts 20": {"20:1–6": "After the riot in Ephesus, Paul travels through Macedonia, encouraging the believers before arriving in Greece. He stays there for three months, but because of a plot by the Jews, he decides to return through Macedonia rather than sail directly to Syria. He is accompanied by several fellow workers, and they eventually meet in Troas.", "20:7–12": "Paul preaches in Troas, continuing late into the night. A young man named Eutychus falls asleep and falls from a third-floor window, dying from the fall. Paul goes down, embraces him, and restores him to life. The believers are greatly comforted, and Paul continues teaching until daybreak before departing.", "20:13–16": "Paul and his companions travel by ship, stopping at several places along the way, with Paul walking part of the journey to Assos. Paul chooses not to stop in Ephesus, as he is hurrying to reach Jerusalem by Pentecost.", "20:32–35": "Paul calls for the elders of the Ephesian church and delivers a heartfelt farewell speech. He reminds them of his faithful ministry among them, warning them of the dangers that will come after his departure, both from false teachers within and from hostile forces outside. He commends them to God and urges them to care for the church, reminding them that it is more blessed to give than to receive.", "20:36–38": "After his farewell, Paul kneels and prays with the elders, and they weep, embracing him with deep sorrow because he has told them that they will never see him again. They then accompany him to the ship.", "20:17–21": "Paul called the elders of Ephesus together and reminded them how he had served among them with humility and tears, teaching publicly and house to house about turning to God.", "20:22–24": "Paul told them he was compelled to go to Jerusalem, not knowing what awaited him except hardship, but said his life wasn't precious to him compared to finishing the ministry Jesus gave him.", "20:25–27": "Paul told them they wouldn't see his face again, and declared he was innocent of anyone's blood because he hadn't held back from telling them everything God wanted them to know.", "20:28–31": "Paul warned the elders to watch over the church carefully, because after he left, false teachers like savage wolves would rise up and try to lead people away."}, "Acts 21": {"21:10–14": "Paul continues his journey toward Jerusalem, stopping at several places along the way. In Tyre, the disciples warn him through the Spirit not to go to Jerusalem. In Caesarea, the prophet Agabus foretells Paul’s arrest by binding himself with Paul’s belt. Despite the warnings, Paul is determined to go, even if it means his death, and the believers submit to the will of the Lord.", "21:23–26": "Upon arriving in Jerusalem, Paul meets with James and the elders, who are pleased to hear of the success of Paul’s ministry among the Gentiles. However, they inform him of the rumors circulating among Jewish believers that Paul is teaching Jews to abandon the law. To demonstrate that he respects the law, Paul agrees to undergo a purification ritual in the temple with four other men who are under a vow.", "21:33–36": "Paul is recognized in the temple by Jews from Asia, who accuse him of teaching against the law and of bringing Gentiles into the temple. This accusation incites a riot, and Paul is dragged out of the temple, where the crowd begins to beat him. Roman soldiers intervene and arrest Paul, binding him with chains and taking him into custody to prevent further violence.", "21:37–40": "As Paul is about to be taken into the barracks, he asks the Roman commander for permission to speak to the crowd. The commander is surprised that Paul speaks Greek, having mistaken him for an Egyptian revolutionary. Paul explains that he is a Jew from Tarsus, and the commander allows him to address the crowd. Paul then begins to speak to the people in Hebrew.", "21:1–3": "Paul's group sailed on toward Syria, stopping at various ports along the way, and landed at Tyre where the ship was unloading cargo.", "21:4–6": "The disciples in Tyre, through the Spirit, urged Paul not to go to Jerusalem. After a week together, they all prayed on the beach and said goodbye.", "21:7–9": "They continued to Caesarea and stayed with Philip the evangelist, whose four unmarried daughters had the gift of prophecy.", "21:15–16": "They packed up and traveled to Jerusalem, accompanied by some disciples from Caesarea who brought them to stay with a man named Mnason.", "21:17–19": "The believers in Jerusalem welcomed Paul warmly, and he reported to James and the elders everything God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry.", "21:20–22": "The leaders told Paul that thousands of Jewish believers were zealous for the law and had heard rumors that he taught Jews to abandon it, so a crowd was likely to gather.", "21:27–29": "Jews from Asia saw Paul in the temple, stirred up the crowd, and seized him, wrongly accusing him of bringing a Gentile into a restricted area of the temple.", "21:30–32": "The whole city rushed together, dragging Paul out of the temple and trying to kill him, until the Roman commander arrived with soldiers and the crowd stopped."}, "Acts 22": {"22:15–16": "Paul recounts his background to the crowd, explaining his strict Jewish upbringing and his former persecution of Christians. He then describes his dramatic encounter with Jesus on the road to Damascus, where Jesus revealed Himself to Paul and commissioned him to be a witness. Paul tells how Ananias restored his sight and baptized him.", "22:19–21": "Paul continues to tell how, after his conversion, Jesus appeared to him again in Jerusalem, telling him to leave the city because the Jews would not accept his testimony. Jesus then instructed Paul to go to the Gentiles with the gospel.", "22:22–24": "When Paul mentions being sent to the Gentiles, the crowd erupts in anger, shouting that he should be put to death. The Roman commander orders Paul to be brought into the barracks and prepared for scourging to find out why the crowd is so furious.", "22:27–29": "As Paul is about to be scourged, he asserts his rights as a Roman citizen, asking whether it is lawful to scourge a Roman citizen who has not been condemned. The centurion immediately stops and informs the commander, who is alarmed to learn that Paul is a Roman by birth.", "22:30": "The following day, the Roman commander releases Paul from his chains and brings him before the Jewish Sanhedrin to determine the exact nature of the charges against him.", "22:1–3": "Paul addressed the crowd in Hebrew, describing his background as a zealous, law-trained Jew raised under Gamaliel.", "22:4–5": "Paul admitted he had once persecuted believers to the death, arresting them and bringing them to Jerusalem with letters of authority from the high priest.", "22:6–8": "Paul retold how a bright light from heaven struck him near Damascus and a voice identified itself as Jesus, whom he was persecuting.", "22:9–11": "Paul explained that his companions saw the light but didn't hear the voice, and he was led by the hand into Damascus because he'd been blinded by the light.", "22:12–14": "A devout man named Ananias came and told Saul that God had chosen him to know His will and to see and hear the Righteous One.", "22:17–18": "Paul described a later vision in the temple where the Lord told him to leave Jerusalem quickly because the people there wouldn't accept his testimony.", "22:25–26": "As they prepared to flog him, Paul asked if it was legal to flog a Roman citizen without a trial, which alarmed the centurion enough to report it to the commander."}, "Acts 23": {"23:9–10": "Paul is brought before the Sanhedrin, and after being struck on the mouth by order of the high priest, he rebukes them for breaking the law. Realizing that the council is divided between Pharisees and Sadducees, Paul declares that he is on trial because of his belief in the resurrection. This causes a sharp division, and the Roman commander rescues Paul from the violence that follows.", "23:11": "That night, the Lord appears to Paul, encouraging him by telling him that just as he has testified about Jesus in Jerusalem, he must also testify in Rome.", "23:19–22": "More than forty Jews make a plot to kill Paul, taking an oath that they will not eat or drink until they have done so. They plan to ambush Paul when he is brought before the Sanhedrin again. However, Paul’s nephew learns of the plot and informs the Roman commander, who takes immediate action to protect Paul.", "23:34–35": "The Roman commander, Claudius Lysias, prepares a large military escort to take Paul safely to Caesarea, where he will stand trial before Governor Felix. Lysias sends a letter explaining the situation, emphasizing that Paul is a Roman citizen and that the charges against him are related to Jewish law, not criminal offenses. Paul is safely delivered to Felix, who agrees to hear the case when Paul’s accusers arrive.", "23:1–3": "Paul told the council he had lived with a clear conscience before God. When the high priest ordered him struck for saying this, Paul rebuked him sharply.", "23:4–5": "When rebuked for insulting the high priest, Paul said he hadn't realized who it was, and quoted Scripture about not speaking evil of a ruler.", "23:6–8": "Paul, knowing the council was split between Pharisees and Sadducees, declared he was on trial over the resurrection of the dead — a belief the Sadducees rejected and the Pharisees held.", "23:12–15": "More than forty Jews conspired to kill Paul, vowing not to eat until they had done it, and asked the chief priests to help arrange his transfer so they could ambush him.", "23:16–18": "Paul's nephew heard about the ambush plan and reported it, and Paul had him brought to the commander to explain.", "23:23–24": "The commander arranged for two hundred soldiers, horsemen, and spearmen to escort Paul safely to Felix the governor that night.", "23:25–27": "The commander wrote a letter to Felix explaining that he had rescued Paul, a Roman citizen, from a mob that was about to kill him.", "23:28–30": "The letter explained that the accusations against Paul were about Jewish law, not anything deserving death, and that he was being sent to Felix after learning of a plot against him.", "23:31–33": "The soldiers brought Paul safely to Antipatris and then on to Caesarea, delivering him and the letter to the governor."}, "Acts 24": {"24:9": "Five days later, the high priest Ananias and some elders arrive in Caesarea with a lawyer named Tertullus to present their accusations against Paul before Governor Felix. Tertullus accuses Paul of being a troublemaker, stirring up riots among the Jews, and attempting to profane the temple. He also blames the Roman commander Lysias for interfering with their judgment of Paul.", "24:20–21": "In his defense, Paul calmly states that he has only been in Jerusalem for twelve days and was there to worship peacefully, not to cause trouble. He admits to being a follower of the Way but insists that this is not heresy, as he believes in the Scriptures and the resurrection of the dead, a belief shared by some of his accusers. Paul denies the charges of causing unrest and points out that his accusers from Asia are not present to testify against him.", "24:22–23": "Felix, who has some knowledge of the Way (Christianity), postpones the trial until the Roman commander Lysias can come to give his testimony. Meanwhile, Paul is kept under guard but allowed some freedom, and his friends are permitted to visit him and provide for his needs.", "24:26–27": "After a few days, Felix, along with his wife Drusilla, who is Jewish, summons Paul to hear him speak about faith in Christ. As Paul talks about righteousness, self-control, and the coming judgment, Felix becomes frightened and dismisses Paul, promising to call for him again at a more convenient time. Felix also hopes that Paul will offer him a bribe for his release and continues to speak with him frequently. Paul remains in custody for two years, during which Felix is succeeded by Porcius Festus as governor. To appease the Jewish leaders, Felix leaves Paul in prison when he departs.", "24:1–4": "Five days later, the high priest Ananias came with a lawyer named Tertullus to present their case against Paul before Felix.", "24:5–8": "Tertullus accused Paul of being a troublemaker and a ringleader among the Nazarenes, and of trying to defile the temple, claiming Paul could confirm all this under questioning.", "24:10–13": "Paul responded that he had only been in Jerusalem twelve days and hadn't been found arguing or stirring up crowds anywhere, so his accusers couldn't prove their charges.", "24:14–16": "Paul admitted he worshiped God according to the Way, believing everything in the Law and Prophets, including the coming resurrection of both the righteous and the unrighteous.", "24:17–19": "Paul explained he had come to Jerusalem to bring gifts for the poor and was found peacefully in the temple, not causing any disturbance.", "24:24–25": "Felix later sent for Paul to hear him speak about faith in Christ. As Paul spoke about righteousness and self-control and coming judgment, Felix became afraid and sent him away for the time being."}, "Acts 25": {"25:9–12": "Festus, the new governor, goes to Jerusalem, where the Jewish leaders ask him to bring Paul back to Jerusalem, planning to ambush and kill him. Festus, however, insists that Paul remain in Caesarea. When Festus returns, the Jews present accusations against Paul, which they cannot prove. In response, Paul declares that he has not committed any crime and, realizing he will not receive justice from the Jewish authorities, appeals to Caesar.", "25:18–22": "King Agrippa and Bernice visit Festus in Caesarea, and Festus explains Paul’s case to them. Festus shares that the accusations against Paul were not what he expected; they were religious disputes about Jesus, whom Paul claims is alive. Agrippa expresses interest in hearing Paul himself, and Festus agrees to arrange it for the next day.", "25:23–27": "The next day, Paul is brought before Agrippa, Bernice, and many prominent men of the city. Festus presents Paul to Agrippa, explaining that although the Jews want Paul executed, Festus has found no evidence of a crime deserving death. He is uncertain what to write to Caesar regarding Paul’s charges and hopes that Agrippa’s examination will help clarify the situation.", "25:1–5": "When Festus took office, the Jewish leaders asked him to bring Paul to Jerusalem, secretly planning to ambush and kill him along the way, but Festus said he would hear the case in Caesarea instead.", "25:6–8": "Festus brought Paul before him, and though the Jews made many serious accusations they couldn't prove, Paul denied doing anything wrong against the law, the temple, or Caesar.", "25:13–17": "King Agrippa visited Festus, who described Paul's case to him, explaining that Roman custom required the accused to face his accusers before judgment."}, "Acts 26": {"26:9–11": "Paul begins his defense before Agrippa, expressing gratitude for the opportunity to speak to someone knowledgeable about Jewish customs and issues. He explains his background as a Pharisee and his zeal in persecuting Christians. He also points out that the reason for his trial is his belief in the resurrection of the dead, which is central to the Jewish hope.", "26:16–18": "Paul recounts his dramatic encounter with Jesus on the road to Damascus. He describes the heavenly light and the voice of Jesus, who commissioned him to be a witness to both Jews and Gentiles, turning them from darkness to light and from Satan’s power to God, so they might receive forgiveness and an inheritance through faith.", "26:19–23": "Paul explains that he was obedient to the vision from Jesus and began preaching to both Jews and Gentiles, calling them to repentance and faith in God. This, he says, is why the Jews tried to kill him. Yet, he continues to preach, proclaiming what the prophets and Moses foretold: that Christ would suffer, rise from the dead, and bring light to both Jews and Gentiles.", "26:24–26": "Festus interrupts Paul, accusing him of being mad due to too much learning. Paul calmly responds, assuring Festus that he is speaking the truth with sound judgment.", "26:27–29": "Paul appeals to Agrippa’s knowledge of Jewish beliefs and asks him directly if he believes the prophets. Agrippa responds by saying that Paul almost persuades him to become a Christian. Paul expresses his desire that everyone listening, not just Agrippa, might come to faith in Christ, though without being in chains as he is.", "26:30–32": "After Paul’s defense, King Agrippa, Governor Festus, and those present agree that Paul has done nothing deserving of death or imprisonment. Agrippa remarks that Paul could have been set free had he not appealed to Caesar.", "26:1–3": "Agrippa gave Paul permission to speak, and Paul began his defense, glad to make his case before someone familiar with Jewish customs.", "26:4–8": "Paul explained he had lived as a strict Pharisee and was now on trial for the hope of the resurrection that God had promised to Israel's ancestors.", "26:12–15": "Paul again described his encounter on the road to Damascus, when a bright light from heaven knocked him to the ground and a voice identified itself as Jesus, whom he was persecuting."}, "Acts 27": {"27:6–8": "Paul, along with other prisoners, is handed over to Julius, a centurion, to be transported to Rome. They set sail and make several stops along the way, including Sidon, where Julius allows Paul to visit his friends. They encounter strong winds, which slow their progress, and eventually arrive at a place called Fair Havens.", "27:9–12": "As the weather becomes more dangerous for sailing, Paul warns the crew and the centurion that continuing the voyage could result in disaster. However, the centurion trusts the ship's captain and owner more than Paul's warning, and they decide to sail on, hoping to reach a better harbor to winter in.", "27:18–20": "Initially, favorable winds give the sailors hope, but soon a violent storm, known as Euroclydon, strikes. The crew struggles to control the ship, even throwing the cargo and tackle overboard to lighten the load. After many days of being battered by the storm, they lose all hope of survival.", "27:21–26": "Paul stands up and reassures the crew that, although the ship will be lost, no one will die. He tells them that an angel appeared to him, confirming that he will stand before Caesar and that God has granted the lives of all aboard the ship. Paul encourages them to have faith in God’s promise.", "27:33–38": "After two weeks of being driven by the storm, the crew senses they are nearing land. They drop anchors to slow the ship and prepare to abandon it, but Paul warns them that unless they stay aboard, they cannot be saved. The soldiers heed Paul’s advice, cutting the ropes of the lifeboat. Paul then encourages everyone to eat, reassuring them that not a single life will be lost.", "27:42–44": "In daylight, they spot a bay with a beach and decide to run the ship aground there. The ship strikes a sandbar and begins to break apart. The soldiers consider killing the prisoners to prevent their escape, but the centurion, wanting to save Paul, stops them. Everyone either swims or floats on debris to shore, and all 276 people aboard make it safely to land, just as Paul had said.", "27:1–5": "Paul and other prisoners were placed under a centurion named Julius and set sail for Italy, stopping first at Sidon, where Julius kindly allowed Paul to visit friends.", "27:13–17": "A gentle wind turned into a violent storm that swept the ship off course near Crete, and the crew struggled just to keep the ship's boat under control.", "27:27–29": "As the ship drifted through the night, the sailors sensed land was near and took soundings, then dropped anchors and prayed for daylight, afraid of running aground.", "27:30–32": "The sailors tried to secretly abandon ship in the lifeboat, but Paul warned that everyone needed to stay aboard to survive, so the soldiers cut the boat loose.", "27:39–41": "At daylight, the sailors tried to run the ship onto a beach, but it struck a sandbar and began breaking apart under the waves."}, "Acts 28": {"28:1–6": "Paul and the others find themselves on the island of Malta. The locals show kindness to the shipwreck survivors. When a viper bites Paul, the people assume he is doomed to die, but when nothing happens to him, they change their minds, thinking he is divine.", "28:7–10": "Paul heals the father of Publius, the chief official of the island, and many others who are sick. The islanders honor Paul and the crew, providing them with everything they need as they prepare to leave.", "28:15–16": "After three months, Paul and the others board a ship and sail for Rome. Along the way, they stop at several places and meet fellow believers. When they finally reach Rome, Paul is allowed to live by himself under guard rather than being imprisoned with the other prisoners.", "28:21–22": "Paul meets with the Jewish leaders in Rome, explaining that he was arrested in Jerusalem despite committing no offense against the Jewish people or customs. The Jews in Rome have heard no accusations against him but express curiosity about the Christian faith, which is widely opposed.", "28:28–29": "Paul spends a day teaching the Jews about the kingdom of God and explaining how Jesus fulfills the law and the prophets. While some believe, others do not. Paul quotes Isaiah, pointing out the hardness of heart that prevents many from understanding the truth. He declares that salvation has been sent to the Gentiles, who will receive it.", "28:30–31": "Paul remains in Rome for two years, living in his own rented house. He continues to preach the kingdom of God and teach about Jesus Christ with boldness and without hindrance.", "28:11–14": "After three months, they set sail again and eventually arrived in Rome, staying with fellow believers they met along the way.", "28:17–20": "Paul called the local Jewish leaders together and explained that he had done nothing against his people, but had been forced to appeal to Caesar after being handed over as a prisoner.", "28:23–24": "Paul spent a whole day explaining about Jesus from the Law and the Prophets, and some believed while others did not.", "28:25–27": "As they left in disagreement, Paul quoted Isaiah's prophecy that the people would hear and see without truly understanding, because their hearts had grown dull."}, "Romans 1": {"1:1–7": "Paul introduces himself as a servant of Christ, called to be an apostle, and extends greetings to the believers in Rome. He emphasizes that through Jesus, we have received unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and the mission to spread the gospel to all nations.", "1:8–15": "Paul expresses his gratitude for the faith of the believers in Rome and his desire to visit them. He wants to impart a spiritual gift and be mutually encouraged by their faith. He also affirms his readiness to preach the gospel in Rome.", "1:16–17": "Paul boldly declares that he is not ashamed of the gospel because it is God’s power for salvation to everyone who believes. The righteousness of God is revealed through faith, and those who are righteous live by faith.", "1:28–32": "Paul warns of the wrath of God against those who suppress the truth and live in unrighteousness. He describes how humanity turned away from God, worshiped created things instead of the Creator, and engaged in sinful behaviors. As a result, God gave them over to their sinful desires and a depraved mind.", "1:18–23": "Paul says God's anger is directed at people who suppress the truth about Him, even though His existence and power are clear from creation. Even though people could know God, they didn't honor Him and turned to worshiping images instead.", "1:24–27": "Because they exchanged God's truth for a lie, Paul says God let them go their own way into shameful desires, including relationships that go against how people were made."}, "Romans 2": {"2:6–11": "Paul warns against judging others while practicing the same sins. He emphasizes that God's judgment is righteous and impartial, based on the truth. It is not through judgment but through God's goodness that repentance comes.", "2:12–16": "Paul explains that all people, whether they have the law or not, will be judged according to their actions. Even Gentiles, who do not have the law, show its effects by their conscience. God will judge the secrets of everyone through Jesus Christ.", "2:17–24": "Paul addresses the hypocrisy of the Jews who boast in the law yet break it. Their failure to live according to God's commandments brings dishonor to God's name among the Gentiles.", "2:25–29": "Paul teaches that true circumcision is not merely an outward, physical sign but an inward change of the heart. It is not the outward keeping of the law that makes someone right with God, but rather a heart transformed by God's Spirit.", "2:1–5": "Paul says anyone who judges others for doing wrong has no excuse, since they do the same things themselves. He warns that ignoring God's patience and kindness only stores up judgment for later."}, "Romans 3": {"3:5–8": "Paul explains that while Jews have the privilege of receiving God’s law, God's faithfulness is not nullified by human unfaithfulness. Even if some were unfaithful, God's truth still stands. Paul rejects the idea that one could do evil for the sake of producing good.", "3:13–18": "Paul emphasizes that both Jews and Gentiles are under the power of sin. He quotes various Old Testament scriptures to show that no one is righteous on their own—no one truly seeks after God, and all have turned aside.", "3:19–20": "The law was given to reveal sin, and no one can be justified by observing it. The law exposes humanity's guilt before God, showing that everyone falls short.", "3:25–26": "God’s righteousness is revealed through faith in Jesus Christ. All have sinned and fallen short, but through Jesus' sacrifice, we are justified freely by His unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor. Christ's death serves as the atonement for our sins, demonstrating God's justice and mercy.", "3:29–31": "Paul concludes that no one can boast in their own righteousness because justification comes through faith, not by works. Both Jews and Gentiles are justified by the same God through faith. This faith puts the law in it's proper place.", "3:1–2": "Paul asks what advantage there is in being Jewish. He says there's a great one — the Jewish people were entrusted with God's own words.", "3:3–4": "Paul says that even if some didn't believe, that doesn't cancel God's faithfulness — God remains true no matter what people do.", "3:9–12": "Paul says both Jews and Gentiles are equally under the power of sin, quoting Scripture that says no one is righteous, no one truly seeks God, and everyone has gone astray.", "3:21–22": "Paul explains that a right standing with God, separate from keeping the law, has now been revealed — it comes through faith in Jesus Christ and is available to everyone who believes.", "3:23–24": "Paul says everyone has sinned and fallen short of God's glory, but everyone is made right with God freely, as a gift, through what Christ did.", "3:27–28": "Paul says there's no room for boasting, because being made right with God comes through faith, not through what a person does."}, "Romans 4": {"4:4–5": "Paul explains that Abraham was not justified by his works but by his faith. His belief in God was counted as righteousness, showing that justification comes through faith, not by works. This righteousness is a gift of God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, not something earned.", "4:6–8": "Paul quotes David, who speaks of the blessedness of those whose sins are forgiven. This further supports the truth that righteousness is imputed by God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and not by human effort.", "4:11–12": "Paul clarifies that righteousness by faith is not limited to those who are circumcised (the Jews) but is also available to the uncircumcised (the Gentiles). Abraham was counted righteous before he was circumcised, making him the father of all who believe.", "4:16–17": "The promise given to Abraham was based on faith, not the law. If the promise were dependent on the law, it would be unattainable due to human weakness. Instead, it is through faith and God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor that the promise is available to all who believe, both Jew and Gentile.", "4:20–22": "Abraham believed God’s promise, even when all human reason said it was impossible. His unwavering faith in God’s power to fulfill His promises was counted to him as righteousness.", "4:23–25": "The righteousness imputed to Abraham is not just for him, but for all who believe in the God who raised Jesus from the dead. Jesus was delivered up for our sins and raised for our justification, making righteousness available through faith.", "4:1–3": "Paul asks about Abraham, and points out that Scripture says Abraham simply believed God, and that faith was counted to him as righteousness.", "4:9–10": "Paul points out that Abraham's faith was counted as righteousness before he was circumcised, not after — showing this blessing wasn't limited to the circumcised.", "4:13–15": "Paul explains that the promise to Abraham came through faith, not through keeping the law. If it depended on the law, faith and the promise would be meaningless.", "4:18–19": "Even though it seemed impossible given his and Sarah's old age, Abraham kept believing God's promise that he would have many descendants, and his faith never wavered."}, "Romans 5": {"5:3–5": "Paul explains that being justified by faith brings peace with God through Jesus Christ. Through Him, we have access to God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and can rejoice even in suffering, knowing that trials produce perseverance, character, and hope.", "5:9–11": "Paul reminds us that Christ died for us while we were still sinners, demonstrating God’s profound love. Through Christ’s sacrifice, we are justified, reconciled to God, and saved from His wrath. This is the ultimate expression of His unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor.", "5:15–17": "Paul contrasts the effects of Adam’s sin and Christ’s obedience. Through Adam, sin and death entered the world and spread to all humanity. But through Jesus Christ, God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor abounds, bringing righteousness and life to all who believe.", "5:20–21": "Just as Adam’s disobedience brought condemnation to all, Jesus' obedience brings justification and life to all who believe. The law was given so sin would increase, but God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor overflows, offering righteousness and eternal life through Jesus Christ.", "5:1–2": "Paul says that because we're made right with God through faith, we now have peace with God and access to His grace, and we can rejoice in the hope of His glory.", "5:6–8": "Paul says Christ died for us while we were still sinners and powerless to save ourselves — proving God's love, since people rarely die even for a good person.", "5:12–14": "Paul explains that sin entered the world through one man, Adam, and death spread to everyone because everyone sinned, even before the law existed.", "5:18–19": "Just as one man's disobedience brought condemnation to everyone, Paul says one man's obedience — Christ's — makes it possible for many to be made righteous."}, "Romans 6": {"6:6–7": "Paul teaches that we are now in Christ and in the mind of God share his death and resurrection. We are buried with Him and raised to live a new life. We are no longer slaves to sin but free because our old selves were crucified with Him.", "6:8–11": "Just as Christ died and now lives for God, we too are called to consider ourselves dead to sin but alive to God in Christ. Our new life is to be lived in this fresh and living way.", "6:12–14": "Believers are encouraged not to let sin control their bodies, but to offer themselves to God as instruments of righteousness. Alive from the dead, leaving our old man in the grave.", "6:17–19": "Paul contrasts being a servant to sin versus a servant to righteousness. When we were enslaved to sin, it led to death. But now, having been set free by the cross, we are to offer ourselves as servants to righteousness, leading to holiness.", "6:22–23": "Before coming to Christ, we were slaves to sin, leading to shame and ultimately death. But now, having been set free by the cross, we can serve God, resulting in holiness and eternal life. The payment for sin is death, but the free gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ.", "6:1–2": "Paul asks if people should keep sinning so grace can increase, and answers firmly no — believers have died to sin and can't keep living in it.", "6:3–5": "Paul explains that being baptized into Christ means being joined with His death and, in the same way, joined with His resurrection into a new kind of life.", "6:15–16": "Paul again says no to the idea of sinning just because believers are under grace instead of law, explaining that whoever you obey becomes your master — either sin, leading to death, or obedience, leading to righteousness.", "6:20–21": "Paul points out that when they were slaves to sin, they got nothing good out of it — those things only led to death."}, "Romans 7": {"7:4–6": "Paul uses marriage as an analogy to show that believers have died to the law through the body of Christ and are now bound to Him. They can now live in the newness of the Spirit, producing fruit for God.", "7:9–12": "Paul explains that the law is not sinful but reveals sin. The law exposes human weakness and sin, which leads to death. Though the law is holy and good, it cannot give life but rather shows humanity’s need for salvation.", "7:24–25": "Paul describes the internal struggle of trying to obey the law while being bound by sin. He expresses frustration over his inability to do what is right and recognizes that only Jesus Christ can deliver him from this body of death.", "7:1–3": "Paul uses the example of marriage law to show that the law only holds authority over someone while they're alive — a widow is free to remarry once her husband has died.", "7:7–8": "Paul asks if the law itself is sin, and says no — but explains that the law showed him what sin actually was, and sin used the commandment to stir up wrong desire in him.", "7:13": "Paul asks if something good caused his death, and explains that it was actually sin working through something good, showing sin for what it truly is.", "7:14–17": "Paul describes an inner struggle — he doesn't do the good he wants to do, but does the very thing he doesn't want to do, concluding it's sin living in him causing it.", "7:18–20": "Paul says nothing good lives in his old nature — he wants to do good but keeps doing the evil he doesn't want to do, and again blames the sin living within him.", "7:21–23": "Paul describes finding a rule at work in him — that whenever he wants to do good, evil is right there too, and a different law wars against his mind and takes him captive to sin."}, "Romans 8": {"8:5–8": "Paul opens by declaring that there is no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus. Through Christ’s work and the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God, believers are set free from the law of sin and death. By living in the Spirit, we fulfill the righteousness that the law demands.", "8:12–14": "The Holy Spirit dwelling within believers is the source of life and power. It is through the Spirit that believers are empowered to put to death the sinful deeds of the body and live a life of righteousness.", "8:15–17": "Believers are adopted into God’s family and are now His children. The Spirit testifies to our spirit that we are God’s children, and as such, we are heirs of God and co-heirs with Christ, sharing in both His sufferings and His glory.", "8:23–25": "Paul encourages believers that their present sufferings are insignificant compared to the glory that awaits them. Creation itself is waiting for the full revelation of the children of God. We live in hope, eagerly awaiting the redemption of our bodies.", "8:26–27": "The Holy Spirit helps believers in their weakness, especially in prayer. Even when we do not know what to pray, the Spirit intercedes for us in alignment with God’s will.", "8:28–30": "Paul assures believers that God works all things for their good. Those whom God foreknew, He also predestined to become like Christ, justified them, and will glorify them.", "8:38–39": "Paul triumphantly declares that nothing can separate believers from the love of God in Christ. Not death, life, spiritual forces, or any other created thing can hinder God's love for us. In all trials, believers are more than conquerors through Christ.", "8:1–4": "Paul declares there is no condemnation for those in Christ Jesus, because the law of the Spirit has set them free from the law of sin and death — something the law itself couldn't do, but God did through sending His Son.", "8:9–11": "Paul says that if God's Spirit lives in a person, their body may be subject to death because of sin, but the Spirit gives life, and the same Spirit that raised Jesus will one day give life to their mortal bodies too.", "8:18–22": "Paul says the suffering of this present time is nothing compared to the coming glory, and describes creation itself waiting and groaning, longing to be set free from decay.", "8:31–34": "Paul asks who can stand against believers if God is for them, pointing out that God, who didn't spare His own Son, will certainly give them everything else, and that Christ Himself is interceding for them.", "8:35–37": "Paul lists hardships like trouble, persecution, and danger, and says none of them can separate believers from Christ's love — in all these things they are more than conquerors through Him."}, "Romans 9": {"9:4–5": "Paul expresses deep sorrow and anguish over his fellow Israelites, wishing he could be accursed for their sake. He acknowledges the privileges they have received, including the adoption, the glory, the covenants, and the law.", "9:10–13": "Paul explains that God's promises have not failed, even though not all Israelites believe. God’s sovereign choice, demonstrated through Isaac and Jacob, is based on His calling and purpose, not on human works or effort.", "9:14–18": "God's righteousness is demonstrated in His sovereign election. It is not based on human will or effort but on God’s mercy. God’s dealings with Pharaoh reveal His power and authority over human hearts.", "9:22–24": "Paul uses the analogy of the potter and the clay to illustrate God's authority over creation. God has the right to shape individuals and nations according to His purpose, demonstrating both His wrath and His mercy.", "9:27–29": "Paul quotes the prophets Hosea and Isaiah to show that God’s calling extends to the Gentiles. He also explains that only a remnant of Israel will be saved, emphasizing God’s sovereignty in election and mercy.", "9:30–33": "Paul concludes that the Gentiles, who did not seek righteousness through the law, have obtained it by faith. Meanwhile, Israel, in seeking righteousness through the law, stumbled over Christ, the stumbling stone. Yet, whoever believes in Him will not be ashamed.", "9:1–3": "Paul expresses deep sorrow and grief for his fellow Israelites, saying he would even wish to be cut off from Christ himself if it could save them.", "9:6–9": "Paul explains that God's word hasn't failed just because not everyone descended from Israel truly belongs to Israel — it's the children of promise, like Isaac, who are counted as true children.", "9:19–21": "Paul answers an objection by saying no one has the right to question God the way clay questions the potter who shaped it.", "9:25–26": "Paul quotes Hosea, where God says He will call people who weren't His people 'my people,' and will call them 'children of the living God.'"}, "Romans 10": {"10:1–4": "Paul expresses his deep longing for the salvation of Israel. He acknowledges that while they are zealous for God, they are trying to establish their own righteousness through the law instead of submitting to God’s righteousness, which is found in Christ.", "10:9–13": "Paul contrasts the righteousness of the law, which depends on works, with the righteousness that comes by faith. Righteousness is not about ascending to heaven or descending to the grave but about confessing and believing in Christ. Those who trust in Him will not be put to shame. Salvation is available to everyone, both Jews and Gentiles, without distinction. God’s riches of mercy and salvation are for all who call on His name.", "10:16–17": "Paul emphasizes the necessity of preaching the gospel so that people may hear and believe. Faith comes by hearing the message of Christ, and this message must be shared by those sent to preach.", "10:18–21": "Paul concludes that Israel has heard the gospel, yet many have rejected it. He quotes Moses and Isaiah, who prophesied that God would provoke Israel to jealousy by turning to the Gentiles and that Israel would remain disobedient despite God’s persistent outreach.", "10:5–8": "Paul contrasts the righteousness that comes from keeping the law with the righteousness that comes from faith, which he says is as near as the words in your own mouth and heart.", "10:14–15": "Paul explains that people can't call on someone they haven't believed in, can't believe without hearing, and can't hear without someone preaching to them."}, "Romans 11": {"11:5–6": "Paul assures his readers that God has not abandoned His people, Israel. Just as in Elijah's time, there remains a remnant of Israelites who are chosen by God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and not by their works.", "11:7–10": "Israel, as a whole, has not attained the righteousness it sought because many were blinded to the truth. Only the elect have received it, while the rest have been hardened, fulfilling what was written in Scripture.", "11:13–16": "Paul explains that Israel’s stumbling has resulted in salvation coming to the Gentiles, and this, in turn, is meant to provoke Israel to jealousy and lead some of them to salvation. Israel’s eventual full restoration will bring even greater blessings.", "11:22–24": "Paul uses the metaphor of an olive tree to explain God’s plan for Israel and the Gentiles. The Gentiles, like wild branches, were grafted into the olive tree of God's people when some of Israel's branches were broken off due to unbelief. But Gentiles must not be arrogant, for God can graft Israel back in again if they believe.", "11:28–32": "Paul reveals the mystery that Israel’s partial blindness will remain until the full number of Gentiles has come in. Then, all Israel will be saved, in fulfillment of God's covenant. God’s plan is to show mercy to both Jews and Gentiles alike.", "11:33–36": "Paul concludes this chapter with a doxology, marveling at the wisdom and knowledge of God. God's plans are beyond human comprehension, and all things are from Him, through Him, and for His glory.", "11:1–4": "Paul asks if God has rejected His people and says no, pointing to himself as an Israelite, and to the story of Elijah, where God kept seven thousand faithful people even when Elijah thought he was the only one left.", "11:11–12": "Paul asks if Israel's stumbling was meant to be final, and says no — instead, their stumbling opened the door for salvation to reach the Gentiles.", "11:17–21": "Paul compares Gentile believers to a wild olive branch grafted into the tree of Israel, warning them not to be arrogant toward the branches that were broken off, since the same could happen to them.", "11:25–27": "Paul reveals a mystery — that part of Israel has been hardened for a time until the full number of Gentiles come in, and then all Israel will be saved."}, "Romans 12": {"12:1–2": "Paul urges believers to offer themselves as living sacrifices, set apart and pleasing to God. He calls them to reject conformity to the world and to be transformed through the renewing of their minds, enabling them to discern God’s perfect will.", "12:6–8": "Paul encourages humility in the use of spiritual gifts, reminding believers that they are part of one body with different functions. Each member is given a unique gift by God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, and they should use it faithfully to serve others.", "12:17–21": "Paul provides practical guidance for Christian living, urging believers to live in love, humility, and peace with one another. He emphasizes the importance of showing kindness even to enemies, entrusting vengeance to God, and overcoming evil with good.", "12:3–5": "Paul tells believers not to think of themselves more highly than they should, but to see themselves as part of one body with many different parts, each belonging to the others.", "12:9–13": "Paul lists practical instructions: love sincerely, hate evil, hold to good, be devoted to one another, stay diligent, rejoice in hope, be patient in trouble, pray constantly, and share with those in need.", "12:14–16": "Paul tells believers to bless those who persecute them instead of cursing them, to share in others' joys and sorrows, and to live in harmony without being proud."}, "Romans 13": {"13:6–7": "Paul teaches that governing authorities are established by God, and believers are called to submit to them, as resisting authority is resisting God’s ordinance. He encourages respect for authority, paying taxes, and fulfilling civic duties as part of living a godly life.", "13:8–10": "Paul emphasizes that love is the fulfillment of the law. If we love our neighbors as ourselves, we will naturally obey the commandments, as love does no harm to others.", "13:11–14": "Paul calls believers to be alert and ready, recognizing that their salvation is nearer than when they first believed. He urges them to live in the light, casting off the works of darkness and clothing themselves with their new Identity.", "13:1–5": "Paul tells believers to submit to governing authorities, since all authority is established by God, and rulers are meant to punish wrongdoing, not good conduct."}, "Romans 14": {"14:1–4": "Paul warns believers not to judge one another over disputable matters, such as food preferences. Each person answers to God, and God is able to make each one stand firm in their faith, regardless of their personal convictions.", "14:7–9": "Paul explains that each believer should be fully convinced in their own mind and live for the Lord in whatever they do, whether they observe special days or not. Christ is the Lord of both the living and the dead, and all of life is lived for Him.", "14:17–21": "Paul urges believers to avoid causing others to stumble over disputable matters like food or drink. Instead of passing judgment on others, they should live in such a way that promotes peace and righteousness, being sensitive to the convictions of others in the body of Christ.", "14:22–23": "Believers should pursue peace and mutual edification, avoiding anything that would cause a fellow believer to stumble. Paul emphasizes that personal convictions must be lived out in faith, for whatever is not done in faith is sin.", "14:5–6": "Paul says some people consider one day more special than others while some treat every day the same, and each person should be fully convinced in their own mind, doing it as unto the Lord.", "14:10–12": "Paul asks why believers judge or look down on each other, reminding them that everyone will stand before God's judgment seat and give an account of themselves.", "14:13–16": "Paul tells believers to stop judging one another and instead be careful not to cause another believer to stumble over something like food, since love means not destroying someone Christ died for."}, "Romans 15": {"15:5–7": "Paul calls on the strong to bear the weaknesses of the weak and to build others up, just as Christ did not seek to please Himself. The scriptures provide patience and comfort, and believers are encouraged to be of one mind, glorifying God together.", "15:13": "Paul emphasizes that Christ came to confirm God’s promises to the Jews and to bring mercy to the Gentiles, fulfilling the prophecies that spoke of the Gentiles glorifying God. Paul prays for believers to be filled with joy, peace, and hope through the Holy Spirit.", "15:17–21": "Paul reflects on his ministry to the Gentiles, which was empowered by God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor. He takes no credit for the fruit of his ministry but attributes it all to the work of Christ through him, proclaiming the gospel to those who had never heard of Christ.", "15:25–29": "Paul shares his travel plans, expressing his desire to visit the Roman believers on his way to Spain. He first intends to go to Jerusalem to deliver aid to the poor saints there, fulfilling the duty of the Gentiles to help those from whom they received spiritual blessings.", "15:30–33": "Paul requests prayers for protection from unbelievers in Judea and for his ministry to be accepted by the saints in Jerusalem. He desires to visit the Roman believers in joy and to be refreshed by their fellowship.", "15:1–4": "Paul says those who are strong in faith should help carry the weaknesses of those who aren't, following the example of Christ, who didn't live to please Himself.", "15:8–12": "Paul explains that Christ served the Jewish people to confirm God's promises, and also so that the Gentiles would glorify God, quoting several Old Testament passages that predicted Gentiles praising Him too.", "15:14–16": "Paul tells the Roman believers he's confident they're full of goodness and knowledge, and explains his own calling as a minister bringing the gospel to the Gentiles.", "15:22–24": "Paul explains that his ministry work has kept him from visiting Rome, but he now hopes to see them on his way to Spain."}, "Romans 16": {"16:14–16": "Paul sends greetings to various individuals and households who have labored in the gospel, highlighting their faithfulness and service to the church. He emphasizes the close-knit relationships in the body of Christ, where love and mutual support are essential.", "16:17–20": "Paul warns the believers to be cautious of those who cause division or teach contrary doctrines. He encourages them to remain wise in doing good and innocent concerning evil, trusting that God will soon defeat Satan under their feet.", "16:21–24": "Paul's final greetings come from his fellow workers, including Timothy, and the one who wrote down the letter, Tertius. They all send their love and blessings to the church in Rome.", "16:25–27": "Paul closes the letter with a doxology, praising God for His power to establish the believers through the gospel. The mystery of salvation has been revealed through Christ, and the wisdom of God is made known to all nations for the obedience of faith.", "16:1–2": "Paul introduces Phoebe, a servant of the church at Cenchrea, and asks the Roman believers to welcome her and help her, since she has helped many people, including Paul himself.", "16:3–5": "Paul sends greetings to Priscilla and Aquila, who risked their lives for him, to the church that meets in their house, and to Epaenetus, an early convert.", "16:6–9": "Paul continues sending greetings to several people who worked hard for the gospel, including Andronicus and Junia, whom he calls outstanding among the apostles.", "16:10–13": "Paul sends final greetings to more believers by name, including Rufus and his mother, whom Paul says had also been like a mother to him."}, "Galatians 1": {"1:3–5": "Paul begins his letter to the Galatians by emphasizing that his apostleship comes directly from Jesus Christ and God the Father, not from human authority. He blesses the churches with unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace, reminding them that Jesus gave Himself for their sins to rescue them from the present evil age.", "1:8–9": "Paul expresses astonishment that the Galatians are quickly turning to a different gospel. He warns them that there is no other true gospel and that anyone—even an angel—who preaches a different message should be accursed. This underscores the seriousness of remaining faithful to the true gospel of Christ.", "1:11–12": "Paul clarifies that his mission is not to please people but to serve Christ. He emphasizes that the gospel he preaches is not of human origin; rather, it came directly from a revelation of Jesus Christ.", "1:17": "Paul recounts his former life of persecuting the church and how God, in His unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, called him to preach Christ among the Gentiles. Paul emphasizes that he did not seek human approval or instruction after his conversion, but was directly led by God.", "1:21–24": "Paul describes his limited contact with the apostles in Jerusalem, visiting only Peter and James, the brother of Jesus. Despite his former life as a persecutor, the believers in Judea heard of his transformation and praised God for the work He had done in Paul’s life.", "1:1–2": "Paul opens by insisting his apostleship came directly from Jesus Christ and God the Father, not from any group of people, and greets the churches of Galatia.", "1:6–7": "Paul says he's shocked at how quickly they're turning away from the true gospel toward a distorted version being pushed by certain troublemakers.", "1:10": "Paul asks whether he's trying to win over people or God, saying that if he were still trying to please people, he wouldn't be serving Christ.", "1:13–14": "Paul describes how, before his conversion, he violently persecuted the church and was far more zealous for Jewish tradition than most people his age.", "1:15–16": "Paul says God, who had set him apart even before birth, chose to reveal His Son to him so he could preach about Jesus to the Gentiles, and he didn't immediately consult anyone about it.", "1:18–20": "Three years after his conversion, Paul went to Jerusalem to meet Peter and stayed fifteen days, seeing only James among the other apostles, and insists he's telling the truth."}, "Galatians 2": {"2:3–5": "Paul recounts a trip to Jerusalem where he communicated the gospel he preached among the Gentiles to the leaders of the church. Despite pressure from false brethren, Titus, a Greek, was not compelled to be circumcised. Paul stood firm in the freedom they had in Christ, refusing to submit to the legalistic demands of these false teachers.", "2:9–10": "Paul explains that the apostles in Jerusalem, including James, Peter (Cephas), and John, recognized the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor given to him to preach to the Gentiles. They extended fellowship to him and Barnabas encouraging them to remember the poor.", "2:14": "Paul recounts an incident where he confronted Peter publicly for withdrawing from the Gentiles out of fear of the circumcision party. Paul accused Peter of hypocrisy, as Peter had previously eaten with Gentiles but now was acting as though Gentiles should follow Jewish customs, which contradicted the gospel of freedom in Christ.", "2:21": "Paul explains that justification comes through faith in Jesus Christ, not through the works of the law. He emphasizes that even though Jews have the law, they are not justified by it but by faith in Christ. He goes on to express that through faith, he is crucified with Christ, and Christ lives in him. If righteousness could be obtained through the law, then Christ's death would be in vain, but it is through God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor that believers are justified.", "2:1–2": "Fourteen years later, Paul went to Jerusalem again with Barnabas and Titus, privately explaining the gospel he preached to make sure his ministry wasn't running in vain.", "2:6": "Paul says the leaders in Jerusalem, whatever their status, didn't add anything to what he already preached, since God doesn't judge by outward appearance.", "2:7–8": "The Jerusalem leaders recognized that Paul had been given the task of preaching to the Gentiles, just as Peter had been given the task of preaching to the Jews.", "2:11–13": "Paul describes confronting Peter publicly at Antioch because Peter had stopped eating with Gentile believers out of fear of certain Jewish visitors, and even Barnabas got swept into the same hypocrisy.", "2:15–16": "Paul explains that even Jewish believers know a person isn't made right with God by keeping the law, but through faith in Jesus Christ — no one is justified by the works of the law.", "2:17–18": "Paul answers an objection, saying that seeking to be made right in Christ doesn't make Christ a promoter of sin — rebuilding what he once tore down would actually make him the lawbreaker.", "2:19–20": "Paul says he died to the law through the law itself, so he could live for God — he has been crucified with Christ, and it's now Christ living in him, by faith in the Son of God who loved him."}, "Galatians 3": {"3:1–5": "Paul rebukes the Galatians for being misled, reminding them that they received the Holy Spirit by faith, not by observing the law. He asks whether their spiritual growth and the miracles they experienced came from their efforts under the law or through faith in Christ.", "3:6–9": "Paul uses the example of Abraham to show that righteousness comes by faith. Abraham believed God, and it was credited to him as righteousness. Therefore, those who live by faith are the true children of Abraham and share in the blessing promised to him.", "3:10–14": "Paul explains that those who rely on the works of the law are under a curse because no one can perfectly keep the law. However, Christ redeemed believers from the curse by becoming a curse for them. Through faith in Christ, both Jews and Gentiles receive the blessing of Abraham and the promise of the Holy Spirit.", "3:15–18": "Paul argues that the law, given 430 years after God’s promise to Abraham, does not cancel out the promise. The inheritance of righteousness comes by faith, not by the law. The promise made to Abraham and his seed (Christ) stands as the means by which all nations are blessed.", "3:19–22": "Paul explains that the law was added to expose sin and to lead people to the promise of Christ. The law could not give life or righteousness, but it pointed to the need for faith in Christ to receive the promised salvation.", "3:26–29": "Paul declares that the law served as a guide leading to Christ, but now that faith has come, believers are no longer under the law. Through faith in Christ, all believers, regardless of their background or status, are united as one in Christ and heirs of the promises made to Abraham.", "3:23–25": "Paul explains that before faith in Christ came, the law acted like a guardian keeping people in check until Christ appeared — but now that faith has come, that guardian role is no longer needed."}, "Galatians 4": {"4:4–7": "Paul explains that while under the law, people were like children under guardians. But when the time came, God sent His Son to redeem those under the law, adopting them as His children. Because believers are sons and daughters of God, they are no longer slaves but heirs through Christ, with the Spirit of his son in their hearts crying out, Abba, Father.", "4:8–11": "Paul expresses concern that the Galatians, after coming to know God, are returning to old religious practices and observing special days, months, and seasons, which can lead them back into bondage. He fears that his labor among them might be in vain if they revert to legalism.", "4:12–16": "Paul reminds the Galatians of their past relationship, recalling how they received him with great love and care, despite him being physically weak. He questions why they have turned against him now that he speaks the truth, urging them to return to their former attitude of love and faithfulness.", "4:17–20": "Paul warns the Galatians about false teachers who are zealously trying to win them over, not for their good, but to alienate them from the true gospel. Paul compares his care for them to that of a mother laboring in childbirth until Christ is fully formed in them, expressing his desire to be with them in person to guide them.", "4:21–27": "Paul uses the story of Hagar and Sarah to illustrate two covenants. Hagar, the slave woman, represents the old covenant of the law given at Mount Sinai, which leads to bondage. Sarah, the free woman, represents the new covenant of freedom and promise through faith in Christ. The heavenly Jerusalem, the city of freedom, is the mother of all believers.", "4:28–31": "Paul concludes the allegory by reminding the Galatians that they, like Isaac, are children of the promise, born of the Spirit. Just as Isaac was persecuted by Ishmael, so too will believers face persecution from those under the law. Yet, they are called to live as free people, not under the bondage of the law.", "4:1–3": "Paul compares believers under the old system to a child heir who, though he owns everything, is treated like a slave under guardians until he comes of age."}, "Galatians 5": {"5:5–6": "Paul urges the Galatians to stand firm in the freedom Christ has given them and not to submit again to the legalism of the law. He warns them that if they seek justification through circumcision, they are obligated to follow the whole law and have fallen from God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor. True righteousness comes by faith, working through love.", "5:11–12": "Paul expresses concern over those who are persuading the Galatians to turn from the truth, reminding them that a little false teaching can spread and corrupt everything. He expresses confidence that they will return to the right path and warns that those leading them astray will face judgment.", "5:13–15": "Paul reminds the Galatians that they were called to freedom, but this freedom should not be used to indulge the flesh. Instead, it should be used to serve one another in love. The entire law is fulfilled in the command to love your neighbor as yourself, and they are warned not to destroy one another through division and conflict.", "5:19–21": "Paul exhorts believers to walk in the Spirit, which opposes the desires of the flesh. If they follow the Spirit, they will not be under the law. He warns against the works of the flesh, which include sins such as immorality, idolatry, and strife, warning that those who live in such a way will not inherit the kingdom of God.", "5:25–26": "Paul contrasts the works of the flesh with the fruit of the Spirit, which includes love, joy, peace, and other virtues. Those who belong to Christ have crucified their sinful nature. Paul encourages believers to walk in the Spirit, avoiding pride, provocation, and envy.", "5:1": "Paul urges the Galatians to stand firm in the freedom Christ gave them, and not to let themselves be pulled back into slavery to the law.", "5:2–4": "Paul warns that if they rely on circumcision to be right with God, Christ becomes useless to them, and they've fallen away from grace by trying to be justified through the law.", "5:7–10": "Paul asks who distracted them from following the truth, comparing false teaching to a little yeast that spreads through the whole batch of dough, but says he trusts they'll come back around.", "5:16–18": "Paul tells them to walk by the Spirit instead of giving in to the flesh, explaining that the flesh and the Spirit are constantly opposed to each other, and that being led by the Spirit means no longer being under the law.", "5:22–24": "Paul lists the fruit the Spirit produces — love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control — and says believers have crucified their sinful desires along with the flesh."}, "Galatians 6": {"6:4–5": "Paul encourages believers to gently restore those who have fallen into sin, with humility and a spirit of meekness, knowing that they too could be tempted. He calls on them to bear each other's burdens, fulfilling the law of Christ, and to avoid self-deception by thinking too highly of themselves.", "6:9–10": "Paul warns that God cannot be mocked, and everyone who lives for the flesh will reap corruption, but those who sow to the Spirit will reap eternal life. He encourages believers to persevere in doing good and not to grow weary, reminding them that in due time, they will reap a reward if they remain faithful.", "6:14–16": "Paul concludes by reminding the Galatians that some are trying to force circumcision on them to avoid persecution. However, Paul boasts only in the cross of Christ, declaring that circumcision and uncircumcision are meaningless compared to becoming a new creation in Christ. He prays for peace and mercy on those who follow this truth.", "6:17–18": "Paul closes his letter by affirming the marks he bears for Christ, signifying his suffering for the gospel. He ends with a blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor from the Lord Jesus Christ, wishing that it be with their spirit.", "6:1–3": "Paul instructs believers to gently restore someone caught in sin, watching themselves so they aren't tempted too, and to carry each other's burdens instead of thinking too highly of themselves.", "6:6": "Paul tells anyone being taught God's word to share good things with the person who teaches them.", "6:7–8": "Paul warns that God isn't mocked — whatever someone plants, they will harvest, whether decay from following the flesh or eternal life from following the Spirit.", "6:11–13": "Paul points out he's now writing in large letters with his own hand, and exposes that those pushing circumcision on the Galatians are only doing it to avoid persecution and to boast, since they don't even keep the law themselves."}, "Ephesians 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul opens his letter by greeting the believers in Ephesus, blessing them with unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace from God and Jesus Christ.", "1:11–14": "Paul praises God for the spiritual blessings believers have received in Christ. Before the foundation of the world, God chose believers to be holy and blameless and without fault in his sight. Through Christ’s blood, they have redemption and forgiveness, all according to the riches of God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor. God’s ultimate plan is to bring everything together under Christ. Paul explains that after believers heard the gospel and trusted in Christ, they were sealed with the Holy Spirit. The Spirit is the guarantee of their inheritance, ensuring that they will receive the fullness of their redemption when Christ returns.", "1:20–23": "Paul prays that the believers would receive wisdom and revelation to truly know Christ, and that their hearts would be enlightened to understand the hope of their calling, the riches of their inheritance, and the greatness of God’s power, which raised Christ from the dead and seated Him far above all powers and authorities. Christ is the head of the church, which is His body, the fullness of Him who fills everything.", "1:3–6": "Paul praises God for blessing believers with every spiritual blessing in Christ, choosing them before the world began, and deciding beforehand to adopt them as His own children.", "1:7–10": "Paul explains that through Christ's blood believers are redeemed and forgiven, and that God has revealed His plan to eventually bring everything in heaven and earth together under Christ.", "1:15–19": "Paul says he constantly thanks God for their faith and prays that they would receive wisdom to understand the hope, the riches, and the great power available to those who believe."}, "Ephesians 2": {"2:4–7": "Paul explains that though believers were once dead in sin, God, in His mercy and great love, made them alive together with Christ. They are saved by unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, raised up with Christ, and seated with Him in the heavenly places. This is so that, in the coming ages, God might display the surpassing riches of His favor and kindness.", "2:8–10": "Salvation is a gift from God, not something that can be earned by works. Believers are saved by unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor through faith, and they are God’s handiwork, created in Christ to do good works, which God prepared for them to live out.", "2:11–13": "Paul reminds the Gentile believers that they were once separated from Christ and alienated from the people of Israel, without hope and without God. But now, through Christ’s blood, they have been brought near to God and included in His promises.", "2:17–19": "Christ is the believer’s peace, having destroyed the division between Jew and Gentile by His death. He created one new humanity, reconciling both groups to God through the cross. Now, through Christ, all believers—Jew and Gentile—have access to the Father through the Holy Spirit.", "2:20–22": "Paul concludes by affirming that Gentile believers are no longer outsiders, but are fellow citizens with God’s people and members of His household. They are part of a spiritual temple, built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, with Christ as the cornerstone, where God’s Spirit dwells.", "2:1–3": "Paul reminds them that they were once spiritually dead in their sins, living under the influence of the world and its ruler, following their own desires just like everyone else.", "2:14–16": "Paul explains that Christ made peace between Jews and Gentiles by breaking down the wall of hostility between them, creating one new people and reconciling both groups to God through the cross."}, "Ephesians 3": {"3:5–7": "Paul explains that by God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, the mystery of Christ was revealed to him: that the Gentiles are fellow heirs and partakers in the promises of Christ. This mystery, hidden for ages, is now made known by the Spirit to God’s apostles and prophets.", "3:10–12": "Paul acknowledges that, although he sees himself as the least of all saints, God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor empowered him to preach the riches of Christ to the Gentiles. His ministry makes known the mystery of Christ and God’s eternal purpose, revealing God’s wisdom to all, including spiritual authorities in the heavenly realms. Through Christ, believers have boldness and access to God with confidence through faith.", "3:17–19": "Paul prays that the believers in Ephesus would be strengthened by the Holy Spirit, so that Christ may dwell in their hearts through faith. He desires that they be rooted in love and fully comprehend the vastness of Christ’s love, which surpasses knowledge, and that they be filled with the fullness of God.", "3:20–21": "Paul concludes with a powerful doxology, praising God for His ability to do far more than anyone could ask or imagine, through His power at work in believers. He gives glory to God in the church through Christ for all eternity.", "3:1–4": "Paul explains that he is a prisoner for the sake of the Gentiles, and that God revealed to him a mystery about Christ that he had briefly written about earlier.", "3:8–9": "Paul, calling himself the least of all believers, says he was given grace to preach the unsearchable riches of Christ and to explain a mystery that had been hidden in God since the beginning.", "3:13": "Paul asks them not to be discouraged over his suffering on their behalf, since it's actually for their glory.", "3:14–16": "Paul describes kneeling before the Father and praying that they would be strengthened with power through His Spirit in their inner being."}, "Ephesians 4": {"4:4–6": "Paul urges believers to live in a manner worthy of their calling, showing humility, gentleness, patience, and love. He emphasizes the unity of the church, reminding them that there is one body, one Spirit, one hope, one Lord, one faith, and one baptism.", "4:11–13": "Paul explains that each believer has been given unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor in the form of spiritual gifts according to Christ’s will. Christ, who ascended to heaven after descending to the earth, distributed gifts to build up the body of Christ. These gifts are for the equipping of the saints, the work of ministry, and the edification of the church, all leading to unity and maturity in Christ.", "4:14–16": "Paul encourages the church to grow in spiritual maturity, no longer being like children who are easily deceived. Instead, they are to speak the truth in love and grow into Christ, who is the head of the body. Every part of the body works together to build itself up in love, contributing to the growth and health of the church.", "4:20–24": "Paul calls the believers to live differently from the Gentiles, who walk in darkness and alienation from God. He urges them to put off their old way of life, which is corrupt and driven by deceitful desires, and to be renewed in their minds. They are to put on the new self, created to be like God in true righteousness and holiness.", "4:30–32": "Paul gives practical instructions for Christian living. Believers are to speak truthfully, control their anger, avoid giving the devil a foothold, work honestly, and use their words to build up others. They are also called to kindness, forgiveness, and to avoid bitterness and anger, following the example of God’s forgiveness through Christ.", "4:1–3": "Paul urges them to live in a way worthy of their calling — humble, gentle, patient, and making every effort to keep the unity the Spirit gives, bound together by peace.", "4:7–8": "Paul says each believer was given grace according to what Christ decided, quoting Scripture about Christ ascending and giving gifts to people.", "4:9–10": "Paul explains that the same One who ascended also first descended to the earth, so that He might fill everything.", "4:17–19": "Paul tells them not to live the way unbelievers do, whose thinking is empty and whose hearts have grown hard, leading them into every kind of impurity.", "4:25–27": "Paul instructs them to stop lying and speak truthfully to each other, since they belong to one body, and to deal with anger before the day ends so it doesn't give the devil an opening.", "4:28–29": "Paul tells a former thief to stop stealing and instead work honestly so he has something to share, and tells them to speak only words that build others up."}, "Ephesians 5": {"5:1–2": "Paul encourages believers to imitate God by walking in love, following the example of Christ, who offered Himself as a sacrifice to God on behalf of humanity.", "5:5–7": "Paul warns against all forms of sexual immorality, impurity, and greed, urging believers to avoid even speaking of such things. These behaviors have no place among God’s people, and those who practice them will not inherit the kingdom of God.", "5:11–14": "Paul reminds the believers that they are now light in the Lord and should walk as children of light, producing the fruit of goodness, righteousness, and truth. They are to expose and reject the deeds of darkness, for light reveals all things.", "5:18–21": "Paul encourages the believers to walk wisely, making the most of their time because the days are evil. Instead of being controlled by wine, they are to be filled with the Spirit, worshiping together with songs and hymns, giving thanks to God, and submitting to one another out of reverence for Christ.", "5:31–33": "Paul instructs husbands and wives in how to relate to one another. Wives are to submit to their husbands as the church submits to Christ. Husbands are called to love their wives as Christ loved the church, sacrificially giving Himself for her. This mutual love and respect reflect the relationship between Christ and the church.", "5:3–4": "Paul says sexual immorality, impurity, and greed shouldn't even be mentioned among them, nor crude joking, but thanksgiving instead.", "5:8–10": "Paul reminds them they were once darkness but are now light in the Lord, and tells them to live as children of light, finding out what pleases Him.", "5:15–17": "Paul tells them to live carefully and wisely, making the most of their time since the days are evil, and to understand what the Lord wants.", "5:22–24": "Paul instructs wives to submit to their husbands as to the Lord, comparing the husband's role to Christ's headship over the church.", "5:25–27": "Paul instructs husbands to love their wives the way Christ loved the church, giving Himself up to make her holy and without blemish.", "5:28–30": "Paul says husbands should love their wives as they love their own bodies, since no one hates their own flesh, just as Christ nourishes and cares for the church, which is His body."}, "Ephesians 6": {"6:1–4": "Paul instructs children to obey and honor their parents as part of God’s design for the family, promising a blessing for doing so. He also advises fathers not to provoke their children but to raise them in the discipline and instruction of the Lord.", "6:9": "Paul speaks to servants, encouraging them to obey their earthly masters as they would obey Christ, with sincerity and from the heart, knowing that the Lord will reward their faithfulness. He also instructs masters to treat their servants kindly, knowing that they too have a Master in heaven.", "6:18–20": "Paul urges believers to be strong in the Lord and to put on the full armor of God in order to stand against the schemes of the devil. The armor includes truth, righteousness, the gospel of peace, faith, salvation, and the word of God. He emphasizes the importance of continual prayer and vigilance in spiritual warfare. Paul asks the believers to pray for him, that he might boldly proclaim the gospel, even while he is in chains for Christ.", "6:21–24": "Paul closes his letter by sending Tychicus to update the believers on his situation and to encourage their hearts. He ends with a blessing of peace, love, faith, and unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor to all who love Christ with sincerity.", "6:5–8": "Paul instructs servants to obey their earthly masters wholeheartedly, as if serving Christ Himself, knowing that God will reward whatever good they do, whether slave or free.", "6:10–13": "Paul tells them to be strong in the Lord and put on God's full armor, since their fight isn't against people but against spiritual powers and evil forces in the heavenly realms.", "6:14–17": "Paul describes the armor of God piece by piece — truth like a belt, righteousness like a breastplate, readiness from the gospel like shoes, faith like a shield, salvation like a helmet, and God's word like a sword."}, "Philippians 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul, along with Timothy, greets the saints at Philippi, extending unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.", "1:7–8": "Paul gives thanks to God for the Philippians every time he remembers them. He expresses his joy in their partnership in the gospel and his confidence that God, who began a good work in them, will complete it until the day of Christ Jesus.", "1:9–11": "Paul prays that the Philippians’ love would continue to grow in knowledge and discernment, so they may choose what is excellent and be pure and blameless until the day of Christ. He desires that they be filled with the fruit of righteousness that comes through Jesus Christ, bringing glory to God.", "1:12–14": "Paul explains that his imprisonment has actually served to advance the gospel. His chains have become known throughout the palace and beyond, and other believers have gained courage to speak the word of God more boldly and without fear.", "1:15–18": "Paul acknowledges that some preach Christ out of envy and rivalry, while others do so out of goodwill and love. Yet, Paul rejoices regardless, because Christ is being preached, whether with good motives or not.", "1:19–20": "Paul expresses his confidence that his situation will lead to his deliverance through the prayers of the Philippians and the help of the Holy Spirit. His hope is that Christ will be exalted in his body, whether by life or by death.", "1:25–26": "Paul shares his inner struggle between wanting to depart and be with Christ, which is far better, and remaining in the flesh, which is necessary for the sake of the believers. He is confident that his continued presence will help their progress and joy in the faith.", "1:27–30": "Paul exhorts the Philippians to live in a manner worthy of the gospel of Christ, standing firm in unity and not being afraid of their opponents. He reminds them that suffering for Christ is a part of their calling, just as Paul himself has experienced suffering for the gospel.", "1:3–6": "Paul thanks God every time he thinks of the Philippians and prays joyfully for them, confident that the good work God started in them will be completed.", "1:21–24": "Paul says that for him, living means serving Christ and dying means gain, and admits he's torn between wanting to be with Christ and needing to stay for their sake."}, "Philippians 2": {"2:3–4": "Paul encourages the believers to be united in love and humility, putting the needs of others above their own. He asks them to have one mind and spirit, considering others more significant than themselves, and not seeking their own interests.", "2:9–11": "Paul presents Christ as the ultimate example of humility and obedience. Though He was equal with God, He humbled Himself by becoming a man and dying on the cross. Because of this, God exalted Him above all, and one day every knee will bow and every tongue will confess that Jesus is Lord.", "2:14–16": "Paul exhorts the believers to live out their salvation with reverence, knowing that God is working in them. He urges them to do everything without complaining or arguing so that they may be pure and blameless, shining as lights in a dark world.", "2:17–18": "Paul expresses his willingness to be poured out like a drink offering, even if it means suffering for the sake of their faith. He rejoices in this and invites the Philippians to share in his joy, even amid suffering.", "2:19–24": "Paul speaks highly of Timothy, whom he hopes to send to the Philippians soon. He praises Timothy’s genuine concern for their well-being and his faithfulness in serving the gospel like a son with his father.", "2:25–30": "Paul commends Epaphroditus, who nearly died for the work of Christ while ministering to Paul’s needs. He urges the Philippians to receive Epaphroditus with joy and honor him for his faithful service, as he risked his life for the sake of the gospel.", "2:1–2": "Paul appeals to whatever encouragement and comfort they have in Christ, asking them to be united in mind and love, working together with one purpose.", "2:5–8": "Paul tells them to have the same attitude as Christ, who didn't cling to His equality with God but emptied Himself, became a servant, and humbled Himself even to death on a cross.", "2:12–13": "Paul tells them to continue working out their salvation with reverence, since it's God who's at work in them, giving them both the desire and the ability to do what pleases Him."}, "Philippians 3": {"3:2–3": "Paul begins by urging the Philippians to rejoice in the Lord and warns them against false teachers who promote confidence in external religious practices rather than faith in Christ. True believers, he says, worship by the Spirit and place their confidence in Christ, not in outward rituals.", "3:7–8": "Paul lists his own reasons for confidence in the flesh—his Jewish heritage, strict observance of the law, and zeal as a Pharisee. Yet, he counts all of this as loss compared to the surpassing worth of knowing Christ. He gladly gives up all worldly gains to win Christ.", "3:10–11": "Paul desires to be found in Christ, not with a righteousness based on the law, but a righteousness that comes through faith in Christ. His deepest longing is to know Christ, experience the power of His resurrection, share in His sufferings, and be conformed to His death, with the hope of the resurrection from the dead.", "3:13–14": "Paul admits that he has not yet fully attained spiritual perfection, but he is determined to press forward, forgetting the past and striving for the prize of God’s high calling in Christ Jesus.", "3:15–16": "Paul encourages mature believers to adopt the same mindset of pressing forward in faith. He assures them that if they have any other perspective, God will reveal the truth to them in due time. In the meantime, they should continue living according to what they have already attained.", "3:17–19": "Paul warns the Philippians about those who live as enemies of the cross of Christ. These individuals focus on worldly pleasures, satisfying their own desires, and find glory in shameful things. Their destiny, Paul says, is destruction.", "3:20–21": "Paul contrasts the focus of believers with that of the enemies of the cross. While they focus on earthly things, believers are citizens of heaven, eagerly awaiting the return of the Lord Jesus Christ, who will transform their lowly bodies to be like His glorious body.", "3:1": "Paul tells them to rejoice in the Lord, saying that repeating what he's already told them is no burden and helps keep them grounded.", "3:4–6": "Paul lists his own impressive religious credentials — circumcised properly, from the tribe of Benjamin, a zealous Pharisee who kept the law faultlessly by outward standards.", "3:9": "Paul says his goal is to be found in Christ, not with a righteousness earned by keeping the law, but the righteousness that comes from God through faith.", "3:12": "Paul admits he hasn't already achieved this or become perfect, but he keeps pressing forward to take hold of what Christ took hold of him for."}, "Philippians 4": {"4:2–3": "Paul calls for unity and reconciliation between two women, Euodias and Syntyche, urging them to be of the same mind in the Lord. He also asks a fellow believer to help them, along with other co-laborers in the gospel.", "4:6–7": "Paul encourages the Philippians to rejoice in the Lord always and to let their gentleness be evident to all. He advises them not to worry but to pray with thanksgiving, presenting their requests to God, who will give them peace beyond understanding, guarding their hearts and minds in Christ Jesus.", "4:9": "Paul encourages the believers to focus their minds on whatever is true, noble, right, pure, lovely, and admirable. He calls them to put into practice what they have learned from him, assuring them that the God of peace will be with them.", "4:13": "Paul expresses his gratitude for the Philippians' renewed concern for him but clarifies that he has learned to be content in all circumstances, whether in need or in plenty. He shares the secret to his contentment: his strength comes from Christ, who enables him to endure all things.", "4:19–20": "Paul acknowledges the Philippians' generosity in supporting him, even when no other church did. He reassures them that their gifts are pleasing to God and that God will meet all their needs according to His glorious riches in Christ Jesus.", "4:21–23": "Paul closes with greetings from the believers with him, including those of Caesar’s household. He ends with a blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor from the Lord Jesus Christ.", "4:1": "Paul calls the Philippians his joy and crown, and tells them, since he loves and misses them, to stand firm in the Lord.", "4:4–5": "Paul tells them to rejoice in the Lord always and to let their gentleness be evident to everyone, reminding them that the Lord is near.", "4:8": "Paul tells them to fill their minds with whatever is true, honorable, right, pure, lovely, and admirable.", "4:10": "Paul expresses joy that their concern for him has been renewed, acknowledging they had cared all along but hadn't had the opportunity to show it.", "4:11–12": "Paul says he's learned to be content whatever his circumstances, whether he has plenty or little, having learned the secret of being satisfied in any situation.", "4:14–16": "Paul thanks them for sharing in his hardships, recalling how they were the only church that supported him financially in the early days, even sending help more than once while he was in Thessalonica.", "4:17–18": "Paul says he's not chasing after the gift itself but the blessing it brings to their account, and that he now has everything he needs through what Epaphroditus brought him."}, "Colossians 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul, writing to the believers at Colosse, begins his letter with a greeting, extending unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace from God and Jesus Christ.", "1:3–8": "Paul expresses gratitude for the Colossians’ faith, love, and hope, which they received through the truth of the gospel. He praises their growth in spiritual fruit and acknowledges the faithful ministry of Epaphras, who shared the good news with them.", "1:9–12": "Paul prays that the believers in Colosse would grow in spiritual wisdom and understanding, so they can live lives worthy of the Lord, bearing fruit and growing in their knowledge of God. He also prays for their strength, patience, and joyful endurance, giving thanks to God for allowing them to share in the inheritance of the saints.", "1:15–20": "Paul proclaims the supremacy of Christ, who is the image of the invisible God and the firstborn over all creation. Through Christ, all things were created, and He holds everything together. He is the head of the church, and through His blood, He reconciled all things to Himself, bringing peace through His sacrifice on the cross.", "1:21–23": "Paul reminds the Colossians that, although they were once alienated from God because of their sinful behavior, they have been reconciled through Christ’s death, and are now presented as holy and blameless in His sight. He encourages them to remain firm in their faith and not to be swayed from the hope of the gospel.", "1:28–29": "Paul speaks of his joy in suffering for the sake of the church and his role as a minister to proclaim the mystery of Christ, now revealed to the saints. This mystery is Christ in you, the hope of glory. Paul labors and strives with God’s power working in him to present everyone fully mature in Christ.", "1:13–14": "Paul says God rescued believers from the power of darkness and brought them into the kingdom of His beloved Son, where they have redemption and forgiveness through His blood.", "1:24–27": "Paul says he rejoices in his sufferings for the church, and explains his calling to make known a mystery that was hidden for ages but is now revealed — Christ living in believers, the hope of glory."}, "Colossians 2": {"2:1–3": "Paul expresses his deep concern for the Colossians and other believers who have not met him personally. He desires that their hearts be encouraged and united in love, so they may fully understand the mystery of God in Christ, in whom all wisdom and knowledge are hidden.", "2:4–7": "Paul warns the Colossians not to be deceived by persuasive arguments or empty words. Though he is not physically with them, he rejoices in their good discipline and strong faith in Christ.", "2:8–10": "Paul encourages the believers to walk in Christ, being rooted and built up in Him, overflowing with gratitude. He warns them against being taken captive by deceptive philosophies and human traditions, reminding them that all the fullness of God dwells in Christ and that they are complete in Him, who is the head over all rulers and authorities.", "2:11–15": "Paul explains that believers have received a spiritual circumcision in Christ, putting off their sinful nature. Through baptism, they are buried with Christ and raised with Him by faith in the power of God, who raised Jesus from the dead. Paul reminds the Colossians that they were once dead in their sins, but God made them alive with Christ, forgiving all their sins. Christ has canceled the record of debt that stood against them, nailing it to the cross. He has also triumphed over the powers and authorities, making a public display of His victory over them.", "2:16–19": "Paul instructs the Colossians not to let anyone judge them by food, drink, religious festivals, or Sabbath days, as these are only a shadow of things to come. He warns against those who promote false humility and angel worship, encouraging them instead to hold fast to Christ, the Head of the body, from whom all spiritual nourishment flows.", "2:20–23": "Paul reminds the Colossians that since they died with Christ, they are no longer bound by the worldly rules and regulations imposed by men. He warns against following human commands and traditions, which may appear wise but have no true value in restraining sinful desires."}, "Colossians 3": {"3:1–4": "Paul encourages the believers to focus their hearts and minds on heavenly things, because their lives are now hidden with Christ. When Christ returns, they will share in His glory.", "3:8–11": "Paul urges believers to put to death their earthly nature—sexual immorality, impurity, evil desires, and greed. These are the things that once characterized their old lives but are no longer fitting for those who are in Christ. They are now called to put on the new man that is being renewed in the image of God.", "3:15–17": "Paul exhorts the believers to clothe themselves with compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, patience, and forgiveness, just as Christ forgave them. Above all, they are to put on love, which binds everything together. They should let the peace of Christ rule in their hearts and let His word dwell richly in them, expressing their faith through song and thanksgiving.", "3:22–25": "Paul provides practical instructions for Christian households, calling wives to submit to their husbands, husbands to love their wives, children to obey their parents, and fathers not to provoke their children. Servants are to serve their masters wholeheartedly, knowing that their ultimate reward comes from the Lord, for they serve Christ.", "3:5–7": "Paul tells them to put to death sinful earthly desires like sexual immorality and greed, which he calls a form of idolatry, reminding them this was how they used to live.", "3:12–14": "Paul tells them, as God's chosen people, to clothe themselves with compassion, kindness, humility, and patience, forgiving each other as Christ forgave them, with love holding everything together.", "3:18–21": "Paul gives instructions for the household — wives submitting to husbands, husbands loving their wives without bitterness, children obeying parents, and fathers not discouraging their children."}, "Colossians 4": {"4:1": "Paul instructs masters to treat their servants with fairness and justice, reminding them that they too have a Master in heaven.", "4:2–4": "Paul urges the believers to be devoted to prayer, being watchful and thankful. He also asks them to pray for him, that God would open doors for him to proclaim the mystery of Christ clearly, even as he is in chains.", "4:5–6": "Paul encourages the believers to be wise in their interactions with outsiders, making the most of every opportunity. He advises them to speak with unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and with wisdom, so they may know how to respond to everyone.", "4:7–9": "Paul sends Tychicus, a beloved brother and faithful minister, to update the Colossians on his circumstances and to encourage their hearts. He is accompanied by Onesimus, a fellow believer who will also share news from Paul.", "4:15–17": "Paul sends greetings from his fellow workers, including Aristarchus, Mark, Justus, Epaphras, Luke, and Demas. He highlights Epaphras’ fervent prayers for the Colossians and others in the region. He also sends greetings to the believers in Laodicea and to Nymphas, who hosts a church in his home.", "4:18": "Paul instructs the Colossians to share his letter with the church in Laodicea and to read their letter as well. He specifically encourages Archippus to faithfully carry out the ministry he received from the Lord. Paul closes with a personal greeting and a final blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, reminding the believers to remember his imprisonment.", "4:10–11": "Paul sends greetings from his fellow workers Aristarchus, Mark, and Jesus called Justus, noting these Jewish believers had been a comfort to him.", "4:12–14": "Paul sends greetings from Epaphras, who prays hard for them, and from Luke the beloved doctor and Demas."}, "Titus 1": {"1:1–4": "Paul introduces himself as a servant of God and an apostle of Jesus Christ, sent to further the faith of God’s elect and the knowledge of the truth that leads to godliness. He expresses hope in the eternal life promised by God before time began and extends blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, mercy, and peace to Titus, his spiritual son in the faith.", "1:9": "Paul instructs Titus to appoint elders in every town in Crete, providing specific qualifications for their selection. Elders must be blameless, faithful in their marriages, and have children who are well-behaved. They must not be arrogant, quick-tempered, or greedy but instead hospitable, self-controlled, upright, and committed to sound doctrine so they can encourage others and refute those who oppose the truth.", "1:16": "Paul warns Titus about false teachers, especially those from the circumcision group who are deceiving others for personal gain. He instructs Titus to rebuke them sharply so they may become sound in the faith, warning against paying attention to myths and human commands that distort the truth. Paul emphasizes that while everything is pure to those who are pure, to those corrupted by unbelief, nothing is pure, and their actions reveal their rejection of God despite their claims to know Him.", "1:5": "Paul explains he left Titus in Crete to finish organizing things and appoint elders in every town, just as instructed.", "1:6": "Paul lists that an elder must have a good reputation, be faithful to one wife, and have children who believe and aren't known for wild or disobedient behavior.", "1:7": "Paul says an overseer, since he manages God's household, must not be arrogant, quick-tempered, a heavy drinker, violent, or greedy for dishonest gain.", "1:8": "Instead, Paul says the overseer should be hospitable, love what is good, be sensible, fair, holy, and disciplined.", "1:10–11": "Paul warns about many rebellious people, especially from the Jewish converts, who deceive others and disrupt whole households by teaching wrong things for dishonest profit.", "1:12–14": "Paul quotes a Cretan's own prophet, who said Cretans are liars, savage, and lazy, and tells Titus to correct them firmly so they turn away from myths and human rules and become sound in faith.", "1:15": "Paul says that to those who are pure, everything is pure, but to those who are corrupt and unbelieving, nothing is pure, since even their minds and consciences are corrupted."}, "Titus 2": {"2:1–2": "Paul instructs Titus to teach what is in line with sound doctrine, urging older men to be self-controlled, worthy of respect, temperate, and sound in faith, love, and endurance.", "2:3–5": "Paul urges older women to live in a way that honors God, to avoid slander and excessive drinking, and to teach younger women to love their families, be self-controlled, pure, and good managers of their homes, so that God’s word will not be dishonored.", "2:7–8": "Titus is encouraged to urge young men to be self-controlled and to set an example by doing good works, showing integrity, seriousness, and soundness in teaching. He is to speak in such a way that no one can criticize him, so that opponents will be ashamed, having nothing bad to say about him.", "2:9–10": "Paul instructs Titus to teach servants to be subject to their masters in everything, to please them and not talk back or steal from them, but to show they can be fully trusted. This behavior will make the teaching about God our Savior attractive.", "2:13–14": "Paul explains that the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God has appeared, offering salvation to all people. This favor teaches believers to say no to ungodliness and worldly passions, and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives while waiting for the blessed hope—the glorious return of Jesus Christ. Christ gave Himself to redeem us from all wickedness and to purify all people.", "2:15": "Paul concludes by telling Titus to teach these things, encourage, and rebuke with all authority. He instructs Titus not to let anyone disregard his ministry.", "2:6": "Paul tells Titus to likewise encourage young men to be self-controlled.", "2:11–12": "Paul says God's grace has appeared, bringing salvation to everyone, and teaches believers to say no to ungodliness and worldly desires, living self-controlled, upright, and godly lives now."}, "Titus 3": {"3:3": "Paul reminds Titus to encourage believers to be submissive to authorities, gentle, and ready for good works, remembering that all were once disobedient and lived in sin before experiencing God’s mercy.", "3:4–7": "Paul emphasizes that God’s mercy, not human effort, brought salvation. Through the renewal of the Holy Spirit, poured out richly in Christ, believers are justified by unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and receive the hope of eternal life as heirs of God.", "3:9": "Paul instructs Titus to encourage believers to maintain good works while avoiding pointless arguments and disputes over the law, as they are unprofitable.", "3:10–11": "Paul advises that anyone who is divisive and refuses correction should be avoided after multiple warnings, recognizing that such a person is self-condemned.", "3:15": "Paul concludes with personal instructions for Titus and sends greetings, reminding believers once again to focus on good works. He closes with a blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor for all.", "3:1–2": "Paul tells Titus to remind believers to submit to rulers and authorities, be ready to do good, avoid slander and quarreling, and be gentle and considerate toward everyone.", "3:8": "Paul calls this a trustworthy saying and wants Titus to stress it so that believers make it their focus to devote themselves to good works, which are excellent and profitable.", "3:12–13": "Paul asks Titus to come meet him at Nicopolis once Artemas or Tychicus arrives to replace him, and to make sure Zenas and Apollos are well provided for on their journey.", "3:14": "Paul says believers should also learn to devote themselves to good works to meet urgent needs, so their lives aren't unproductive."}, "Philemon 1": {"1:1–3": "Paul, imprisoned for the sake of Christ, writes to Philemon, a dear friend and fellow worker in the gospel. He includes greetings to Apphia, Archippus, and the church that meets in Philemon’s home, extending blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace.", "1:7": "Paul expresses his gratitude to God for Philemon, commending him for his love and faith toward Jesus and the saints. He prays that Philemon’s faith will be effective as he continues to recognize the goodness in him through Christ. Paul also acknowledges the comfort and joy he receives from knowing that Philemon has refreshed the hearts of the saints.", "1:13–14": "Paul makes a heartfelt appeal on behalf of Onesimus, who had once been unprofitable but is now useful to both Philemon and Paul. Though Paul could command Philemon to act, he instead appeals out of love, asking Philemon to receive Onesimus, now a beloved brother, as he would receive Paul himself. Paul emphasizes that he is sending Onesimus back, even though he could have kept him to serve while he was in chains.", "1:17": "Paul suggests that perhaps Onesimus’ separation was part of God’s plan so that he could return, not just as a servant, but as a beloved brother in Christ. Paul encourages Philemon to welcome Onesimus as he would welcome Paul himself, emphasizing their newfound relationship in the Lord.", "1:20": "Paul offers to take on any debt that Onesimus owes to Philemon, personally guaranteeing repayment. At the same time, Paul gently reminds Philemon of the spiritual debt Philemon owes to him, hoping that Philemon will refresh his heart by receiving Onesimus with love.", "1:22": "Paul expresses confidence that Philemon will respond with more generosity than what he has asked, and he asks Philemon to prepare a place for him to stay, trusting that through the prayers of the saints, he will soon be released and able to visit.", "1:25": "Paul concludes his letter with greetings from Epaphras, Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, and Luke, who are also fellow workers in Christ. He ends with a blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor for Philemon and the church.", "1:4–5": "Paul thanks God for Philemon every time he prays, having heard about his faith and his love for the Lord and for other believers.", "1:6": "Paul prays that Philemon's generous faith would become effective as he fully understands every good thing he has in Christ.", "1:8–9": "Paul says he could command Philemon as an apostle, but chooses instead to make his request out of love, appealing as an old man and a prisoner.", "1:10–11": "Paul appeals on behalf of Onesimus, whom he calls his son, converted while Paul was in chains — once useless to Philemon, but now genuinely useful to them both.", "1:12": "Paul says he's sending Onesimus back to Philemon, sending along with him his own deep affection.", "1:15–16": "Paul suggests Onesimus may have been separated from Philemon for a time so that Philemon could receive him back permanently, no longer as a slave but as a beloved brother.", "1:18–19": "Paul offers to personally repay anything Onesimus owes Philemon, writing it in his own hand, while also reminding Philemon that he owes Paul his very life.", "1:21": "Paul says he's confident Philemon will do what he's asking, and even more.", "1:23–24": "Paul sends greetings from Epaphras, who is in prison with him, along with Mark, Aristarchus, Demas, and Luke, his fellow workers."}, "1 Corinthians 1": {"1:4–9": "Paul opens his letter with thanksgiving for the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God at work in the Corinthian church. He reminds them of the spiritual gifts they have received and encourages them to remain steadfast until the return of Christ.", "1:10–17": "Paul appeals for unity among the believers in Corinth, rebuking the divisions based on allegiance to different leaders. He emphasizes that their faith should not be centered on human figures but on Christ, who alone was crucified for them.", "1:18–25": "Paul explains that the message of the cross seems foolish to those who are perishing, but to those who are being saved, it is the power of God. God’s wisdom, revealed in the cross, surpasses human understanding, and it confounds both the Jewish and Greek expectations.", "1:26–31": "Paul emphasizes that God has deliberately chosen what the world considers weak and foolish to shame the strong and wise. This ensures that no one can boast before God. Christ is the believer's wisdom, righteousness, sanctification, and redemption—our boasting should only be in Him.", "1:1–3": "Paul greets the church in Corinth, along with Sosthenes, calling them people set apart in Christ, alongside all believers everywhere who call on Jesus."}, "1 Corinthians 2": {"2:1–5": "Paul explains that when he preached to the Corinthians, he did not rely on eloquence or human wisdom. Instead, he focused on the simple yet profound message of Christ crucified, demonstrating the power of the Holy Spirit, so that their faith would rest on God's power and not on human knowledge.", "2:10–13": "Paul contrasts the wisdom of the world with the wisdom of God, which is revealed by the Spirit. Human wisdom could not comprehend God's plan of salvation, but the Holy Spirit reveals to believers the deep things of God, enabling them to understand His hidden wisdom.", "2:14–16": "Paul explains that the natural, unspiritual person cannot accept the truths of God because they are spiritually discerned. Believers, through the Holy Spirit, have the mind of Christ, enabling them to understand spiritual realities that are hidden from those who rely solely on human wisdom.", "2:6–9": "Paul says he does speak wisdom, but not the wisdom of this world — a wisdom hidden by God for those who love Him, beyond anything anyone has seen, heard, or imagined."}, "1 Corinthians 3": {"3:1–4": "Paul addresses the Corinthians' spiritual immaturity, referring to them as babes in Christ who are still living in a worldly manner. Their divisions, based on loyalty to different leaders, reveal their carnality.", "3:5–9": "Paul explains that both he and Apollos are merely servants through whom God works. It is God who gives the increase. Paul planted the seed, Apollos watered it, but only God can cause growth. Believers are God’s field and His building.", "3:10–15": "Paul teaches that Christ is the only foundation for the church, and each person must build upon this foundation with care. The quality of one’s work will be tested by fire, and while some will receive a reward, others will suffer loss, though they themselves will be saved.", "3:16–17": "Paul reminds the Corinthians that they are God’s temple and that His Spirit dwells within them. Defiling the temple of God will lead to destruction, as God’s temple is holy.", "3:18–23": "Paul warns against relying on worldly wisdom, which is foolishness in God’s eyes. True wisdom comes from recognizing our dependence on God, not on human understanding. Paul concludes by reminding the Corinthians not to boast in human leaders. All things belong to them through Christ, and they belong to Christ, who belongs to God."}, "1 Corinthians 4": {"4:1–5": "Paul emphasizes that he and other leaders are merely servants of Christ and stewards of God’s mysteries. He stresses the importance of being faithful stewards, while reminding the Corinthians that ultimate judgment belongs to the Lord, who will reveal all things in time.", "4:6–7": "Paul urges the Corinthians not to boast in human leaders or become prideful, as everything they have is received from God. There is no room for boasting, for all are dependent on God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor.", "4:8–13": "Paul contrasts the Corinthians’ sense of sufficiency with the hardships of the apostles. He highlights the apostles’ sufferings, showing how they endure mockery, persecution, and poverty for the sake of Christ, yet continue to bless others in the process.", "4:14–17": "Paul speaks to the Corinthians as a spiritual father, reminding them of the relationship they have through the gospel. He urges them to follow his example as he follows Christ and sends Timothy to remind them of the truths he has taught.", "4:18–21": "Paul concludes by warning those who are arrogant, thinking he won’t return to Corinth. He plans to come soon, and when he does, he will not focus on words, but on the demonstration of God’s power. He gives them the choice to receive him either with correction or in a spirit of love and gentleness."}, "1 Corinthians 5": {"5:3–5": "Paul rebukes the Corinthians for tolerating sexual immorality in the church, specifically a case of incest that even Gentiles would condemn. He calls for the removal of the offender from their midst to preserve the purity of the church and ultimately to bring about repentance and restoration.", "5:6–8": "Paul uses the metaphor of leaven (yeast) to illustrate how sin can spread through the entire community if left unchecked. He urges the Corinthians to purge the sinful influence from among them, reminding them that Christ, their Passover lamb, has already been sacrificed, and they should live in sincerity and truth.", "5:12–13": "Paul clarifies that while believers are not to completely separate themselves from sinners in the world, they are to avoid close fellowship with anyone within the church who lives in unrepentant sin. Judgment within the church is necessary for maintaining its holiness, but God judges those outside.", "5:1–2": "Paul addresses a serious case of sexual sin in the church — a man living with his father's wife — and is shocked they're proud about it instead of grieved.", "5:9–11": "Paul clarifies that he wasn't telling them to avoid sinful people outside the church entirely, but to not associate closely with someone who calls himself a believer while living in open sin."}, "1 Corinthians 6": {"6:7–8": "Paul rebukes the Corinthians for taking their disputes with fellow believers to secular courts instead of resolving them within the church. He reminds them that believers are destined to judge the world and even angels, so they should be able to settle disputes among themselves. Paul calls them to endure wrongs for the sake of unity rather than seeking justice before unbelievers.", "6:9–11": "Paul lists various sinful behaviors that prevent people from inheriting the kingdom of God. He reminds the Corinthians that some of them used to live in such ways, but through the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God, they have been washed, sanctified, and justified in Christ.", "6:18–20": "Paul teaches that believers’ bodies are members of Christ, and thus should not be defiled by sexual immorality. The body is a temple of the Holy Spirit, and believers are to honor God with their bodies, as they have been bought with a price—the sacrifice of Christ.", "6:1–3": "Paul rebukes believers for taking disputes to secular courts instead of settling them among themselves, pointing out that believers will one day judge the world and even angels.", "6:4–6": "Paul is ashamed that they can't find anyone wise enough among them to settle disputes, so instead believers are suing each other in front of unbelievers.", "6:12–14": "Paul says while many things are allowed, not everything is good for him, and he won't be controlled by anything — the body belongs to the Lord, not to sexual immorality.", "6:15–17": "Paul explains that believers' bodies are joined to Christ, so sexual immorality wrongly joins that body to something else, while true union with the Lord makes a person one spirit with Him."}, "1 Corinthians 7": {"7:6–9": "Paul begins by addressing marriage, advising that to avoid immorality, each man should have his own wife and each woman her own husband. He emphasizes mutual respect and responsibility in the marriage relationship, while acknowledging that celibacy, like his own, is a gift from God for some. Paul advises the unmarried and widows that it is good to remain single, as he is, but if they cannot exercise self-control, it is better to marry than to struggle with sexual desire.", "7:10–11": "Paul relays the Lord’s command that married couples should not separate. If separation occurs, reconciliation should be pursued.", "7:15–16": "Paul gives counsel for believers married to unbelievers, encouraging them to remain together if the unbelieving spouse is willing, as the believer’s influence can have a sanctifying effect on the family. However, if the unbeliever chooses to leave, the believer is not bound in such situations.", "7:21–24": "Paul advises believers to remain in the life situation they were in when they were called to follow Christ, whether circumcised or uncircumcised, free or slave. All are equal in Christ, who has bought them with a price, and they should serve God faithfully wherever they are.", "7:29–31": "Paul gives practical advice concerning marriage, engagement, and singleness, encouraging believers to live with an eternal perspective, given the fleeting nature of this world. He acknowledges that marriage is not sinful but warns of the challenges that come with it.", "7:32–35": "Paul explains that singleness allows for undivided devotion to the Lord, while marriage brings worldly concerns and responsibilities. He offers this advice for the benefit of believers, encouraging them to focus on serving God, whether married or unmarried.", "7:39–40": "Paul concludes that both marriage and singleness are valid choices, but he encourages singleness for those who can remain so, as it allows for greater devotion to the Lord. However, those who choose to marry do well, and it is not a sin to do so.", "7:1–2": "Paul answers questions about marriage, saying that because of temptation to sexual immorality, each man and woman should have their own spouse.", "7:3–5": "Paul says husbands and wives owe each other in marriage and shouldn't withhold from one another, except temporarily by mutual agreement for prayer.", "7:12–14": "Paul instructs that if a believer is married to an unbeliever who's willing to stay, they shouldn't divorce, since the marriage sets the unbelieving spouse and children apart in a special way.", "7:17–20": "Paul tells believers to remain in the situation they were in when they were called — circumcised or not — since what matters is keeping God's commandments, not outward status.", "7:25–28": "Paul gives his own judgment, not a command from the Lord, that because of present difficulties it's good to remain unmarried, though marrying isn't a sin either.", "7:36–38": "Paul addresses a specific cultural situation about whether to allow an unmarried daughter to marry, saying either choice is acceptable, though not marrying her off is slightly better in his view."}, "1 Corinthians 8": {"8:1–3": "Paul begins by discussing the topic of food sacrificed to idols, acknowledging that knowledge can lead to pride. However, love, which builds up others, is the key. Loving God and others is more important than knowledge alone.", "8:4–6": "Paul affirms that idols have no real existence and that there is only one true God, the Father, and one Lord, Jesus Christ. For believers, this knowledge is foundational, but it must be exercised with love for others who may not fully understand this truth.", "8:9–13": "Paul cautions believers to be mindful of their liberty and how it might affect others. Though eating food offered to idols is not inherently sinful, doing so could lead a weaker brother or sister in the faith to stumble. Out of love, believers are called to forgo certain freedoms if it causes harm to another’s conscience.", "8:7–8": "Paul explains that not every believer understands that idols are nothing, so eating food offered to idols can trouble a weaker conscience — but eating or not eating doesn't change one's standing with God."}, "1 Corinthians 9": {"9:7–10": "Paul begins by defending his rights as an apostle. He highlights his freedom in Christ and his right to material support as a worker in the ministry. Yet, he points out that he has chosen not to use these rights in order to avoid placing any obstacle before the gospel.", "9:11–14": "Paul appeals to the law of Moses to show that those who work should be able to benefit from their labor. In the same way, those who preach the gospel have the right to receive material support. However, Paul and Barnabas chose not to exercise this right to avoid any hindrance to the gospel.", "9:15–18": "Paul emphasizes his willingness to preach the gospel freely, without demanding material compensation. He considers it his reward to preach without being a burden to others and to ensure that his motives are pure, for he is compelled by God to share the message of Christ.", "9:19–23": "Paul explains his approach to ministry—he adapts himself to different groups of people in order to win as many as possible for Christ. Whether Jew or Gentile, under the law or without the law, he becomes whatever is needed.", "9:24–27": "Paul compares the Christian life to a race, urging believers to run in such a way as to win the prize. Just as athletes exercise self-discipline for a temporary reward, Christians must live with purpose and self-control, striving for an eternal crown.", "9:1–2": "Paul defends his right to be recognized as an apostle, pointing out that he saw Jesus and that the Corinthians themselves are proof of his ministry.", "9:3–6": "Paul defends his right, like other apostles, to receive support and even to travel with a believing wife, questioning why he and Barnabas alone would have to work for a living."}, "1 Corinthians 10": {"10:6–10": "Paul recalls the history of Israel, showing how they all experienced God’s miraculous provision and deliverance, yet many still fell into disobedience. He urges the Corinthians to learn from these examples and avoid the same pitfalls of idolatry and rebellion.", "10:11–13": "Paul warns the Corinthians to avoid idolatry and immorality, reminding them of how Israel’s disobedience led to severe judgment. These examples are written as warnings for believers, urging them to remain vigilant in their walk with God. Paul assures the believers that no temptation is unique to them, and God is faithful. He promises that He will provide a way of escape so that they can endure any temptation that comes their way.", "10:18–22": "Paul continues to warn against idolatry, emphasizing that believers cannot participate in both the Lord’s table and the table of demons. There must be a clear separation from idolatrous practices, as they provoke the Lord’s jealousy.", "10:27–30": "Paul reminds the Corinthians that while they have freedom in Christ, they must use it wisely and consider the impact their actions have on others. Their freedom should not become a stumbling block to those with weaker consciences.", "10:31–33": "Paul concludes with a call to live in a way that brings glory to God in all things. Believers should strive to avoid offending others and to live for the benefit of others, that they might lead more people to salvation in Christ.", "10:1–5": "Paul reminds them that Israel's ancestors were all under the cloud and passed through the sea, ate spiritual food, and drank from a spiritual rock which was Christ — yet most of them still displeased God.", "10:14–17": "Paul tells them to flee idolatry, explaining that the cup and bread they share represent a real participation in Christ, uniting them as one body since they all share the one loaf.", "10:23–26": "Paul repeats that not everything permitted is beneficial, and tells them not to seek only their own good but also the good of others — and to eat what's sold in the market without worrying about its origin."}, "1 Corinthians 11": {"11:1–2": "Paul encourages the Corinthians to follow his example as he follows Christ, emphasizing the importance of living a Christ-centered life.", "11:7–12": "Paul addresses the cultural practice of head coverings in worship, tying it to the concept of divine order: God, Christ, man, and woman. He emphasizes mutual interdependence between men and women, while maintaining the created order.", "11:13–16": "Paul encourages the Corinthians to judge these matters for themselves based on the customs and practices of their culture. He asserts that these customs should not become a point of contention.", "11:20–22": "Paul rebukes the Corinthians for their improper conduct during the Lord’s Supper. Instead of coming together to remember Christ, they are dividing themselves and treating it as a common meal, leading to disorder and disrespect in the church.", "11:23–26": "Paul reminds the Corinthians of the true meaning of the Lord’s Supper. It is a sacred time of remembrance of Christ’s sacrifice, symbolized by the bread and the cup. Each time believers partake in it, they proclaim His death until He returns.", "11:31–34": "Paul urges the Corinthians to examine themselves before partaking of the Lord’s Supper, warning that to do so in an unworthy manner brings judgment. He emphasizes the importance of self-reflection, unity, and proper conduct during this sacred act of worship.", "11:3–6": "Paul explains a cultural practice about head coverings during prayer and prophecy, tying it to an order of headship between Christ, man, and woman.", "11:17–19": "Paul criticizes the Corinthians for their divisions when they meet together, saying such factions actually reveal who among them has genuine character.", "11:27–30": "Paul warns against taking communion carelessly, urging each person to examine themselves first, since taking it without recognizing its meaning had caused weakness and sickness among them."}, "1 Corinthians 12": {"12:1–3": "Paul begins his teaching on spiritual gifts, reminding the Corinthians of their previous idolatry and emphasizing that the confession of Jesus as Lord comes only by the Holy Spirit.", "12:8–11": "Paul explains that although there are a variety of spiritual gifts, all are given by the same Spirit for the common good. Each believer receives different gifts, but they all come from the same source and serve the same purpose: to build up the body of Christ.", "12:15–20": "Paul uses the analogy of the human body to explain how the church functions. Just as the body has many parts, each with a unique role, so the church is made up of many members, each with different spiritual gifts. All members are equally important and contribute to the overall health and function of the body of Christ.", "12:27–28": "Paul emphasizes the need for mutual care and unity in the body of Christ. No member is insignificant, and each one has a vital role to play. When one member suffers, all suffer; when one is honored, all rejoice. This interconnectedness is crucial for the health of the church.", "12:29–31": "Paul concludes by listing various roles and gifts within the church, emphasizing that not everyone has the same gift. However, he encourages believers to desire the most beneficial gifts, and he prepares to introduce a more excellent way—the way of love, which he will elaborate on in the next chapter.", "12:4–7": "Paul explains there are different spiritual gifts, different ways of serving, and different works, but the same Spirit, Lord, and God work through all of them, given for the good of everyone.", "12:12–14": "Paul compares the church to a body with many parts that all belong together, explaining that all believers were baptized by one Spirit into that one body, regardless of background.", "12:21–26": "Paul says no part of the body can claim it doesn't need another part — even the less honorable parts are treated with special care, so that all the parts share in each other's suffering and joy."}, "1 Corinthians 13": {"13:1–3": "Paul emphasizes that no matter how great one’s spiritual gifts, knowledge, or acts of sacrifice may be, they are meaningless without love. Love is the essential foundation for all Christian actions and spiritual gifts.", "13:4–8": "Paul describes the qualities of true love: it is patient, kind, humble, and selfless. Love endures all things, believes the best in others, and finds joy in truth, not in wrongdoing.", "13:9–12": "Paul explains that while spiritual gifts like prophecy, tongues, and knowledge are temporary and will one day pass away, love endures forever. He acknowledges that our current understanding is limited, but when perfection comes, we will fully understand God’s ways, just as we are fully known by Him.", "13:13": "Paul concludes by highlighting the three virtues that remain: faith, hope, and love. However, love is the greatest of these, as it reflects the very nature of God and is the foundation of all Christian living."}, "1 Corinthians 14": {"14:1–5": "Paul encourages the believers to pursue love and desire spiritual gifts, especially prophecy, which builds up the church. While speaking in tongues is a gift from God, prophecy is of greater benefit to the community because it edifies and comforts others.", "14:6–12": "Paul emphasizes that speaking in tongues without interpretation benefits no one. Just as musical instruments need to produce clear sounds, so must the words spoken in the church be understandable for the benefit of all.", "14:13–19": "Paul advises that those who speak in tongues should pray for the ability to interpret, so that their words benefit the church. He highlights the importance of speaking in a way that others can understand and be edified, even if that means fewer words.", "14:20–25": "Paul explains that speaking in tongues serves as a sign for unbelievers, while prophecy benefits believers. If unbelievers hear only tongues without interpretation, they may be confused. However, prophecy can convict and lead them to worship God.", "14:26–33": "Paul calls for order in worship, with a focus on edification. Speaking in tongues should be limited and done with interpretation, while prophecy should be weighed carefully. God desires peace and order, not confusion, in the gathering of believers.", "14:34–36": "Paul gives instructions about the role of women in worship, in line with the cultural practices of his time, encouraging order and respect within the community.", "14:37–40": "Paul concludes by affirming the importance of prophecy and encouraging the orderly use of tongues. He emphasizes that all worship should be done in a way that reflects God’s order and peace."}, "1 Corinthians 15": {"15:3–8": "Paul reminds the Corinthians of the foundational message of the gospel: that Christ died for their sins, was buried, and rose again on the third day, all according to the Scriptures. He lists the many witnesses who saw the risen Christ, including himself.", "15:9–11": "Paul acknowledges that he is the least of the apostles because he once persecuted the church, but it is through God's unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor that he is who he is. His ministry and hard work are a result of God’s favor and not his own effort.", "15:12–19": "Paul stresses the vital importance of the resurrection. If Christ has not been raised from the dead, then the Christian faith is in vain, and believers are still in their sins. The resurrection is the cornerstone of the gospel.", "15:23–28": "Paul explains that Christ’s resurrection is the guarantee of the future resurrection of all believers. Christ is the firstfruits, and when He returns, those who belong to Him will also be raised. Christ will ultimately defeat death, and all things will be subject to God.", "15:33–34": "Paul underscores the practical implications of the resurrection. Without the hope of resurrection, all suffering for Christ would be in vain. He warns against the influence of bad company and calls believers to live righteously, in light of the resurrection.", "15:42–44": "Paul uses the analogy of a seed to explain the resurrection body. Just as a seed is sown in the ground and transformed into something different, so the earthly body is sown in corruption but raised in glory. The resurrection body will be imperishable and spiritual.", "15:50–52": "Paul contrasts Adam, the earthly man, with Christ, the heavenly man. Just as we bear the image of Adam in our earthly bodies, so we will bear the image of Christ in our resurrected bodies. Our current flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God, but we will be transformed.", "15:56–57": "Paul reveals the mystery of the resurrection: not all believers will die, but all will be transformed at the return of Christ. Death will be defeated, and believers will be given immortal, imperishable bodies, fulfilling God’s promise of eternal victory over death.", "15:58": "In light of the resurrection, Paul encourages believers to stand firm and continue working diligently for the Lord, knowing that their labor will not be in vain.", "15:1–2": "Paul reminds them of the good news he originally preached to them, which they received and are standing on, urging them to hold firmly to it.", "15:20–22": "Paul declares that Christ has been raised from the dead as the first of many, and just as death came through Adam, resurrection life comes through Christ.", "15:29–32": "Paul argues that if there's no resurrection, practices like being baptized for the dead and his own daily risking of his life would make no sense at all.", "15:35–38": "Paul answers the question of how the dead are raised by comparing it to a seed that must die before it grows into something different, given a new body just as God chooses.", "15:39–41": "Paul points out that not all flesh or glory is the same — people, animals, and heavenly bodies like the sun, moon, and stars each have their own distinct kind of glory.", "15:45–49": "Paul contrasts Adam, the first man made from dust, with Christ, the last Adam who is a life-giving spirit from heaven — and says believers will one day bear the likeness of the heavenly Man.", "15:53–55": "Paul says our decaying, mortal bodies must be transformed into imperishable, immortal ones, so that the promise of Scripture comes true: death is swallowed up in victory."}, "1 Corinthians 16": {"16:1–4": "Paul instructs the Corinthians to set aside a collection for the saints in Jerusalem, to be gathered on the first day of the week. He encourages them to give according to how God has blessed them, ensuring the collection is ready when he arrives.", "16:5–9": "Paul shares his travel plans with the Corinthians, hoping to visit them after passing through Macedonia. He mentions the opportunity for ministry in Ephesus but also acknowledges the opposition he faces there.", "16:10–12": "Paul instructs the Corinthians to welcome Timothy, who is doing the Lord’s work, and to treat him with respect. He also mentions that Apollos is not able to come at the present time but may come later.", "16:13–14": "Paul urges the Corinthians to remain watchful, stand firm in their faith, and act with courage and strength. Above all, he emphasizes that everything they do should be done in love.", "16:15–18": "Paul commends the household of Stephanas for their dedication to serving the saints and encourages the Corinthians to submit to and support those who labor for the Lord. He also expresses gratitude for the refreshment and support provided by Stephanas, Fortunatus, and Achaicus.", "16:21–24": "Paul closes his letter with greetings from the churches in Asia and Aquila and Priscilla, who host a church in their home. He writes his final salutation with his own hand, offering a blessing of the Lord's unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and expressing his love for the Corinthians.", "16:19–20": "Paul closes with greetings from the churches of Asia and from Aquila and Priscilla, telling the Corinthians to greet each other warmly."}, "2 Corinthians 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul begins his second letter to the Corinthians, sending greetings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ to the believers in Corinth and Achaia.", "1:3–7": "Paul praises God as the source of all comfort, reminding the Corinthians that God comforts them in their tribulations so that they, in turn, can comfort others. Just as they share in Christ’s sufferings, they also share in His comfort and consolation.", "1:8–11": "Paul shares with the Corinthians how he and his companions experienced severe trials in Asia, even to the point of despairing of life itself. Yet through these hardships, they learned to rely on God, who raises the dead, for deliverance. Paul also acknowledges the power of the Corinthians’ prayers in helping them through these trials.", "1:12–14": "Paul expresses his joy in having a clear conscience, knowing that his conduct toward the Corinthians has been sincere and guided by the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God. He hopes the Corinthians will continue to recognize this sincerity and rejoice in their shared faith.", "1:21–22": "Paul explains his change of plans to visit the Corinthians, emphasizing that his decisions were not made lightly or based on human reasoning. Instead, his actions were guided by the faithfulness of God, all his promises are yes and in him amen. Paul reminds the Corinthians that God has established and sealed them with the Holy Spirit as a guarantee of their salvation.", "1:23–24": "Paul explains that he delayed his visit to Corinth to spare them, not because he wanted to assert authority over their faith, but because he sought to help them find joy in their relationship with God. He reassures them that their faith is the foundation on which they stand.", "1:15–20": "Paul explains his travel plans and defends himself against accusations of being wishy-washy, saying that just as God's promises are always 'Yes' in Christ, his word to them can be trusted too."}, "2 Corinthians 2": {"2:1–4": "Paul explains that he delayed his visit to Corinth to avoid bringing sorrow upon the church. He had written to them out of deep love and concern, with the hope that they would recognize his love for them, even though the letter was written in tears and affliction.", "2:9–11": "Paul urges the Corinthians to forgive the individual who caused grief, emphasizing the importance of forgiveness and comfort. By forgiving, they prevent Satan from taking advantage of their bitterness. Paul assures them that he has already forgiven the offender in Christ for the sake of unity in the church.", "2:14–17": "Paul rejoices in the triumph that Christ provides, even amidst personal struggles. He likens the gospel ministry to a sweet fragrance that spreads the knowledge of Christ everywhere. To those being saved, it is the fragrance of life; to those perishing, it is the fragrance of death. Paul emphasizes that they preach the gospel with sincerity and in the sight of God.", "2:5–8": "Paul tells the church to forgive and comfort a man who had caused pain and had already been punished enough by the church, so he isn't overwhelmed with sorrow.", "2:12–13": "Paul describes how, even with an open door to preach in Troas, he couldn't rest because he hadn't found Titus, so he moved on to Macedonia."}, "2 Corinthians 3": {"3:1–3": "Paul begins by addressing whether he and his companions need letters of recommendation, reminding the Corinthians that they themselves are a living letter of Christ, written by the Spirit of God on their hearts. Their transformed lives are the testimony of Paul’s ministry.", "3:4–6": "Paul emphasizes that their sufficiency to minister comes from God, not from their own abilities. He contrasts the old covenant, which was based on the letter of the law and brought death, with the new covenant, which is of the Spirit and brings life.", "3:7–11": "Paul compares the glory of the old covenant, represented by the law given to Moses, with the surpassing glory of the new covenant. The old covenant brought condemnation, but the new covenant brings righteousness. The glory of the new covenant far exceeds the fading glory of the old.", "3:16–18": "Because of the surpassing glory of the new covenant, Paul and his companions speak boldly and clearly. Unlike Moses, who covered his face with a veil, Paul speaks openly of Christ. The veil of misunderstanding remains over those who read the old covenant without turning to Christ, but in Christ, the veil is removed. Paul concludes by emphasizing the freedom found in Christ through the Spirit. With unveiled faces, believers behold the glory of the Lord and are being transformed into His image, growing in glory through the work of the Spirit.", "3:12–15": "Paul says he speaks with boldness, unlike Moses who wore a veil, and explains that a veil still covers people's understanding when the old covenant is read, a veil only removed in Christ."}, "2 Corinthians 4": {"4:5–6": "Paul explains that, despite the challenges, they do not lose heart in their ministry because they have received God's mercy. He contrasts the truth of the gospel with the deceitfulness of the world, and he explains that unbelievers are blinded by the god of this world (Satan), preventing them from seeing the light of the gospel. Paul and his companions preach Christ, and God has illuminated their hearts to reveal His glory through Jesus Christ.", "4:8–12": "Paul uses the metaphor of treasures in earthen vessels to describe how God's power is revealed through human weakness. Though they face trials and sufferings, they are sustained by God's strength. Their hardships reflect the dying of Jesus, while His life is also revealed in their mortal bodies. Paul emphasizes that their suffering brings spiritual life to others.", "4:13–15": "Paul’s faith in the resurrection empowers him to continue proclaiming the gospel. Just as God raised Jesus from the dead, He will also raise believers. Everything they endure is for the benefit of others, and the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God brings thanksgiving and glory to Him.", "4:16–18": "Paul encourages believers not to lose heart, even though outwardly they may be wasting away. Inwardly, they are being renewed daily. The temporary afflictions they endure will result in eternal glory. He calls for a focus on eternal realities rather than on the temporary things of this world.", "4:1–2": "Paul says that because of God's mercy in giving him this ministry, he doesn't lose heart, and refuses to use deception or distort God's word, instead speaking the truth openly.", "4:3–4": "Paul explains that if the gospel seems hidden, it's only to those who are perishing, whose minds have been blinded by the god of this age so they can't see Christ's glory.", "4:7": "Paul says believers carry this treasure in ordinary, fragile 'jars of clay,' so that the power at work is clearly seen as coming from God, not from them."}, "2 Corinthians 5": {"5:1–5": "Paul explains the hope believers have in the resurrection. While living in their earthly bodies (the tabernacle), they groan with a longing for their eternal heavenly bodies. God has prepared them for this transformation and given them the Holy Spirit as a guarantee.", "5:9–10": "Paul encourages the believers to live by faith, not by sight, knowing that being absent from the body means being present with the Lord. He emphasizes the importance of living in a way that pleases God, as everyone will stand before Christ to give an account for their actions.", "5:14–15": "Paul explains that the love of Christ compels him to preach the gospel, knowing that Christ died for all so that those who live should no longer live for themselves but for Christ, who died and rose again for their sake. His ministry is one of persuasion, urging others to turn to God.", "5:20–21": "Paul proclaims that those who are in Christ are new creations—the old life has passed away, and all things have become new. Through Christ, God has reconciled the world to Himself, not counting their sins against them. Paul and his companions are ambassadors for Christ, urging others to be reconciled to God through the finished work of Jesus, who became sin for us so that we might become the righteousness of God.", "5:6–8": "Paul says believers stay confident, knowing that while at home in this body they're away from the Lord, walking by faith and not by sight, and would gladly prefer to be with the Lord instead.", "5:11–13": "Paul explains that understanding the fear of the Lord motivates him to persuade people, and that his life is fully known to God and, he hopes, to their consciences too.", "5:16–17": "Paul says believers no longer judge people by human standards, and that anyone in Christ is a new creation — the old life is gone and a new one has begun.", "5:18–19": "Paul explains that God reconciled us to Himself through Christ, not counting our sins against us, and has given believers the task of sharing that same message of reconciliation."}, "2 Corinthians 6": {"6:1–2": "Paul urges the Corinthians not to receive God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor in vain, reminding them that now is the day of salvation. God’s favor is a gift that should be embraced and lived out in obedience to the faith.", "6:8–10": "Paul details the hardships and challenges he and his companions have faced in their ministry, including physical sufferings and trials. Despite these challenges, they continue to serve faithfully, demonstrating purity, knowledge, patience, and love, empowered by the Holy Spirit. They maintain armor of righteousness through all circumstances, knowing that their mission is to bring riches to others.", "6:11–13": "Paul expresses his open-hearted love toward the Corinthians and encourages them to respond with the same openness and love. He desires for them to experience the fullness of fellowship and mutual affection in Christ.", "6:17–18": "Paul calls the Corinthians to live separate from unbelievers in their conduct and relationships. He emphasizes that believers are the temple of the living God, and therefore they should not be yoked with those who do not share their faith. God promises to dwell among them, be their God, and adopt them as His children if they maintain their purity and separation from worldly influences.", "6:3": "Paul says he tries not to put any obstacle in anyone's path, so that his ministry won't be discredited.", "6:4–5": "Paul lists the hardships he's endured as proof of his genuine service — troubles, beatings, imprisonments, hard labor, and sleepless nights.", "6:6–7": "Paul continues the list, describing how he's served through purity, understanding, patience, kindness, the Holy Spirit, sincere love, truthful speech, and God's power.", "6:14–16": "Paul warns against being closely bound together with unbelievers, since light and darkness have nothing in common, and reminds them that believers themselves are the temple of the living God."}, "2 Corinthians 7": {"7:1": "Paul encourages believers to cleanse themselves from anything that defiles body or spirit, seeking to perfect holiness out of reverence for God. This call to holiness is based on the promises of God, including His promise to dwell among His people.", "7:5–7": "Paul expresses his deep affection and joy for the Corinthians, even in the midst of his trials. He recalls the comfort and encouragement he received from Titus, who brought news of the Corinthians’ longing for reconciliation, and their concern for Paul.", "7:10–11": "Paul rejoices that his letter, though painful, led the Corinthians to experience godly sorrow, which produced repentance. He contrasts godly sorrow, which leads to salvation, with worldly sorrow, which leads to death. The Corinthians’ repentance resulted in a renewed commitment to righteousness.", "7:14–16": "Paul finds comfort in the Corinthians’ response to his letter, noting how they received Titus with obedience and sincerity. His confidence in their growth and commitment to righteousness is affirmed, and he rejoices in their transformation.", "7:2–4": "Paul asks the Corinthians to open their hearts to him again, insisting he's wronged no one, and expresses how proud of them he is despite their troubles.", "7:8–9": "Paul says that although his earlier letter caused them sorrow, he's now glad, because their sorrow led them to genuine change rather than harm.", "7:12–13": "Paul explains he wrote not mainly because of the wrongdoer or the wronged, but so their own devotion would become clear, and he was comforted to see how their response had refreshed Titus."}, "2 Corinthians 8": {"8:3–5": "Paul shares the extraordinary generosity of the Macedonian churches, who, despite their poverty and trials, gave abundantly and joyfully to support other believers. Their giving was not out of obligation but a willing sacrifice, first dedicating themselves to the Lord and then to the service of others.", "8:6–8": "Paul encourages the Corinthians to follow the example of the Macedonians and excel in the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of giving, just as they excel in other areas like faith, speech, knowledge, diligence, and love.", "8:9": "Paul points to the ultimate example of generosity—Jesus Christ. Though He was rich, He became poor for the sake of humanity so that through His poverty, believers might become rich in their finances.", "8:13–15": "Paul advises the Corinthians to complete their commitment to give, reminding them that it is the willingness to give that matters more than the amount. The goal is not to burden them but to create a balance, where those with abundance can help those in need, ensuring equality among believers.", "8:20–21": "Paul commends Titus for his dedication and care for the Corinthians, as well as another unnamed brother who is well-regarded in all the churches. These men are entrusted with delivering the collection, ensuring that everything is done with integrity, both before God and people.", "8:23–24": "Paul emphasizes the importance of honoring and supporting those involved in the ministry of the gospel. He encourages the Corinthians to show their love and generosity as a testimony to the churches, validating Paul’s confidence in them.", "8:1–2": "Paul tells about the grace God gave the Macedonian churches, who, even in deep poverty and hard trials, gave generously out of overflowing joy.", "8:10–12": "Paul encourages the Corinthians to finish the giving they had begun a year earlier, since a gift is measured by willingness, not by how much someone has.", "8:16–17": "Paul thanks God for putting the same concern for the Corinthians into Titus' heart, who eagerly went to them on his own initiative.", "8:18–19": "Paul mentions sending along a well-respected brother, chosen by the churches to help carry this gift for God's glory.", "8:22": "Paul also sent another brother whom he had tested many times and found faithful, now even more eager because of his confidence in the Corinthians."}, "2 Corinthians 9": {"9:5": "Paul encourages the Corinthians to be prepared in their giving, noting that their readiness and zeal had been an inspiration to others. He wants them to be fully prepared when the collection is gathered so that their generosity is seen as a willing gift and not an obligation.", "9:10–11": "Paul teaches the principle of sowing and reaping: those who give generously will also receive generously. He emphasizes that God loves a cheerful giver and is able to provide for all their needs, allowing them to continue doing good works. God multiplies what is sown and increases the harvest of righteousness, resulting in thanksgiving to God.", "9:15": "Paul highlights the broader impact of generosity. Not only does it meet the needs of the saints, but it also causes others to give thanks and glorify God. Their giving demonstrates their obedience to the gospel and strengthens the bond of love between believers, who in turn pray for them and thank God for His incredible unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor.", "9:1–2": "Paul says he almost doesn't need to write about the collection for believers, since he's already bragged to the Macedonians about the Corinthians' eagerness to give.", "9:3–4": "Paul explains he sent the brothers ahead to make sure the Corinthians' gift was actually ready, so neither of them would be embarrassed if Macedonians arrived and found them unprepared.", "9:6–7": "Paul says the amount someone sows determines what they reap, and that each person should give what they've decided in their heart, not reluctantly, since God loves a cheerful giver.", "9:8–9": "Paul says God is able to provide abundantly so that believers always have enough and plenty left over to give, quoting Scripture about generosity that lasts forever.", "9:12–14": "Paul explains that this gift doesn't just meet needs — it also produces overflowing thanksgiving to God and causes people to praise Him for the Corinthians' generosity."}, "2 Corinthians 10": {"10:3–6": "Paul begins by defending the authority of his ministry, appealing to the Corinthians with meekness and gentleness. He explains that although they live in the flesh, their spiritual warfare is not fought with physical weapons but with divine power to demolish strongholds and bring every thought into obedience to Christ.", "10:10–11": "Paul addresses those who judge him based on outward appearances, asserting that his authority comes from the Lord for building up the church, not tearing it down. He confronts the criticism that his letters are strong, but his physical presence is weak, affirming that his actions will be as bold as his letters when he visits in person.", "10:17–18": "Paul refuses to compare himself with those who commend themselves, noting that such comparisons are unwise. Instead, he focuses on the ministry God has given him, which extends even to the Corinthians. Paul’s only boast is in the Lord, knowing that true approval comes from God, not from human commendation.", "10:1–2": "Paul appeals to them by the gentleness of Christ, asking that he won't need to be as bold in person as he's prepared to be against those who accuse him of living by worldly standards.", "10:7–9": "Paul tells them to look beyond surface appearances, since he belongs to Christ just as much as anyone else, and reminds them his authority is meant to build them up, not tear them down.", "10:12": "Paul says he won't compare himself to those who boast about themselves, since measuring yourself against yourself isn't wise.", "10:13–14": "Paul says he'll only boast within the limits of the ministry God actually assigned him, which genuinely includes reaching the Corinthians.", "10:15–16": "Paul hopes that as their faith grows, his ministry among them will expand so he can preach the gospel even further beyond them, without taking credit for someone else's work."}, "2 Corinthians 11": {"11:3–4": "Paul expresses deep concern for the Corinthians, likening his role to that of a father who has engaged them to Christ as a pure bride. He fears they might be led astray by false teachings, just as Eve was deceived by the serpent, and warns them against accepting another Jesus or another gospel.", "11:10–12": "Paul defends his ministry, explaining that he freely preached the gospel to the Corinthians, accepting financial support from other churches to avoid being a burden to them. He asserts that his humility and sacrifice were for their benefit, and he remains committed to not burdening them.", "11:13–15": "Paul warns the Corinthians about false apostles who disguise themselves as true servants of Christ. Just as Satan disguises himself as an angel of light, these deceitful workers appear to be righteous but lead people astray. Paul assures that their ultimate judgment will reflect their deeds.", "11:19–21": "Paul acknowledges that his boasting may seem foolish, but he engages in it to contrast himself with the false apostles who boast in the flesh. The Corinthians tolerate those who exploit and mistreat them, so Paul highlights his own sacrifices and sufferings for Christ’s sake as a true apostle.", "11:28–29": "Paul details the extensive hardships he has endured as a minister of Christ, including beatings, imprisonments, shipwrecks, and constant dangers. His sufferings stand as a stark contrast to the self-serving false apostles, showing his genuine commitment to the gospel and the care of the churches.", "11:32–33": "Paul concludes by saying that if he must boast, he will boast in his weaknesses and sufferings, which showcase God’s strength working through him. He recounts a specific instance in Damascus where he narrowly escaped death, highlighting how his ministry has often been marked by danger and dependence on God’s deliverance.", "11:1–2": "Paul asks them to bear with a little foolishness from him, explaining his godly jealousy for them, since he wants to present them to Christ like a pure bride to one husband.", "11:5–6": "Paul says he isn't inferior to the most impressive apostles, even if he isn't a polished speaker, since his knowledge has already been made clear to them.", "11:7–9": "Paul points out that he humbled himself by preaching to them without charging anything, being supported instead by other churches so he wouldn't burden the Corinthians.", "11:16–18": "Paul asks them not to think him foolish, but says if they do, to let him boast a little too, since so many others are boasting in worldly ways.", "11:22–23": "Paul matches every credential his opponents claim — Hebrew, Israelite, descendant of Abraham, servant of Christ — and says he has endured even more hardship than they have.", "11:24–25": "Paul lists specific sufferings he endured for the gospel: multiple floggings, beatings, a stoning, shipwrecks, and a day and night adrift at sea.", "11:26–27": "Paul continues listing dangers he faced constantly — from rivers, robbers, his own people, the Gentiles, and false believers — along with hunger, cold, and exhaustion.", "11:30–31": "Paul says if he must boast, he'll boast about his weaknesses instead, and calls God as his witness that he isn't lying."}, "2 Corinthians 12": {"12:5–7": "Paul speaks of a remarkable vision where he was caught up to paradise, but he refuses to boast in it. Instead, he chooses to boast in his weaknesses, so that no one thinks more highly of him than is warranted.", "12:8–10": "Paul reveals that to keep him humble, a thorn in the flesh was given to him. He prayed three times for its removal, but God’s response was that His unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor is sufficient. Paul’s weakness became the platform for God’s power, and thus, Paul took pleasure in his weaknesses, knowing that Christ’s strength is displayed in them.", "12:11–13": "Paul sarcastically points out that the Corinthians had forced him to defend himself, despite having demonstrated the signs of a true apostle through miracles, patience, and endurance. He reminds them that he never burdened them financially, and asks for forgiveness if this caused any offense.", "12:17–18": "Paul reassures the Corinthians of his love for them, explaining that he desires them, not their possessions. He is willing to spend and be spent for their benefit, even if his love is not reciprocated. He reminds them that neither he nor Titus ever took advantage of them financially.", "12:19–21": "Paul expresses his concern that when he visits the Corinthians again, he may find them living in sin and strife. He fears that if they have not repented of their immoral behavior, he will be grieved and humbled, and will have to mourn over their lack of repentance.", "12:1–4": "Paul describes, almost reluctantly, a man he knew (himself) who was caught up into paradise fourteen years earlier and heard things too wonderful to describe.", "12:14–16": "Paul says he's ready to visit a third time and won't be a financial burden, since he wants them, not their possessions, and will gladly spend himself for their sake."}, "2 Corinthians 13": {"13:3–4": "Paul issues a final warning to the Corinthians, preparing them for his upcoming visit. He intends to address sin directly and emphasizes that Christ, though crucified in weakness, now lives by the power of God. This power also strengthens believers, making them able to live in righteousness.", "13:7–9": "Paul urges the Corinthians to self-examine their faith, encouraging them to see whether they are truly living in Christ. He reassures them that he and his co-workers are not disqualified, praying for the Corinthians to live in integrity, not for his own validation but for their spiritual well-being.", "13:10": "Paul assures the Corinthians that his goal is their growth and maturity in faith, not condemnation. He desires them to be strong in truth, even if he appears weak, because his authority and correction are intended for their building up, not for tearing down.", "13:14": "Paul closes with a heartfelt benediction, encouraging unity, comfort, and peace among the believers. He extends blessings from the triune God—Jesus Christ, God the Father, and the Holy Spirit—asking that God’s grace, love, and fellowship remain with them.", "13:1–2": "Paul warns that on this third visit, he won't hold back from dealing firmly with those who have continued in sin, following the principle of confirming matters through witnesses.", "13:5–6": "Paul tells them to examine themselves to see if they're really in the faith, reminding them that Christ is in them unless they fail the test.", "13:11–13": "Paul closes with final encouragement — rejoice, be restored, be united, live in peace — and sends greetings along with everyone else's."}, "1 Thessalonians 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul, along with Silvanus (Silas) and Timothy, greets the church in Thessalonica with blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace from God the Father and Jesus Christ.", "1:3–5": "Paul expresses his gratitude to God for the Thessalonians, constantly praying for them. He remembers their faith, love, and hope in the Lord Jesus Christ, recognizing that they are chosen by God.", "1:6–7": "Paul reminds the Thessalonians that the gospel they received came not only with words but with power, the Holy Spirit, and deep assurance. Despite suffering, they embraced the message with joy and became examples to believers throughout Macedonia and Achaia.", "1:8–10": "The Thessalonians’ faith became known everywhere, as the message of the Lord spread through their witness. They turned from idols to serve the living and true God, waiting expectantly for Jesus, whom God raised from the dead, and who will deliver them from the coming wrath."}, "1 Thessalonians 2": {"2:1–4": "Paul reminds the Thessalonians that his visit to them was not without purpose, even though he and his companions had suffered previously in Philippi. They proclaimed the gospel boldly, not seeking to deceive or please men, but to please God, who tests the hearts.", "2:5–8": "Paul emphasizes that they did not use flattery or seek financial gain. Instead, they acted gently and lovingly toward the Thessalonians, caring for them like a mother caring for her children, even willing to share their very lives because of their deep affection.", "2:9–12": "Paul reminds them of how he and his companions worked hard to avoid being a financial burden while preaching the gospel. They lived holy, righteous lives among the Thessalonians, encouraging, comforting, and urging them to live lives worthy of God, who calls them into His kingdom and glory.", "2:13–16": "Paul thanks God continually because the Thessalonians received the message as God’s word, which is at work in believers. He acknowledges that they have endured suffering, just as the Judean churches did. He condemns those who persecuted the prophets, Jesus, and the apostles, and who hinder the preaching of the gospel.", "2:17–20": "Paul expresses his deep longing to visit the Thessalonians again, but he explains that Satan hindered his efforts. He ends by acknowledging that the Thessalonians are his hope, joy, and crown of rejoicing before the Lord at His return, declaring that they are his glory and joy."}, "1 Thessalonians 3": {"3:4–5": "Paul expresses his deep concern for the Thessalonians’ faith during times of persecution. Since he couldn’t visit, he sent Timothy to strengthen and encourage them so that they wouldn’t be unsettled by their trials, reminding them that suffering was something they had already been warned about.", "3:6–8": "Paul shares his relief and joy upon receiving Timothy’s positive report about the Thessalonians’ faith and love. Their steadfastness in the Lord gave Paul great comfort and encouragement during his own afflictions.", "3:11–13": "Paul expresses his desire to see the Thessalonians again to further strengthen their faith. He prays for them to increase in love for one another and for all people, and that their hearts would be established in holiness and blamelessness before God at the return of Christ.", "3:1–3": "Paul explains he sent Timothy to strengthen their faith and encourage them through their troubles, since Paul and his companions could no longer bear being separated from them.", "3:9–10": "Paul says he can't thank God enough for them, praying earnestly night and day to see them again and fill in whatever their faith still lacks."}, "1 Thessalonians 4": {"4:3–8": "Paul urges the Thessalonians to live in a way that pleases God, reminding them that God’s will is their sanctification. They are called to live holy and honorable lives, avoiding sexual immorality and deceit, knowing that God is the avenger of wrongdoing. Rejecting these teachings is rejecting God, who has given them His Holy Spirit.", "4:9–12": "Paul commends the Thessalonians for their love for each other and encourages them to continue growing in this love. He also exhorts them to live quietly, mind their own affairs, and work diligently so that they may win the respect of outsiders and not be in need.", "4:16–18": "Paul provides hope and comfort to the Thessalonians regarding those who have died (fallen asleep). He assures them that in the rapture, the dead in Christ will rise first, followed by those who are still alive. Together, they will meet the Lord in the air and be with Him forever. Paul encourages them to comfort each other with this hope.", "4:1–2": "Paul urges them to keep living in the way that pleases God, as they had learned from him, and to keep growing in it more and more.", "4:13–15": "Paul explains that believers shouldn't grieve like people with no hope for those who have died, since just as Jesus rose, God will bring back those who died in Him."}, "1 Thessalonians 5": {"5:4–8": "Paul reminds the Thessalonians that the day of the Lord will come unexpectedly, like a thief in the night. However, they are not in darkness and should not be caught off guard, for they are children of the light and of the day.", "5:9–11": "Paul urges believers to live watchfully and soberly, being spiritually alert. They are to arm themselves with faith, love, and the hope of salvation, knowing that God has destined them for salvation through Christ, not for wrath or judgment. Christ died so that they may live with Him, whether in life or in death.", "5:14–15": "Paul encourages the Thessalonians to continue comforting and building one another up. He also instructs them to respect their leaders and live in peace. They are to warn the idle, comfort the discouraged, help the weak, and show patience to everyone. They are not to seek revenge but always pursue what is good for each other and for everyone.", "5:19–22": "Paul gives a series of final exhortations: to always rejoice, pray continuously, and give thanks in all circumstances. He warns them not to quench the Spirit or despise prophecies but to test everything and hold on to what is good, abstaining from every form of evil.", "5:25–28": "Paul prays for the Thessalonians’ complete sanctification and preservation until the return of Christ. He encourages them with the reminder that God, who calls them, is faithful and will bring it to pass. He closes by asking for their prayers and sending greetings with a blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor.", "5:1–3": "Paul says they already know that the day of the Lord will come unexpectedly, like a thief in the night, catching people off guard while they feel safe.", "5:12–13": "Paul asks them to respect and appreciate those who work hard leading and correcting them, and to live at peace with one another.", "5:16–18": "Paul tells them to always rejoice, pray continually, and give thanks in every circumstance, since this is God's will for them in Christ.", "5:23–24": "Paul prays that God would make them completely holy and keep them blameless until Christ returns, trusting that the One who calls them will be faithful to do it."}, "2 Thessalonians 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul, along with Silvanus (Silas) and Timothy, greets the church in Thessalonica with blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace from God the Father and Jesus Christ.", "1:5–7": "Paul gives thanks to God for the Thessalonians because their faith is growing and their love for each other is increasing. He praises them for their perseverance and faith in the midst of persecution and suffering, which is evidence that God is preparing them for His kingdom.", "1:8–10": "Paul reassures the Thessalonians that God will bring justice. He will repay their persecutors with trouble, while granting rest to the suffering believers. When Jesus returns with His mighty angels, He will punish those who do not know God or obey the gospel, but He will be glorified among His people.", "1:11–12": "Paul prays that God would count the Thessalonians worthy of their calling and enable them to fulfill every good purpose through faith. His desire is that the name of the Lord Jesus would be glorified in them, and they in Him, through the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God.", "1:3–4": "Paul thanks God for their growing faith and increasing love for each other, and says he boasts about their endurance through persecution to other churches."}, "2 Thessalonians 2": {"2:3–4": "Paul urges the Thessalonians not to be easily disturbed or deceived regarding the timing of the Lord’s return. He explains that the day of the Lord will not come until there is a great rebellion and the man of sin, also called the son of perdition, is revealed. This man will exalt himself above God, even proclaiming himself to be God.", "2:8–10": "Paul reminds them that he had already taught them about these things. He speaks of a restraining force that is currently holding back the man of sin until the proper time. Once the restrainer is removed, the lawless one will be revealed, but Jesus will destroy him with the breath of His mouth and the splendor of His coming.", "2:11–12": "The coming of the lawless one will be in accordance with Satan’s power, marked by counterfeit miracles and deceptive signs. Those who reject the truth and take pleasure in wickedness will be deceived, as God allows them to believe lies, leading to their condemnation.", "2:15": "Paul gives thanks to God for the Thessalonians, acknowledging that they were chosen for salvation through the sanctifying work of the Holy Spirit and their belief in the truth. He encourages them to stand firm and hold fast to the teachings they received from him.", "2:16–17": "Paul prays that the Lord Jesus Christ and God the Father, who loves them and has given them eternal comfort and hope through unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, will strengthen and encourage their hearts, enabling them to continue in every good work and word.", "2:1–2": "Paul urges them not to be alarmed or deceived by any claim that the day of the Lord has already come.", "2:5–7": "Paul reminds them of what he'd already taught about a restraining force holding back the coming of lawlessness until the right time.", "2:13–14": "Paul thanks God that they were chosen from the beginning for salvation, set apart by the Spirit and belief in the truth, called through the gospel to share in Christ's glory."}, "2 Thessalonians 3": {"3:3–5": "Paul asks the Thessalonians to pray for him and his companions, that the word of the Lord would spread quickly and be honored, and that they would be delivered from wicked and faithless people. He expresses confidence in God’s faithfulness to establish and protect the Thessalonians, praying that their hearts will be directed into God’s love and the perseverance of Christ.", "3:10–12": "Paul commands the Thessalonians to avoid any believer who is idle and does not live according to the teachings they received. He reminds them of his own example of hard work and not being a burden to anyone. He strongly emphasizes that those unwilling to work should not eat and warns against idleness and gossip. Instead, they are to work quietly and provide for themselves.", "3:13–15": "Paul encourages the believers not to grow weary in doing good. He instructs them to take note of anyone who refuses to follow the instructions in the letter and not to associate with them, hoping that this will lead them to repentance. However, they are not to treat such a person as an enemy but to warn them as a brother.", "3:16–18": "Paul closes with a blessing of peace from the Lord and signs the letter with his own hand, as was his custom. He ends with a prayer that the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of the Lord Jesus Christ be with them all.", "3:1–2": "Paul asks them to pray that the Lord's message would spread rapidly and be honored, and that he and his companions would be protected from wicked people.", "3:6–9": "Paul instructs them to keep away from believers living undisciplined lives, reminding them how he and his companions worked hard and didn't take advantage of anyone while setting an example to follow."}, "1 Timothy 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul greets Timothy, his spiritual son, and extends blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, mercy, and peace from God the Father and Jesus Christ.", "1:5–7": "Paul instructs Timothy to stay in Ephesus and warn certain people not to teach false doctrines or pay attention to myths and endless genealogies, which lead to confusion rather than promoting God’s work. The goal of the command is love, which comes from a pure heart, a good conscience, and sincere faith. Some have wandered from this and turned to meaningless talk.", "1:8–11": "Paul explains that the law is good when used correctly, but it was made for the lawless and rebellious, not for the righteous. The law is meant to curb behaviors that are contrary to sound doctrine, as outlined in the glorious gospel that Paul has been entrusted with.", "1:15–17": "Paul expresses gratitude to Jesus Christ for calling him into ministry despite his past as a blasphemer and persecutor. He acknowledges that he received mercy because he acted in ignorance, and the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of the Lord was poured out on him abundantly. Paul sees himself as the worst of sinners, yet Christ showed him mercy to demonstrate His patience to all who would believe. Paul breaks into a doxology, praising God as the eternal, immortal, invisible King, worthy of all honor and glory forever.", "1:18–20": "Paul charges Timothy to fight the good fight, holding on to faith and a clear conscience. He warns that some have rejected this and suffered spiritual shipwreck, mentioning Hymenaeus and Alexander, whom Paul handed over to Satan to teach them not to blaspheme.", "1:3–4": "Paul reminds Timothy to stay in Ephesus and confront certain teachers who were spreading false teaching and pointless myths and genealogies that only cause arguments.", "1:12–14": "Paul thanks Christ for considering him trustworthy despite once persecuting and blaspheming Him, saying he was shown mercy because he acted in ignorance, and grace overflowed to him."}, "1 Timothy 2": {"2:1–4": "Paul urges that prayers, petitions, intercessions, and thanksgiving be offered for all people, especially for those in authority, so that believers may live peaceful and godly lives. This is pleasing to God, who desires all people to be saved and to know the truth.", "2:5–7": "Paul emphasizes that there is only one God and one mediator between God and humanity—Jesus Christ, who gave Himself as a ransom for all. Paul affirms his calling as a preacher, apostle, and teacher of the Gentiles in faith and truth.", "2:11–15": "Paul instructs men to pray with pure hearts and without anger or doubt, and he urges women to dress modestly, focusing on good deeds rather than outward adornment. He provides guidance on the role of women in learning and teaching within the church, referencing the order of creation and the fall, while also highlighting the importance of faith, love, and holiness.", "2:8": "Paul instructs that men everywhere should pray with clean hands, free of anger and arguing.", "2:9–10": "Paul instructs women to dress modestly and sensibly, focusing on good deeds rather than elaborate hair, jewelry, or expensive clothing."}, "1 Timothy 3": {"3:1–7": "Paul outlines the qualifications for an overseer (bishop) in the church, emphasizing that they must be above reproach, faithful in marriage, self-controlled, hospitable, able to teach, and manage their household well. They must not be recent converts or prone to pride, and they should have a good reputation with outsiders to avoid falling into disgrace.", "3:8–13": "Paul gives similar qualifications for deacons, highlighting the importance of integrity, self-control, and faithfulness in managing their households. Deacons should be tested and found blameless before serving. Those who serve well gain respect and confidence in their faith.", "3:16": "Paul concludes with a declaration of the mystery of godliness: that God was manifested in the flesh, vindicated by the Spirit, seen by angels, preached to the nations, believed on in the world, and taken up in glory.", "3:14–15": "Paul says he's writing so that, even if delayed, Timothy will know how people should conduct themselves in God's household, the church, which supports and displays the truth."}, "1 Timothy 4": {"4:4–5": "Paul warns that in the latter days, some will abandon the faith, following deceptive teachings and demonic doctrines. They will promote false asceticism, such as forbidding marriage and certain foods, which God created to be received with thanksgiving. Everything created by God is good when received with gratitude and sanctified by prayer.", "4:9–10": "Paul encourages Timothy to remind the believers of these truths, showing that he is a good minister of Christ, nourished in faith and sound doctrine. He advises Timothy to avoid pointless myths and instead focus on spiritual discipline, which has lasting value for both this life and the life to come.", "4:14–16": "Paul commands Timothy to teach these principles with authority, encouraging him not to let anyone look down on him for his youth but to set an example for believers in speech, conduct, love, faith, and purity. He reminds Timothy to devote himself to Scripture meditation, reading, teaching, and using the spiritual gift given to him, so that his progress will be evident to all.", "4:1–3": "Paul warns that in later times some will abandon the faith, following deceptive teachings that forbid marriage and certain foods that God actually created to be received with thanksgiving.", "4:6–8": "Paul tells Timothy that pointing these things out to the believers makes him a good servant of Christ, and encourages him to train himself for godliness, which is valuable for both this life and the next.", "4:11–13": "Paul tells Timothy not to let anyone look down on him for being young, but to be an example in speech, conduct, love, faith, and purity, and to focus on reading Scripture, exhortation, and teaching."}, "1 Timothy 5": {"5:1–2": "Paul instructs Timothy to treat older men in the church with the respect due to a father, and younger men as brothers. Likewise, older women should be treated as mothers, and younger women as sisters, with purity.", "5:7–8": "Paul encourages the church to care for widows who are truly in need. He stresses that if a widow has family, they should first provide for her, as this is pleasing to God. True widows, those who are alone and place their hope in God, should be supported by the church. However, those who live in self-indulgence are spiritually dead. Paul also emphasizes the responsibility of believers to care for their own families, warning that failing to do so is worse than unbelief.", "5:19–20": "Paul instructs the church to honor elders who lead well, especially those who work hard in preaching and teaching. He also emphasizes fair treatment of elders, urging that accusations against them should only be accepted on the testimony of two or three witnesses. Those who sin should be publicly rebuked, as a warning to others.", "5:22": "Paul urges Timothy to act impartially and fairly in his leadership, not showing favoritism. He also advises Timothy to be cautious in appointing leaders (laying on of hands) and to avoid being complicit in the sins of others by acting hastily. Timothy is encouraged to maintain his purity.", "5:23": "Paul gives Timothy practical advice for his health, suggesting that he drink a little wine to help with his stomach and frequent ailments.", "5:24–25": "Paul reminds Timothy that the sins of some people are obvious and lead to judgment, while the sins of others may only become evident later. Similarly, good deeds may be clear to all or may remain hidden, but eventually, they will be revealed.", "5:3–4": "Paul instructs that widows who are truly alone should be cared for, but if a widow has children or grandchildren, they should learn to care for her first, repaying their parents.", "5:5–6": "Paul describes a widow who is truly alone as one who puts her hope in God and continues in prayer, while a widow who lives for pleasure is spiritually dead even while alive.", "5:9–10": "Paul lists qualifications for a widow to be added to the church's official list of support — at least sixty, faithful to one husband, and known for good deeds like raising children and helping others.", "5:11–13": "Paul says younger widows shouldn't be added to the list, since they may later want to remarry and break their earlier commitment, and risk becoming idle gossips.", "5:14–15": "Paul recommends that younger widows remarry, have children, and manage their households, so they give no opportunity for criticism, since some had already gone astray.", "5:16": "Paul instructs believing family members to care for their own widows so the church isn't burdened, freeing it to help widows who truly have no one else.", "5:17–18": "Paul says elders who lead well, especially those who preach and teach, deserve to be honored and supported, quoting Scripture about not muzzling an ox and the worker deserving his wages.", "5:21": "Paul solemnly charges Timothy, before God and the angels, to follow these instructions without favoritism or prejudging anyone."}, "1 Timothy 6": {"6:1–2": "Paul instructs servants to show respect to their masters, whether they are believers or not, so that God’s name and His teachings will not be slandered. Servants who have believing masters should not take advantage of their shared faith but should serve them faithfully, recognizing that both are partakers of the blessings in Christ.", "6:9–10": "Paul warns against false teachers who promote division and view godliness as a means of financial gain. He contrasts this with the true gain that comes from godliness with contentment. Paul cautions against the desire to be rich, which leads to temptation and destruction, declaring that the love of money is the root of all kinds of evil.", "6:13–16": "Paul encourages Timothy to flee from the love of money and pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patience, and meekness. He calls on Timothy to fight the good fight of faith and hold firmly to eternal life. Paul charges Timothy to remain faithful and blameless until the return of Jesus Christ, the only immortal King of kings and Lord of lords.", "6:17–19": "Paul instructs Timothy to warn the rich not to be arrogant or to place their hope in uncertain wealth, but to trust in God. The rich are called to be generous and willing to share, storing up treasure for themselves in heaven and securing their place in eternal life.", "6:20–21": "Paul urges Timothy to guard the faith entrusted to him, avoiding godless chatter and false knowledge that has led some astray from the faith. He closes the letter with a blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor.", "6:3–5": "Paul warns about people who teach differently and don't agree with sound doctrine, describing them as conceited, obsessed with arguments, and wrongly treating godliness as a way to get rich.", "6:6–8": "Paul says that godliness paired with contentment is truly valuable, since we brought nothing into the world and can take nothing out, so having food and covering is enough.", "6:11–12": "Paul tells Timothy, as a man of God, to run from these temptations and instead pursue righteousness, faith, love, and endurance, fighting the good fight of faith and holding on to eternal life."}, "2 Timothy 1": {"1:1–2": "Paul begins his second letter to Timothy, his beloved spiritual son, extending blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, mercy, and peace from God the Father and Jesus Christ.", "1:3–5": "Paul expresses his constant gratitude to God for Timothy and remembers him in prayer day and night. He recalls Timothy's sincere faith, which was passed down from his grandmother Lois and his mother Eunice, and he is confident that this same faith is present in Timothy.", "1:6–7": "Paul encourages Timothy to rekindle the spiritual gift God gave him through the laying on of hands, reminding him that God has not given them a spirit of fear but of power, love, and self-discipline.", "1:11–12": "Paul urges Timothy not to be ashamed of the gospel or of Paul, who is in prison for the gospel's sake. Instead, Timothy is encouraged to join in suffering for the gospel by relying on God's power. Paul reminds Timothy that God has saved them and called them to a holy life—not because of their works, but because of His purpose and unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, given in Christ before time began. Christ has now revealed this by His defeat of death and His bringing of eternal life through the gospel.", "1:13–14": "Paul, though suffering for the gospel, declares his unwavering confidence in Christ. He knows whom he believes in and is convinced that Christ will guard what Paul has entrusted to Him. Paul exhorts Timothy to hold on to sound teaching, in faith and love, and to guard the gospel through the power of the Holy Spirit.", "1:16–18": "Paul mentions that many in Asia have abandoned him, including Phygellus and Hermogenes. However, he praises Onesiphorus for his loyalty, as he often refreshed Paul and was not ashamed of his imprisonment. Onesiphorus even sought Paul out while in Rome, and Paul prays for mercy for his household and for Onesiphorus at the day of the Lord.", "1:8–10": "Paul tells Timothy not to be ashamed of testifying about the Lord or of Paul's imprisonment, but to share in suffering for the gospel, since God saved and called them by His own purpose, now revealed through Christ, who defeated death and brought life to light.", "1:15": "Paul mentions sadly that everyone in the province of Asia has deserted him, including Phygellus and Hermogenes."}, "2 Timothy 2": {"2:1–2": "Paul encourages Timothy to be strong in the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of Christ and to entrust the teachings he received to faithful men who can teach others, continuing the work of spreading the gospel.", "2:3–7": "Paul compares the Christian life to that of a soldier, athlete, and farmer, each of whom must endure hardship and stay focused on their goal. A soldier avoids distractions to please his commander, an athlete competes according to the rules, and a farmer works hard to share in the harvest. Paul prays for Timothy to have understanding in all these things.", "2:8–10": "Paul reminds Timothy to remember Jesus Christ, who was raised from the dead, and to remain focused on the gospel. Though Paul suffers imprisonment for the sake of the gospel, the word of God is never bound. He endures everything for the sake of God’s chosen people, so they may receive salvation and eternal glory in Christ.", "2:11–13": "Paul shares a trustworthy statement: If believers die with Christ, they will also live with Him; if they endure, they will reign with Him. However, those who deny Christ will be denied by Him. Even when believers falter, Christ remains faithful to His promises, as He cannot deny His own nature.", "2:16–18": "Paul urges Timothy to remind the believers not to engage in pointless debates that lead to the destruction of their faith. He encourages Timothy to be a diligent worker, handling the word of truth accurately, and to avoid godless chatter that leads to ungodliness. He mentions the harmful teachings of Hymenaeus and Philetus, who have led some astray by claiming the resurrection has already happened.", "2:20–21": "Paul reassures Timothy that God’s foundation remains firm. God knows who belongs to Him, and those who claim to follow Christ must turn away from wickedness. Paul compares the church to a large house with vessels for noble and ignoble purposes. Those who cleanse themselves from dishonorable behavior will become useful vessels for God’s work.", "2:24–26": "Paul encourages Timothy to flee from youthful desires and instead pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace, along with those who call on the Lord with a pure heart. He warns against engaging in foolish arguments that lead to quarrels and instructs Timothy to be kind, patient, and gentle when correcting opponents, hoping that God will lead them to a change of heart and freedom from the devil’s snare.", "2:14–15": "Paul tells Timothy to remind people not to argue over words, since it does no good, and instead to work hard to be approved by God, correctly teaching the truth.", "2:19": "Paul says God's solid foundation stands firm, sealed with the truths that the Lord knows who belongs to Him, and that everyone who names Christ should turn from wrongdoing.", "2:22–23": "Paul tells Timothy to flee youthful desires and instead pursue righteousness, faith, love, and peace alongside other sincere believers, avoiding foolish arguments that only produce quarrels."}, "2 Timothy 3": {"3:1–5": "Paul warns Timothy that the last days will bring perilous times, marked by selfishness, pride, disobedience, and a love for pleasure rather than God. Although people may appear godly, they will deny the power of true godliness. Paul advises Timothy to avoid such individuals.", "3:8–9": "Paul describes false teachers who prey on vulnerable people and are always learning but never able to acknowledge the truth. He compares them to Jannes and Jambres, who opposed Moses, and declares that their foolishness will eventually be exposed.", "3:13": "Paul reminds Timothy of his teaching, conduct, purpose, faith, patience, love, and perseverance in the face of persecution. He notes that all who desire to live a godly life in Christ will face persecution, and warns that evil people will continue to deceive and be deceived as they grow worse.", "3:16–17": "Paul encourages Timothy to remain steadfast in the teachings he has received, reminding him that he has known the Scriptures from childhood, and they are able to lead him to salvation through faith in Christ. Paul affirms that all Scripture is inspired by God and is useful for teaching, rebuking, correcting, and training in righteousness, so that the servant of God may be fully equipped for every good work.", "3:6–7": "Paul warns about people who worm their way into households and take advantage of vulnerable women burdened by sin, who are always learning but never able to understand the truth.", "3:10–12": "Paul recalls how Timothy has closely followed his teaching, conduct, and faith, including the persecutions he endured, noting that everyone who wants to live a godly life in Christ will be persecuted.", "3:14–15": "Paul tells Timothy to continue in what he learned and became convinced of, since he has known the Scriptures from childhood, which are able to make him wise for salvation through faith in Christ."}, "2 Timothy 4": {"4:6–8": "Paul encourages Timothy to stay vigilant in all circumstances, to endure hardship, and to fulfill his ministry as an evangelist. Paul reflects on his own life, knowing that his time is near, and confidently declares that he has fought the good fight, finished his race, and kept the faith. He looks forward to the crown of righteousness that awaits him, which the Lord will give him and all who long for His appearing.", "4:11–13": "Paul makes personal requests, asking Timothy to come to him quickly. He notes that Demas has deserted him, and others have left for various reasons. Only Luke remains with him, and Paul asks Timothy to bring Mark, as he is helpful in ministry. He also asks for his cloak and important documents to be brought.", "4:16–18": "Paul recalls how Alexander the coppersmith opposed him and warns Timothy to be cautious of him. Paul also recounts how no one stood by him during his first defense, but he forgives them and trusts in the Lord, who strengthened him and delivered him. Paul expresses his confidence that the Lord will continue to deliver him and preserve him for His heavenly kingdom.", "4:22": "Paul sends final greetings to Prisca, Aquila, and the household of Onesiphorus, and mentions others who send their greetings. He urges Timothy to come before winter and concludes the letter with a blessing of the Lord’s presence and unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor.", "4:1–2": "Paul solemnly charges Timothy, before God and Christ, to preach the word at all times, correcting, rebuking, and encouraging with great patience and careful teaching.", "4:3–4": "Paul warns that a time is coming when people will reject sound teaching and instead gather teachers who tell them what they want to hear, turning to myths instead of truth.", "4:5": "Paul tells Timothy to stay level-headed through hardship, do the work of an evangelist, and fully carry out his ministry.", "4:9–10": "Paul asks Timothy to come quickly, sadly noting that Demas had abandoned him out of love for the world, while Crescens and Titus had gone elsewhere.", "4:14–15": "Paul warns Timothy about Alexander the metalworker, who did him great harm and strongly opposed their message.", "4:19–21": "Paul sends greetings to several people, mentions leaving Trophimus behind sick, and asks Timothy to come before winter, passing along greetings from others with him."}, "Hebrews 1": {"1:4–5": "The writer begins by declaring that God, who spoke to His people through prophets in the past, has now spoken through His Son, Jesus. Jesus, the heir of all things and the creator of the universe, radiates God’s glory and perfectly reflects His nature. After purging our sins, He sat down at the right hand of God, exalted far above the angels.", "1:13–14": "The author emphasizes that Jesus is far superior to the angels. God never called any angel His Son or told them to sit at His right hand. Instead, angels are created to serve God and minister to those who will inherit salvation, but Jesus is the eternal King whose throne lasts forever, and He is worshiped by angels."}, "Hebrews 2": {"2:1–4": "The author warns that if the message delivered by angels was binding and disobedience was justly punished, how much more will we face judgment if we neglect the salvation offered through Jesus Christ? This salvation was first announced by the Lord, confirmed by witnesses, and further testified by God through signs, wonders, and the gifts of the Holy Spirit.", "2:5–8": "The author references Psalm 8, noting that God made man a little lower than the angels and crowned him with glory and honor. God subjected all things to humanity, but the full realization of this is not yet seen.", "2:9–10": "Jesus was made lower than the angels for a time, in order to suffer death for everyone. Through His suffering, Jesus was crowned with glory and honor, and God used His sufferings to bring many to glory and to perfect the captain of their salvation.", "2:14–16": "Jesus, the one who sanctifies, shares the same humanity as those He sanctifies. He calls them His brothers and sisters, and through His death, He destroyed the power of death held by the devil, freeing those who were enslaved by the fear of death.", "2:17–18": "Jesus did not take on the nature of angels, but rather the seed of Abraham, becoming fully human. He did this so He could serve as a merciful and faithful high priest, making atonement for the sins of the people. Because He Himself suffered and was tempted, He is able to help those who are being tempted."}, "Hebrews 3": {"3:3–6": "The writer of Hebrews calls the Hebrews to consider Jesus, the faithful Apostle and High Priest, who is greater than Moses. While Moses was a faithful servant in God’s house, Christ is the Son over God’s house, and we are His house if we remain firm in our faith and hope.", "3:7–11": "The writer warns the readers not to harden their hearts as the Israelites did during their time in the wilderness. Despite seeing God’s works, they rebelled, and as a result, they did not enter God’s rest.", "3:12–15": "Believers are urged to encourage each other daily to remain faithful to God and to avoid being deceived. They are reminded that they share in Christ if they remain steadfast in their faith until the end.", "3:16–19": "The writer reflects on the Israelites who provoked God through their unbelief and rebellion during the forty years in the wilderness. Their unbelief prevented them from entering God’s promised rest, serving as a warning to the readers."}, "Hebrews 4": {"4:4–7": "The writer of Hebrews warns not to miss the promise of entering God’s rest, which is available to those who believe. Although the message was preached to those before, their lack of faith kept them from entering God’s rest. The writer emphasizes that today is the day to respond in faith and not harden hearts as in the past.", "4:8–11": "The rest that God offers is not just about physical rest but spiritual rest through faith in Christ. The writer urges believers to make every effort to enter this rest and not fall into the same unbelief that kept others from it.", "4:12–13": "The Word of God is alive and powerful, penetrating deep into the soul, discerning the thoughts and intentions of the heart. Nothing is hidden from God's sight; He sees everything, and all are accountable to Him.", "4:14–16": "Jesus is our great high priest, who understands our weaknesses because He was tempted in every way yet did not sin. Because of this, believers can approach God's throne of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor with confidence, to receive mercy and help in times of need."}, "Hebrews 5": {"5:1–3": "The role of the high priest is to represent people before God, offering gifts and sacrifices for their sins. The high priest, being human, understands the weaknesses of others because he shares in those same weaknesses. This role is not self-appointed but a divine calling, as it was for Aaron.", "5:5–6": "Christ did not exalt Himself to become a high priest. Instead, God appointed Him, saying, You are My Son; today I have become Your Father, and declaring Him a priest forever in the order of Melchizedek.", "5:9–10": "During His earthly life, Jesus offered up prayers with loud cries and tears to the One who could save Him from death. Even though He was God's Son, He learned obedience through His suffering. Once perfected through this, He became the source of eternal salvation for all who obey Him, appointed by God as a high priest in the order of Melchizedek.", "5:13–14": "The writer expresses concern that the believers have become spiritually dull and need to be taught the basics of God’s word again. Instead of being teachers, they need spiritual milk because they are not yet ready for solid food. Mature believers, on the other hand, can handle deeper teachings because they have trained themselves to distinguish between good and evil."}, "Hebrews 6": {"6:3": "The writer of Hebrews encourages believers to move beyond the basic teachings about Christ and press on to maturity, without needing to continually lay again the foundational truths of repentance from dead works, faith in God, baptisms, laying on of hands, the resurrection, and eternal judgment.", "6:4–6": "The writer warns that those who have experienced the enlightenment of salvation, been partakers of the Holy Spirit, and tasted the power of God’s Word, if they fall away, it is impossible to bring them back to repentance because they are essentially crucifying Christ again and exposing Him to public disgrace.", "6:7–8": "The writer compares believers to land that receives rain. When the land produces useful crops, it is blessed by God. However, land that produces thorns and thistles is worthless and in danger of being cursed and burned, illustrating the outcome of fruitlessness and unfaithfulness.", "6:11–12": "Although the writer issues a stern warning, he expresses confidence that the believers will show the fruits of salvation. He reassures them that God will not forget their acts of love and service toward His people, urging them to continue diligently in faith, hope, and patience, just as others have done who have inherited God’s promises.", "6:16–17": "The writer reminds the readers of God’s promise to Abraham. Since there was no one greater to swear by, God swore by Himself, assuring Abraham of His blessing and the multiplying of his descendants. After Abraham patiently waited, he received what was promised. People use oaths to confirm things, and God did so to assure His promise to Abraham.", "6:19–20": "God confirmed His unchanging promise to Abraham with an oath, providing assurance to all who are heirs of His promises. Because it is impossible for God to lie, believers can have confidence in the hope set before them. This hope serves as a secure and steadfast anchor for the soul, reaching into the very presence of God, where Jesus, the forerunner, has gone ahead, becoming our high priest forever after the order of Melchizedek."}, "Hebrews 7": {"7:3": "The author introduces Melchizedek, a unique figure who is both king and priest. He met Abraham and blessed him, and Abraham gave him a tenth of everything. Melchizedek’s priesthood is eternal, without genealogy, and foreshadows Christ’s eternal priesthood.", "7:6–7": "The author highlights the greatness of Melchizedek by showing that even Abraham, the patriarch, gave him a tenth of the spoils. The Levitical priests collected tithes from their fellow Israelites, but Melchizedek, who is not of their lineage, received tithes from Abraham and blessed him.", "7:11–12": "The argument points out that if the Levitical priesthood could bring perfection, there would be no need for another priest to arise in the order of Melchizedek. This highlights the limitations of the old priesthood and the need for a superior one.", "7:13–14": "The change in priesthood also necessitates a change in the law. Jesus, who came from the tribe of Judah, not Levi, represents a new kind of priesthood, different from the Levitical one.", "7:18–19": "Jesus’ priesthood, like Melchizedek’s, is based not on lineage but on the power of an indestructible life. The old commandment is set aside because it was weak and could not bring perfection. Jesus introduces a better hope, through which we can draw near to God.", "7:20–22": "Jesus’ priesthood is guaranteed by an oath from God, unlike the Levitical priests who were not sworn in by an oath. This oath makes Jesus the guarantor of a better covenant.", "7:23–25": "Unlike the Levitical priests who were many because they died, Jesus has a permanent priesthood. He is able to save completely those who come to God through Him because He always lives to intercede for them.", "7:26–28": "Jesus, our perfect High Priest, is holy, blameless, pure, and exalted above the heavens. Unlike the Levitical priests who had to offer sacrifices daily for their own sins and for the people’s, Jesus offered Himself once for all. The law appointed weak men as priests, but God’s oath has appointed His Son, perfect forever."}, "Hebrews 8": {"8:3–5": "The writer summarizes the earlier discussions, declaring that Jesus is the superior High Priest seated at the right hand of God in heaven. Jesus serves in the true tabernacle, not made by human hands, and, as a High Priest, He must also offer something to God.", "8:6–7": "If Jesus were serving on earth, He would not be a priest in the Levitical system, as those priests already serve according to the law. But their ministry was only a shadow and copy of heavenly things. Jesus, however, has obtained a far superior ministry as the mediator of a better covenant, established on better promises.", "8:8–9": "The need for a new covenant arose because the first covenant was not faultless. God Himself declared that He would establish a new covenant with the people of Israel and Judah, different from the covenant made with their ancestors when they were brought out of Egypt—a covenant they did not keep.", "8:10–12": "The new covenant is different because God will put His laws directly into the minds and hearts of His people. They will have a direct relationship with Him, and He will be their God. Everyone, from the least to the greatest, will know the Lord personally. Furthermore, God promises to be merciful toward their wrongdoing and will remember their sins no more.", "8:13": "By calling this a new covenant, God made the first one obsolete. And what is obsolete and aging will soon disappear."}, "Hebrews 9": {"9:3–5": "The earthly tabernacle had specific regulations and items for worship, divided into sections—the first part known as the holy place and the second part, behind the veil, called the Most Holy Place. Inside were the Ark of the Covenant, the golden pot of manna, Aaron’s rod, and the stone tablets of the covenant, with cherubim overshadowing the mercy seat.", "9:8–10": "Under the old covenant, the priests regularly entered the outer part of the tabernacle to perform their duties, but only the high priest could enter the Most Holy Place once a year with blood sacrifices for himself and the people’s sins. These rituals pointed to a time of greater fulfillment, but they could not cleanse the conscience.", "9:13–14": "Christ entered the greater, heavenly tabernacle—not by the blood of animals but by His own blood—once for all, securing eternal redemption. If animal sacrifices could purify outwardly, how much more will the blood of Christ cleanse our consciences from dead works to serve the living God?", "9:18–20": "Christ is the mediator of the new covenant, through His death, redeeming the transgressions under the first covenant so that those called may receive the promise of eternal inheritance. Like a will, the new covenant came into effect through the death of Christ, just as the old covenant was established with blood.", "9:27–28": "The law required the shedding of blood for the forgiveness of sins, but Christ offered a better sacrifice, entering heaven itself on our behalf. Unlike the high priests who offered yearly sacrifices, Christ offered Himself once for all to put away sin. He will return a second time to bring salvation to those who eagerly await Him."}, "Hebrews 10": {"10:3–4": "The law and its sacrifices were only a shadow of the good things to come through Christ. The sacrifices, offered continually, could never make people perfect or cleanse their consciences fully. They served as a reminder of sins year after year, but the blood of animals could not take away sins.", "10:10": "Christ came into the world to fulfill God’s will, and through His obedience, He offered Himself as the perfect sacrifice. He took away the old system of sacrifices and established the new covenant. Through His once-for-all sacrifice, believers are sanctified.", "10:14": "While the priests repeatedly offered sacrifices that could never take away sins, Jesus offered one sacrifice for all time and then sat down at the right hand of God. Through His single offering, He has perfected forever those who are being sanctified.", "10:18": "The Holy Spirit testifies to the reality of the new covenant, in which God writes His laws on believers’ hearts and minds. He promises to remember their sins no more, and because their sins are forgiven, there is no longer any need for offerings for sin.", "10:23–25": "With confidence in Jesus’ sacrifice, believers are encouraged to draw near to God, hold fast to their faith, and spur one another on toward love and good deeds. They are not to neglect meeting together but should encourage each other as they await the day of Christ’s return.", "10:28–29": "The writer warns that those who deliberately continue in sin after knowing the truth face the fearful expectation of judgment. He compares the severe punishment for rejecting the law of Moses to the even greater punishment for those who trample on the Son of God and insult the Spirit of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor.", "10:34": "The writer encourages believers to remember how they endured suffering and persecution after they came to faith. He reminds them that they showed compassion for him in his imprisonment and joyfully accepted the loss of earthly possessions, knowing that they have eternal treasures in heaven.", "10:37–39": "The writer urges believers not to throw away their confidence in Christ, as it brings great reward. He encourages them to be patient in doing God’s will so they may receive His promise. He reminds them that the righteous live by faith, and assures them that they are not of those who shrink back but are of those who believe and are saved."}, "Hebrews 11": {"11:1–3": "Faith is described as the assurance of things hoped for and the conviction of things not seen. It was by faith that the elders (ancient believers) were commended by God. Through faith, we understand that the universe was formed at God's command.", "11:4–7": "Abel offered a better sacrifice than Cain because of his faith, and though he is dead, his faith still speaks. Enoch's faith was so strong that he never experienced death, and his life was a testimony of pleasing God. It is impossible to please God without faith, as faith acknowledges His existence and His reward for those who earnestly seek Him. Noah's faith led him to obey God's warning about things not yet seen. He built the ark, saving his family and becoming an heir of the righteousness that comes by faith.", "11:8–12": "Abraham obeyed God and left his homeland, not knowing where he was going. He lived in the land of promise as a foreigner, looking forward to the city built by God. By faith, Sarah received the power to conceive even though she was past childbearing age, trusting in God's faithfulness. From Abraham, as good as dead, came descendants as numerous as the stars and the sand by the seashore.", "11:13–16": "All these people died in faith without receiving the promises, but they saw them from a distance, embraced them, and acknowledged they were strangers and pilgrims on earth. They longed for a better, heavenly country, and because of their faith, God was not ashamed to be called their God.", "11:17–22": "By faith, Abraham was willing to sacrifice his son Isaac, believing that God could raise him from the dead. Abraham trusted that the promises of God would still be fulfilled through Isaac. Isaac, Jacob, and Joseph demonstrated faith by blessing their descendants and speaking of the future that God had promised. Joseph, in particular, spoke of the Exodus of Israel from Egypt and gave instructions about his burial in the promised land.", "11:23–29": "Moses' faith led him to reject the riches and power of Egypt, choosing instead to identify with the suffering people of God. He valued suffering for Christ above the treasures of Egypt and led Israel out of bondage, keeping the Passover by faith, trusting in God's deliverance.", "11:30–31": "By faith, the Israelites passed through the Red Sea on dry ground, while the Egyptians were drowned. By faith, the walls of Jericho fell after Israel marched around them for seven days. By faith, Rahab was saved because she welcomed the spies in peace.", "11:36–38": "The writer of Hebrews highlights more heroes of faith—people like Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David, and Samuel—who conquered kingdoms, received promises, and endured persecution by faith. These individuals overcame unimaginable hardships, and some even faced death without accepting deliverance, all for the hope of a better resurrection.", "11:39–40": "Though these faithful ones received commendation for their faith, they did not receive the ultimate fulfillment of God's promise. That promise was made perfect through Christ, and now, both they and we share in the fullness of God's plan."}, "Hebrews 12": {"12:1–4": "The author encourages believers to lay aside every hindrance and sin and to run the race of faith with endurance, looking to Jesus, who is the author and finisher of their faith. Jesus endured the cross, disregarding its shame, because of the joy set before Him, and now He is seated at the right hand of God.", "12:5–11": "Believers are encouraged to consider Jesus’ endurance in the face of opposition from sinners, so they won’t grow weary. They are reminded that God disciplines those He loves, just as a father disciplines his children. This discipline is a sign of God’s love and His acceptance of them as His children. Enduring God’s discipline is evidence that believers are His children. Earthly fathers discipline their children physically, and those children respect them. How much more should believers submit to their heavenly Father, who disciplines us spiritually, that they may share in His holiness.", "12:12–17": "Although discipline is painful at the moment, it later produces a harvest of righteousness and peace for those who have been trained by it. Believers are encouraged to strengthen their weak hands and knees and to walk in a straight path, so they may be healed. Believers are urged to pursue peace and holiness because without holiness, no one will see the Lord. They are also warned against missing out on God’s unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and allowing bitterness to grow. The example of Esau is given, who sold his birthright for a single meal and was later unable to reclaim it, even with tears.", "12:18–24": "The writer contrasts the old covenant, represented by Mount Sinai with its fear and trembling, with the new covenant, represented by Mount Zion and the heavenly Jerusalem. Believers have come to the joyful assembly of God’s people, to God Himself, and to Jesus, the mediator of a new covenant, whose blood speaks a better word than the blood of Abel.", "12:25–29": "Believers are warned not to refuse God’s voice. If the Israelites did not escape when they refused God on earth, how much more serious is it to turn away from Him who speaks from heaven. God’s voice will shake not only the earth but also the heavens, removing all that can be shaken so that what cannot be shaken will remain. Since believers are receiving an unshakable kingdom, they are called to worship God with reverence and awe, knowing that He is a consuming fire."}, "Hebrews 13": {"13:1–6": "The writer encourages believers to continue in brotherly love, hospitality, and compassion, reminding them that by showing kindness to strangers, some have unknowingly hosted angels. He also urges them to remember those who are imprisoned or suffering as though they are sharing in their pain. The writer affirms that marriage is to be honored and kept pure, warning that God will judge those who engage in sexual immorality. He encourages contentment and trust in God's provision, reminding believers that God has promised never to leave or forsake them, so they can confidently declare, The Lord is my helper; I will not fear.", "13:7–14": "The writer urges believers to remember and follow the faith of their leaders who taught them the word of God, reminding them that Jesus Christ remains the same forever. He warns against being swayed by strange teachings and encourages them to be strengthened by unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, not by dietary regulations that hold no lasting value. The writer explains that believers have a spiritual altar, distinct from the old sacrificial system, and points to Jesus, who sanctified the people through His blood by suffering outside the city. He calls believers to follow Jesus, bearing any shame or reproach, knowing that their true home is in the heavenly city yet to come.", "13:15–19": "The writer encourages believers to continually offer sacrifices of praise and thanksgiving to God, while also remembering to do good and share with others, as these acts of kindness are pleasing to God. The writer instructs believers to obey and submit to their spiritual leaders, who care for their souls and must give an account to God. He asks for their prayers, confident that they are striving to live with a clear conscience and integrity.", "13:20–25": "The writer closes his letter with a benediction, asking God to equip the believers with everything they need to do His will, working in them through Jesus Christ for His glory. He asks the believers to receive his words of exhortation and mentions Timothy’s release. He sends greetings and ends with a final blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor."}, "James 1": {"1:1": "James opens his letter by identifying himself as a servant of God and the Lord Jesus Christ. He addresses it to the twelve tribes scattered among the nations, acknowledging their dispersion.", "1:4": "James encourages believers to view trials as opportunities for growth, as these tests strengthen their patience and develop spiritual maturity, leading them toward lacking nothing.", "1:8": "James teaches that God generously grants wisdom to those who ask in faith without doubting. Doubt leads to instability, as wavering in faith makes a person spiritually unsettled.", "1:11": "James highlights the contrast between the humble and the wealthy. While the poor can rejoice in their exaltation, the rich are reminded of the fleeting nature of their wealth, which fades like a withering flower under the sun.", "1:15": "James explains that God is not the source of temptation; rather, temptation arises from personal desires that lead to sin. When sin matures, it results in death, but enduring temptation leads to the reward of eternal life.", "1:18": "James affirms that all good and perfect gifts come from God, who is unchanging in His goodness. He reminds believers that they are a new creation, brought to life by God’s truth, chosen to be His firstfruits.", "1:25": "James emphasizes the importance of being not just hearers of the Word, but doers. Hearing without action leads to self-deception, while listening and responding to God’s Word brings about salvation.", "1:27": "James concludes that true religion involves both care for the vulnerable and living a life of purity. Words must be controlled, and actions must reflect genuine compassion and holiness"}, "James 2": {"2:4": "James warns against showing favoritism in the community of believers. He uses the example of treating the rich and poor differently, teaching that partiality reveals a judgmental and sinful heart.", "2:7": "James reminds believers that God has chosen the poor to be rich in faith and heirs of His kingdom. He questions why they would favor the rich, who often oppress them and blaspheme the name of Christ.", "2:13": "James teaches that the royal law calls believers to love their neighbors as themselves. Breaking even one part of God’s law makes a person guilty of breaking it all. He encourages believers to speak and act as those judged by the law of liberty, which brings mercy over judgment.", "2:17": "James challenges the idea that a mere profession of faith without works is sufficient, using the example of someone offering words without providing real help. Faith that is not accompanied by works is dead and useless.", "2:24": "James uses Abraham’s example to show that true faith in Christ Jesus produces good fruit which is then visible to those around you. A mere belief or mental assent to a God is dead faith.", "2:26": "James concludes with the story of Rahab, who demonstrated her faith by hiding the spies, showing that her actions were the proof of her belief. Faith, without works, is dead just like a body without the spirit."}, "James 3": {"3:6–8": "James emphasizes the immense power of the tongue. Like a bit in a horse’s mouth or a rudder steering a ship, the tongue has the potential to influence the whole body. It can be destructive, like a small spark igniting a great fire, and can lead to widespread harm if not controlled.", "3:9–12": "James teaches that while humans can tame all kinds of animals, the tongue is notoriously difficult to control. It has the capacity to bless God and curse people, yet such contradictions should not exist in the lives of believers. Just as a fountain cannot produce both fresh and salt water, the tongue should not be used for both blessing and cursing.", "3:17–18": "James contrasts earthly, selfish wisdom with wisdom from above. Earthly wisdom leads to envy, strife, and disorder, while heavenly wisdom is pure, peace-loving, gentle, full of mercy, and sincere. True righteousness grows in an environment of peace and is nurtured by those who promote harmony."}, "James 4": {"4:4–6": "James explains that conflicts and strife come from selfish desires within people. These desires cause people to seek fulfillment from worldly sources rather than from God. He warns against friendship with the world, which places a person at odds with God. Yet, God's unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor is available to the humble, while the proud face resistance.", "4:7–10": "James urges believers to submit to God and resist the devil. By drawing near to God, they can experience a close relationship with Him. He encourages humility, as those who humble themselves before God will be exalted by Him.", "4:11–12": "James warns against speaking evil or judging others. Since God alone is the ultimate judge, believers should refrain from taking on that role. Instead, they are called to follow God and leave judgment to Him.", "4:13–17": "James cautions against boasting about future plans, as life is uncertain and short, like a vapor that vanishes. Instead, believers should acknowledge God’s will in their plans. Boasting in one’s own abilities and control over the future is sinful, and failing to do what one knows is right is also a sin."}, "James 5": {"5:4–6": "James delivers a strong warning to the wealthy who have hoarded riches and lived indulgent lives while oppressing others. Their accumulated wealth will testify against them in the last days, and their fraudulent actions will bring God’s judgment.", "5:10–11": "James encourages believers to be patient and endure suffering, knowing that the Lord’s return is near. He points to the example of farmers who wait patiently for their crops and to the prophets and Job, who endured affliction with faith. God’s mercy will prevail for those who persevere.", "5:16–18": "James emphasizes the power of prayer in all situations, from times of suffering to times of joy. He encourages believers to pray for the sick and one another, explaining that the prayer of a righteous person is powerful and effective, as illustrated by the prophet Elijah’s fervent prayers.", "5:19–20": "James concludes by encouraging believers to restore those who wander from the truth. Turning a sinner back to God brings salvation and covers over many sins."}, "1 Peter 1": {"1:1–2": "Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ, addresses believers scattered throughout various regions. He reminds them that they are chosen according to God's foreknowledge, sanctified by the Spirit, and called to obedience through the blood of Christ. He extends blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace.", "1:4–5": "Peter praises God for His abundant mercy, which has given believers new birth into a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ. This hope leads to an inheritance that is imperishable, undefiled, and kept in heaven. Believers are guarded by God's power through faith, awaiting the full revelation of salvation in the last days.", "1:8–9": "Peter encourages believers to rejoice even in the midst of trials, as these test their faith, which is more valuable than gold. The outcome of their faith is the salvation of their souls, a truth that fills them with joy in Christ, even though they have not seen Him.", "1:12": "Peter explains that the prophets of old searched and inquired about the salvation that was prophesied for the believers. They were guided by the Spirit of Christ as they foretold the sufferings and subsequent glory of the Messiah. This salvation, now preached through the gospel, was not for the prophets themselves but for the believers, and even angels long to understand it.", "1:14–16": "Peter calls believers to prepare their minds for action, to be sober-minded, and to set their hope fully on the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor that will be revealed when Jesus returns. As children of obedience, they are to reject their former ways and strive to live holy lives, reflecting the holiness of the One who has called them.", "1:20–21": "Peter reminds believers that they were redeemed not with perishable things like silver or gold, but with the precious blood of Christ, a perfect sacrifice chosen before the world began. Through Christ’s death and resurrection, believers have faith and hope in God.", "1:24–25": "Peter encourages believers to love one another deeply from a pure heart, as they have been born again through the eternal and incorruptible word of God. While human life and glory fade like grass and flowers, the word of the Lord endures forever, and this is the gospel message they have received."}, "1 Peter 2": {"2:4–5": "Peter encourages believers to lay aside malice and deceit, desiring the pure milk of God's word so they may grow spiritually. He describes believers as living stones being built into a spiritual house, a holy priesthood offering spiritual sacrifices through Jesus Christ.", "2:7–8": "Peter refers to Jesus as the cornerstone, chosen by God and precious. For those who believe, He is a foundation of strength. But for those who reject Him, He becomes a stumbling stone, causing offense because they do not obey God's word.", "2:10": "Peter declares that believers are a chosen people, a royal priesthood, and a holy nation, called to proclaim God's praises. They have been called out of darkness into His marvelous light, having once been without mercy but now receiving mercy.", "2:11–12": "Peter urges believers to abstain from sinful desires that wage war against their souls and to live good lives among non-believers so that their good deeds may lead others to glorify God.", "2:15–17": "Peter instructs believers to submit to human authorities for the Lord’s sake, recognizing that it is God’s will for them to do good, which silences the ignorance of foolish people. They are to honor everyone, love their fellow believers, fear God, and respect those in authority.", "2:19–20": "Peter advises slaves to submit to their masters, whether kind or harsh. He points out that enduring unjust suffering for doing good is commendable before God, as it shows obedience to God’s will.", "2:25": "Peter reminds believers that they are called to endure suffering, following the example of Christ, who did no wrong but endured suffering without retaliation. Jesus bore our sins in His body on the cross so that we might die to sin and live righteously. By His wounds, we are healed, and through Him, we have returned to the Shepherd of our souls."}, "1 Peter 3": {"3:4": "Peter encourages wives to submit to their husbands in a way that reflects a godly character, so that even those who do not believe may be won over by their pure conduct. He advises wives to focus on inner beauty—gentle and quiet spirits that are precious in God's sight—rather than outward adornments.", "3:6": "Peter reminds wives that holy women of old, like Sarah, adorned themselves with godliness by submitting to their husbands and trusting in God. He encourages them to follow their example without fear.", "3:7": "Peter instructs husbands to treat their wives with understanding, honoring them as co-heirs of the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of life, so that their prayers will not be hindered.", "3:9": "Peter calls on all believers to live in harmony, showing love, compassion, and humility. Instead of repaying evil with evil or insult with insult, they are called to bless others, knowing that they themselves will inherit a blessing.", "3:12": "Peter quotes from the Psalms, urging believers to turn away from evil, seek peace, and pursue good, knowing that the Lord watches over the righteous and hears their prayers, while His face is against those who do evil.", "3:17": "Peter encourages believers not to fear persecution or suffering for doing good. Instead, they should honor Christ in their hearts and be prepared to defend their hope with gentleness and respect, keeping a clear conscience. It is better to suffer for doing good, if it is God’s will, than for doing evil.", "3:20": "Peter reminds them that Christ also suffered, the righteous for the unrighteous, to bring people to God. After His death, He made a proclamation to the spirits in prison, who had been disobedient in the days of Noah. Only a few—eight souls—were saved in the ark.", "3:22": "Peter explains that baptism symbolizes salvation, not by cleansing the body, but by providing a clear conscience before God through the resurrection of Jesus Christ. Christ has ascended into heaven, where He reigns at the right hand of God, with all angels, authorities, and powers in submission to Him."}, "1 Peter 4": {"4:6": "Peter reminds believers to take on the mindset of Christ, who suffered in the flesh, and to no longer live according to sinful human desires, but to the will of God. He explains that those who reject the Christian way may speak evil of believers but will be held accountable to God, the judge of the living and the dead.", "4:11": "Peter urges believers to live soberly, with constant prayer, knowing that the end is near. He calls for fervent love, hospitality, and the faithful use of spiritual gifts to serve others, ensuring that in all things, God may be glorified through Jesus Christ.", "4:16": "Peter encourages believers not to be surprised by suffering but to rejoice in it, as it is a participation in Christ’s sufferings. He reminds them that if they are insulted for Christ’s name, they are blessed, for the Spirit of God rests upon them. However, suffering should not come as a result of wrongdoing. If one suffers for being a Christian, they should not be ashamed but should glorify God.", "4:19": "Peter explains that judgment begins with the household of God and that if it starts with the righteous, the fate of those who disobey the gospel will be even more severe. He concludes by encouraging believers who suffer according to God's will to entrust their souls to their faithful Creator and continue to do good."}, "1 Peter 5": {"5:4": "Peter exhorts the elders of the church to shepherd God’s flock willingly and eagerly, not out of compulsion or for personal gain. They are to be examples to the believers and await the unfading crown of glory when Christ, the Chief Shepherd, returns.", "5:7": "Peter calls on the younger believers to submit to their elders and urges everyone to live humbly toward one another, for God opposes the proud but gives unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor to the humble. He encourages them to humble themselves under God’s mighty hand, trusting Him to exalt them at the proper time, and to cast all their anxieties on Him because He cares for them.", "5:9": "Peter warns believers to be sober-minded and watchful, as the devil prowls like a roaring lion seeking someone to devour. He encourages them to resist the devil, standing firm in the faith, knowing that fellow believers around the world are enduring similar trials.", "5:11": "Peter comforts the believers by reminding them that the God of all unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor has called them to eternal glory in Christ. After they have suffered for a little while, God Himself will restore, strengthen, and establish them. To Him be glory and power forever.", "5:14": "Peter closes the letter with greetings, noting that Silvanus helped him write it and testifying that the believers stand in the true unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God. He sends greetings from the church in Babylon and from Mark, and ends with a blessing of peace for all in Christ."}, "2 Peter 1": {"1:1–2": "Peter begins his letter by addressing believers who share the same precious faith through the righteousness of God and Jesus Christ. He extends blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace, praying that these be multiplied as they grow in their knowledge of God and Jesus.", "1:3–4": "Peter reminds believers that God’s divine power has provided everything necessary for life and godliness through the knowledge of Him. By His great promises, believers can partake in the divine nature, escaping the corruption in the world caused by evil desires.", "1:8–9": "Peter encourages believers to make every effort to grow in faith, virtue, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly kindness, and love. He explains that if these qualities abound in them, they will be effective and fruitful in their knowledge of Jesus Christ.", "1:10–11": "Peter warns that those who do not grow in these virtues are spiritually shortsighted and forget that they have been cleansed from their past sins. He urges believers to be diligent in confirming their calling and election, assuring them that doing so will keep them from falling and will provide a rich welcome into Christ's eternal kingdom.", "1:14–15": "Peter acknowledges that the believers already know the truth but commits to continually reminding them as long as he lives. He knows his death is near, as Jesus had revealed to him, and he wants to ensure that they remember these truths even after he is gone.", "1:16–18": "Peter affirms that the message of Jesus’ power and return is not based on myths or human invention. He and others were eyewitnesses to Jesus' majesty, recalling the moment they heard God’s voice affirming Jesus as His beloved Son during the Transfiguration on the holy mountain.", "1:20–21": "Peter encourages believers to pay close attention to the prophetic word, which is like a light shining in a dark place, until the day when Christ, the morning star, rises in their hearts. He reminds them that Scripture's prophecy is not open to private interpretation but came as men were moved by the Holy Spirit, not by their own will."}, "2 Peter 2": {"2:1–3": "Peter warns that just as there were false prophets in the past, there will be false teachers who will secretly introduce destructive heresies, even denying the Lord who bought them. Many will follow their corrupt ways, and because of them, the way of truth will be slandered. Their greed will lead them to exploit others with lies, but their judgment is certain and imminent.", "2:10–11": "Peter emphasizes that if God did not spare the angels who sinned, nor the ancient world but brought judgment through the flood, nor Sodom and Gomorrah but turned them to ashes, He will certainly bring judgment upon the wicked. However, God also knows how to rescue the godly from trials, as He did with Noah and Lot.", "2:15–16": "False teachers are described as bold, arrogant, and slanderous, lacking understanding of spiritual matters. They indulge in sinful pleasures, deceive others, and have hearts trained in greed. Peter compares them to Balaam, who was led astray by the love of money and was rebuked by a donkey.", "2:17–19": "False teachers are compared to empty wells and storm-driven clouds, offering nothing of substance. They speak boastfully and entice people with sinful desires, promising freedom while being enslaved by corruption themselves.", "2:20–22": "Peter warns that those who have known the way of righteousness and then turn back to the corruptions of the world are in a worse state than before. He likens their return to sin to a dog returning to its vomit or a washed pig returning to the mud."}, "2 Peter 3": {"3:7": "Peter writes this second letter to remind the believers of the teachings of the prophets and apostles, warning them that scoffers will come in the last days, questioning the promise of Christ’s return. These scoffers willfully ignore the fact that the heavens and earth were created by God's word, and the same word keeps the present heavens and earth for the day of judgment by fire.", "3:10": "Peter explains that God’s sense of time is different from ours. Though it may seem like a delay, the Lord is not slow in fulfilling His promises but is patient, wanting everyone to come to repentance. However, the day of the Lord will come unexpectedly, like a thief, and the heavens and earth will be destroyed by fire.", "3:13": "In light of the coming judgment and the destruction of the present world, Peter urges believers to live holy and godly lives, looking forward to and eagerly awaiting the day of God. He reminds them of the promise of a new heaven and new earth, where righteousness will dwell.", "3:16": "Peter encourages believers to be diligent in living blameless and peaceful lives as they await the fulfillment of God’s promises. He also acknowledges that Paul, in his letters, wrote about these same things, though some of Paul’s writings are difficult to understand and are twisted by unstable people to their own destruction.", "3:18": "Peter concludes by warning believers to be on guard against being led astray by false teachings. Instead, they should grow in unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and the knowledge of Jesus Christ, giving Him glory both now and forever."}, "1 John 1": {"1:3–4": "John begins by proclaiming the eternal Word of life, which they have seen and touched—Jesus Christ. He shares this so that believers may have fellowship with one another and with the Father and the Son, and that their joy may be complete.", "1:7": "John declares that God is light, and there is no darkness in Him. If we claim to have fellowship with God but live in darkness, we are lying. However, if we walk in the light, we have fellowship with each other, and Jesus’ blood cleanses us from all sin.", "1:10": "John emphasizes that if we claim to be without sin, we are deceiving ourselves. But if we confess our sins, God is faithful and just, and He will forgive and cleanse us from all unrighteousness. Denying our sin makes us liars and contradicts God's truth."}, "1 John 2": {"2:2": "John encourages believers not to sin but reassures them that if anyone does sin, they have an advocate in Jesus Christ, who is the atoning sacrifice for their sins and for the sins of the entire world.", "2:5–6": "John explains that the evidence of knowing God is obedience to His commands. Those who claim to know Him but do not obey are deceiving themselves. True believers, however, show their love for God by keeping His word and walking as Jesus did.", "2:10–11": "John reminds the believers of the command to love one another, which they have heard from the beginning. He points out that those who claim to live in the light but harbor hatred toward others are still in darkness, while those who love others truly live in the light and will not stumble.", "2:14": "John offers encouragement to believers of all stages—children, fathers, and young men—reassuring them that their sins are forgiven, that they have known God, and that they have overcome the evil one by the strength of God's word.", "2:16–17": "John warns against loving the world and its temptations—physical desires, materialism, and pride—reminding believers that the world is temporary, but those who do God's will live forever.", "2:20–21": "John speaks of the antichrists—false teachers who had left the faith. He assures the believers that they have received the Holy Spirit (the anointing) and know the truth, which sets them apart from the deception of these false teachers.", "2:24–25": "John emphasizes that those who deny Jesus as the Christ are antichrists and are separated from the Father. He encourages the believers to remain in the truth they have known from the beginning, which ensures their relationship with the Father and the Son and promises them eternal life.", "2:28–29": "John reminds the believers that they have received the Holy Spirit, who teaches them all things and keeps them in the truth. He urges them to continue abiding in Christ, so they will have confidence when He returns and not be ashamed. Those who practice righteousness show that they are born of God."}, "1 John 3": {"3:2–3": "John marvels at the great love of God, which allows us to be called His children. Though the world may not recognize us because it did not know Him, we have the hope that when Christ returns, we shall be like Him, for we shall see Him as He is. This hope leads believers to pursue purity, just as Christ is pure.", "3:9": "John explains that anyone who continues in sin is living in rebellion against God’s law. However, Jesus came to take away sin, and those who remain in Him do not continue to sin. A clear distinction is made: those who live righteously are of God, while those who practice sin are of the devil. Christ came to destroy the works of the devil, and those born of God cannot live in ongoing sin.", "3:15": "John defines the clear distinction between the children of God and the children of the devil: those who live righteously and love others are of God. John emphasizes the command to love one another, contrasting this with Cain’s hatred, which led to murder. Hatred, John teaches, is akin to murder, and those who hate cannot have eternal life in them.", "3:17–18": "John teaches that we understand love by looking to Christ, who laid down His life for us. We, too, should be willing to lay down our lives for one another. True love is not just spoken but demonstrated through action. If someone has material possessions and sees a brother in need but does not show compassion, God’s love is not in them.", "3:21–22": "John assures believers that even when our hearts condemn us, we can trust that God is greater than our hearts and knows everything. When we do not feel condemned, we can approach God with confidence, knowing that He hears our prayers because we seek to do His will and obey His commands.", "3:23–24": "John sums up God’s command: to believe in Jesus Christ and to love one another. Those who obey His commands live in Him, and He in them. We know that Christ lives in us because of the Holy Spirit He has given us."}, "1 John 4": {"4:6": "John encourages believers to test every spirit to determine whether it is from God, reminding them that false prophets exist in the world. A true spirit will confess that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh, while a false one will deny this truth. John assures the believers that they belong to God and have overcome the false spirits because God's power within them is greater than anything in the world.", "4:11": "John reminds the believers that love originates from God and that everyone who loves is born of God and knows Him. He declares that God is love, and He demonstrated His love by sending His only Son into the world to save humanity. Therefore, if God has loved us in this way, we ought to love one another.", "4:16": "John explains that no one has seen God, but His love is made complete when we love one another. God's Spirit living in us confirms that we belong to Him. John testifies that the Father sent His Son to be the Savior of the world, and anyone who confesses Jesus as the Son of God has God living in them.", "4:19": "John teaches that God's love is perfected in us, giving us confidence for the day of judgment. Perfect love drives out fear, for fear involves punishment. Those who live in fear have not yet fully embraced God’s perfect love. We love because God first loved us.", "4:21": "John concludes by stressing the necessity of loving others. A person cannot claim to love God while hating his brother. The commandment is clear: those who love God must also love their brothers and sisters in Christ."}, "1 John 5": {"5:5": "John emphasizes that those who believe that Jesus is the Christ are born of God. The love for God is shown by keeping His commandments, which are not burdensome. Through faith in Jesus, believers overcome the world.", "5:10": "John speaks of the testimony of the water, the blood, and the Spirit that bear witness to Jesus. Those who believe in the Son of God accept God's testimony about His Son, while those who reject it make God a liar.", "5:13": "John assures believers that eternal life is found in Jesus Christ. Those who have the Son have life, and those who do not have the Son of God do not have life. This letter is written to affirm their assurance of eternal life in Him.", "5:15": "John reminds the believers that if they ask anything according to God's will, He hears them. If they know that God hears their prayers, they can be confident that He will answer them.", "5:17": "John encourages believers to pray for fellow Christians who commit sins that do not lead to death, knowing that God will give life. However, there is a sin that leads to death, for which John does not recommend prayer.", "5:21": "John affirms that those who are born of God do not continue in sin. God protects them, and the evil one cannot touch them. John concludes with a reminder that believers are of God, that Jesus gives them true understanding, and they are in Him who is true—Jesus Christ, the true God and eternal life."}, "2 John 1": {"1:1–3": "John addresses the letter to a chosen lady and her children, whom he loves in the truth, extending blessings of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor, mercy, and peace from God the Father and Jesus Christ.", "1:5–6": "John expresses joy that some of the lady’s children are walking in truth, and he urges her to continue following the commandment to love one another, as they were taught from the beginning. True love is demonstrated by living according to God’s commandments.", "1:9": "John warns the believers about deceivers who deny that Jesus came in the flesh, calling them antichrists. He urges them to stay vigilant and continue in Christ’s teaching, as those who abandon His doctrine do not have God. Those who remain in the teaching of Christ have both the Father and the Son.", "1:11": "John instructs the believers not to welcome or support anyone who promotes false teachings about Christ. To do so would make them complicit in the false teacher’s evil deeds.", "1:13": "John concludes by expressing his hope to visit them in person soon to share more, so that their joy may be complete. He also sends greetings from the children of her sister."}, "3 John 1": {"1:3–4": "The apostle John greets Gaius, expressing his love for him and praying for his well-being in all things. John rejoices in hearing that Gaius is walking in the truth and emphasizes that his greatest joy is seeing his spiritual children living in the truth.", "1:8": "John commends Gaius for his faithful hospitality toward traveling ministers, both to fellow believers and strangers. He encourages Gaius to continue supporting them in their ministry, as they have gone out for the sake of Christ, relying on the support of the church rather than unbelievers.", "1:10": "John warns Gaius about Diotrephes, a leader in the church who seeks to have authority over others. Diotrephes refuses to welcome John’s messengers and speaks maliciously against them. John plans to address Diotrephes’ actions when he visits, as Diotrephes not only refuses hospitality but also prevents others from showing kindness and expels them from the church.", "1:12": "John encourages Gaius to imitate what is good and not evil, explaining that those who do good belong to God, while those who do evil do not know God. He also speaks highly of Demetrius, who has a good reputation among all believers and is well-regarded for his commitment to the truth.", "1:13–14": "John closes his letter by expressing his desire to visit Gaius and speak in person, rather than continuing the conversation through writing. He sends peace and greetings from mutual friends and asks Gaius to greet the believers there by name."}, "Jude 1": {"1:1–2": "Jude, the brother of James, addresses his letter to those who are called, sanctified by God the Father, and preserved in Jesus Christ, offering them mercy, peace, and unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor in abundance.", "1:3–4": "Jude urges believers to contend earnestly for the faith, warning that certain ungodly people have secretly slipped into the church, transposing the unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor of God into lasciviousness and denying Jesus Christ as their Lord and Master.", "1:5–7": "Jude reminds the believers of how God delivered the Israelites from Egypt but later destroyed those who did not believe. He also recalls the fallen angels, who are kept in darkness until judgment, and the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah, who serve as examples of the consequences of immoral living.", "1:9–10": "Jude condemns false teachers for their irreverent behavior, noting that even the archangel Michael, in his dispute with the devil, did not bring accusations but relied on the Lord’s rebuke. In contrast, these men slander what they don’t understand and are destroyed by their corrupt instincts.", "1:12–13": "Jude pronounces woe upon these false teachers, likening them to Cain, Balaam, and Korah, who were driven by greed and rebellion. He describes them as dangerous blemishes in the church, useless and fruitless, destined for eternal darkness.", "1:16": "Jude quotes Enoch’s prophecy of the Lord’s coming judgment, declaring that Christ will return with His holy ones to judge the ungodly for their sinful deeds and blasphemous words. These false teachers are characterized by grumbling, lust, arrogance, and flattery for personal gain.", "1:20–21": "Jude reminds the believers of the apostles' warning that in the last days, there will be mockers driven by ungodly desires. He encourages them to build themselves up in their faith, praying in the Holy Spirit, and keeping themselves in God’s love as they await the mercy of Jesus Christ for eternal life.", "1:22–23": "Jude encourages the believers to show mercy to those who doubt and to rescue others from sin, doing so with fear and avoiding the contamination of sin themselves.", "1:24–25": "Jude concludes with a beautiful doxology, praising God, who is able to keep believers from stumbling and present them blameless before His glorious presence with great joy. To Him be all glory, majesty, dominion, and power forever."}, "Revelation 1": {"1:1–3": "John introduces the book as a revelation given by Jesus Christ, which God showed him through an angel to reveal future events. John records what he saw and testifies of the word of God and Jesus Christ. A blessing is pronounced upon those who read, hear, and obey the words of this prophecy, as the time is near.", "1:4–6": "John greets the seven churches in Asia, extending unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor and peace from God, who is eternal, and from the seven Spirits before His throne, and from Jesus Christ, the faithful witness, the firstborn from the dead, and ruler of the kings of the earth. John praises Jesus for loving us, freeing us from our sins by His blood, and making us a kingdom of priests to serve God.", "1:8": "John declares the coming of Jesus in the clouds, visible to all, even those who pierced Him. People from all over the world will mourn because of Him. Jesus proclaims Himself as the Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end, who is, who was, and who is to come—the Almighty.", "1:11": "John identifies himself as a brother and companion in suffering for the kingdom of Christ. While on the island of Patmos because of his testimony for Jesus, he was in the Spirit on the Lord’s Day and heard a loud voice like a trumpet, instructing him to write what he saw and send it to the seven churches.", "1:14–16": "John turns and sees Jesus standing among seven golden lampstands, dressed in a long robe with a golden sash around His chest. His hair was white like wool, His eyes blazed like fire, and His feet were like glowing bronze. His voice thundered like rushing waters, and He held seven stars in His right hand. A sharp, double-edged sword came from His mouth, and His face shone like the sun.", "1:17–18": "Overwhelmed by the sight of the glorified Christ, John falls at His feet as though dead. But Jesus places His right hand on John and reassures him, declaring that He is the First and the Last, the Living One who was dead but is now alive forever, holding the keys of death and Hades.", "1:19–20": "Jesus instructs John to write what he has seen, what is now, and what will take place. He explains the mystery of the seven stars and seven lampstands. The seven stars represent the angels of the seven churches, and the seven lampstands are the seven churches."}, "Revelation 2": {"2:7": "Jesus commends the church in Ephesus for their hard work, perseverance, and discernment in testing false apostles. However, He warns them that they have abandoned their first love and urges them to repent and return to their earlier deeds, or He will remove their lampstand. He praises them for rejecting the practices of the Nicolaitans, which He also hates. Those who overcome will eat from the tree of life in the paradise of God.", "2:11": "Jesus acknowledges the suffering and poverty of the church in Smyrna, yet He calls them spiritually rich. He is aware of the slander they endure from those who claim to be Jews but are not. He encourages them not to fear the persecution they will face, including imprisonment and trials, and promises the crown of life to those who remain faithful until death. Those who overcome will not be harmed by the second death.", "2:17": "The church in Pergamos is commended for holding fast to Jesus’ name despite living in a place referred to as Satan’s seat, where persecution is severe. However, they are criticized for tolerating those who follow the teachings of Balaam, leading to idolatry and immorality, and for accepting the practices of the Nicolaitans. Jesus calls them to repent or face His judgment. Those who overcome will receive hidden manna and a white stone with a new name written on it.", "2:29": "Jesus commends the church in Thyatira for their love, service, faith, patience, and growing good works. However, He rebukes them for tolerating Jezebel, a false prophetess who leads people into immorality and idolatry. Jesus gives her time to repent, but since she refuses, judgment will come upon her and those who follow her. For the faithful who have not embraced false teachings, Jesus encourages them to hold fast until His return, promising that those who overcome will rule the nations with Him and receive the morning star."}, "Revelation 3": {"3:6": "The message to the church in Sardis warns that although they appear alive, they are spiritually dead. Jesus calls them to wake up and strengthen what remains before it dies completely. He encourages them to repent, hold fast to the truth they received, and watch, lest His coming be sudden like a thief. Those who remain faithful will walk with Him, clothed in white, and their names will remain in the book of life.", "3:13": "The message to the church in Philadelphia commends them for their faithfulness despite their little strength. Jesus assures them that no one can shut the door He has opened for them and promises to protect them from the hour of trial that will come upon the earth. Those who overcome will be made pillars in God’s temple and bear the name of God and the new Jerusalem.", "3:22": "The message to the church in Laodicea rebukes them for being lukewarm—neither hot nor cold—and Jesus warns that He will spit them out unless they repent. He counsels them to seek spiritual riches, purity, and true vision from Him. Jesus offers His love and rebuke, urging them to be earnest and repent. Those who overcome will share in His throne, as He overcame and now sits with the Father."}, "Revelation 4": {"4:5–6": "John is shown a vision of heaven where he sees a door open, and he hears a voice calling him up. He is immediately in the Spirit and sees a throne with someone sitting on it, described in radiant glory. Surrounding the throne are 24 elders seated on thrones, clothed in white and wearing crowns of gold. From the throne come flashes of lightning, rumblings, and peals of thunder, with seven lamps representing the seven Spirits of God.", "4:7–8": "In front of the throne, John sees a sea of glass, clear as crystal. Around the throne are four living creatures, full of eyes in front and behind. The first is like a lion, the second like an ox, the third has a face like a man, and the fourth is like a flying eagle. These creatures, with six wings each, never cease to declare God’s holiness, saying, Holy, holy, holy, Lord God Almighty, who was, and is, and is to come.", "4:9–11": "Whenever the four living creatures give glory, honor, and thanks to the One seated on the throne, the 24 elders fall down and worship Him. They lay their crowns before the throne and declare that the Lord is worthy to receive glory, honor, and power because He created all things, and by His will, they exist and were created."}, "Revelation 5": {"5:3–4": "John sees a scroll sealed with seven seals in the right hand of God, and a strong angel proclaims a search for someone worthy to open it. No one in heaven, on earth, or under the earth is found worthy to open the scroll or even to look at it, causing John to weep.", "5:7–8": "One of the elders tells John not to weep because the Lion of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has triumphed and is worthy to open the scroll. John sees a Lamb that looks as though it has been slain, standing in the center of the throne, symbolizing Jesus Christ. The Lamb has seven horns and seven eyes, representing the fullness of the Spirit of God sent out into the earth, and He takes the scroll from God's hand.", "5:9–10": "When the Lamb takes the scroll, the four living creatures and the twenty-four elders fall down before Him. They each hold harps and golden bowls filled with incense, representing the prayers of God’s people. They sing a new song, praising the Lamb for being worthy to open the scroll because He was slain and redeemed people from every nation through His blood, making them kings and priests to serve God and reign on earth.", "5:11–12": "John hears the voices of thousands upon thousands of angels encircling the throne, the living creatures, and the elders, all proclaiming that the Lamb is worthy to receive power, wealth, wisdom, strength, honor, glory, and praise.", "5:13–14": "John hears every creature in heaven, on earth, under the earth, and in the sea praising both the One seated on the throne and the Lamb. They give them blessing, honor, glory, and power forever. The living creatures respond with Amen, and the elders fall down and worship."}, "Revelation 6": {"6:1–2": "The first seal reveals a rider on a white horse, symbolizing conquest. The rider is given a bow and a crown, and he rides out to conquer, signifying a time of victory and power.", "6:3–4": "The second seal unleashes a rider on a red horse, representing war and bloodshed. This rider is given the power to remove peace from the earth, leading to widespread conflict and violence.", "6:5–6": "The third seal reveals a rider on a black horse, symbolizing famine. The rider carries scales, and the voice describes inflated prices for basic foodstuffs, indicating a time of scarcity and economic hardship, though luxury goods like oil and wine remain untouched.", "6:7–8": "The fourth seal brings forth a pale horse, whose rider is named Death, followed closely by Hell. This rider is given authority over a quarter of the earth, bringing death through violence, famine, plague, and wild animals.", "6:9–11": "The fifth seal reveals the souls of martyrs who have been slain for their testimony of God. They cry out for justice, asking how long before their blood is avenged. They are given white robes and told to rest until the full number of martyrs is completed.", "6:15–17": "The sixth seal brings a cataclysmic earthquake, causing cosmic disturbances such as the darkening of the sun, the moon turning to blood, and the stars falling from the sky. The heavens roll up like a scroll, and mountains and islands are displaced. All people—regardless of status—are terrified and seek to hide from the impending wrath of God and the Lamb"}, "Revelation 7": {"7:8": "John sees four angels holding back the winds to prevent harm until the servants of God are sealed on their foreheads. He hears the number of those sealed—144,000 from the twelve tribes of Israel, with 12,000 from each tribe.", "7:11–12": "After the sealing, John sees a great multitude from every nation and language, too vast to count, standing before the throne and the Lamb. They are clothed in white robes and hold palm branches, crying out that salvation belongs to God and the Lamb. The angels, elders, and living creatures worship God with a declaration of praise.", "7:15–17": "An elder explains that the multitude in white robes are those who have come out of the great tribulation, having washed their robes in the blood of the Lamb. Because of this, they stand before God's throne and serve Him continually. The Lamb will care for them, guiding them to living waters, and God will wipe away every tear from their eyes."}, "Revelation 8": {"8:3–5": "The opening of the seventh seal brings a profound silence in heaven for half an hour. Seven angels are given seven trumpets, and another angel offers incense with the prayers of the saints before God. The angel then throws the censer, filled with fire from the altar, onto the earth, resulting in thunder, lightning, and a great earthquake, signaling the beginning of judgment.", "8:6–7": "When the first trumpet sounds, hail and fire mixed with blood are thrown to the earth, burning up a third of the trees and all the green grass, symbolizing destruction and judgment upon the earth’s vegetation.", "8:8–9": "At the sound of the second trumpet, a massive burning object like a mountain is cast into the sea, turning a third of the sea to blood, killing a third of marine life, and destroying a third of the ships, symbolizing devastation to the oceans.", "8:10–11": "The third trumpet brings a great star, named Wormwood, falling from the sky. It poisons a third of the rivers and fresh water, making the waters bitter and causing many people to die.", "8:12": "With the sounding of the fourth trumpet, a third of the sun, moon, and stars are darkened, reducing the amount of light reaching the earth by a third, bringing partial darkness over both day and night, signifying cosmic disturbance.", "8:13": "An eagle angel flies through the sky, warning of the three remaining trumpets that will bring even greater woes upon the earth. These final three trumpets signal the most severe judgments yet to come."}, "Revelation 9": {"9:5–6": "At the sounding of the fifth trumpet, a star falls from heaven to earth, and the key to the bottomless pit is given. When the pit is opened, smoke and locusts emerge, but these locusts are commanded not to harm the earth or those sealed by God. Instead, they torment humanity for five months with painful stings, causing great suffering.", "9:11–12": "The locusts are described with terrifying features: like horses prepared for battle, wearing crowns of gold, with faces like men, hair like women, and teeth like lions. Their wings sound like chariots rushing into battle, and their tails are like scorpions, capable of stinging. They are ruled by a king, the angel of the abyss, named Abaddon (Hebrew) or Apollyon (Greek), meaning Destroyer.", "9:16–17": "The sixth trumpet sounds, and a voice commands the release of four angels bound at the Euphrates River. These angels, prepared for a specific moment in time, are released to kill a third of mankind. They are followed by an army of 200 million horsemen.", "9:18–19": "The horses in John's vision have heads like lions, and from their mouths come fire, smoke, and brimstone, killing a third of mankind. Their tails are like serpents, which also cause harm.", "9:20–21": "Despite the devastation and plagues, those who survive refuse to repent of their evil deeds, continuing to worship idols and persist in their sins—murder, sorcery, immorality, and theft."}, "Revelation 10": {"10:3–4": "John sees a mighty angel descending from heaven, clothed in a cloud with a rainbow over his head, and his face shining like the sun. The angel has a little book in his hand and stands with one foot on the sea and one foot on the land. He cries out with a loud voice, and seven thunders speak, but John is instructed not to write down what the thunders have revealed.", "10:7": "The mighty angel raises his hand to heaven and swears by God, the Creator of all things, that there will be no more delay. When the seventh angel sounds his trumpet, the mystery of God will be fulfilled, as declared to the prophets.", "10:10–11": "John is instructed to take the little book from the angel and eat it. The angel tells him that the book will taste sweet in his mouth but will make his stomach bitter. After eating the book, John finds it sweet as honey but experiences bitterness in his stomach. The angel tells John that he must prophesy again to many nations, peoples, languages, and kings."}, "Revelation 11": {"11:1–2": "John is given a reed to measure the temple of God, but he is told not to measure the outer court, which is given to the Gentiles, who will trample the holy city for forty-two months.", "11:5–6": "God gives authority to two witnesses who will prophesy for 1,260 days, clothed in sackcloth. They are described as olive trees and candlesticks standing before God. They have power to call down fire, stop rain, turn water into blood, and bring plagues upon the earth.", "11:9–10": "After completing their testimony, the two witnesses are killed by the beast from the abyss. Their bodies lie in the streets of the great city, which spiritually represents Sodom and Egypt, where Jesus was crucified. People from all nations will see their bodies and celebrate their death, exchanging gifts in their joy.", "11:11–12": "Three and a half days after their death, the breath of life from God enters the two witnesses, and they stand up, causing great fear among those who see them. A voice from heaven calls them up, and they ascend to heaven in a cloud while their enemies watch.", "11:13–14": "At the same time as the witnesses’ ascension, a massive earthquake strikes, causing a tenth of the city to collapse and killing seven thousand people. The survivors are terrified and give glory to God. The second woe is now over, and the third woe is approaching quickly.", "11:17–18": "When the seventh trumpet sounds, loud voices in heaven proclaim that the kingdoms of the world have become the kingdoms of the Lord and His Christ, and He will reign forever. The twenty-four elders fall on their faces and worship God, thanking Him for His power and reign. They acknowledge the coming judgment and reward for the prophets, saints, and those who fear God, as well as the destruction of those who destroy the earth.", "11:19": "The chapter concludes with the opening of God's temple in heaven, where the ark of the covenant is revealed. Lightning, thunder, an earthquake, and a hailstorm follow this divine revelation, signaling the awe and majesty of God's presence."}, "Revelation 12": {"12:1–2": "John describes a great sign in heaven: a woman clothed with the sun, with the moon under her feet and a crown of twelve stars on her head. She is pregnant and in the pains of childbirth.", "12:3–4": "Another sign appears: a great red dragon with seven heads, ten horns, and seven crowns. The dragon’s tail sweeps a third of the stars from the sky and hurls them to the earth. The dragon stands ready to devour the woman’s child as soon as it is born.", "12:5–6": "The woman gives birth to a male child who is destined to rule all nations with an iron rod. The child is caught up to God and His throne, while the woman flees to the wilderness, where she is cared for by God for 1,260 days.", "12:9": "A great war breaks out in heaven. Michael and his angels fight against the dragon and his angels. The dragon is defeated and no longer has a place in heaven. The great dragon, also known as the Devil or Satan, who deceives the whole world, is cast down to earth along with his angels.", "12:12": "A loud voice in heaven proclaims victory: the salvation, strength, and kingdom of God have come because the accuser, Satan, has been cast down. The faithful overcame him by the blood of the Lamb and by their testimony, not loving their lives even unto death. Heaven rejoices, but woe is pronounced on the earth and sea, as Satan comes down in great anger, knowing his time is short.", "12:13–14": "The dragon, now cast to the earth, pursues the woman who gave birth to the child. But the woman is given the wings of a great eagle to escape into the wilderness, where she is protected and nourished for a time, times, and half a time, safe from the serpent.", "12:15–17": "The serpent tries to destroy the woman by spewing a flood from his mouth to sweep her away, but the earth helps the woman by swallowing the flood."}, "Revelation 13": {"13:3–4": "John describes a vision of a beast rising from the sea with seven heads, ten horns, and ten crowns, representing blasphemous authority and power. The beast resembles a combination of dangerous animals, and it receives its authority from the dragon (Satan). The world is amazed when the beast recovers from a deadly wound, and people begin to worship both the dragon and the beast.", "13:9–10": "The beast is given authority to blaspheme against God and wage war against the saints for 42 months. He is granted control over all nations and people, and everyone whose name is not written in the Lamb’s book of life will worship him. John calls for those who hear to heed this message, encouraging believers to endure and remain faithful in the face of persecution.", "13:13–15": "John sees another beast rising from the earth, which has two horns like a lamb but speaks like a dragon. This beast wields the authority of the first beast and compels the world to worship the first beast by performing miraculous signs, including making fire fall from heaven. He deceives people into creating an image of the first beast and even brings life to this image, forcing everyone to worship it or face death.", "13:18": "The second beast enforces the mark of the beast, requiring everyone to receive this mark on their right hand or forehead in order to buy or sell. The mark represents the name or number of the beast, and John reveals that the number is 666, calling for wisdom to discern its meaning."}, "Revelation 14": {"14:4–5": "John sees the Lamb standing on Mount Zion with 144,000 people, each marked with the name of the Father. These redeemed ones sing a new song that only they can learn. They are described as pure and blameless, following the Lamb wherever He goes, and being firstfruits to God.", "14:11": "Three angels deliver messages of warning to the world. The first calls for people to fear God and worship Him because the hour of His judgment has come. The second announces the fall of Babylon, the city that caused nations to drink the wine of her immorality. The third angel warns that anyone who worships the beast or receives its mark will face eternal torment in the presence of the Lamb.", "14:12–13": "John emphasizes the endurance of the saints, those who keep God’s commandments and remain faithful to Jesus. A voice from heaven pronounces a blessing on those who die in the Lord, saying they will find rest from their labors, and their deeds will follow them.", "14:14–16": "John sees a vision of the Son of Man seated on a cloud with a golden crown and a sharp sickle in His hand. An angel from the temple instructs Him to reap the earth, for the time of harvest has come. The Son of Man swings His sickle, and the earth is harvested.", "14:19–20": "Another angel emerges from the temple, also with a sharp sickle, and another angel from the altar commands him to gather the grapes from the earth, representing the wicked. The grapes are thrown into the winepress of God’s wrath, and the blood flows as deep as a horse's bridle for 1,600 furlongs (about 180 miles)."}, "Revelation 15": {"15:2": "John witnesses a vision of seven angels holding the final plagues, representing the culmination of God’s wrath. He also sees victorious believers, those who overcame the beast, his image, his mark, and his number, standing on a sea of glass mixed with fire, holding harps of God.", "15:4": "The victorious believers sing a song of praise, the song of Moses and the Lamb, glorifying God for His great works and righteous judgments. They declare that all nations will come to worship God, acknowledging His holiness and justice.", "15:8": "John describes the seven angels coming out of the heavenly temple, dressed in pure white linen with golden sashes. One of the four living creatures gives them seven golden bowls filled with the wrath of God. The temple is then filled with smoke from God’s glory and power, and no one can enter until the seven plagues are completed."}, "Revelation 16": {"16:2": "The first angel pours out the bowl of God's wrath on the earth, causing painful sores to break out on those who had the mark of the beast and worshiped his image.", "16:3": "The second angel pours out the bowl on the sea, turning it into blood, and every living creature in the sea dies.", "16:7": "The third angel pours out the bowl on the rivers and springs, turning them to blood. The angel praises God’s righteousness in His judgment, declaring it just, as those who shed the blood of the saints and prophets are now given blood to drink.", "16:9": "The fourth angel pours out the bowl on the sun, causing it to scorch people with intense heat. Despite the suffering, they refuse to repent or glorify God, instead blaspheming His name.", "16:11": "The fifth angel pours out the bowl on the throne of the beast, plunging his kingdom into darkness. People gnaw their tongues in agony but continue to blaspheme God and refuse to repent.", "16:16": "The sixth angel pours out the bowl on the Euphrates River, drying it up to prepare the way for the kings of the east. Unclean spirits emerge from the mouths of the dragon, the beast, and the false prophet, performing signs to gather the kings of the earth for battle on the great day of God Almighty at Armageddon.", "16:21": "The seventh angel pours out the bowl into the air, and a loud voice from the throne declares, It is done! A massive earthquake strikes the earth, the great city is split into three parts, the cities of the nations collapse, and Babylon the Great receives the full measure of God's wrath. Islands and mountains disappear, and great hailstones, each weighing about a talent, fall upon people, who continue to blaspheme God because of the severity of the plague."}, "Revelation 17": {"17:6": "One of the seven angels reveals to John the judgment of the great harlot, a symbolic figure representing corruption and idolatry. The kings of the earth have committed spiritual fornication with her, and the people of the earth are influenced by her ways. John sees her riding a scarlet beast and adorned in luxury, holding a cup filled with abominations. She is called Babylon the Great, symbolizing a system of wickedness, and is drunk with the blood of the saints and martyrs of Jesus.", "17:11": "The angel begins to explain the mystery of the beast. The beast, which was and is not, will ascend from the abyss and head toward destruction. The people of the earth, whose names are not written in the book of life, will be amazed by the beast. The seven heads of the beast represent seven mountains and also seven kings. Five of these kings have already fallen, one currently reigns, and one is yet to come.", "17:14": "The ten horns represent ten kings who have not yet received their kingdoms but will be given authority for a short time alongside the beast. They will unite in their purpose and give their power to the beast. However, they will wage war against the Lamb, but the Lamb (Jesus Christ) will overcome them because He is the Lord of lords and King of kings, and His followers are the called, chosen, and faithful.", "17:18": "The angel explains that the waters represent the diverse peoples and nations influenced by the harlot. The ten kings, represented by the horns, will eventually turn against the harlot, destroy her, and burn her with fire. This turn of events is part of God’s plan to bring judgment upon her. The woman symbolizes the great city that holds dominion over the rulers of the earth."}, "Revelation 18": {"18:3": "The angel proclaims the fall of Babylon, describing it as a place of spiritual corruption, filled with evil spirits and unclean things. Babylon’s influence has spread worldwide, leading nations, kings, and merchants into sinful practices and indulgence.", "18:8": "A voice from heaven calls God’s people to separate themselves from Babylon, to avoid sharing in her sins and receiving her punishment. Babylon’s arrogance and indulgence will be met with sudden destruction as God’s judgment comes upon her.", "18:13": "The kings of the earth, merchants, and sailors who prospered through their connection to Babylon mourn her sudden destruction. They lament the loss of wealth and luxury that Babylon provided through her vast trade network.", "18:19": "Merchants and sailors grieve the swift destruction of Babylon, which was once a center of wealth and luxury. They weep over the loss of the city that enriched them, astonished at how quickly everything has been brought to ruin.", "18:21": "While the earth mourns Babylon's fall, heaven rejoices, for God’s justice has been carried out. A mighty angel dramatically declares that Babylon’s downfall is final, like a stone cast into the sea, never to rise again.", "18:24": "Babylon, once a bustling city filled with music, craftsmanship, and celebration, will be silenced forever. Her merchants, once powerful, deceived the nations through corruption and sorcery. Babylon is held accountable for the blood of the prophets, saints, and all who were slain on earth."}, "Revelation 19": {"19:5": "Heaven rejoices over the fall of Babylon, praising God for His true and righteous judgments. The multitude in heaven shouts Alleluia as the smoke of Babylon’s destruction rises forever. The twenty-four elders and the four living creatures worship God, and a voice from the throne calls for all God’s servants to praise Him.", "19:9": "The heavenly multitude praises God for His reign, and they rejoice at the announcement of the marriage of the Lamb. The bride, symbolizing the church, has made herself ready, clothed in fine linen representing the righteousness of the saints. A blessing is pronounced on those invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb.", "19:10": "John is overwhelmed and falls at the feet of the angel to worship him, but the angel tells him not to do so. The angel reminds John that he is a fellow servant and that only God is to be worshiped. The testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy.", "19:16": "John sees heaven open and Christ, called Faithful and True, riding a white horse. His eyes blaze like fire, and He wears many crowns. His robe is dipped in blood, and His name is the Word of God. He leads the heavenly armies, and out of His mouth comes a sharp sword to strike the nations. He rules with an iron scepter and treads the winepress of God’s wrath. His title is King of kings and Lord of lords.", "19:21": "An angel calls all the birds to gather for God’s great feast, to devour the flesh of the defeated kings, captains, and warriors. The beast and the kings of the earth gather their armies to fight against Christ and His heavenly host, but the beast and the false prophet are captured and thrown alive into the lake of fire. The rest of their forces are slain by the sword from Christ’s mouth, and the birds gorge on their flesh."}, "Revelation 20": {"20:1–3": "An angel descends from heaven, binding Satan for a thousand years and casting him into the bottomless pit to prevent him from deceiving the nations. After the thousand years, Satan will be released for a short time.", "20:4–6": "Those who were martyred for their faith in Christ and refused to worship the beast or receive his mark are resurrected and reign with Christ for a thousand years. This is the first resurrection, and those who participate in it are blessed and holy, immune to the second death.", "20:7–10": "After the thousand years, Satan is released to deceive the nations once more. He gathers them for a final battle against the saints, but fire from heaven consumes them, and Satan is thrown into the lake of fire, joining the beast and the false prophet in eternal torment.", "20:13–15": "John describes the final judgment before the great white throne. The dead are judged according to their works as recorded in the books, and those whose names are not found in the book of life are cast into the lake of fire. Death and hell are also thrown into the lake of fire, signifying the second death."}, "Revelation 21": {"21:3–4": "John witnesses a vision of the new heaven and new earth, with the holy city, New Jerusalem, descending from heaven like a bride. God will dwell with His people, wiping away every tear, and there will be no more death, sorrow, or pain because the former things have passed away.", "21:8": "God declares from His throne that He is making all things new. He offers the water of life freely to those who thirst and promises that those who overcome will inherit all things and be called His children. However, those who live in sin and reject God will face eternal separation in the lake of fire, which is the second death.", "21:12–14": "An angel shows John the New Jerusalem, the bride of the Lamb, descending from heaven in the glory of God. The city shines like a precious jewel and has twelve gates, with the names of the twelve tribes of Israel, and twelve foundations, with the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.", "21:18–21": "The angel measures the New Jerusalem, a city that is perfectly square in length, width, and height. Its walls are made of jasper, and the city itself is pure gold, as clear as glass. The foundations of the city’s walls are adorned with twelve precious stones, and the twelve gates are each made of a single pearl. The streets are made of pure, transparent gold.", "21:24–27": "In the New Jerusalem, there is no need for a temple because God and the Lamb are its temple. The glory of God illuminates the city, and the Lamb is its light. The saved nations will walk in its light, and the kings of the earth will bring their splendor into it. The gates will never close because there will be no night, and nothing impure will ever enter the city—only those whose names are written in the Lamb’s book of life."}, "Revelation 22": {"22:1–2": "John is shown the pure, life-giving river that flows from God's throne. Along its banks grows the tree of life, which bears fruit each month and whose leaves bring healing to the nations.", "22:3–5": "The curse that plagued creation will be lifted, and God's servants will serve Him in His eternal kingdom. They will see His face, bear His name, and live in His eternal light, reigning with Him forever.", "22:6–7": "The angel confirms that the words of this prophecy are trustworthy and true. Jesus announces that His return is near and blesses those who keep the teachings of this book.", "22:8–9": "John is overwhelmed by the vision and attempts to worship the angel, but the angel redirects him to worship only God, reminding him that they are both servants of God.", "22:10–11": "Unlike earlier prophecies that were sealed, the message of Revelation is not to be sealed because the time is near. People will continue in their ways, but God’s final judgment is imminent.", "22:14–15": "Jesus declares that He is coming soon, bringing rewards for each person according to their deeds. He reaffirms that He is the beginning and the end, and He blesses those who follow His commandments, granting them access to the tree of life and entry into the holy city.", "22:16–17": "Jesus sends His angel to testify to the churches, affirming that He is the promised Messiah, the root of David, and the bright morning star. The Spirit and the church invite everyone to come and drink freely from the water of life.", "22:18–19": "A stern warning is given to anyone who adds to or takes away from the words of this prophecy: those who add will face the plagues, and those who take away will lose their share in the tree of life and the holy city.", "22:20–21": "Jesus’ final words affirm that He is coming quickly. John’s response is a heartfelt plea: Come, Lord Jesus. The chapter closes with a blessing of unmerited, unearned, undeserved favor from Jesus to all."}};
